-<div class="cx" data-flist data-fl-limit="10"><div class="fl-bar"><div class="fl-seg" role="group" aria-label="Filter by status" data-fl-key="s"><button type="button" data-v="" aria-pressed="true">All 61</button><button type="button" data-v="v" aria-pressed="false">Validated 24</button><button type="button" data-v="m" aria-pressed="false">Modelled 37</button></div><div class="fl-seg" role="group" aria-label="Filter by group" data-fl-key="g"><button type="button" data-v="" aria-pressed="true">Every group</button><button type="button" data-v="Orbits" aria-pressed="false">Orbits</button><button type="button" data-v="Timing" aria-pressed="false">Timing</button><button type="button" data-v="Inertial" aria-pressed="false">Inertial</button><button type="button" data-v="Lunar" aria-pressed="false">Lunar</button><button type="button" data-v="GNSS" aria-pressed="false">GNSS</button><button type="button" data-v="Resilience" aria-pressed="false">Resilience</button><button type="button" data-v="Interop" aria-pressed="false">Interop</button><button type="button" data-v="Machine learning" aria-pressed="false">Machine learning</button><button type="button" data-v="Maritime" aria-pressed="false">Maritime</button></div><label class="fl-q"><span class="vh">Search the capabilities</span><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-search"/></svg><input type="search" data-fl-q placeholder="Search capability, domain or proof"></label><output class="fl-count" data-fl-count aria-live="polite"></output></div><div class="cx-rows"><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Orbits" data-q="orbit propagation &amp; constellation design orbit &amp; geometry orbits sgp4/sdp4 propagation from real two-line elements (a committed celestrak gps snapshot) or synthetic walker constellations whose mean elements realise the i:t/p/f formula to under 1 km over 24 h. multi-constellation visibility, dilution of precision and gnss availability, a constellation-design optimiser, streets-of-coverage sizing, and a walker design sweep that reports the pareto-optimal designs over a planes × satellites grid. 666/666 aiaa vectors · 4.12 mm orbit-sgp4-gps american institute of aeronautics and astronautics simplified general perturbations 4, an orbit model simplified deep-space perturbations 4, an orbit model global positioning system global navigation satellite system"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Orbit propagation &amp; constellation design</b><span class="cx-d">Orbit &amp; geometry</span><span class=cx-p>666/666 AIAA vectors · 4.12 mm</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>SGP4/SDP4 propagation from real two-line elements (a committed Celestrak GPS snapshot) or synthetic Walker constellations whose mean elements realise the i:T/P/F formula to under 1 km over 24 h. Multi-constellation visibility, dilution of precision and GNSS availability, a constellation-design optimiser, streets-of-coverage sizing, and a Walker design sweep that reports the Pareto-optimal designs over a planes × satellites grid.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>AIAA</b> American Institute of Aeronautics and Astronautics · <b>SGP4</b> Simplified General Perturbations 4, an orbit model · <b>SDP4</b> Simplified Deep-space Perturbations 4, an orbit model · <b>GPS</b> Global Positioning System · <b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=orbit-sgp4-gps&amp;tab=overview">Run orbit-sgp4-gps in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Orbits" data-q="cowell propagator &amp; six-perturbation force model numerical propagator orbits a numerical (cowell) propagator complementing the analytic sgp4/sdp4 path, with a hierarchical force model: two-body + the full j2–j6 zonal field, an optional egm2008 tesseral geopotential to degree/order 70, epoch-driven sun and moon third-body gravity (a built-in ephemeris, no de/spk kernel), solar-radiation pressure with a conical umbra/penumbra shadow, atmospheric drag, the post-newtonian schwarzschild relativistic correction, and the lense–thirring frame-dragging term — driven by a choice of rk4 step-doubling or dormand–prince rk5(4) adaptive integrators. conservative tiers &lt; 0.08 m / 24 h vs orekit 12.2  simplified general perturbations 4, an orbit model simplified deep-space perturbations 4, an orbit model earth gravitational model 2008"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Cowell propagator &amp; six-perturbation force model</b><span class="cx-d">Numerical propagator</span><span class=cx-p>conservative tiers &lt; 0.08 m / 24 h vs Orekit 12.2</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A numerical (Cowell) propagator complementing the analytic SGP4/SDP4 path, with a hierarchical force model: two-body + the full J2–J6 zonal field, an optional EGM2008 tesseral geopotential to degree/order 70, epoch-driven Sun and Moon third-body gravity (a built-in ephemeris, no DE/SPK kernel), solar-radiation pressure with a conical umbra/penumbra shadow, atmospheric drag, the post-Newtonian Schwarzschild relativistic correction, and the Lense–Thirring frame-dragging term — driven by a choice of RK4 step-doubling or Dormand–Prince RK5(4) adaptive integrators.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>SGP4</b> Simplified General Perturbations 4, an orbit model · <b>SDP4</b> Simplified Deep-space Perturbations 4, an orbit model · <b>EGM2008</b> Earth Gravitational Model 2008</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Orbits" data-q="burns, lambert &amp; porkchop maneuvers &amp; trajectory design orbits impulsive δv nodes with 6×6 covariance propagation, finite-burn integration checked against the tsiolkovsky rocket equation to under 0.01 %, an izzo-2015 single-revolution lambert solver and an exact universal-variable kepler propagator, and a porkchop (launch × arrival) c3 / arrival-v∞ sweep emitted as a json contour grid — the performance-simulation layer above gmat/orekit. tsiolkovsky &lt; 0.01 % · lambert round-trips to two-body truth  javascript object notation"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Burns, Lambert &amp; porkchop</b><span class="cx-d">Maneuvers &amp; trajectory design</span><span class=cx-p>Tsiolkovsky &lt; 0.01 % · Lambert round-trips to two-body truth</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Impulsive ΔV nodes with 6×6 covariance propagation, finite-burn integration checked against the Tsiolkovsky rocket equation to under 0.01 %, an Izzo-2015 single-revolution Lambert solver and an exact universal-variable Kepler propagator, and a porkchop (launch × arrival) C3 / arrival-V∞ sweep emitted as a JSON contour grid — the performance-simulation layer above GMAT/Orekit.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>JSON</b> JavaScript Object Notation</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Timing" data-q="time scales &amp; earth-orientation frames time systems timing iers leap-second utc / tai / tt / ut1 scales, a julian-date api, the iau-2000 earth rotation angle, gmst-based teme ↔ ecef and wgs-84 geodetic frames, full iau 2000a/2000b nutation and iau 2006 precession, and the equinox-free cio-based iau 2006/2000a gcrs↔itrs reduction with iers polar motion — validated bit-for-bit against the sofa/erfa vectors and independently cross-checked against anise (pure-rust spice): gcrs→itrs vs anise itrf93, same iers eop, agree to ≤ 0.86 m on the ground / ≤ 3.6 m at gnss orbit. bit-for-bit vs sofa/erfa · vallado end-to-end 4 mm  standards of fundamental astronomy essential routines for fundamental astronomy international earth rotation and reference systems service coordinated universal time international atomic time terrestrial time universal time 1, set by earth&#x27;s rotation application programming interface international astronomical union greenwich mean sidereal time true equator, mean equinox frame earth-centred, earth-fixed world geodetic system 1984 celestial intermediate origin geocentric celestial reference system international terrestrial reference system nasa&#x27;s spacecraft, planet, instrument, camera-matrix and events toolkit international terrestrial reference frame 1993 earth orientation parameters global navigation satellite system"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Time scales &amp; Earth-orientation frames</b><span class="cx-d">Time systems</span><span class=cx-p>bit-for-bit vs SOFA/ERFA · Vallado end-to-end 4 mm</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>IERS leap-second UTC / TAI / TT / UT1 scales, a Julian-date API, the IAU-2000 Earth Rotation Angle, GMST-based TEME ↔ ECEF and WGS-84 geodetic frames, full IAU 2000A/2000B nutation and IAU 2006 precession, and the equinox-free CIO-based IAU 2006/2000A GCRS↔ITRS reduction with IERS polar motion — validated bit-for-bit against the SOFA/ERFA vectors and independently cross-checked against ANISE (pure-Rust SPICE): GCRS→ITRS vs ANISE ITRF93, same IERS EOP, agree to ≤ 0.86 m on the ground / ≤ 3.6 m at GNSS orbit.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>SOFA</b> Standards of Fundamental Astronomy · <b>ERFA</b> Essential Routines for Fundamental Astronomy · <b>IERS</b> International Earth Rotation and Reference Systems Service · <b>UTC</b> Coordinated Universal Time · <b>TAI</b> International Atomic Time · <b>TT</b> Terrestrial Time · <b>UT1</b> Universal Time 1, set by Earth&#x27;s rotation · <b>API</b> application programming interface · <b>IAU</b> International Astronomical Union · <b>GMST</b> Greenwich mean sidereal time · <b>TEME</b> true equator, mean equinox frame · <b>ECEF</b> Earth-centred, Earth-fixed · <b>WGS-84</b> World Geodetic System 1984 · <b>CIO</b> Celestial Intermediate Origin · <b>GCRS</b> Geocentric Celestial Reference System · <b>ITRS</b> International Terrestrial Reference System · <b>SPICE</b> NASA&#x27;s spacecraft, planet, instrument, camera-matrix and events toolkit · <b>ITRF93</b> International Terrestrial Reference Frame 1993 · <b>EOP</b> Earth orientation parameters · <b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Timing" data-q="real-time earth-orientation prediction time systems timing an operational-style earth-orientation predictor — least squares over bias, rate and the principal periodic terms across a trailing window — measured against genuinely archived bulletin a predictions rather than against persistence. the offline default input is a real iers finals2000a product carrying bulletin a prediction rows, so a bare run emits a populated per-horizon table instead of an empty one. ut1 and polar motion are reported over a common row set together with their combination, so the joint figure is one statistic rather than a quadrature sum of two different samples. 12 leads against archived bulletin a: ut1 2.93 ms, polar motion 1.76 mas realtime-frame-eop universal time 1, set by earth&#x27;s rotation international earth rotation and reference systems service"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Real-time Earth-orientation prediction</b><span class="cx-d">Time systems</span><span class=cx-p>12 leads against archived Bulletin A: UT1 2.93 ms, polar motion 1.76 mas</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>An operational-style Earth-orientation predictor — least squares over bias, rate and the principal periodic terms across a trailing window — measured against genuinely archived Bulletin A predictions rather than against persistence. The offline default input is a real IERS finals2000A product carrying Bulletin A prediction rows, so a bare run emits a populated per-horizon table instead of an empty one. UT1 and polar motion are reported over a common row set together with their combination, so the joint figure is one statistic rather than a quadrature sum of two different samples.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>UT1</b> Universal Time 1, set by Earth&#x27;s rotation · <b>IERS</b> International Earth Rotation and Reference Systems Service</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=realtime-frame-eop&amp;tab=overview">Run realtime-frame-eop in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Inertial" data-q="strapdown ins &amp; map-aided navigation inertial inertial three-axis strapdown ins — quaternion attitude, wgs-84 ned mechanization, coning/sculling compensation, and a full deterministic imu error model — plus a first-principles cold-atom-interferometer accelerometer (mach–zehnder phase, projection noise, contrast decay, vibration coupling) and a particle filter for terrain-/gravity-referenced gps-denied navigation. first-principles cai accelerometer · derived vrw cross-checked vs an independent integrator imu-deadreckoning inertial navigation system cold-atom interferometer velocity random walk world geodetic system 1984 north-east-down frame inertial measurement unit global positioning system"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Strapdown INS &amp; map-aided navigation</b><span class="cx-d">Inertial</span><span class=cx-p>first-principles CAI accelerometer · derived VRW cross-checked vs an independent integrator</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Three-axis strapdown INS — quaternion attitude, WGS-84 NED mechanization, coning/sculling compensation, and a full deterministic IMU error model — plus a first-principles cold-atom-interferometer accelerometer (Mach–Zehnder phase, projection noise, contrast decay, vibration coupling) and a particle filter for terrain-/gravity-referenced GPS-denied navigation.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>INS</b> inertial navigation system · <b>CAI</b> cold-atom interferometer · <b>VRW</b> velocity random walk · <b>WGS-84</b> World Geodetic System 1984 · <b>NED</b> north-east-down frame · <b>IMU</b> inertial measurement unit · <b>GPS</b> Global Positioning System</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=imu-deadreckoning&amp;tab=overview">Run imu-deadreckoning in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Inertial" data-q="inertial and terrain-relative coasting through an outage inertial inertial position error against coast duration from an imu error budget — bias, random walk and scale factor, each with its own growth law and its own share of the crossing — so the cost of a navigation outage is a computed curve rather than a swept drift rate. threshold crossings are engine output with the dominant contribution named at each one, across four imu grades, and a terrain-fix interval is scored in its own block so the headline crossings stay unaided. 10 m at 249 s, 50 m at 595 s, accelerometer bias dominant at both ins-trn-coast inertial measurement unit"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Inertial and terrain-relative coasting through an outage</b><span class="cx-d">Inertial</span><span class=cx-p>10 m at 249 s, 50 m at 595 s, accelerometer bias dominant at both</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Position error against coast duration from an IMU error budget — bias, random walk and scale factor, each with its own growth law and its own share of the crossing — so the cost of a navigation outage is a computed curve rather than a swept drift rate. Threshold crossings are engine output with the dominant contribution named at each one, across four IMU grades, and a terrain-fix interval is scored in its own block so the headline crossings stay unaided.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>IMU</b> inertial measurement unit</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=ins-trn-coast&amp;tab=overview">Run ins-trn-coast in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Inertial" data-q="gravity, terrain &amp; magnetic map-matching alt-pnt (gps-denied) inertial a cold-atom gravimeter measurement model whose white-noise floor is derived from the cai accelerometer physics, a low-degree fully-normalised spherical-harmonic gravity-anomaly field plus synthetic mascons, and a gravity-map-matching particle filter — extended to terrain-referenced navigation against an srtm elevation dem (tercom/sitan), an igrf-14 geomagnetic main field to degree/order 13, and a combined gravity + magnetic + terrain navigator that fuses all three scalar channels. a 60-minute gps-denied benchmark — a ~700 km / one-hour outage where the inertial solution drifts to ~70 km — is recovered to ~145 m by a hierarchical coarse-to-fine matcher: the esa navisp quantum wayfarer target. 60-min gps-denied · ~145 m (&lt; 500 m) gps-denied-gravity-nav positioning, navigation and timing global positioning system cold-atom interferometer shuttle radar topography mission digital elevation model terrain contour matching sandia inertial terrain-aided navigation international geomagnetic reference field, 14th generation european space agency"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Gravity, terrain &amp; magnetic map-matching</b><span class="cx-d">Alt-PNT (GPS-denied)</span><span class=cx-p>60-min GPS-denied · ~145 m (&lt; 500 m)</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A cold-atom gravimeter measurement model whose white-noise floor is derived from the CAI accelerometer physics, a low-degree fully-normalised spherical-harmonic gravity-anomaly field plus synthetic mascons, and a gravity-map-matching particle filter — extended to terrain-referenced navigation against an SRTM elevation DEM (TERCOM/SITAN), an IGRF-14 geomagnetic main field to degree/order 13, and a combined gravity + magnetic + terrain navigator that fuses all three scalar channels. A 60-minute GPS-denied benchmark — a ~700 km / one-hour outage where the inertial solution drifts to ~70 km — is recovered to ~145 m by a hierarchical coarse-to-fine matcher: the ESA NAVISP Quantum Wayfarer target.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>PNT</b> positioning, navigation and timing · <b>GPS</b> Global Positioning System · <b>CAI</b> cold-atom interferometer · <b>SRTM</b> Shuttle Radar Topography Mission · <b>DEM</b> digital elevation model · <b>TERCOM</b> terrain contour matching · <b>SITAN</b> Sandia inertial terrain-aided navigation · <b>IGRF-14</b> International Geomagnetic Reference Field, 14th generation · <b>ESA</b> European Space Agency</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=gps-denied-gravity-nav&amp;tab=overview">Run gps-denied-gravity-nav in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Inertial" data-q="gnss / ins sensor fusion fusion inertial a 15-state error-state ekf that coasts through gnss outages on a calibrated inertial solution; a tightly-coupled pseudorange mode that keeps correcting with fewer than four satellites; a coupled clock + position filter; a general unscented (sigma-point) kalman estimator; a tightly-coupled gnss/ins ukf navigator validated to 0.77 m rms through a 120 s outage on a curving leo pass; and a full 17-state tightly-coupled gnss/ins ukf whose quantum-cai dead-reckoning coasts the outage on the cold-atom accelerometer&#x27;s derived velocity-random-walk. tightly-coupled ukf · 0.77 m rms gnss-ins global navigation satellite system inertial navigation system unscented kalman filter root mean square extended kalman filter low earth orbit cold-atom interferometer"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">GNSS / INS sensor fusion</b><span class="cx-d">Fusion</span><span class=cx-p>tightly-coupled UKF · 0.77 m RMS</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A 15-state error-state EKF that coasts through GNSS outages on a calibrated inertial solution; a tightly-coupled pseudorange mode that keeps correcting with fewer than four satellites; a coupled clock + position filter; a general unscented (sigma-point) Kalman estimator; a tightly-coupled GNSS/INS UKF navigator validated to 0.77 m RMS through a 120 s outage on a curving LEO pass; and a full 17-state tightly-coupled GNSS/INS UKF whose quantum-CAI dead-reckoning coasts the outage on the cold-atom accelerometer&#x27;s derived velocity-random-walk.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System · <b>INS</b> inertial navigation system · <b>UKF</b> unscented Kalman filter · <b>RMS</b> root mean square · <b>EKF</b> extended Kalman filter · <b>LEO</b> low Earth orbit · <b>CAI</b> cold-atom interferometer</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=gnss-ins&amp;tab=overview">Run gnss-ins in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Orbits" data-q="batch &amp; sequential od orbit determination orbits recovery of an orbital state from ground-station range tracking, composing the two-body + j2 force model and rk4 integrator with a gauss–newton batch differential corrector (sub-metre / mm·s⁻¹ from noiseless ranges, ~2 m at a 5 m noise floor) and a sequential unscented-filter variant. batch + sequential od vs orekit 12.2 batchlsestimator/kalmanestimator  orbit determination"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Batch &amp; sequential OD</b><span class="cx-d">Orbit determination</span><span class=cx-p>batch + sequential OD vs Orekit 12.2 BatchLSEstimator/KalmanEstimator</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Recovery of an orbital state from ground-station range tracking, composing the two-body + J2 force model and RK4 integrator with a Gauss–Newton batch differential corrector (sub-metre / mm·s⁻¹ from noiseless ranges, ~2 m at a 5 m noise floor) and a sequential unscented-filter variant.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>OD</b> orbit determination</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Orbits" data-q="fisher information &amp; observability observability &amp; estimation theory orbits a general, reusable fisher-information / cramér–rao layer (src/fim.rs): the information matrix m = hᵀwh, the cramér–rao lower bound, observability rank and the datum-defect null space (moore–penrose pseudo-inverse), and d/a/e/t-optimal experiment-design scalars from a symmetric jacobi eigensolver. it underpins the dop engine, the passive-geolocation bound, and the lunar absolute-station observability result — internal ranging leaves a six-dof rigid-body datum defect that an earth-baseline vlbi tie restores for a sparse constellation and sharpens for a rich one, the absolute datum closing at three non-collinear earth stations. eigenvalues vs numpy.linalg.eigh · crlb vs σ²(xᵀx)⁻¹ via numpy.linalg.inv · gnss dop from the information matrix, all to 1e-9 · kay (1993) closed forms + monte-carlo crlb attainment  cramér–rao lower bound global navigation satellite system dilution of precision degrees of freedom very long baseline interferometry"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Fisher information &amp; observability</b><span class="cx-d">Observability &amp; estimation theory</span><span class=cx-p>eigenvalues vs numpy.linalg.eigh · CRLB vs σ²(XᵀX)⁻¹ via numpy.linalg.inv · GNSS DOP from the information matrix, all to 1e-9 · Kay (1993) closed forms + Monte-Carlo CRLB attainment</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A general, reusable Fisher-information / Cramér–Rao layer (src/fim.rs): the information matrix M = HᵀWH, the Cramér–Rao lower bound, observability rank and the datum-defect null space (Moore–Penrose pseudo-inverse), and D/A/E/T-optimal experiment-design scalars from a symmetric Jacobi eigensolver. It underpins the DOP engine, the passive-geolocation bound, and the lunar absolute-station observability result — internal ranging leaves a six-DOF rigid-body datum defect that an Earth-baseline VLBI tie restores for a sparse constellation and sharpens for a rich one, the absolute datum closing at three non-collinear Earth stations.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>CRLB</b> Cramér–Rao lower bound · <b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System · <b>DOP</b> dilution of precision · <b>DOF</b> degrees of freedom · <b>VLBI</b> very long baseline interferometry</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Lunar" data-q="cr3bp propagation &amp; lunanet integrity lunar &amp; cislunar lunar an earth–moon circular restricted three-body (cr3bp) propagator in the rotating frame — a conserved jacobi constant and all five lagrange points — with a 6×6 state-transition matrix and a single-shooting differential corrector that produces genuinely periodic halo / near-rectilinear halo orbits (nrho): it reproduces the published l2 southern 9:2 nrho (the gateway orbit) at a period of ~6.57 days and a perilune of ~3,250 km. plus lunanet / lnis cislunar pnt geometry (mci↔mcmf reduction, selenographic coordinates) with a lunar south-pole araim pass that honestly surfaces the integrity gap: a ~30 m σ_ure drives the protection level well above a 50 m alert limit. reproduces the published l2 9:2 nrho (~6.57 d period · ~3,250 km perilune); stm checked vs finite differences lunanet-araim circular restricted three-body problem near-rectilinear halo orbit state transition matrix positioning, navigation and timing moon-centred inertial frame moon-centred, moon-fixed frame advanced receiver autonomous integrity monitoring"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">CR3BP propagation &amp; LunaNet integrity</b><span class="cx-d">Lunar &amp; cislunar</span><span class=cx-p>Reproduces the published L2 9:2 NRHO (~6.57 d period · ~3,250 km perilune); STM checked vs finite differences</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>An Earth–Moon circular restricted three-body (CR3BP) propagator in the rotating frame — a conserved Jacobi constant and all five Lagrange points — with a 6×6 state-transition matrix and a single-shooting differential corrector that produces genuinely periodic halo / near-rectilinear halo orbits (NRHO): it reproduces the published L2 southern 9:2 NRHO (the Gateway orbit) at a period of ~6.57 days and a perilune of ~3,250 km. Plus LunaNet / LNIS cislunar PNT geometry (MCI↔MCMF reduction, selenographic coordinates) with a lunar south-pole ARAIM pass that honestly surfaces the integrity gap: a ~30 m σ_URE drives the protection level well above a 50 m alert limit.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>CR3BP</b> circular restricted three-body problem · <b>NRHO</b> near-rectilinear halo orbit · <b>STM</b> state transition matrix · <b>PNT</b> positioning, navigation and timing · <b>MCI</b> Moon-centred inertial frame · <b>MCMF</b> Moon-centred, Moon-fixed frame · <b>ARAIM</b> advanced receiver autonomous integrity monitoring</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=lunanet-araim&amp;tab=overview">Run lunanet-araim in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Lunar" data-q="lunar reference-frame realisation lunar &amp; cislunar lunar realises a lunar body-fixed frame from an observing campaign: a weighted gauss–newton similarity fit over station–reflector geometry, reporting the datum, its covariance and the worst-determined axis. the coupled frame-and-timescale gauge is carried explicitly rather than absorbed, so the rank deficiency is visible in the report instead of buried in a pseudo-inverse. vs numpy/scipy umeyama–horn svd lunar-frame-realisation singular value decomposition"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Lunar reference-frame realisation</b><span class="cx-d">Lunar &amp; cislunar</span><span class=cx-p>vs numpy/scipy Umeyama–Horn SVD</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Realises a lunar body-fixed frame from an observing campaign: a weighted Gauss–Newton similarity fit over station–reflector geometry, reporting the datum, its covariance and the worst-determined axis. The coupled frame-and-timescale gauge is carried explicitly rather than absorbed, so the rank deficiency is visible in the report instead of buried in a pseudo-inverse.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>SVD</b> singular value decomposition</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=lunar-frame-realisation&amp;tab=overview">Run lunar-frame-realisation in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Lunar" data-q="lunar navigation service volume &amp; araim lunar &amp; cislunar lunar sweeps an illustrative moonlight/lcns-class constellation over a selenographic grid: visibility, dilution of precision, coverage, and per-epoch araim protection levels against a configurable alert limit. the signal-in-space ranging accuracy is a parameter, so the sweep answers a ranging requirement over the whole volume rather than pass/fail at one fixed value. anise 0.10.2 orbits · rtklib 2.4.2-p13 moonlight-service-volume advanced receiver autonomous integrity monitoring an open-source gnss positioning library"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Lunar navigation service volume &amp; ARAIM</b><span class="cx-d">Lunar &amp; cislunar</span><span class=cx-p>ANISE 0.10.2 orbits · RTKLIB 2.4.2-p13</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Sweeps an illustrative Moonlight/LCNS-class constellation over a selenographic grid: visibility, dilution of precision, coverage, and per-epoch ARAIM protection levels against a configurable alert limit. The signal-in-space ranging accuracy is a parameter, so the sweep answers a ranging requirement over the whole volume rather than pass/fail at one fixed value.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>ARAIM</b> advanced receiver autonomous integrity monitoring · <b>RTKLIB</b> an open-source GNSS positioning library</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=moonlight-service-volume&amp;tab=overview">Run moonlight-service-volume in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Lunar" data-q="cross-provider lunar interoperability budget lunar &amp; cislunar lunar measures how far three independent authoritative lunar ephemerides actually disagree, and turns that into a design law: the frame-versus-ephemeris error budget a mixed-provider user inherits, and the consistency tolerance a provider has to hold to keep that budget inside a stated bound. de440 · inpop21a · epm2021 via calceph lunar-interop-export"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Cross-provider lunar interoperability budget</b><span class="cx-d">Lunar &amp; cislunar</span><span class=cx-p>DE440 · INPOP21a · EPM2021 via calceph</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Measures how far three independent authoritative lunar ephemerides actually disagree, and turns that into a design law: the frame-versus-ephemeris error budget a mixed-provider user inherits, and the consistency tolerance a provider has to hold to keep that budget inside a stated bound.</p><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=lunar-interop-export&amp;tab=overview">Run lunar-interop-export in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Lunar" data-q="autonomous free-network fault observability lunar &amp; cislunar lunar the linear algebra a self-referential lunar constellation needs to detect its own faults with no ground truth: the weighted parity projector in the rank-deficient pseudo-inverse form that survives the datum-and-timescale gauge, per-node detectability, the baarda minimum detectable bias, and byzantine block-spark detect/identify counts over real de440 inter-node geometry. vs independent numpy/scipy parity algebra "><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Autonomous free-network fault observability</b><span class="cx-d">Lunar &amp; cislunar</span><span class=cx-p>vs independent numpy/scipy parity algebra</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>The linear algebra a self-referential lunar constellation needs to detect its own faults with no ground truth: the weighted parity projector in the rank-deficient pseudo-inverse form that survives the datum-and-timescale gauge, per-node detectability, the Baarda minimum detectable bias, and Byzantine block-spark detect/identify counts over real DE440 inter-node geometry.</p></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Lunar" data-q="lunar frame datum from real laser ranging lunar &amp; cislunar lunar replaces the simulated campaign with real data: 337 archived ilrs lunar laser-ranging normal points across five retroreflector arrays, each observation weighted by its own recorded precision out of the file. the reflector coordinates are a principal-axis de440 catalogue kept under its own filename, separate from the mean-earth de430 table — feeding one to the other&#x27;s rotation moves a reflector by hundreds of metres and nothing objects, because both are well-formed coordinates. 337 ilrs normal points · median 5.13 mm lunar-llr-datum international laser ranging service"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Lunar frame datum from real laser ranging</b><span class="cx-d">Lunar &amp; cislunar</span><span class=cx-p>337 ILRS normal points · median 5.13 mm</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Replaces the simulated campaign with real data: 337 archived ILRS lunar laser-ranging normal points across five retroreflector arrays, each observation weighted by its own recorded precision out of the file. The reflector coordinates are a principal-axis DE440 catalogue kept under its own filename, separate from the mean-earth DE430 table — feeding one to the other&#x27;s rotation moves a reflector by hundreds of metres and nothing objects, because both are well-formed coordinates.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>ILRS</b> International Laser Ranging Service</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=lunar-llr-datum&amp;tab=overview">Run lunar-llr-datum in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Lunar" data-q="lunar differential pnt lunar &amp; cislunar lunar differential corrections between a lunar reference station and a user. the common-mode cancellation is an exact identity; the spatial-decorrelation residual is a first-order geometric model that grows with baseline, and the correction-link residual budget is reported beside it so the limiting term stays visible rather than folded into one number. exact common-mode identity · modelled decorrelation lunar-differential-pnt positioning, navigation and timing"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Lunar differential PNT</b><span class="cx-d">Lunar &amp; cislunar</span><span class=cx-p>Exact common-mode identity · modelled decorrelation</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Differential corrections between a lunar reference station and a user. The common-mode cancellation is an exact identity; the spatial-decorrelation residual is a first-order geometric model that grows with baseline, and the correction-link residual budget is reported beside it so the limiting term stays visible rather than folded into one number.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>PNT</b> positioning, navigation and timing</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=lunar-differential-pnt&amp;tab=overview">Run lunar-differential-pnt in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Lunar" data-q="lunar time scales &amp; geodetic vlbi lunar &amp; cislunar lunar per-clock-class crossover times for a lunar timescale — when each oscillator class stops meeting a stated bound without an update — together with a lunar geodetic vlbi observable and the fisher information it contributes to the datum. per-clock-class crossover table lunar-time-budget very long baseline interferometry"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Lunar time scales &amp; geodetic VLBI</b><span class="cx-d">Lunar &amp; cislunar</span><span class=cx-p>Per-clock-class crossover table</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Per-clock-class crossover times for a lunar timescale — when each oscillator class stops meeting a stated bound without an update — together with a lunar geodetic VLBI observable and the Fisher information it contributes to the datum.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>VLBI</b> very long baseline interferometry</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=lunar-time-budget&amp;tab=overview">Run lunar-time-budget in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Lunar" data-q="real, retrieved lunar constellation geometry lunar &amp; cislunar lunar the service volume runs on published constellation designs instead of an illustrative one: the five-satellite lans interoperability-demonstration reference set, the lncss case studies at 8, 12 and 16 satellites, and the four spacecraft actually in lunar orbit on 2023-01-01 from jpl horizons. each file states its own frame and its own provenance class, a horizon past the end of a tabulated ephemeris is refused rather than extrapolated, and the illustrative keplerian result is emitted beside the retrieved one with the difference between them, never replaced by it. 5 committed fixtures, each regenerable from its recorded source moonlight-service-volume jet propulsion laboratory"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Real, retrieved lunar constellation geometry</b><span class="cx-d">Lunar &amp; cislunar</span><span class=cx-p>5 committed fixtures, each regenerable from its recorded source</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>The service volume runs on published constellation designs instead of an illustrative one: the five-satellite LANS interoperability-demonstration reference set, the LNCSS case studies at 8, 12 and 16 satellites, and the four spacecraft actually in lunar orbit on 2023-01-01 from JPL Horizons. Each file states its own frame and its own provenance class, a horizon past the end of a tabulated ephemeris is refused rather than extrapolated, and the illustrative Keplerian result is emitted beside the retrieved one with the difference between them, never replaced by it.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>JPL</b> Jet Propulsion Laboratory</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=moonlight-service-volume&amp;tab=overview">Run moonlight-service-volume in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Lunar" data-q="lunar denial contour from a measured c/n₀ spread lunar &amp; cislunar lunar per-satellite jammer-to-signal ratio over real lunar slant ranges, with the wanted-signal side kept as a distribution rather than collapsed to a median, so the denial contour carries the uncertainty band the relay geometry actually produces. two criteria are reported side by side: the power-ratio threshold, and loss of lock computed from tracking-loop dynamics. the orbital capture footprint is swept over transmitter altitude and beamwidth, and where the grid never reaches limb capture the report says so and prints the shortfall. standoff 49.2 km by power ratio against 19.4 km by loss of lock lunar-jamming"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Lunar denial contour from a measured C/N₀ spread</b><span class="cx-d">Lunar &amp; cislunar</span><span class=cx-p>Standoff 49.2 km by power ratio against 19.4 km by loss of lock</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Per-satellite jammer-to-signal ratio over real lunar slant ranges, with the wanted-signal side kept as a distribution rather than collapsed to a median, so the denial contour carries the uncertainty band the relay geometry actually produces. Two criteria are reported side by side: the power-ratio threshold, and loss of lock computed from tracking-loop dynamics. The orbital capture footprint is swept over transmitter altitude and beamwidth, and where the grid never reaches limb capture the report says so and prints the shortfall.</p><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=lunar-jamming&amp;tab=overview">Run lunar-jamming in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Lunar" data-q="vlbi schedule to station-coordinate covariance lunar &amp; cislunar lunar accumulates the lunar vlbi delay partials into a fisher information matrix over a baselines-by-epochs schedule and returns the station coordinate covariance, so the step from delay precision to coordinate sigma is estimated rather than assumed. the free network is reported beside the datum-fixed one, with its rank defect and the unobservable direction named. the surface-point covariance is kept distinct from the earth-station one, because the near-field beacon partial is a difference of two station unit vectors and does not behave like the far-field case. rank 6, defect 0; free-network defect 1 named as a common translation lunar-vlbi-fim very long baseline interferometry"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">VLBI schedule to station-coordinate covariance</b><span class="cx-d">Lunar &amp; cislunar</span><span class=cx-p>Rank 6, defect 0; free-network defect 1 named as a common translation</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Accumulates the lunar VLBI delay partials into a Fisher information matrix over a baselines-by-epochs schedule and returns the station coordinate covariance, so the step from delay precision to coordinate sigma is estimated rather than assumed. The free network is reported beside the datum-fixed one, with its rank defect and the unobservable direction named. The surface-point covariance is kept distinct from the Earth-station one, because the near-field beacon partial is a difference of two station unit vectors and does not behave like the far-field case.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>VLBI</b> very long baseline interferometry</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=lunar-vlbi-fim&amp;tab=overview">Run lunar-vlbi-fim in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Lunar" data-q="radiometric navigation &amp; relay-pnt deep-space &amp; mars pnt lunar an open deep-space / mars radiometric navigation engine: iterative light-time + shapiro delay, two-/one-/three-way doppler &amp; range (moyer two-leg), coherent transponder turnaround, regenerative/pn ranging and δ-dor; ccsds-tdm i/o; a reduced-dynamic square-root information filter (empirical accelerations + onboard clock + mars drag) that does mars-lmo orbit determination in a synthetic closed loop; one-way+two-way fusion; and a marconi areostationary relay constellation with an end-to-end gse performance simulator. simulation-validated (covariance / closed-loop figures of merit, not certified protection levels); the sun-central mars dynamics are cross-checked against jpl de440 to 137 m @ a 1-day arc; mars-lmo od ≈ 0.2 m. mars-pnt-lmo positioning, navigation and timing jet propulsion laboratory low mars orbit orbit determination consultative committee for space data systems tracking data message ground support equipment"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Radiometric navigation &amp; relay-PNT</b><span class="cx-d">Deep-space &amp; Mars PNT</span><span class=cx-p>Simulation-validated (covariance / closed-loop figures of merit, not certified protection levels); the Sun-central Mars dynamics are cross-checked against JPL DE440 to 137 m @ a 1-day arc; Mars-LMO OD ≈ 0.2 m.</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>An open deep-space / Mars radiometric navigation engine: iterative light-time + Shapiro delay, two-/one-/three-way Doppler &amp; range (Moyer two-leg), coherent transponder turnaround, regenerative/PN ranging and Δ-DOR; CCSDS-TDM I/O; a reduced-dynamic Square-Root Information Filter (empirical accelerations + onboard clock + Mars drag) that does Mars-LMO orbit determination in a synthetic closed loop; one-way+two-way fusion; and a MARCONI areostationary relay constellation with an end-to-end GSE performance simulator.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>PNT</b> positioning, navigation and timing · <b>JPL</b> Jet Propulsion Laboratory · <b>LMO</b> low Mars orbit · <b>OD</b> orbit determination · <b>CCSDS</b> Consultative Committee for Space Data Systems · <b>TDM</b> tracking data message · <b>GSE</b> ground support equipment</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=mars-pnt-lmo&amp;tab=overview">Run mars-pnt-lmo in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Lunar" data-q="optical and rf handover with cross-modality integrity optical / rf pnt lunar fault injection gives the cross-modality monitor a detection power rather than a fault-free pass: a minimum detectable bias per axis at a stated false-alarm and missed-detection budget. after a handover the covariance re-growth is propagated against the alert limits, so the report states how long the single-modality solution stays inside them rather than only that the handover was continuous. the optical and rf ranging legs are compared only at matched accumulation time and path, and the report refuses to form a ratio against a value that was chosen rather than measured. minimum detectable bias 8.20 m horizontal, 12.30 m vertical, 24.6 ns timing hybrid-optical-rf radio frequency positioning, navigation and timing"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Optical and RF handover with cross-modality integrity</b><span class="cx-d">Optical / RF PNT</span><span class=cx-p>Minimum detectable bias 8.20 m horizontal, 12.30 m vertical, 24.6 ns timing</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Fault injection gives the cross-modality monitor a detection power rather than a fault-free pass: a minimum detectable bias per axis at a stated false-alarm and missed-detection budget. After a handover the covariance re-growth is propagated against the alert limits, so the report states how long the single-modality solution stays inside them rather than only that the handover was continuous. The optical and RF ranging legs are compared only at matched accumulation time and path, and the report refuses to form a ratio against a value that was chosen rather than measured.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>RF</b> radio frequency · <b>PNT</b> positioning, navigation and timing</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=hybrid-optical-rf&amp;tab=overview">Run hybrid-optical-rf in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="GNSS" data-q="receiver autonomous integrity (araim) integrity gnss snapshot and solution-separation raim with horizontal and vertical protection levels (hpl/vpl), fault detection and exclusion, and stanford integrity diagrams — plus an explicit integrity-risk-budget (mhss) protection level with the dual-/multi-constellation constellation-wide fault mode (eu araim / do-316). mhss on a real gps + galileo snapshot · do-316 dual-constellation integrity-raim advanced receiver autonomous integrity monitoring multiple hypothesis solution separation global positioning system rtca minimum operational performance standards for gps aircraft-based augmentation receivers receiver autonomous integrity monitoring horizontal protection level vertical protection level"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Receiver autonomous integrity (ARAIM)</b><span class="cx-d">Integrity</span><span class=cx-p>MHSS on a real GPS + Galileo snapshot · DO-316 dual-constellation</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Snapshot and solution-separation RAIM with horizontal and vertical protection levels (HPL/VPL), fault detection and exclusion, and Stanford integrity diagrams — plus an explicit integrity-risk-budget (MHSS) protection level with the dual-/multi-constellation constellation-wide fault mode (EU ARAIM / DO-316).</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>ARAIM</b> advanced receiver autonomous integrity monitoring · <b>MHSS</b> multiple hypothesis solution separation · <b>GPS</b> Global Positioning System · <b>DO-316</b> RTCA minimum operational performance standards for GPS aircraft-based augmentation receivers · <b>RAIM</b> receiver autonomous integrity monitoring · <b>HPL</b> horizontal protection level · <b>VPL</b> vertical protection level</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=integrity-raim&amp;tab=overview">Run integrity-raim in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="GNSS" data-q="araim against published reference vectors integrity gnss the engine&#x27;s mhss protection levels run against the eu-u.s. working group c reference airborne algorithm&#x27;s own published worked examples, each transcribed with its retrieval url, date, page and file sha-256 and held in two places that a test asserts agree. the acceptance vector is the internally consistent reference airborne algorithm description document v3.1, appendix d. the milestone 3 report carries two internal defects — a sign typo in the geometry, and a false-alarm multiplier evaluated at 57 fault modes while the text states one — so its protection levels are reported as measured discrepancies rather than graded, and the defects are demonstrated by running the document&#x27;s own numbers. worst error 0.044 m against the sources&#x27; own 0.05 m tolerance araim-reference-check advanced receiver autonomous integrity monitoring multiple hypothesis solution separation"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">ARAIM against published reference vectors</b><span class="cx-d">Integrity</span><span class=cx-p>Worst error 0.044 m against the sources&#x27; own 0.05 m tolerance</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>The engine&#x27;s MHSS protection levels run against the EU-U.S. Working Group C reference airborne algorithm&#x27;s own published worked examples, each transcribed with its retrieval URL, date, page and file SHA-256 and held in two places that a test asserts agree. The acceptance vector is the internally consistent Reference Airborne Algorithm Description Document v3.1, Appendix D. The Milestone 3 report carries two internal defects — a sign typo in the geometry, and a false-alarm multiplier evaluated at 57 fault modes while the text states one — so its protection levels are reported as measured discrepancies rather than graded, and the defects are demonstrated by running the document&#x27;s own numbers.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>ARAIM</b> advanced receiver autonomous integrity monitoring · <b>MHSS</b> multiple hypothesis solution separation</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=araim-reference-check&amp;tab=overview">Run araim-reference-check in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="GNSS" data-q="sbas / waas protection levels &amp; dual-frequency augmentation (sbas) gnss sbas / waas protection levels in the do-229e weighted-least-squares form (precision-approach and en-route k-factors) and the l1/l5 dual-frequency ionosphere-free combination (is-gps-705) that underpins do-316, with the protection-level geometry cross-checked against a reference linear-algebra implementation. do-229e k-factors · l1/l5 iono-free  satellite-based augmentation system wide area augmentation system rtca minimum operational performance standards for satellite-based augmentation system receivers, revision e gps interface specification for the l5 signal global positioning system rtca minimum operational performance standards for gps aircraft-based augmentation receivers"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">SBAS / WAAS protection levels &amp; dual-frequency</b><span class="cx-d">Augmentation (SBAS)</span><span class=cx-p>DO-229E K-factors · L1/L5 iono-free</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>SBAS / WAAS protection levels in the DO-229E weighted-least-squares form (precision-approach and en-route K-factors) and the L1/L5 dual-frequency ionosphere-free combination (IS-GPS-705) that underpins DO-316, with the protection-level geometry cross-checked against a reference linear-algebra implementation.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>SBAS</b> satellite-based augmentation system · <b>WAAS</b> Wide Area Augmentation System · <b>DO-229E</b> RTCA minimum operational performance standards for satellite-based augmentation system receivers, revision E · <b>IS-GPS-705</b> GPS interface specification for the L5 signal · <b>GPS</b> Global Positioning System · <b>DO-316</b> RTCA minimum operational performance standards for GPS aircraft-based augmentation receivers</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Timing" data-q="holdover, stability &amp; time-and-frequency transfer clock &amp; timing timing two-state kalman holdover, allan-family stability (adev / mdev / tdev / hdev) with confidence intervals and a full ieee-1139 five-coefficient power-law fit, and the operational transfer methods — twstft with the bipm sagnac closed form, gnss common-view, ppp ionosphere-free transfer, a free-space optical link with turbulence scintillation, and an inverse-variance clock-ensemble timescale. a gnss-denied clock-holdover calculator inverts the van-loan coast-error growth to a holdover-to-threshold — how long a clock free-runs before its timing error exceeds budget — across classical and quantum-clock classes; modelled, and honest that for a very stable clock the holdover to a tight threshold is set by the assumed long-tau noise floor, not the cited adev. calibrated to clock datasheets · holdover cross-checked vs the van-loan covariance recursion clock-holdover allan deviation modified allan deviation time deviation hadamard deviation institute of electrical and electronics engineers two-way satellite time and frequency transfer international bureau of weights and measures global navigation satellite system precise point positioning"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Holdover, stability &amp; time-and-frequency transfer</b><span class="cx-d">Clock &amp; timing</span><span class=cx-p>calibrated to clock datasheets · holdover cross-checked vs the van-Loan covariance recursion</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Two-state Kalman holdover, Allan-family stability (ADEV / MDEV / TDEV / HDEV) with confidence intervals and a full IEEE-1139 five-coefficient power-law fit, and the operational transfer methods — TWSTFT with the BIPM Sagnac closed form, GNSS common-view, PPP ionosphere-free transfer, a free-space optical link with turbulence scintillation, and an inverse-variance clock-ensemble timescale. A GNSS-denied clock-holdover calculator inverts the van-Loan coast-error growth to a holdover-to-threshold — how long a clock free-runs before its timing error exceeds budget — across classical and quantum-clock classes; modelled, and honest that for a very stable clock the holdover to a tight threshold is set by the assumed long-tau noise floor, not the cited ADEV.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>ADEV</b> Allan deviation · <b>MDEV</b> modified Allan deviation · <b>TDEV</b> time deviation · <b>HDEV</b> Hadamard deviation · <b>IEEE</b> Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers · <b>TWSTFT</b> two-way satellite time and frequency transfer · <b>BIPM</b> International Bureau of Weights and Measures · <b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System · <b>PPP</b> precise point positioning</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=clock-holdover&amp;tab=overview">Run clock-holdover in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="GNSS" data-q="pseudorange, ionosphere &amp; troposphere gnss measurement domain gnss forward pseudorange / doppler synthesis with klobuchar (broadcast) and ionex / tec-grid (measured) ionosphere — including an ionex file parser, time interpolation, and the thin-shell slant-obliquity mapping — saastamoinen + niell troposphere, and snapshot raim. saastamoinen zhd ≈ 2.3 m · niell unity at zenith · klobuchar per is-gps-200 gnss-sim-raim global navigation satellite system zenith hydrostatic delay gps interface specification for the l1 and l2 signals global positioning system ionosphere map exchange format total electron content receiver autonomous integrity monitoring"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Pseudorange, ionosphere &amp; troposphere</b><span class="cx-d">GNSS measurement domain</span><span class=cx-p>Saastamoinen ZHD ≈ 2.3 m · Niell unity at zenith · Klobuchar per IS-GPS-200</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Forward pseudorange / Doppler synthesis with Klobuchar (broadcast) and IONEX / TEC-grid (measured) ionosphere — including an IONEX file parser, time interpolation, and the thin-shell slant-obliquity mapping — Saastamoinen + Niell troposphere, and snapshot RAIM.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System · <b>ZHD</b> zenith hydrostatic delay · <b>IS-GPS-200</b> GPS interface specification for the L1 and L2 signals · <b>GPS</b> Global Positioning System · <b>IONEX</b> Ionosphere Map Exchange format · <b>TEC</b> total electron content · <b>RAIM</b> receiver autonomous integrity monitoring</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=gnss-sim-raim&amp;tab=overview">Run gnss-sim-raim in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="GNSS" data-q="single-point positioning (pvt) gnss positioning gnss a real receiver position solved from real rinex code pseudoranges + broadcast ephemeris by iterated weighted least squares — sagnac/earth-rotation correction, broadcast clock with tgd, the dual-frequency l1/l2 ionosphere-free combination (or single-frequency klobuchar), saastamoinen/niell troposphere, elevation weighting, dop, and snapshot raim. validated on real igs data (station abmf, 2018-05-13) to 5.7 m 3-d rms / 1.1 m horizontal against the surveyed coordinate. pvt-abmf position, velocity and time global navigation satellite system international gnss service root mean square receiver independent exchange format timing group delay dilution of precision receiver autonomous integrity monitoring"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Single-point positioning (PVT)</b><span class="cx-d">GNSS positioning</span><span class=cx-p>Validated on real IGS data (station ABMF, 2018-05-13) to 5.7 m 3-D RMS / 1.1 m horizontal against the surveyed coordinate.</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A real receiver position solved from real RINEX code pseudoranges + broadcast ephemeris by iterated weighted least squares — Sagnac/Earth-rotation correction, broadcast clock with TGD, the dual-frequency L1/L2 ionosphere-free combination (or single-frequency Klobuchar), Saastamoinen/Niell troposphere, elevation weighting, DOP, and snapshot RAIM.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>PVT</b> position, velocity and time · <b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System · <b>IGS</b> International GNSS Service · <b>RMS</b> root mean square · <b>RINEX</b> Receiver Independent Exchange Format · <b>TGD</b> timing group delay · <b>DOP</b> dilution of precision · <b>RAIM</b> receiver autonomous integrity monitoring</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=pvt-abmf&amp;tab=overview">Run pvt-abmf in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Resilience" data-q="jamming &amp; multi-layer spoof detection resilience resilience a link-budget jamming model (j/s → effective c/n₀ → loss of lock), a stochastic time-spoof detector (neyman–pearson / χ²₁ energy test with monte-carlo p_fa/p_md and a security fom of 1 − p_md), and a multi-layer spoof detector fusing a raim-consistency parity test, an rf agc-power monitor, and a signal-quality (sqm) monitor. plus a quantum-inertial dead-reckoning error budget composing cold-atom-interferometer white-noise drift with residual bias (cross-checked against an independent integrator) and scale-factor error into a position-drift-over-holdover figure — the inertial twin of the clock holdover. χ²₁ np detector · closed-form and monte-carlo p_fa/p_md agree spoof-attack neyman–pearson figure of merit receiver autonomous integrity monitoring radio frequency automatic gain control signal-quality monitoring"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Jamming &amp; multi-layer spoof detection</b><span class="cx-d">Resilience</span><span class=cx-p>χ²₁ NP detector · closed-form and Monte-Carlo P_fa/P_md agree</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A link-budget jamming model (J/S → effective C/N₀ → loss of lock), a stochastic time-spoof detector (Neyman–Pearson / χ²₁ energy test with Monte-Carlo P_fa/P_md and a Security FoM of 1 − P_md), and a multi-layer spoof detector fusing a RAIM-consistency parity test, an RF AGC-power monitor, and a signal-quality (SQM) monitor. Plus a quantum-inertial dead-reckoning error budget composing cold-atom-interferometer white-noise drift with residual bias (cross-checked against an independent integrator) and scale-factor error into a position-drift-over-holdover figure — the inertial twin of the clock holdover.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>NP</b> Neyman–Pearson · <b>FoM</b> figure of merit · <b>RAIM</b> receiver autonomous integrity monitoring · <b>RF</b> radio frequency · <b>AGC</b> automatic gain control · <b>SQM</b> signal-quality monitoring</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=spoof-attack&amp;tab=overview">Run spoof-attack in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Resilience" data-q="modulation, anti-jam &amp; code tracking nav-signal performance resilience the signal-performance layer between the link budget and the measurement domain: unit-area power spectral densities for bpsk-r(n) and sine-boc(m,n), the spectral-separation coefficient that derives the anti-jam q the jamming model consumes directly from the actual signal and jammer spectra, the rms (gabor) bandwidth that sets the ranging-information content (boc &gt; bpsk), the coherent early–late dll code-tracking thermal-noise jitter, and the coherent early-minus-late multipath error envelope with narrow-correlator suppression, and a ranging-code design-trade (m-sequence/gold autocorrelation sidelobe and the three-valued gold cross-correlation bound, plus the code-length-to-unambiguous-range inversion). this is signal-performance analysis, not rf-payload or antenna hardware design. bpsk self-ssc = 2/(3r_c) · sub-metre c/a dll jitter at 45 db-hz · gps c/a gold cross-corr -23.9 db  binary phase-shift keying delay-locked loop global positioning system binary offset carrier modulation root mean square radio frequency"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Modulation, anti-jam &amp; code tracking</b><span class="cx-d">Nav-signal performance</span><span class=cx-p>BPSK self-SSC = 2/(3R_c) · sub-metre C/A DLL jitter at 45 dB-Hz · GPS C/A Gold cross-corr -23.9 dB</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>The signal-performance layer between the link budget and the measurement domain: unit-area power spectral densities for BPSK-R(n) and sine-BOC(m,n), the spectral-separation coefficient that derives the anti-jam Q the jamming model consumes directly from the actual signal and jammer spectra, the RMS (Gabor) bandwidth that sets the ranging-information content (BOC &gt; BPSK), the coherent early–late DLL code-tracking thermal-noise jitter, and the coherent early-minus-late multipath error envelope with narrow-correlator suppression, and a ranging-code design-trade (m-sequence/Gold autocorrelation sidelobe and the three-valued Gold cross-correlation bound, plus the code-length-to-unambiguous-range inversion). This is signal-performance analysis, not RF-payload or antenna hardware design.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>BPSK</b> binary phase-shift keying · <b>DLL</b> delay-locked loop · <b>GPS</b> Global Positioning System · <b>BOC</b> binary offset carrier modulation · <b>RMS</b> root mean square · <b>RF</b> radio frequency</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Resilience" data-q="passive rf geolocation (tdoa/fdoa) passive rf geolocation resilience locate a jammer or spoofer (or an opportunistic source for reverse-pnt) from time-difference-of-arrival hyperboloids across a receiver network, solved by gauss–newton least squares; adding frequency-difference-of-arrival with moving receivers jointly recovers the emitter&#x27;s position and velocity, with the cramér–rao bound on the position covariance from the network geometry. a point-source line-of-sight model — no multipath / nlos, receiver-clock-bias, or refraction terms. noiseless tdoa forward→inverse to 1e-6 m · j·crlb = i · gdop monotone in receivers · estimator attains its crlb over 4000 monte-carlo trials  radio frequency time difference of arrival frequency difference of arrival cramér–rao lower bound geometric dilution of precision positioning, navigation and timing non-line-of-sight reception"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Passive RF geolocation (TDOA/FDOA)</b><span class="cx-d">Passive RF geolocation</span><span class=cx-p>noiseless TDOA forward→inverse to 1e-6 m · J·CRLB = I · GDOP monotone in receivers · estimator attains its CRLB over 4000 Monte-Carlo trials</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Locate a jammer or spoofer (or an opportunistic source for reverse-PNT) from time-difference-of-arrival hyperboloids across a receiver network, solved by Gauss–Newton least squares; adding frequency-difference-of-arrival with moving receivers jointly recovers the emitter&#x27;s position and velocity, with the Cramér–Rao bound on the position covariance from the network geometry. A point-source line-of-sight model — no multipath / NLOS, receiver-clock-bias, or refraction terms.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>RF</b> radio frequency · <b>TDOA</b> time difference of arrival · <b>FDOA</b> frequency difference of arrival · <b>CRLB</b> Cramér–Rao lower bound · <b>GDOP</b> geometric dilution of precision · <b>PNT</b> positioning, navigation and timing · <b>NLOS</b> non-line-of-sight reception</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Interop" data-q="rinex, sp3 &amp; ccsds formats interoperability interop multi-gnss rinex-3 broadcast-ephemeris ingestion (gps, galileo, qzss, beidou, glonass), a rinex-3 observation parser (the rinex 4.00 observation layout is expected to parse but no 4.00 file has been read through it; rinex 4 navigation files are refused by name), an sp3-c/d precise-ephemeris reader/writer with lagrange interpolation, and ccsds oem 2.0 + omm (mean-elements) export for flight-dynamics tools (gmat, orekit, stk), and ccsds-tdm (503) tracking-data-message parse + emit for deep-space radiometric tracking. broadcast-eph + sp3 interp vs rtklib; oem vs an independent ccsds parser oem-interop receiver independent exchange format standard product 3, a precise-orbit format consultative committee for space data systems an open-source gnss positioning library orbit ephemeris message global navigation satellite system global positioning system quasi-zenith satellite system orbit mean-elements message tracking data message"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">RINEX, SP3 &amp; CCSDS formats</b><span class="cx-d">Interoperability</span><span class=cx-p>broadcast-eph + SP3 interp vs RTKLIB; OEM vs an independent CCSDS parser</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Multi-GNSS RINEX-3 broadcast-ephemeris ingestion (GPS, Galileo, QZSS, BeiDou, GLONASS), a RINEX-3 observation parser (the RINEX 4.00 observation layout is expected to parse but no 4.00 file has been read through it; RINEX 4 navigation files are refused by name), an SP3-c/d precise-ephemeris reader/writer with Lagrange interpolation, and CCSDS OEM 2.0 + OMM (mean-elements) export for flight-dynamics tools (GMAT, Orekit, STK), and CCSDS-TDM (503) tracking-data-message parse + emit for deep-space radiometric tracking.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>RINEX</b> Receiver Independent Exchange Format · <b>SP3</b> Standard Product 3, a precise-orbit format · <b>CCSDS</b> Consultative Committee for Space Data Systems · <b>RTKLIB</b> an open-source GNSS positioning library · <b>OEM</b> orbit ephemeris message · <b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System · <b>GPS</b> Global Positioning System · <b>QZSS</b> Quasi-Zenith Satellite System · <b>OMM</b> orbit mean-elements message · <b>TDM</b> tracking data message</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=oem-interop&amp;tab=overview">Run oem-interop in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Interop" data-q="machine-checked verification matrix verification &amp; assurance interop a requirement-to-module-to-test-to-oracle-to-status cross-reference rendered from the engine itself (markdown/csv), with unit-tested honesty invariants: a row may be labelled validated only if it carries an independent external oracle (nist / aiaa / sofa / igs / a published value), so an internal self-consistency check cannot masquerade as a validation — and the hardware/pa capabilities kshana does not provide are recorded as partner-owned gaps. the executable backbone for an ecss-e-st-10-02 verification cross-reference. validated rows require an external oracle (invariant enforced in ci)  continuous integration comma-separated values national institute of standards and technology american institute of aeronautics and astronautics standards of fundamental astronomy international gnss service"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Machine-checked verification matrix</b><span class="cx-d">Verification &amp; assurance</span><span class=cx-p>validated rows require an external oracle (invariant enforced in CI)</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A requirement-to-module-to-test-to-oracle-to-status cross-reference rendered from the engine itself (Markdown/CSV), with unit-tested honesty invariants: a row may be labelled validated only if it carries an independent external oracle (NIST / AIAA / SOFA / IGS / a published value), so an internal self-consistency check cannot masquerade as a validation — and the hardware/PA capabilities Kshana does not provide are recorded as partner-owned gaps. The executable backbone for an ECSS-E-ST-10-02 verification cross-reference.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>CI</b> continuous integration · <b>CSV</b> comma-separated values · <b>NIST</b> National Institute of Standards and Technology · <b>AIAA</b> American Institute of Aeronautics and Astronautics · <b>SOFA</b> Standards of Fundamental Astronomy · <b>IGS</b> International GNSS Service</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Interop" data-q="unit and provenance on every reported quantity verification &amp; assurance interop every numeric field a scenario emits carries a unit and a provenance class drawn from a closed vocabulary, with a definition where one exists, published as a machine-readable schema beside the engine. a ratcheted gate runs every built-in kind and fails on a field that carries none; the kinds not yet complete are named one by one with the reason each is still open, and the list can only shrink. reports that state their own constants — the one-way link budget, the optical and rf budget — let a reader recompute the headline from the report alone. 92 of 92 kinds, 3300 of 3300 numeric fields  radio frequency"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Unit and provenance on every reported quantity</b><span class="cx-d">Verification &amp; assurance</span><span class=cx-p>92 of 92 kinds, 3300 of 3300 numeric fields</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Every numeric field a scenario emits carries a unit and a provenance class drawn from a closed vocabulary, with a definition where one exists, published as a machine-readable schema beside the engine. A ratcheted gate runs every built-in kind and fails on a field that carries none; the kinds not yet complete are named one by one with the reason each is still open, and the list can only shrink. Reports that state their own constants — the one-way link budget, the optical and RF budget — let a reader recompute the headline from the report alone.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>RF</b> radio frequency</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Orbits" data-q="launch, re-entry, coverage &amp; pointing budgets mission analysis orbits first-order mission-design budgets, each a runnable scenario: two-body launch &amp; ascent geometry (launch azimuth, minimum inclination, earth-rotation bonus, dogleg plane-change δv, daily opportunities), an allen–eggers ballistic re-entry corridor (peak deceleration, peak-g and peak-heating velocities), earth-observation coverage geometry (swath / nadir gsd / off-nadir access / revisit via the smad space triangle), a 3-dof attitude &amp; pointing error budget (gravity-gradient torque + rss pointing budget), ground-station pass prediction (aos/tca/los, max elevation, access), and a one-way link budget over the ccsds 401 / dsn 810-005 link equation (fspl, c/n₀, eb/n₀, margin, closure). the pre-hardware analytic layer below stk/gmat/basilisk — modelled first-order budgets, not a 6-dof or radiometric replacement. checked vs closed-form (launch azimuth · allen-eggers · smad)  space mission analysis and design (the reference textbook) ground sample distance degrees of freedom root sum square acquisition of signal time of closest approach loss of signal consultative committee for space data systems deep space network free-space path loss"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Launch, re-entry, coverage &amp; pointing budgets</b><span class="cx-d">Mission analysis</span><span class=cx-p>checked vs closed-form (launch azimuth · Allen-Eggers · SMAD)</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>First-order mission-design budgets, each a runnable scenario: two-body launch &amp; ascent geometry (launch azimuth, minimum inclination, Earth-rotation bonus, dogleg plane-change Δv, daily opportunities), an Allen–Eggers ballistic re-entry corridor (peak deceleration, peak-g and peak-heating velocities), Earth-observation coverage geometry (swath / nadir GSD / off-nadir access / revisit via the SMAD space triangle), a 3-DOF attitude &amp; pointing error budget (gravity-gradient torque + RSS pointing budget), ground-station pass prediction (AOS/TCA/LOS, max elevation, access), and a one-way link budget over the CCSDS 401 / DSN 810-005 link equation (FSPL, C/N₀, Eb/N₀, margin, closure). The pre-hardware analytic layer below STK/GMAT/Basilisk — modelled first-order budgets, not a 6-DoF or radiometric replacement.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>SMAD</b> Space Mission Analysis and Design (the reference textbook) · <b>GSD</b> ground sample distance · <b>DOF</b> degrees of freedom · <b>RSS</b> root sum square · <b>AOS</b> acquisition of signal · <b>TCA</b> time of closest approach · <b>LOS</b> loss of signal · <b>CCSDS</b> Consultative Committee for Space Data Systems · <b>DSN</b> Deep Space Network · <b>FSPL</b> free-space path loss</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Orbits" data-q="navigation-versus-communications aperture sharing mission analysis orbits a contact plan and an aperture count in; navigation duty, communications duty, per-session outage and contention out. the arbitration policy is named in the report rather than implied, and a session that cannot be fully served reports the fraction of it that was lost instead of disappearing into an average. per-session outage and contention, not one duty figure aperture-duty-cycle"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Navigation-versus-communications aperture sharing</b><span class="cx-d">Mission analysis</span><span class=cx-p>Per-session outage and contention, not one duty figure</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A contact plan and an aperture count in; navigation duty, communications duty, per-session outage and contention out. The arbitration policy is named in the report rather than implied, and a session that cannot be fully served reports the fraction of it that was lost instead of disappearing into an average.</p><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=aperture-duty-cycle&amp;tab=overview">Run aperture-duty-cycle in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Orbits" data-q="space-weather &amp; thermospheric density space environment orbits a space-weather environment model: solar (f10.7 / centred-81-day f10.7a) and geomagnetic (kp, with the definitional kp↔ap table) activity indices, the jacchia-1971 exospheric temperature they drive, and the activity-corrected versus static thermospheric neutral density at altitude — the solar-cycle density dependence a static atmosphere omits. a calibrated first-order scale-height coupling, not a data-validated nrlmsise atmosphere. jacchia-71 validated vs published solar min / mean / max space-weather"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Space-weather &amp; thermospheric density</b><span class="cx-d">Space environment</span><span class=cx-p>Jacchia-71 validated vs published solar min / mean / max</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A space-weather environment model: solar (F10.7 / centred-81-day F10.7a) and geomagnetic (Kp, with the definitional Kp↔ap table) activity indices, the Jacchia-1971 exospheric temperature they drive, and the activity-corrected versus static thermospheric neutral density at altitude — the solar-cycle density dependence a static atmosphere omits. A calibrated first-order scale-height coupling, not a data-validated NRLMSISE atmosphere.</p><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=space-weather&amp;tab=overview">Run space-weather in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Machine learning" data-q="rf-impairment detection eval &amp; pnt trade machine-learning evaluation &amp; trade machine learning an rf-impairment detection evaluation testbed: a labelled, parameter-grounded synthetic corpus (nominal / jamming / spoof-time / spoof-position / multipath), a detector-agnostic roc/auc harness scoring any detector with per-class pd at a target pfa, and the in- versus out-of-distribution optimism gap. a deeper optimism-gap study extends this to a 13-detector panel (energy / agc / sqm / parity plus seeded logistic-regression and one-hidden-layer-mlp detectors), in- versus out-of-distribution scaling laws with a permutation null, and a leave-one-out predictor of out-of-distribution degradation; a software-defined-receiver front end (raw iq/if to correlator early/prompt/late taps to sqm) and real-data ingest adapters (rinex, u-blox ubx, gnsslogger, jammertest, yunnan, satgrid) let the same detectors run over recordings supplied locally. plus a quantum-vs-classical pnt trade that quantifies a candidate clock&#x27;s timing and inertial holdover benefit from a measured-adev curve against a classical baseline, with a gnss-denied resilience-vs-time envelope. modelled operating characteristics — never field/iq data, no good/bad verdict. auc harness verified vs binormal closed form φ(d′/√2) (gneiting &amp; vogel 2018); eval metrics validated bit-for-bit vs scikit-learn 1.9.0 — synthetic corpus, not raw-iq/texbat impairment-eval radio frequency positioning, navigation and timing area under the curve in-phase and quadrature samples receiver operating characteristic automatic gain control signal-quality monitoring multilayer perceptron receiver independent exchange format the u-blox binary receiver protocol allan deviation global navigation satellite system"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">RF-impairment detection eval &amp; PNT trade</b><span class="cx-d">Machine-learning evaluation &amp; trade</span><span class=cx-p>AUC harness verified vs binormal closed form Φ(d′/√2) (Gneiting &amp; Vogel 2018); eval metrics validated bit-for-bit vs scikit-learn 1.9.0 — synthetic corpus, not raw-IQ/TEXBAT</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>An RF-impairment detection evaluation testbed: a labelled, parameter-grounded synthetic corpus (nominal / jamming / spoof-time / spoof-position / multipath), a detector-agnostic ROC/AUC harness scoring any detector with per-class Pd at a target Pfa, and the in- versus out-of-distribution optimism gap. A deeper optimism-gap study extends this to a 13-detector panel (energy / AGC / SQM / parity plus seeded logistic-regression and one-hidden-layer-MLP detectors), in- versus out-of-distribution scaling laws with a permutation null, and a leave-one-out predictor of out-of-distribution degradation; a software-defined-receiver front end (raw IQ/IF to correlator early/prompt/late taps to SQM) and real-data ingest adapters (RINEX, u-blox UBX, GnssLogger, JammerTest, Yunnan, SatGrid) let the same detectors run over recordings supplied locally. Plus a quantum-vs-classical PNT trade that quantifies a candidate clock&#x27;s timing and inertial holdover benefit from a measured-ADEV curve against a classical baseline, with a GNSS-denied resilience-vs-time envelope. Modelled operating characteristics — never field/IQ data, no good/bad verdict.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>RF</b> radio frequency · <b>PNT</b> positioning, navigation and timing · <b>AUC</b> area under the curve · <b>IQ</b> in-phase and quadrature samples · <b>ROC</b> receiver operating characteristic · <b>AGC</b> automatic gain control · <b>SQM</b> signal-quality monitoring · <b>MLP</b> multilayer perceptron · <b>RINEX</b> Receiver Independent Exchange Format · <b>UBX</b> the u-blox binary receiver protocol · <b>ADEV</b> Allan deviation · <b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=impairment-eval&amp;tab=overview">Run impairment-eval in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Machine learning" data-q="quantum-vs-classical resilience crossover map machine-learning evaluation &amp; trade machine learning a reproducible map of where a quantum sensor overtakes its classical counterpart under parameter uncertainty: cold-atom versus navigation-grade dead-reckoning and optical versus chip-scale clock holdover, over outage duration and platform vibration, each curve carrying a technology-readiness label. reproducible via cargo run --bin crossover_study. modelled performance budgets — the inputs are published or bracketed coefficients, not flight data. regenerates the inertial and clock crossover studies behind the results figures; trade kernels validated vs scipy 1.17.1 "><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Quantum-vs-classical resilience crossover map</b><span class="cx-d">Machine-learning evaluation &amp; trade</span><span class=cx-p>regenerates the inertial and clock crossover studies behind the Results figures; trade kernels validated vs scipy 1.17.1</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A reproducible map of where a quantum sensor overtakes its classical counterpart under parameter uncertainty: cold-atom versus navigation-grade dead-reckoning and optical versus chip-scale clock holdover, over outage duration and platform vibration, each curve carrying a technology-readiness label. Reproducible via cargo run --bin crossover_study. Modelled performance budgets — the inputs are published or bracketed coefficients, not flight data.</p></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Timing" data-q="conditional timing protection level timing integrity timing a holdover-limited bound on the undetected time error under gnss spoofing: given an independent cross-check, the served-time error is bounded by a k-sigma monitor floor, the van-loan coast variance over the detection latency, and a cusum time-to-alarm. calibrated on a real recorded spoof (jammertest 2024) and reproducible via cargo run --example tpl_jammertest. modelled composition, conditional on detection — there is no finite unconditional bound, since a slow-enough ramp evades a single clock-aided monitor. composition cross-checked vs the multi-step clock_state covariance recursion; calibrated on jammertest 2024 (zenodo 15911589)  global navigation satellite system cumulative sum"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Conditional Timing Protection Level</b><span class="cx-d">Timing integrity</span><span class=cx-p>composition cross-checked vs the multi-step clock_state covariance recursion; calibrated on JammerTest 2024 (Zenodo 15911589)</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A holdover-limited bound on the undetected time error under GNSS spoofing: given an independent cross-check, the served-time error is bounded by a k-sigma monitor floor, the van-Loan coast variance over the detection latency, and a CUSUM time-to-alarm. Calibrated on a real recorded spoof (JammerTest 2024) and reproducible via cargo run --example tpl_jammertest. Modelled composition, conditional on detection — there is no finite unconditional bound, since a slow-enough ramp evades a single clock-aided monitor.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System · <b>CUSUM</b> cumulative sum</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Resilience" data-q="pnt-resilience scoring &amp; decision-instability resilience assessment resilience a framework-aligned scoring engine (dhs rpcf categories) that maps an architecture&#x27;s simulated behaviour to per-dimension sub-scores, then asks whether any single composite score or maturity level is a stable basis for a decision — via a dirichlet weighting simplex, kendall-tau rank instability, a top-1 winner flip rate, and common-mode diversity collapse (hill-n2) — with an integrity-hashed assurance report. reproducible via cargo run --example resilience_report. modelled synthetic architectures aligned to rpcf v2.0; a self-assessment, not a certification. 35 hand-derived oracle tests; byte-deterministic study artifact (fixed seed); aligned to dhs rpcf v2.0 conflict-resilience positioning, navigation and timing united states department of homeland security resilient pnt conformance framework"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">PNT-resilience scoring &amp; decision-instability</b><span class="cx-d">Resilience assessment</span><span class=cx-p>35 hand-derived oracle tests; byte-deterministic study artifact (fixed seed); aligned to DHS RPCF v2.0</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A framework-aligned scoring engine (DHS RPCF categories) that maps an architecture&#x27;s simulated behaviour to per-dimension sub-scores, then asks whether any single composite score or maturity Level is a stable basis for a decision — via a Dirichlet weighting simplex, Kendall-tau rank instability, a top-1 winner flip rate, and common-mode diversity collapse (Hill-N2) — with an integrity-hashed assurance report. Reproducible via cargo run --example resilience_report. Modelled synthetic architectures aligned to RPCF v2.0; a self-assessment, not a certification.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>PNT</b> positioning, navigation and timing · <b>DHS</b> United States Department of Homeland Security · <b>RPCF</b> Resilient PNT Conformance Framework</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=conflict-resilience&amp;tab=overview">Run conflict-resilience in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="GNSS" data-q="detection-miss → integrity regions integrity impact gnss a detection-miss-to-integrity bridge: an undetected bias left by a missed spoof / jam / raim detection inflates the effective position error, which — held against context-specific alert limits — is classified into the stanford-esa integrity regions (available / unavailable / misleading / hazardous). composes the externally-anchored raim stanford classification; adds no new integrity mathematics. stanford-esa integrity regions (tossaint et al., navigation 2007)  european space agency receiver autonomous integrity monitoring"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Detection-miss → integrity regions</b><span class="cx-d">Integrity impact</span><span class=cx-p>Stanford-ESA integrity regions (Tossaint et al., NAVIGATION 2007)</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A detection-miss-to-integrity bridge: an undetected bias left by a missed spoof / jam / RAIM detection inflates the effective position error, which — held against context-specific alert limits — is classified into the Stanford-ESA integrity regions (available / unavailable / misleading / hazardous). Composes the externally-anchored RAIM Stanford classification; adds no new integrity mathematics.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>ESA</b> European Space Agency · <b>RAIM</b> receiver autonomous integrity monitoring</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Machine learning" data-q="cost-per-coverage roi decision support machine learning a cost-per-coverage roi lens — cost per unit of delivered coverage for a constellation / architecture trade. the coverage component composes the validated coverage geometry and the arithmetic is unit-tested, but the cost is a user input — so the ratio is a planning aid, not an externally-validated quantity (hence, honestly, no proof badge).   return on investment"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Cost-per-coverage ROI</b><span class="cx-d">Decision support</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A cost-per-coverage ROI lens — cost per unit of delivered coverage for a constellation / architecture trade. The coverage component composes the validated coverage geometry and the arithmetic is unit-tested, but the cost is a user input — so the ratio is a planning aid, not an externally-validated quantity (hence, honestly, no proof badge).</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>ROI</b> return on investment</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Interop" data-q="kshana interchange format (kif) artifact interchange interop a versioned, self-describing envelope that wraps a scenario result with its kind, schema version, and modelled/validated labels, so a stored artifact stays self-documenting and older envelopes remain forward-compatibly readable. versioned envelope · round-trip + forward-compatible read verified lunar-interop-export kshana interchange format"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Kshana Interchange Format (KIF)</b><span class="cx-d">Artifact interchange</span><span class=cx-p>versioned envelope · round-trip + forward-compatible read verified</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A versioned, self-describing envelope that wraps a scenario result with its kind, schema version, and modelled/validated labels, so a stored artifact stays self-documenting and older envelopes remain forward-compatibly readable.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>KIF</b> Kshana Interchange Format</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=lunar-interop-export&amp;tab=overview">Run lunar-interop-export in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Maritime" data-q="receiver-trust score for a vessel maritime trust maritime scores whether a vessel&#x27;s navigation fix can be trusted from the nmea 0183 its receiver and instruments already emit: a 0-100 score, three bands and the reasons behind them, per epoch, in batch, live or as a gate. monitors compare the fix with the vessel&#x27;s own motion, heading sensor, speed log and satellite signal levels. advisory software, not type-approved navigation equipment: the operator stays responsible. the monitors&#x27; arithmetic is checked against independent libraries; the score&#x27;s rule and thresholds are the project&#x27;s own and stay modelled. monitor arithmetic checked against pynmea2 and geographiclib "><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Receiver-trust score for a vessel</b><span class="cx-d">Maritime trust</span><span class=cx-p>monitor arithmetic checked against pynmea2 and GeographicLib</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Scores whether a vessel&#x27;s navigation fix can be trusted from the NMEA 0183 its receiver and instruments already emit: a 0-100 score, three bands and the reasons behind them, per epoch, in batch, live or as a gate. Monitors compare the fix with the vessel&#x27;s own motion, heading sensor, speed log and satellite signal levels. Advisory software, not type-approved navigation equipment: the operator stays responsible. The monitors&#x27; arithmetic is checked against independent libraries; the score&#x27;s rule and thresholds are the project&#x27;s own and stay modelled.</p></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Maritime" data-q="crew-training nmea streams maritime trust maritime writes synthetic bridge nmea 0183 for a scripted voyage with instructor-scripted jamming, position drag-off, time-spoof and replay events, with an instructor log, so a crew can see what a degraded fix looks like on their own instruments. text only, never for a vessel&#x27;s live navigation systems; nothing is transmitted and no radio signal is made. the sentences and their decoded values are checked; the receiver, sky and event models are modelled. 225,227 sentences parsed and decoded by pynmea2 "><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Crew-training NMEA streams</b><span class="cx-d">Maritime trust</span><span class=cx-p>225,227 sentences parsed and decoded by pynmea2</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Writes synthetic bridge NMEA 0183 for a scripted voyage with instructor-scripted jamming, position drag-off, time-spoof and replay events, with an instructor log, so a crew can see what a degraded fix looks like on their own instruments. Text only, never for a vessel&#x27;s live navigation systems; nothing is transmitted and no radio signal is made. The sentences and their decoded values are checked; the receiver, sky and event models are modelled.</p></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Maritime" data-q="interference map and route exposure maritime trust maritime builds a per-day geojson map of where aircraft (ads-b) and ships (ais) reported degraded navigation data, from openly licensed inputs, and the share of a route that passes through degraded cells. a degraded cell is a statement about reported accuracy fields or implausible positions, not a finding of interference, and a cell nobody observed is not evidence of a clear route. past reports, not a forecast. code meanings and geometry checked against pymodes, pyais, shapely and geographiclib "><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Interference map and route exposure</b><span class="cx-d">Maritime trust</span><span class=cx-p>code meanings and geometry checked against pyModeS, pyais, shapely and GeographicLib</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Builds a per-day GeoJSON map of where aircraft (ADS-B) and ships (AIS) reported degraded navigation data, from openly licensed inputs, and the share of a route that passes through degraded cells. A degraded cell is a statement about reported accuracy fields or implausible positions, not a finding of interference, and a cell nobody observed is not evidence of a clear route. Past reports, not a forecast.</p></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Maritime" data-q="trust telemetry for security operations maritime trust maritime exports the trust stream as prometheus metrics, opentelemetry (otlp/http) and cef or leef syslog events over stdout, http, udp or tcp, so a security operations centre sees a navigation fault next to its other alerts. a dead collector neither stalls the assessment nor goes unnoticed. prometheus, otlp and cef are read back by independent parsers; leef is checked against its own specification only. prometheus, otlp and cef read back by independent parsers "><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Trust telemetry for security operations</b><span class="cx-d">Maritime trust</span><span class=cx-p>Prometheus, OTLP and CEF read back by independent parsers</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Exports the trust stream as Prometheus metrics, OpenTelemetry (OTLP/HTTP) and CEF or LEEF syslog events over stdout, HTTP, UDP or TCP, so a security operations centre sees a navigation fault next to its other alerts. A dead collector neither stalls the assessment nor goes unnoticed. Prometheus, OTLP and CEF are read back by independent parsers; LEEF is checked against its own specification only.</p></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Maritime" data-q="signed evidence packs maritime trust maritime bundles the window around a trust event into a hash-chained, ed25519-signed pack that anyone can verify offline: a changed, removed, swapped or added file, a re-signed pack or a wrong pinned key is named. an optional rfc 3161 timestamp token is bound to it. a technical record, not a legal opinion, finding of fact or attestation. rfc 8032 vectors byte for byte; a clean-room verifier agrees on 71 packs "><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Signed evidence packs</b><span class="cx-d">Maritime trust</span><span class=cx-p>RFC 8032 vectors byte for byte; a clean-room verifier agrees on 71 packs</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Bundles the window around a trust event into a hash-chained, Ed25519-signed pack that anyone can verify offline: a changed, removed, swapped or added file, a re-signed pack or a wrong pinned key is named. An optional RFC 3161 timestamp token is bound to it. A technical record, not a legal opinion, finding of fact or attestation.</p></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Interop" data-q="test-bench export interoperability interop writes a scenario&#x27;s vehicle motion and events as a csv trajectory and nmea 0183 for a laboratory gnss simulator: earth-fixed, geodetic and local-velocity columns, attitude and utc. motion and events only: no signal is written and no simulator or receiver performance is claimed. conversions and encodings checked against proj, geographiclib, pynmea2 and scipy  comma-separated values global navigation satellite system coordinated universal time"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Test-bench export</b><span class="cx-d">Interoperability</span><span class=cx-p>conversions and encodings checked against PROJ, GeographicLib, pynmea2 and SciPy</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Writes a scenario&#x27;s vehicle motion and events as a CSV trajectory and NMEA 0183 for a laboratory GNSS simulator: Earth-fixed, geodetic and local-velocity columns, attitude and UTC. Motion and events only: no signal is written and no simulator or receiver performance is claimed.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>CSV</b> comma-separated values · <b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System · <b>UTC</b> Coordinated Universal Time</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Maritime" data-q="compliance mapping to public frameworks maritime trust maritime maps kshana outputs to the clauses of public frameworks (imo guidance for ships, eu nis2 article 21, en 16803, the us dhs resilient-pnt framework and easa aviation guidance) by clause number only, filled from the runs you give it, and says which rows a set of runs supports evidence for, with each row&#x27;s gap. it is not a finding that any framework is met and not a rating or approval by anyone.   united states department of homeland security positioning, navigation and timing"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Compliance mapping to public frameworks</b><span class="cx-d">Maritime trust</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Maps Kshana outputs to the clauses of public frameworks (IMO guidance for ships, EU NIS2 Article 21, EN 16803, the US DHS resilient-PNT framework and EASA aviation guidance) by clause number only, filled from the runs you give it, and says which rows a set of runs supports evidence for, with each row&#x27;s gap. It is not a finding that any framework is met and not a rating or approval by anyone.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>DHS</b> United States Department of Homeland Security · <b>PNT</b> positioning, navigation and timing</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="GNSS" data-q="galileo osnma verification gnss authentication gnss an open, pure-rust receiver-side verifier for galileo open service navigation message authentication: it checks which satellites&#x27; navigation data a receiver can authenticate from i/nav pages or a u-blox ubx stream, through the public-key, key-chain and tag checks of the signal-in-space specification, and reports authentication per satellite and per time. it authenticates data; it does not claim any anti-spoofing performance. all 18 official test-vector sets: 186,382 tags authenticated, 0 failed  global navigation satellite system the u-blox binary receiver protocol"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Galileo OSNMA verification</b><span class="cx-d">GNSS authentication</span><span class=cx-p>all 18 official test-vector sets: 186,382 tags authenticated, 0 failed</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>An open, pure-Rust receiver-side verifier for Galileo Open Service Navigation Message Authentication: it checks which satellites&#x27; navigation data a receiver can authenticate from I/NAV pages or a u-blox UBX stream, through the public-key, key-chain and tag checks of the signal-in-space specification, and reports authentication per satellite and per time. It authenticates data; it does not claim any anti-spoofing performance.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System · <b>UBX</b> the u-blox binary receiver protocol</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="GNSS" data-q="skyline-masked visibility, dop and error ellipse sky geometry gnss which satellites a site can see when buildings, quays, cranes or terrain block part of the sky, and what that does to the dilution of precision (dop) and the horizontal error ellipse. an azimuth-dependent skyline replaces the single elevation cut-off; the visible set, its dop and the ellipse come out per epoch and per constellation. a skyline removes line-of-sight satellites and nothing more: it models no reflections, no diffraction and no signal strength. 1173 look angles and 142 visible sets match orekit 12.2 and gnss_lib_py 1.0.4 to 1e-12; the ellipse matches numpy on 270 cases  dilution of precision"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Skyline-masked visibility, DOP and error ellipse</b><span class="cx-d">Sky geometry</span><span class=cx-p>1173 look angles and 142 visible sets match Orekit 12.2 and gnss_lib_py 1.0.4 to 1e-12; the ellipse matches numpy on 270 cases</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Which satellites a site can see when buildings, quays, cranes or terrain block part of the sky, and what that does to the dilution of precision (DOP) and the horizontal error ellipse. An azimuth-dependent skyline replaces the single elevation cut-off; the visible set, its DOP and the ellipse come out per epoch and per constellation. A skyline removes line-of-sight satellites and nothing more: it models no reflections, no diffraction and no signal strength.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>DOP</b> dilution of precision</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="GNSS" data-q="skyline presets and the sky-geometry functions sky geometry gnss the street-canyon, sector and terrain reductions to a skyline table, four representative presets (city street, harbour approach, fjord, container terminal) and the functions that compose them into a skyplot and a monte carlo cloud, from the cli, mcp, python and the browser. the presets are representative geometries with chosen, stated dimensions, not surveys, and the reductions are checked only against hand-computed values. hand-computed values and the geometry the cloud samples; no external oracle "><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Skyline presets and the sky-geometry functions</b><span class="cx-d">Sky geometry</span><span class=cx-p>hand-computed values and the geometry the cloud samples; no external oracle</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>The street-canyon, sector and terrain reductions to a skyline table, four representative presets (city street, harbour approach, fjord, container terminal) and the functions that compose them into a skyplot and a Monte Carlo cloud, from the CLI, MCP, Python and the browser. The presets are representative geometries with chosen, stated dimensions, not surveys, and the reductions are checked only against hand-computed values.</p></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Maritime" data-q="geometry-aware trust classification maritime trust maritime puts the satellite geometry beside the receiver-trust score and names a class on every deduction: signal loss, poor geometry or an inconsistency between the receiver&#x27;s stated dop and the one derived from the satellites in view. geometry is judged only when it is known, and the label is the headline: only a poor geometry costs points. advisory software, not type-approved navigation equipment. the classification rule and thresholds are the project&#x27;s own, checked against themselves  dilution of precision"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Geometry-aware trust classification</b><span class="cx-d">Maritime trust</span><span class=cx-p>the classification rule and thresholds are the project&#x27;s own, checked against themselves</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Puts the satellite geometry beside the receiver-trust score and names a class on every deduction: signal loss, poor geometry or an inconsistency between the receiver&#x27;s stated DOP and the one derived from the satellites in view. Geometry is judged only when it is known, and the label is the headline: only a poor geometry costs points. Advisory software, not type-approved navigation equipment.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>DOP</b> dilution of precision</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Lunar" data-q="lunar reference-station study lunar lunar one reproducible study of what a lunar south-pole reference station could do under public assumptions: a coverage and accuracy map over the polar region (percentile corrected and uncorrected position error, availability with a reason for every unavailable epoch, protection levels and the correction-link budget), a time laboratory that holds a lunar time reference on an atomic clock, and the geodesy of an added laser retroreflector and vlbi beacon against the five-array datum. every input is labelled with its public source or as illustrative. it is not a statement about the performance of any real system, and kshana is not affiliated with any agency. composes tested lunar differential, clock and frame functions; checked against themselves and a numpy reference, no external flight data  very long baseline interferometry"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Lunar reference-station study</b><span class="cx-d">Lunar</span><span class=cx-p>composes tested lunar differential, clock and frame functions; checked against themselves and a numpy reference, no external flight data</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>One reproducible study of what a lunar south-pole reference station could do under public assumptions: a coverage and accuracy map over the polar region (percentile corrected and uncorrected position error, availability with a reason for every unavailable epoch, protection levels and the correction-link budget), a time laboratory that holds a lunar time reference on an atomic clock, and the geodesy of an added laser retroreflector and VLBI beacon against the five-array datum. Every input is labelled with its public source or as illustrative. It is not a statement about the performance of any real system, and Kshana is not affiliated with any agency.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>VLBI</b> very long baseline interferometry</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Lunar" data-q="optical timing links through clouds and turbulence optical / rf pnt lunar how often an optical time-transfer link is up when the atmosphere is in the path. for each ground site the engine gives the clear-air loss, the hufnagel-valley turbulence state with gamma-gamma scintillation fades and aperture averaging, the availability against the link margin from a stated cloud climate, the ceiling no margin can lift, the outage-duration distribution and the timing error an outage costs a holdover clock. it then shows what extra sites, a larger aperture, another wavelength or adaptive optics buy. the bundled cloud climates are illustrative, not measured; the output is advisory. 7 of 7 pre-registered numerical bars met against an independent mpmath program; no measured availability record has been compared  radio frequency positioning, navigation and timing"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Optical timing links through clouds and turbulence</b><span class="cx-d">Optical / RF PNT</span><span class=cx-p>7 of 7 pre-registered numerical bars met against an independent mpmath program; no measured availability record has been compared</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>How often an optical time-transfer link is up when the atmosphere is in the path. For each ground site the engine gives the clear-air loss, the Hufnagel-Valley turbulence state with gamma-gamma scintillation fades and aperture averaging, the availability against the link margin from a stated cloud climate, the ceiling no margin can lift, the outage-duration distribution and the timing error an outage costs a holdover clock. It then shows what extra sites, a larger aperture, another wavelength or adaptive optics buy. The bundled cloud climates are illustrative, not measured; the output is advisory.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>RF</b> radio frequency · <b>PNT</b> positioning, navigation and timing</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="GNSS" data-q="gnss beyond the constellation: the space service volume and past it nav-signal performance gnss for a spacecraft on a sphere (the global shell of the icg space service volume booklet) or on a keplerian trajectory from leo to lunar transfer, counts per constellation (gps, glonass, galileo, bds, qzss, navic) and for all together the signals above a c/n0 threshold: availability of one and of four signals, longest outage, received c/n0, dilution of precision, and main-lobe against sidelobe links. transmit patterns are the booklet&#x27;s, a parametric stand-in or one you supply; a limb-ray ionosphere, plasmasphere and atmosphere model and a weak-signal receiver design are optional; an onboard gnss filter for high and lunar-transfer orbits and formation relative navigation are included. modelled: compared with the booklet&#x27;s tables under a pre-registration committed before the run, the minimum-radiated-power constants and the lunar bar agree, the other bars are not met under the pre-registered reading (almost all through the booklet&#x27;s inconsistent glonass beam angle), and the parametric sidelobe stand-in does not reproduce the measured gps panel patterns. icg ssv booklet tables, pre-registered; most bars not met (xval/gnss-ssv/results.md) gnss-ssv-shell global navigation satellite system low earth orbit global positioning system quasi-zenith satellite system"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">GNSS beyond the constellation: the Space Service Volume and past it</b><span class="cx-d">Nav-signal performance</span><span class=cx-p>ICG SSV booklet tables, pre-registered; most bars NOT MET (xval/gnss-ssv/RESULTS.md)</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>For a spacecraft on a sphere (the global shell of the ICG Space Service Volume booklet) or on a Keplerian trajectory from LEO to lunar transfer, counts per constellation (GPS, GLONASS, Galileo, BDS, QZSS, NavIC) and for all together the signals above a C/N0 threshold: availability of one and of four signals, longest outage, received C/N0, dilution of precision, and main-lobe against sidelobe links. Transmit patterns are the booklet&#x27;s, a parametric stand-in or one you supply; a limb-ray ionosphere, plasmasphere and atmosphere model and a weak-signal receiver design are optional; an onboard GNSS filter for high and lunar-transfer orbits and formation relative navigation are included. MODELLED: compared with the booklet&#x27;s tables under a pre-registration committed before the run, the minimum-radiated-power constants and the lunar bar agree, the other bars are NOT MET under the pre-registered reading (almost all through the booklet&#x27;s inconsistent GLONASS beam angle), and the parametric sidelobe stand-in does not reproduce the measured GPS panel patterns.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System · <b>LEO</b> low Earth orbit · <b>GPS</b> Global Positioning System · <b>QZSS</b> Quasi-Zenith Satellite System</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=gnss-ssv-shell&amp;tab=overview">Run gnss-ssv-shell in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Lunar" data-q="lunar orbit and surface as navigation and geodetic infrastructure lunar &amp; cislunar lunar five studies in a restricted three-body model: an onboard earth-gnss orbit determination in a near-rectilinear halo orbit against an independent ground range and range-rate determination; joint surface-station and orbiter carrier-phase integer ambiguity fixing (lambda); the fisher information of a seven-parameter lunar datum with and without orbiter-to-surface ranges; gnss, vlbi and laser-ranging offsets against the local tie; and sensitivity to the lunar tidal and libration parameters. modelled with illustrative inputs: the numerics are checked against independent numpy and scipy programs, not the physics. under the pre-registered bars, three are not met: an injected force error is not detected at the stated rate (c3), the bootstrapped success rate differs from the numpy oracle by 1.7e-6 against a bar of 1e-9 (q3), and adding a ranging family does not always lower the pseudo-inverse trace (g3, trace half). numpy/scipy oracles of the numerics; three pre-registered bars not met (xval/lunar-infra/results.md) lunar-infrastructure-od-consistency global navigation satellite system very long baseline interferometry"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Lunar orbit and surface as navigation and geodetic infrastructure</b><span class="cx-d">Lunar &amp; cislunar</span><span class=cx-p>numpy/scipy oracles of the numerics; three pre-registered bars NOT MET (xval/lunar-infra/RESULTS.md)</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Five studies in a restricted three-body model: an onboard Earth-GNSS orbit determination in a near-rectilinear halo orbit against an independent ground range and range-rate determination; joint surface-station and orbiter carrier-phase integer ambiguity fixing (LAMBDA); the Fisher information of a seven-parameter lunar datum with and without orbiter-to-surface ranges; GNSS, VLBI and laser-ranging offsets against the local tie; and sensitivity to the lunar tidal and libration parameters. MODELLED with illustrative inputs: the numerics are checked against independent numpy and scipy programs, not the physics. Under the pre-registered bars, three are NOT MET: an injected force error is not detected at the stated rate (C3), the bootstrapped success rate differs from the numpy oracle by 1.7e-6 against a bar of 1e-9 (Q3), and adding a ranging family does not always lower the pseudo-inverse trace (G3, trace half).</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System · <b>VLBI</b> very long baseline interferometry</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=lunar-infrastructure-od-consistency&amp;tab=overview">Run lunar-infrastructure-od-consistency in Kshana Studio</a></div></details></div><p class="fl-empty" data-fl-empty hidden>Nothing matches that filter.</p><button class="fl-more" type="button" data-fl-more>Show all 61 capabilities</button></div>
+<div class="cx" data-flist data-fl-limit="10"><div class="fl-bar"><div class="fl-seg" role="group" aria-label="Filter by status" data-fl-key="s"><button type="button" data-v="" aria-pressed="true">All 61</button><button type="button" data-v="v" aria-pressed="false">Validated 24</button><button type="button" data-v="m" aria-pressed="false">Modelled 37</button></div><div class="fl-seg" role="group" aria-label="Filter by group" data-fl-key="g"><button type="button" data-v="" aria-pressed="true">Every group</button><button type="button" data-v="Orbits" aria-pressed="false">Orbits</button><button type="button" data-v="Timing" aria-pressed="false">Timing</button><button type="button" data-v="Inertial" aria-pressed="false">Inertial</button><button type="button" data-v="Lunar" aria-pressed="false">Lunar</button><button type="button" data-v="GNSS" aria-pressed="false">GNSS</button><button type="button" data-v="Resilience" aria-pressed="false">Resilience</button><button type="button" data-v="Interop" aria-pressed="false">Interop</button><button type="button" data-v="Machine learning" aria-pressed="false">Machine learning</button><button type="button" data-v="Maritime" aria-pressed="false">Maritime</button></div><label class="fl-q"><span class="vh">Search the capabilities</span><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-search"/></svg><input type="search" data-fl-q placeholder="Search capability, domain or proof"></label><output class="fl-count" data-fl-count aria-live="polite"></output></div><div class="cx-rows"><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Orbits" data-q="orbit propagation &amp; constellation design orbit &amp; geometry orbits sgp4/sdp4 propagation from real two-line elements (a committed celestrak gps snapshot) or synthetic walker constellations whose mean elements realise the i:t/p/f formula to under 1 km over 24 h. multi-constellation visibility, dilution of precision and gnss availability, a constellation-design optimiser, streets-of-coverage sizing, and a walker design sweep that reports the pareto-optimal designs over a planes × satellites grid. 666/666 aiaa vectors · 4.12 mm orbit-sgp4-gps american institute of aeronautics and astronautics simplified general perturbations 4, an orbit model simplified deep-space perturbations 4, an orbit model global positioning system global navigation satellite system"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Orbit propagation &amp; constellation design</b><span class="cx-d">Orbit &amp; geometry</span><span class=cx-p>666/666 AIAA vectors · 4.12 mm</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>SGP4/SDP4 propagation from real two-line elements (a committed Celestrak GPS snapshot) or synthetic Walker constellations whose mean elements realise the i:T/P/F formula to under 1 km over 24 h. Multi-constellation visibility, dilution of precision and GNSS availability, a constellation-design optimiser, streets-of-coverage sizing, and a Walker design sweep that reports the Pareto-optimal designs over a planes × satellites grid.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>AIAA</b> American Institute of Aeronautics and Astronautics · <b>SGP4</b> Simplified General Perturbations 4, an orbit model · <b>SDP4</b> Simplified Deep-space Perturbations 4, an orbit model · <b>GPS</b> Global Positioning System · <b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=orbit-sgp4-gps&amp;tab=overview">Run orbit-sgp4-gps in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Orbits" data-q="cowell propagator &amp; six-perturbation force model numerical propagator orbits a numerical (cowell) propagator complementing the analytic sgp4/sdp4 path, with a hierarchical force model: two-body + the full j2–j6 zonal field, an optional egm2008 tesseral geopotential to degree/order 70, epoch-driven sun and moon third-body gravity (a built-in ephemeris, no de/spk kernel), solar-radiation pressure with a conical umbra/penumbra shadow, atmospheric drag, the post-newtonian schwarzschild relativistic correction, and the lense–thirring frame-dragging term — driven by a choice of rk4 step-doubling or dormand–prince rk5(4) adaptive integrators. conservative tiers &lt; 0.08 m / 24 h vs orekit 12.2  simplified general perturbations 4, an orbit model simplified deep-space perturbations 4, an orbit model earth gravitational model 2008"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Cowell propagator &amp; six-perturbation force model</b><span class="cx-d">Numerical propagator</span><span class=cx-p>conservative tiers &lt; 0.08 m / 24 h vs Orekit 12.2</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A numerical (Cowell) propagator complementing the analytic SGP4/SDP4 path, with a hierarchical force model: two-body + the full J2–J6 zonal field, an optional EGM2008 tesseral geopotential to degree/order 70, epoch-driven Sun and Moon third-body gravity (a built-in ephemeris, no DE/SPK kernel), solar-radiation pressure with a conical umbra/penumbra shadow, atmospheric drag, the post-Newtonian Schwarzschild relativistic correction, and the Lense–Thirring frame-dragging term — driven by a choice of RK4 step-doubling or Dormand–Prince RK5(4) adaptive integrators.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>SGP4</b> Simplified General Perturbations 4, an orbit model · <b>SDP4</b> Simplified Deep-space Perturbations 4, an orbit model · <b>EGM2008</b> Earth Gravitational Model 2008</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Orbits" data-q="burns, lambert &amp; porkchop maneuvers &amp; trajectory design orbits impulsive δv nodes with 6×6 covariance propagation, finite-burn integration checked against the tsiolkovsky rocket equation to under 0.01 %, an izzo-2015 single-revolution lambert solver and an exact universal-variable kepler propagator, and a porkchop (launch × arrival) c3 / arrival-v∞ sweep emitted as a json contour grid — the performance-simulation layer above gmat/orekit. tsiolkovsky &lt; 0.01 % · lambert round-trips to two-body truth  javascript object notation"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Burns, Lambert &amp; porkchop</b><span class="cx-d">Maneuvers &amp; trajectory design</span><span class=cx-p>Tsiolkovsky &lt; 0.01 % · Lambert round-trips to two-body truth</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Impulsive ΔV nodes with 6×6 covariance propagation, finite-burn integration checked against the Tsiolkovsky rocket equation to under 0.01 %, an Izzo-2015 single-revolution Lambert solver and an exact universal-variable Kepler propagator, and a porkchop (launch × arrival) C3 / arrival-V∞ sweep emitted as a JSON contour grid — the performance-simulation layer above GMAT/Orekit.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>JSON</b> JavaScript Object Notation</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Timing" data-q="time scales &amp; earth-orientation frames time systems timing iers leap-second utc / tai / tt / ut1 scales, a julian-date api, the iau-2000 earth rotation angle, gmst-based teme ↔ ecef and wgs-84 geodetic frames, full iau 2000a/2000b nutation and iau 2006 precession, and the equinox-free cio-based iau 2006/2000a gcrs↔itrs reduction with iers polar motion — validated bit-for-bit against the sofa/erfa vectors and independently cross-checked against anise (pure-rust spice): gcrs→itrs vs anise itrf93, same iers eop, agree to ≤ 0.86 m on the ground / ≤ 3.6 m at gnss orbit. bit-for-bit vs sofa/erfa · vallado end-to-end 4 mm  standards of fundamental astronomy essential routines for fundamental astronomy international earth rotation and reference systems service coordinated universal time international atomic time terrestrial time universal time 1, set by earth&#x27;s rotation application programming interface international astronomical union greenwich mean sidereal time true equator, mean equinox frame earth-centred, earth-fixed world geodetic system 1984 celestial intermediate origin geocentric celestial reference system international terrestrial reference system nasa&#x27;s spacecraft, planet, instrument, camera-matrix and events toolkit international terrestrial reference frame 1993 earth orientation parameters global navigation satellite system"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Time scales &amp; Earth-orientation frames</b><span class="cx-d">Time systems</span><span class=cx-p>bit-for-bit vs SOFA/ERFA · Vallado end-to-end 4 mm</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>IERS leap-second UTC / TAI / TT / UT1 scales, a Julian-date API, the IAU-2000 Earth Rotation Angle, GMST-based TEME ↔ ECEF and WGS-84 geodetic frames, full IAU 2000A/2000B nutation and IAU 2006 precession, and the equinox-free CIO-based IAU 2006/2000A GCRS↔ITRS reduction with IERS polar motion — validated bit-for-bit against the SOFA/ERFA vectors and independently cross-checked against ANISE (pure-Rust SPICE): GCRS→ITRS vs ANISE ITRF93, same IERS EOP, agree to ≤ 0.86 m on the ground / ≤ 3.6 m at GNSS orbit.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>SOFA</b> Standards of Fundamental Astronomy · <b>ERFA</b> Essential Routines for Fundamental Astronomy · <b>IERS</b> International Earth Rotation and Reference Systems Service · <b>UTC</b> Coordinated Universal Time · <b>TAI</b> International Atomic Time · <b>TT</b> Terrestrial Time · <b>UT1</b> Universal Time 1, set by Earth&#x27;s rotation · <b>API</b> application programming interface · <b>IAU</b> International Astronomical Union · <b>GMST</b> Greenwich mean sidereal time · <b>TEME</b> true equator, mean equinox frame · <b>ECEF</b> Earth-centred, Earth-fixed · <b>WGS-84</b> World Geodetic System 1984 · <b>CIO</b> Celestial Intermediate Origin · <b>GCRS</b> Geocentric Celestial Reference System · <b>ITRS</b> International Terrestrial Reference System · <b>SPICE</b> NASA&#x27;s spacecraft, planet, instrument, camera-matrix and events toolkit · <b>ITRF93</b> International Terrestrial Reference Frame 1993 · <b>EOP</b> Earth orientation parameters · <b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Timing" data-q="real-time earth-orientation prediction time systems timing an operational-style earth-orientation predictor — least squares over bias, rate and the principal periodic terms across a trailing window — measured against genuinely archived bulletin a predictions rather than against persistence. the offline default input is a real iers finals2000a product carrying bulletin a prediction rows, so a bare run emits a populated per-horizon table instead of an empty one. ut1 and polar motion are reported over a common row set together with their combination, so the joint figure is one statistic rather than a quadrature sum of two different samples. 12 leads against archived bulletin a: ut1 2.93 ms, polar motion 1.76 mas realtime-frame-eop universal time 1, set by earth&#x27;s rotation international earth rotation and reference systems service"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Real-time Earth-orientation prediction</b><span class="cx-d">Time systems</span><span class=cx-p>12 leads against archived Bulletin A: UT1 2.93 ms, polar motion 1.76 mas</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>An operational-style Earth-orientation predictor — least squares over bias, rate and the principal periodic terms across a trailing window — measured against genuinely archived Bulletin A predictions rather than against persistence. The offline default input is a real IERS finals2000A product carrying Bulletin A prediction rows, so a bare run emits a populated per-horizon table instead of an empty one. UT1 and polar motion are reported over a common row set together with their combination, so the joint figure is one statistic rather than a quadrature sum of two different samples.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>UT1</b> Universal Time 1, set by Earth&#x27;s rotation · <b>IERS</b> International Earth Rotation and Reference Systems Service</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=realtime-frame-eop&amp;tab=overview">Run realtime-frame-eop in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Inertial" data-q="strapdown ins &amp; map-aided navigation inertial inertial three-axis strapdown ins — quaternion attitude, wgs-84 ned mechanization, coning/sculling compensation, and a full deterministic imu error model — plus a first-principles cold-atom-interferometer accelerometer (mach–zehnder phase, projection noise, contrast decay, vibration coupling) and a particle filter for terrain-/gravity-referenced gps-denied navigation. first-principles cai accelerometer · derived vrw cross-checked vs an independent integrator imu-deadreckoning inertial navigation system cold-atom interferometer velocity random walk world geodetic system 1984 north-east-down frame inertial measurement unit global positioning system"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Strapdown INS &amp; map-aided navigation</b><span class="cx-d">Inertial</span><span class=cx-p>first-principles CAI accelerometer · derived VRW cross-checked vs an independent integrator</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Three-axis strapdown INS — quaternion attitude, WGS-84 NED mechanization, coning/sculling compensation, and a full deterministic IMU error model — plus a first-principles cold-atom-interferometer accelerometer (Mach–Zehnder phase, projection noise, contrast decay, vibration coupling) and a particle filter for terrain-/gravity-referenced GPS-denied navigation.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>INS</b> inertial navigation system · <b>CAI</b> cold-atom interferometer · <b>VRW</b> velocity random walk · <b>WGS-84</b> World Geodetic System 1984 · <b>NED</b> north-east-down frame · <b>IMU</b> inertial measurement unit · <b>GPS</b> Global Positioning System</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=imu-deadreckoning&amp;tab=overview">Run imu-deadreckoning in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Inertial" data-q="inertial and terrain-relative coasting through an outage inertial inertial position error against coast duration from an imu error budget — bias, random walk and scale factor, each with its own growth law and its own share of the crossing — so the cost of a navigation outage is a computed curve rather than a swept drift rate. threshold crossings are engine output with the dominant contribution named at each one, across four imu grades, and a terrain-fix interval is scored in its own block so the headline crossings stay unaided. 10 m at 249 s, 50 m at 595 s, accelerometer bias dominant at both ins-trn-coast inertial measurement unit"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Inertial and terrain-relative coasting through an outage</b><span class="cx-d">Inertial</span><span class=cx-p>10 m at 249 s, 50 m at 595 s, accelerometer bias dominant at both</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Position error against coast duration from an IMU error budget — bias, random walk and scale factor, each with its own growth law and its own share of the crossing — so the cost of a navigation outage is a computed curve rather than a swept drift rate. Threshold crossings are engine output with the dominant contribution named at each one, across four IMU grades, and a terrain-fix interval is scored in its own block so the headline crossings stay unaided.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>IMU</b> inertial measurement unit</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=ins-trn-coast&amp;tab=overview">Run ins-trn-coast in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Inertial" data-q="gravity, terrain &amp; magnetic map-matching alt-pnt (gps-denied) inertial a cold-atom gravimeter measurement model whose white-noise floor is derived from the cai accelerometer physics, a low-degree fully-normalised spherical-harmonic gravity-anomaly field plus synthetic mascons, and a gravity-map-matching particle filter — extended to terrain-referenced navigation against an srtm elevation dem (tercom/sitan), an igrf-14 geomagnetic main field to degree/order 13, and a combined gravity + magnetic + terrain navigator that fuses all three scalar channels. a 60-minute gps-denied benchmark — a ~700 km / one-hour outage where the inertial solution drifts to ~70 km — is recovered to ~145 m by a hierarchical coarse-to-fine matcher: the esa navisp quantum wayfarer target. 60-min gps-denied · ~145 m (&lt; 500 m) gps-denied-gravity-nav positioning, navigation and timing global positioning system cold-atom interferometer shuttle radar topography mission digital elevation model terrain contour matching sandia inertial terrain-aided navigation international geomagnetic reference field, 14th generation european space agency"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Gravity, terrain &amp; magnetic map-matching</b><span class="cx-d">Alt-PNT (GPS-denied)</span><span class=cx-p>60-min GPS-denied · ~145 m (&lt; 500 m)</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A cold-atom gravimeter measurement model whose white-noise floor is derived from the CAI accelerometer physics, a low-degree fully-normalised spherical-harmonic gravity-anomaly field plus synthetic mascons, and a gravity-map-matching particle filter — extended to terrain-referenced navigation against an SRTM elevation DEM (TERCOM/SITAN), an IGRF-14 geomagnetic main field to degree/order 13, and a combined gravity + magnetic + terrain navigator that fuses all three scalar channels. A 60-minute GPS-denied benchmark — a ~700 km / one-hour outage where the inertial solution drifts to ~70 km — is recovered to ~145 m by a hierarchical coarse-to-fine matcher: the ESA NAVISP Quantum Wayfarer target.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>PNT</b> positioning, navigation and timing · <b>GPS</b> Global Positioning System · <b>CAI</b> cold-atom interferometer · <b>SRTM</b> Shuttle Radar Topography Mission · <b>DEM</b> digital elevation model · <b>TERCOM</b> terrain contour matching · <b>SITAN</b> Sandia inertial terrain-aided navigation · <b>IGRF-14</b> International Geomagnetic Reference Field, 14th generation · <b>ESA</b> European Space Agency</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=gps-denied-gravity-nav&amp;tab=overview">Run gps-denied-gravity-nav in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Inertial" data-q="gnss / ins sensor fusion fusion inertial a 15-state error-state ekf that coasts through gnss outages on a calibrated inertial solution; a tightly-coupled pseudorange mode that keeps correcting with fewer than four satellites; a coupled clock + position filter; a general unscented (sigma-point) kalman estimator; a tightly-coupled gnss/ins ukf navigator validated to 0.77 m rms through a 120 s outage on a curving leo pass; and a full 17-state tightly-coupled gnss/ins ukf whose quantum-cai dead-reckoning coasts the outage on the cold-atom accelerometer&#x27;s derived velocity-random-walk. tightly-coupled ukf · 0.77 m rms gnss-ins global navigation satellite system inertial navigation system unscented kalman filter root mean square extended kalman filter low earth orbit cold-atom interferometer"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">GNSS / INS sensor fusion</b><span class="cx-d">Fusion</span><span class=cx-p>tightly-coupled UKF · 0.77 m RMS</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A 15-state error-state EKF that coasts through GNSS outages on a calibrated inertial solution; a tightly-coupled pseudorange mode that keeps correcting with fewer than four satellites; a coupled clock + position filter; a general unscented (sigma-point) Kalman estimator; a tightly-coupled GNSS/INS UKF navigator validated to 0.77 m RMS through a 120 s outage on a curving LEO pass; and a full 17-state tightly-coupled GNSS/INS UKF whose quantum-CAI dead-reckoning coasts the outage on the cold-atom accelerometer&#x27;s derived velocity-random-walk.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System · <b>INS</b> inertial navigation system · <b>UKF</b> unscented Kalman filter · <b>RMS</b> root mean square · <b>EKF</b> extended Kalman filter · <b>LEO</b> low Earth orbit · <b>CAI</b> cold-atom interferometer</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=gnss-ins&amp;tab=overview">Run gnss-ins in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Orbits" data-q="batch &amp; sequential od orbit determination orbits recovery of an orbital state from ground-station range tracking, composing the two-body + j2 force model and rk4 integrator with a gauss–newton batch differential corrector (sub-metre / mm·s⁻¹ from noiseless ranges, ~2 m at a 5 m noise floor) and a sequential unscented-filter variant. batch + sequential od vs orekit 12.2 batchlsestimator/kalmanestimator  orbit determination"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Batch &amp; sequential OD</b><span class="cx-d">Orbit determination</span><span class=cx-p>batch + sequential OD vs Orekit 12.2 BatchLSEstimator/KalmanEstimator</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Recovery of an orbital state from ground-station range tracking, composing the two-body + J2 force model and RK4 integrator with a Gauss–Newton batch differential corrector (sub-metre / mm·s⁻¹ from noiseless ranges, ~2 m at a 5 m noise floor) and a sequential unscented-filter variant.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>OD</b> orbit determination</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Orbits" data-q="fisher information &amp; observability observability &amp; estimation theory orbits a general, reusable fisher-information / cramér–rao layer (src/fim.rs): the information matrix m = hᵀwh, the cramér–rao lower bound, observability rank and the datum-defect null space (moore–penrose pseudo-inverse), and d/a/e/t-optimal experiment-design scalars from a symmetric jacobi eigensolver. it underpins the dop engine, the passive-geolocation bound, and the lunar absolute-station observability result — internal ranging leaves a six-dof rigid-body datum defect that an earth-baseline vlbi tie restores for a sparse constellation and sharpens for a rich one, the absolute datum closing at three non-collinear earth stations. eigenvalues vs numpy.linalg.eigh · crlb vs σ²(xᵀx)⁻¹ via numpy.linalg.inv · gnss dop from the information matrix, all to 1e-9 · kay (1993) closed forms + monte-carlo crlb attainment  cramér–rao lower bound global navigation satellite system dilution of precision degrees of freedom very long baseline interferometry"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Fisher information &amp; observability</b><span class="cx-d">Observability &amp; estimation theory</span><span class=cx-p>eigenvalues vs numpy.linalg.eigh · CRLB vs σ²(XᵀX)⁻¹ via numpy.linalg.inv · GNSS DOP from the information matrix, all to 1e-9 · Kay (1993) closed forms + Monte-Carlo CRLB attainment</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A general, reusable Fisher-information / Cramér–Rao layer (src/fim.rs): the information matrix M = HᵀWH, the Cramér–Rao lower bound, observability rank and the datum-defect null space (Moore–Penrose pseudo-inverse), and D/A/E/T-optimal experiment-design scalars from a symmetric Jacobi eigensolver. It underpins the DOP engine, the passive-geolocation bound, and the lunar absolute-station observability result — internal ranging leaves a six-DOF rigid-body datum defect that an Earth-baseline VLBI tie restores for a sparse constellation and sharpens for a rich one, the absolute datum closing at three non-collinear Earth stations.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>CRLB</b> Cramér–Rao lower bound · <b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System · <b>DOP</b> dilution of precision · <b>DOF</b> degrees of freedom · <b>VLBI</b> very long baseline interferometry</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Lunar" data-q="cr3bp propagation &amp; lunanet integrity lunar &amp; cislunar lunar an earth–moon circular restricted three-body (cr3bp) propagator in the rotating frame — a conserved jacobi constant and all five lagrange points — with a 6×6 state-transition matrix and a single-shooting differential corrector that produces genuinely periodic halo / near-rectilinear halo orbits (nrho): it reproduces the published l2 southern 9:2 nrho (the gateway orbit) at a period of ~6.57 days and a perilune of ~3,250 km. plus lunanet / lnis cislunar pnt geometry (mci↔mcmf reduction, selenographic coordinates) with a lunar south-pole araim pass that honestly surfaces the integrity gap: a ~30 m σ_ure drives the protection level well above a 50 m alert limit. reproduces the published l2 9:2 nrho (~6.57 d period · ~3,250 km perilune); stm checked vs finite differences lunanet-araim circular restricted three-body problem near-rectilinear halo orbit state transition matrix positioning, navigation and timing moon-centred inertial frame moon-centred, moon-fixed frame advanced receiver autonomous integrity monitoring"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">CR3BP propagation &amp; LunaNet integrity</b><span class="cx-d">Lunar &amp; cislunar</span><span class=cx-p>Reproduces the published L2 9:2 NRHO (~6.57 d period · ~3,250 km perilune); STM checked vs finite differences</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>An Earth–Moon circular restricted three-body (CR3BP) propagator in the rotating frame — a conserved Jacobi constant and all five Lagrange points — with a 6×6 state-transition matrix and a single-shooting differential corrector that produces genuinely periodic halo / near-rectilinear halo orbits (NRHO): it reproduces the published L2 southern 9:2 NRHO (the Gateway orbit) at a period of ~6.57 days and a perilune of ~3,250 km. Plus LunaNet / LNIS cislunar PNT geometry (MCI↔MCMF reduction, selenographic coordinates) with a lunar south-pole ARAIM pass that honestly surfaces the integrity gap: a ~30 m σ_URE drives the protection level well above a 50 m alert limit.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>CR3BP</b> circular restricted three-body problem · <b>NRHO</b> near-rectilinear halo orbit · <b>STM</b> state transition matrix · <b>PNT</b> positioning, navigation and timing · <b>MCI</b> Moon-centred inertial frame · <b>MCMF</b> Moon-centred, Moon-fixed frame · <b>ARAIM</b> advanced receiver autonomous integrity monitoring</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=lunanet-araim&amp;tab=overview">Run lunanet-araim in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Lunar" data-q="lunar reference-frame realisation lunar &amp; cislunar lunar realises a lunar body-fixed frame from an observing campaign: a weighted gauss–newton similarity fit over station–reflector geometry, reporting the datum, its covariance and the worst-determined axis. the coupled frame-and-timescale gauge is carried explicitly rather than absorbed, so the rank deficiency is visible in the report instead of buried in a pseudo-inverse. vs numpy/scipy umeyama–horn svd lunar-frame-realisation singular value decomposition"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Lunar reference-frame realisation</b><span class="cx-d">Lunar &amp; cislunar</span><span class=cx-p>vs numpy/scipy Umeyama–Horn SVD</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Realises a lunar body-fixed frame from an observing campaign: a weighted Gauss–Newton similarity fit over station–reflector geometry, reporting the datum, its covariance and the worst-determined axis. The coupled frame-and-timescale gauge is carried explicitly rather than absorbed, so the rank deficiency is visible in the report instead of buried in a pseudo-inverse.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>SVD</b> singular value decomposition</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=lunar-frame-realisation&amp;tab=overview">Run lunar-frame-realisation in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Lunar" data-q="lunar navigation service volume &amp; araim lunar &amp; cislunar lunar sweeps an illustrative moonlight/lcns-class constellation over a selenographic grid: visibility, dilution of precision, coverage, and per-epoch araim protection levels against a configurable alert limit. the signal-in-space ranging accuracy is a parameter, so the sweep answers a ranging requirement over the whole volume rather than pass/fail at one fixed value. anise 0.10.2 orbits · rtklib 2.4.2-p13 moonlight-service-volume advanced receiver autonomous integrity monitoring an open-source gnss positioning library"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Lunar navigation service volume &amp; ARAIM</b><span class="cx-d">Lunar &amp; cislunar</span><span class=cx-p>ANISE 0.10.2 orbits · RTKLIB 2.4.2-p13</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Sweeps an illustrative Moonlight/LCNS-class constellation over a selenographic grid: visibility, dilution of precision, coverage, and per-epoch ARAIM protection levels against a configurable alert limit. The signal-in-space ranging accuracy is a parameter, so the sweep answers a ranging requirement over the whole volume rather than pass/fail at one fixed value.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>ARAIM</b> advanced receiver autonomous integrity monitoring · <b>RTKLIB</b> an open-source GNSS positioning library</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=moonlight-service-volume&amp;tab=overview">Run moonlight-service-volume in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Lunar" data-q="cross-provider lunar interoperability budget lunar &amp; cislunar lunar measures how far three independent authoritative lunar ephemerides actually disagree, and turns that into a design law: the frame-versus-ephemeris error budget a mixed-provider user inherits, and the consistency tolerance a provider has to hold to keep that budget inside a stated bound. de440 · inpop21a · epm2021 via calceph lunar-interop-export"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Cross-provider lunar interoperability budget</b><span class="cx-d">Lunar &amp; cislunar</span><span class=cx-p>DE440 · INPOP21a · EPM2021 via calceph</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Measures how far three independent authoritative lunar ephemerides actually disagree, and turns that into a design law: the frame-versus-ephemeris error budget a mixed-provider user inherits, and the consistency tolerance a provider has to hold to keep that budget inside a stated bound.</p><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=lunar-interop-export&amp;tab=overview">Run lunar-interop-export in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Lunar" data-q="autonomous free-network fault observability lunar &amp; cislunar lunar the linear algebra a self-referential lunar constellation needs to detect its own faults with no ground truth: the weighted parity projector in the rank-deficient pseudo-inverse form that survives the datum-and-timescale gauge, per-node detectability, the baarda minimum detectable bias, and byzantine block-spark detect/identify counts over real de440 inter-node geometry. vs independent numpy/scipy parity algebra "><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Autonomous free-network fault observability</b><span class="cx-d">Lunar &amp; cislunar</span><span class=cx-p>vs independent numpy/scipy parity algebra</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>The linear algebra a self-referential lunar constellation needs to detect its own faults with no ground truth: the weighted parity projector in the rank-deficient pseudo-inverse form that survives the datum-and-timescale gauge, per-node detectability, the Baarda minimum detectable bias, and Byzantine block-spark detect/identify counts over real DE440 inter-node geometry.</p></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Lunar" data-q="lunar frame datum from real laser ranging lunar &amp; cislunar lunar replaces the simulated campaign with real data: 337 archived ilrs lunar laser-ranging normal points across five retroreflector arrays, each observation weighted by its own recorded precision out of the file. the reflector coordinates are a principal-axis de440 catalogue kept under its own filename, separate from the mean-earth de430 table — feeding one to the other&#x27;s rotation moves a reflector by hundreds of metres and nothing objects, because both are well-formed coordinates. 337 ilrs normal points · median 5.13 mm lunar-llr-datum international laser ranging service"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Lunar frame datum from real laser ranging</b><span class="cx-d">Lunar &amp; cislunar</span><span class=cx-p>337 ILRS normal points · median 5.13 mm</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Replaces the simulated campaign with real data: 337 archived ILRS lunar laser-ranging normal points across five retroreflector arrays, each observation weighted by its own recorded precision out of the file. The reflector coordinates are a principal-axis DE440 catalogue kept under its own filename, separate from the mean-earth DE430 table — feeding one to the other&#x27;s rotation moves a reflector by hundreds of metres and nothing objects, because both are well-formed coordinates.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>ILRS</b> International Laser Ranging Service</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=lunar-llr-datum&amp;tab=overview">Run lunar-llr-datum in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Lunar" data-q="lunar differential pnt lunar &amp; cislunar lunar differential corrections between a lunar reference station and a user. the common-mode cancellation is an exact identity; the spatial-decorrelation residual is a first-order geometric model that grows with baseline, and the correction-link residual budget is reported beside it so the limiting term stays visible rather than folded into one number. exact common-mode identity · modelled decorrelation lunar-differential-pnt positioning, navigation and timing"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Lunar differential PNT</b><span class="cx-d">Lunar &amp; cislunar</span><span class=cx-p>Exact common-mode identity · modelled decorrelation</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Differential corrections between a lunar reference station and a user. The common-mode cancellation is an exact identity; the spatial-decorrelation residual is a first-order geometric model that grows with baseline, and the correction-link residual budget is reported beside it so the limiting term stays visible rather than folded into one number.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>PNT</b> positioning, navigation and timing</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=lunar-differential-pnt&amp;tab=overview">Run lunar-differential-pnt in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Lunar" data-q="lunar time scales &amp; geodetic vlbi lunar &amp; cislunar lunar per-clock-class crossover times for a lunar timescale — when each oscillator class stops meeting a stated bound without an update — together with a lunar geodetic vlbi observable and the fisher information it contributes to the datum. per-clock-class crossover table lunar-time-budget very long baseline interferometry"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Lunar time scales &amp; geodetic VLBI</b><span class="cx-d">Lunar &amp; cislunar</span><span class=cx-p>Per-clock-class crossover table</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Per-clock-class crossover times for a lunar timescale — when each oscillator class stops meeting a stated bound without an update — together with a lunar geodetic VLBI observable and the Fisher information it contributes to the datum.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>VLBI</b> very long baseline interferometry</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=lunar-time-budget&amp;tab=overview">Run lunar-time-budget in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Lunar" data-q="real, retrieved lunar constellation geometry lunar &amp; cislunar lunar the service volume runs on published constellation designs instead of an illustrative one: the five-satellite lans interoperability-demonstration reference set, the lncss case studies at 8, 12 and 16 satellites, and the four spacecraft actually in lunar orbit on 2023-01-01 from jpl horizons. each file states its own frame and its own provenance class, a horizon past the end of a tabulated ephemeris is refused rather than extrapolated, and the illustrative keplerian result is emitted beside the retrieved one with the difference between them, never replaced by it. 5 committed fixtures, each regenerable from its recorded source moonlight-service-volume jet propulsion laboratory"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Real, retrieved lunar constellation geometry</b><span class="cx-d">Lunar &amp; cislunar</span><span class=cx-p>5 committed fixtures, each regenerable from its recorded source</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>The service volume runs on published constellation designs instead of an illustrative one: the five-satellite LANS interoperability-demonstration reference set, the LNCSS case studies at 8, 12 and 16 satellites, and the four spacecraft actually in lunar orbit on 2023-01-01 from JPL Horizons. Each file states its own frame and its own provenance class, a horizon past the end of a tabulated ephemeris is refused rather than extrapolated, and the illustrative Keplerian result is emitted beside the retrieved one with the difference between them, never replaced by it.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>JPL</b> Jet Propulsion Laboratory</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=moonlight-service-volume&amp;tab=overview">Run moonlight-service-volume in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Lunar" data-q="lunar denial contour from a measured c/n₀ spread lunar &amp; cislunar lunar per-satellite jammer-to-signal ratio over real lunar slant ranges, with the wanted-signal side kept as a distribution rather than collapsed to a median, so the denial contour carries the uncertainty band the relay geometry actually produces. two criteria are reported side by side: the power-ratio threshold, and loss of lock computed from tracking-loop dynamics. the orbital capture footprint is swept over transmitter altitude and beamwidth, and where the grid never reaches limb capture the report says so and prints the shortfall. standoff 49.2 km by power ratio against 19.4 km by loss of lock lunar-jamming"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Lunar denial contour from a measured C/N₀ spread</b><span class="cx-d">Lunar &amp; cislunar</span><span class=cx-p>Standoff 49.2 km by power ratio against 19.4 km by loss of lock</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Per-satellite jammer-to-signal ratio over real lunar slant ranges, with the wanted-signal side kept as a distribution rather than collapsed to a median, so the denial contour carries the uncertainty band the relay geometry actually produces. Two criteria are reported side by side: the power-ratio threshold, and loss of lock computed from tracking-loop dynamics. The orbital capture footprint is swept over transmitter altitude and beamwidth, and where the grid never reaches limb capture the report says so and prints the shortfall.</p><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=lunar-jamming&amp;tab=overview">Run lunar-jamming in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Lunar" data-q="vlbi schedule to station-coordinate covariance lunar &amp; cislunar lunar accumulates the lunar vlbi delay partials into a fisher information matrix over a baselines-by-epochs schedule and returns the station coordinate covariance, so the step from delay precision to coordinate sigma is estimated rather than assumed. the free network is reported beside the datum-fixed one, with its rank defect and the unobservable direction named. the surface-point covariance is kept distinct from the earth-station one, because the near-field beacon partial is a difference of two station unit vectors and does not behave like the far-field case. rank 6, defect 0; free-network defect 1 named as a common translation lunar-vlbi-fim very long baseline interferometry"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">VLBI schedule to station-coordinate covariance</b><span class="cx-d">Lunar &amp; cislunar</span><span class=cx-p>Rank 6, defect 0; free-network defect 1 named as a common translation</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Accumulates the lunar VLBI delay partials into a Fisher information matrix over a baselines-by-epochs schedule and returns the station coordinate covariance, so the step from delay precision to coordinate sigma is estimated rather than assumed. The free network is reported beside the datum-fixed one, with its rank defect and the unobservable direction named. The surface-point covariance is kept distinct from the Earth-station one, because the near-field beacon partial is a difference of two station unit vectors and does not behave like the far-field case.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>VLBI</b> very long baseline interferometry</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=lunar-vlbi-fim&amp;tab=overview">Run lunar-vlbi-fim in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Lunar" data-q="radiometric navigation &amp; relay-pnt deep-space &amp; mars pnt lunar an open deep-space / mars radiometric navigation engine: iterative light-time + shapiro delay, two-/one-/three-way doppler &amp; range (moyer two-leg), coherent transponder turnaround, regenerative/pn ranging and δ-dor; ccsds-tdm i/o; a reduced-dynamic square-root information filter (empirical accelerations + onboard clock + mars drag) that does mars-lmo orbit determination in a synthetic closed loop; one-way+two-way fusion; and a marconi areostationary relay constellation with an end-to-end gse performance simulator. simulation-validated (covariance / closed-loop figures of merit, not certified protection levels); the sun-central mars dynamics are cross-checked against jpl de440 to 137 m @ a 1-day arc; mars-lmo od ≈ 0.2 m. mars-pnt-lmo positioning, navigation and timing jet propulsion laboratory low mars orbit orbit determination consultative committee for space data systems tracking data message ground support equipment"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Radiometric navigation &amp; relay-PNT</b><span class="cx-d">Deep-space &amp; Mars PNT</span><span class=cx-p>Simulation-validated (covariance / closed-loop figures of merit, not certified protection levels); the Sun-central Mars dynamics are cross-checked against JPL DE440 to 137 m @ a 1-day arc; Mars-LMO OD ≈ 0.2 m.</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>An open deep-space / Mars radiometric navigation engine: iterative light-time + Shapiro delay, two-/one-/three-way Doppler &amp; range (Moyer two-leg), coherent transponder turnaround, regenerative/PN ranging and Δ-DOR; CCSDS-TDM I/O; a reduced-dynamic Square-Root Information Filter (empirical accelerations + onboard clock + Mars drag) that does Mars-LMO orbit determination in a synthetic closed loop; one-way+two-way fusion; and a MARCONI areostationary relay constellation with an end-to-end GSE performance simulator.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>PNT</b> positioning, navigation and timing · <b>JPL</b> Jet Propulsion Laboratory · <b>LMO</b> low Mars orbit · <b>OD</b> orbit determination · <b>CCSDS</b> Consultative Committee for Space Data Systems · <b>TDM</b> tracking data message · <b>GSE</b> ground support equipment</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=mars-pnt-lmo&amp;tab=overview">Run mars-pnt-lmo in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Lunar" data-q="optical and rf handover with cross-modality integrity optical / rf pnt lunar fault injection gives the cross-modality monitor a detection power rather than a fault-free pass: a minimum detectable bias per axis at a stated false-alarm and missed-detection budget. after a handover the covariance re-growth is propagated against the alert limits, so the report states how long the single-modality solution stays inside them rather than only that the handover was continuous. the optical and rf ranging legs are compared only at matched accumulation time and path, and the report refuses to form a ratio against a value that was chosen rather than measured. minimum detectable bias 8.20 m horizontal, 12.30 m vertical, 24.6 ns timing hybrid-optical-rf radio frequency positioning, navigation and timing"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Optical and RF handover with cross-modality integrity</b><span class="cx-d">Optical / RF PNT</span><span class=cx-p>Minimum detectable bias 8.20 m horizontal, 12.30 m vertical, 24.6 ns timing</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Fault injection gives the cross-modality monitor a detection power rather than a fault-free pass: a minimum detectable bias per axis at a stated false-alarm and missed-detection budget. After a handover the covariance re-growth is propagated against the alert limits, so the report states how long the single-modality solution stays inside them rather than only that the handover was continuous. The optical and RF ranging legs are compared only at matched accumulation time and path, and the report refuses to form a ratio against a value that was chosen rather than measured.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>RF</b> radio frequency · <b>PNT</b> positioning, navigation and timing</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=hybrid-optical-rf&amp;tab=overview">Run hybrid-optical-rf in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="GNSS" data-q="receiver autonomous integrity (araim) integrity gnss snapshot and solution-separation raim with horizontal and vertical protection levels (hpl/vpl), fault detection and exclusion, and stanford integrity diagrams — plus an explicit integrity-risk-budget (mhss) protection level with the dual-/multi-constellation constellation-wide fault mode (eu araim / do-316). mhss on a real gps + galileo snapshot · do-316 dual-constellation integrity-raim advanced receiver autonomous integrity monitoring multiple hypothesis solution separation global positioning system rtca minimum operational performance standards for gps aircraft-based augmentation receivers receiver autonomous integrity monitoring horizontal protection level vertical protection level"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Receiver autonomous integrity (ARAIM)</b><span class="cx-d">Integrity</span><span class=cx-p>MHSS on a real GPS + Galileo snapshot · DO-316 dual-constellation</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Snapshot and solution-separation RAIM with horizontal and vertical protection levels (HPL/VPL), fault detection and exclusion, and Stanford integrity diagrams — plus an explicit integrity-risk-budget (MHSS) protection level with the dual-/multi-constellation constellation-wide fault mode (EU ARAIM / DO-316).</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>ARAIM</b> advanced receiver autonomous integrity monitoring · <b>MHSS</b> multiple hypothesis solution separation · <b>GPS</b> Global Positioning System · <b>DO-316</b> RTCA minimum operational performance standards for GPS aircraft-based augmentation receivers · <b>RAIM</b> receiver autonomous integrity monitoring · <b>HPL</b> horizontal protection level · <b>VPL</b> vertical protection level</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=integrity-raim&amp;tab=overview">Run integrity-raim in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="GNSS" data-q="araim against published reference vectors integrity gnss the engine&#x27;s mhss protection levels run against the eu-u.s. working group c reference airborne algorithm&#x27;s own published worked examples, each transcribed with its retrieval url, date, page and file sha-256 and held in two places that a test asserts agree. the acceptance vector is the internally consistent reference airborne algorithm description document v3.1, appendix d. the milestone 3 report carries two internal defects — a sign typo in the geometry, and a false-alarm multiplier evaluated at 57 fault modes while the text states one — so its protection levels are reported as measured discrepancies rather than graded, and the defects are demonstrated by running the document&#x27;s own numbers. worst error 0.044 m against the sources&#x27; own 0.05 m tolerance araim-reference-check advanced receiver autonomous integrity monitoring multiple hypothesis solution separation"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">ARAIM against published reference vectors</b><span class="cx-d">Integrity</span><span class=cx-p>Worst error 0.044 m against the sources&#x27; own 0.05 m tolerance</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>The engine&#x27;s MHSS protection levels run against the EU-U.S. Working Group C reference airborne algorithm&#x27;s own published worked examples, each transcribed with its retrieval URL, date, page and file SHA-256 and held in two places that a test asserts agree. The acceptance vector is the internally consistent Reference Airborne Algorithm Description Document v3.1, Appendix D. The Milestone 3 report carries two internal defects — a sign typo in the geometry, and a false-alarm multiplier evaluated at 57 fault modes while the text states one — so its protection levels are reported as measured discrepancies rather than graded, and the defects are demonstrated by running the document&#x27;s own numbers.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>ARAIM</b> advanced receiver autonomous integrity monitoring · <b>MHSS</b> multiple hypothesis solution separation</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=araim-reference-check&amp;tab=overview">Run araim-reference-check in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="GNSS" data-q="sbas / waas protection levels &amp; dual-frequency augmentation (sbas) gnss sbas / waas protection levels in the do-229e weighted-least-squares form (precision-approach and en-route k-factors) and the l1/l5 dual-frequency ionosphere-free combination (is-gps-705) that underpins do-316, with the protection-level geometry cross-checked against a reference linear-algebra implementation. do-229e k-factors · l1/l5 iono-free  satellite-based augmentation system wide area augmentation system rtca minimum operational performance standards for satellite-based augmentation system receivers, revision e gps interface specification for the l5 signal global positioning system rtca minimum operational performance standards for gps aircraft-based augmentation receivers"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">SBAS / WAAS protection levels &amp; dual-frequency</b><span class="cx-d">Augmentation (SBAS)</span><span class=cx-p>DO-229E K-factors · L1/L5 iono-free</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>SBAS / WAAS protection levels in the DO-229E weighted-least-squares form (precision-approach and en-route K-factors) and the L1/L5 dual-frequency ionosphere-free combination (IS-GPS-705) that underpins DO-316, with the protection-level geometry cross-checked against a reference linear-algebra implementation.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>SBAS</b> satellite-based augmentation system · <b>WAAS</b> Wide Area Augmentation System · <b>DO-229E</b> RTCA minimum operational performance standards for satellite-based augmentation system receivers, revision E · <b>IS-GPS-705</b> GPS interface specification for the L5 signal · <b>GPS</b> Global Positioning System · <b>DO-316</b> RTCA minimum operational performance standards for GPS aircraft-based augmentation receivers</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Timing" data-q="holdover, stability &amp; time-and-frequency transfer clock &amp; timing timing two-state kalman holdover, allan-family stability (adev / mdev / tdev / hdev) with confidence intervals and a full ieee-1139 five-coefficient power-law fit, and the operational transfer methods — twstft with the bipm sagnac closed form, gnss common-view, ppp ionosphere-free transfer, a free-space optical link with turbulence scintillation, and an inverse-variance clock-ensemble timescale. a gnss-denied clock-holdover calculator inverts the van-loan coast-error growth to a holdover-to-threshold — how long a clock free-runs before its timing error exceeds budget — across classical and quantum-clock classes; modelled, and honest that for a very stable clock the holdover to a tight threshold is set by the assumed long-tau noise floor, not the cited adev. calibrated to clock datasheets · holdover cross-checked vs the van-loan covariance recursion clock-holdover allan deviation modified allan deviation time deviation hadamard deviation institute of electrical and electronics engineers two-way satellite time and frequency transfer international bureau of weights and measures global navigation satellite system precise point positioning"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Holdover, stability &amp; time-and-frequency transfer</b><span class="cx-d">Clock &amp; timing</span><span class=cx-p>calibrated to clock datasheets · holdover cross-checked vs the van-Loan covariance recursion</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Two-state Kalman holdover, Allan-family stability (ADEV / MDEV / TDEV / HDEV) with confidence intervals and a full IEEE-1139 five-coefficient power-law fit, and the operational transfer methods — TWSTFT with the BIPM Sagnac closed form, GNSS common-view, PPP ionosphere-free transfer, a free-space optical link with turbulence scintillation, and an inverse-variance clock-ensemble timescale. A GNSS-denied clock-holdover calculator inverts the van-Loan coast-error growth to a holdover-to-threshold — how long a clock free-runs before its timing error exceeds budget — across classical and quantum-clock classes; modelled, and honest that for a very stable clock the holdover to a tight threshold is set by the assumed long-tau noise floor, not the cited ADEV.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>ADEV</b> Allan deviation · <b>MDEV</b> modified Allan deviation · <b>TDEV</b> time deviation · <b>HDEV</b> Hadamard deviation · <b>IEEE</b> Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers · <b>TWSTFT</b> two-way satellite time and frequency transfer · <b>BIPM</b> International Bureau of Weights and Measures · <b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System · <b>PPP</b> precise point positioning</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=clock-holdover&amp;tab=overview">Run clock-holdover in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="GNSS" data-q="pseudorange, ionosphere &amp; troposphere gnss measurement domain gnss forward pseudorange / doppler synthesis with klobuchar (broadcast) and ionex / tec-grid (measured) ionosphere — including an ionex file parser, time interpolation, and the thin-shell slant-obliquity mapping — saastamoinen + niell troposphere, and snapshot raim. saastamoinen zhd ≈ 2.3 m · niell unity at zenith · klobuchar per is-gps-200 gnss-sim-raim global navigation satellite system zenith hydrostatic delay gps interface specification for the l1 and l2 signals global positioning system ionosphere map exchange format total electron content receiver autonomous integrity monitoring"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Pseudorange, ionosphere &amp; troposphere</b><span class="cx-d">GNSS measurement domain</span><span class=cx-p>Saastamoinen ZHD ≈ 2.3 m · Niell unity at zenith · Klobuchar per IS-GPS-200</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Forward pseudorange / Doppler synthesis with Klobuchar (broadcast) and IONEX / TEC-grid (measured) ionosphere — including an IONEX file parser, time interpolation, and the thin-shell slant-obliquity mapping — Saastamoinen + Niell troposphere, and snapshot RAIM.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System · <b>ZHD</b> zenith hydrostatic delay · <b>IS-GPS-200</b> GPS interface specification for the L1 and L2 signals · <b>GPS</b> Global Positioning System · <b>IONEX</b> Ionosphere Map Exchange format · <b>TEC</b> total electron content · <b>RAIM</b> receiver autonomous integrity monitoring</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=gnss-sim-raim&amp;tab=overview">Run gnss-sim-raim in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="GNSS" data-q="single-point positioning (pvt) gnss positioning gnss a real receiver position solved from real rinex code pseudoranges + broadcast ephemeris by iterated weighted least squares — sagnac/earth-rotation correction, broadcast clock with tgd, the dual-frequency l1/l2 ionosphere-free combination (or single-frequency klobuchar), saastamoinen/niell troposphere, elevation weighting, dop, and snapshot raim. validated on real igs data (station abmf, 2018-05-13) to 5.7 m 3-d rms / 1.1 m horizontal against the surveyed coordinate. pvt-abmf position, velocity and time global navigation satellite system international gnss service root mean square receiver independent exchange format timing group delay dilution of precision receiver autonomous integrity monitoring"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Single-point positioning (PVT)</b><span class="cx-d">GNSS positioning</span><span class=cx-p>Validated on real IGS data (station ABMF, 2018-05-13) to 5.7 m 3-D RMS / 1.1 m horizontal against the surveyed coordinate.</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A real receiver position solved from real RINEX code pseudoranges + broadcast ephemeris by iterated weighted least squares — Sagnac/Earth-rotation correction, broadcast clock with TGD, the dual-frequency L1/L2 ionosphere-free combination (or single-frequency Klobuchar), Saastamoinen/Niell troposphere, elevation weighting, DOP, and snapshot RAIM.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>PVT</b> position, velocity and time · <b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System · <b>IGS</b> International GNSS Service · <b>RMS</b> root mean square · <b>RINEX</b> Receiver Independent Exchange Format · <b>TGD</b> timing group delay · <b>DOP</b> dilution of precision · <b>RAIM</b> receiver autonomous integrity monitoring</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=pvt-abmf&amp;tab=overview">Run pvt-abmf in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Resilience" data-q="jamming &amp; multi-layer spoof detection resilience resilience a link-budget jamming model (j/s → effective c/n₀ → loss of lock), a stochastic time-spoof detector (neyman–pearson / χ²₁ energy test with monte-carlo p_fa/p_md and a security fom of 1 − p_md), and a multi-layer spoof detector fusing a raim-consistency parity test, an rf agc-power monitor, and a signal-quality (sqm) monitor. plus a quantum-inertial dead-reckoning error budget composing cold-atom-interferometer white-noise drift with residual bias (cross-checked against an independent integrator) and scale-factor error into a position-drift-over-holdover figure — the inertial twin of the clock holdover. χ²₁ np detector · closed-form and monte-carlo p_fa/p_md agree spoof-attack neyman–pearson figure of merit receiver autonomous integrity monitoring radio frequency automatic gain control signal-quality monitoring"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Jamming &amp; multi-layer spoof detection</b><span class="cx-d">Resilience</span><span class=cx-p>χ²₁ NP detector · closed-form and Monte-Carlo P_fa/P_md agree</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A link-budget jamming model (J/S → effective C/N₀ → loss of lock), a stochastic time-spoof detector (Neyman–Pearson / χ²₁ energy test with Monte-Carlo P_fa/P_md and a Security FoM of 1 − P_md), and a multi-layer spoof detector fusing a RAIM-consistency parity test, an RF AGC-power monitor, and a signal-quality (SQM) monitor. Plus a quantum-inertial dead-reckoning error budget composing cold-atom-interferometer white-noise drift with residual bias (cross-checked against an independent integrator) and scale-factor error into a position-drift-over-holdover figure — the inertial twin of the clock holdover.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>NP</b> Neyman–Pearson · <b>FoM</b> figure of merit · <b>RAIM</b> receiver autonomous integrity monitoring · <b>RF</b> radio frequency · <b>AGC</b> automatic gain control · <b>SQM</b> signal-quality monitoring</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=spoof-attack&amp;tab=overview">Run spoof-attack in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Resilience" data-q="modulation, anti-jam &amp; code tracking nav-signal performance resilience the signal-performance layer between the link budget and the measurement domain: unit-area power spectral densities for bpsk-r(n) and sine-boc(m,n), the spectral-separation coefficient that derives the anti-jam q the jamming model consumes directly from the actual signal and jammer spectra, the rms (gabor) bandwidth that sets the ranging-information content (boc &gt; bpsk), the coherent early–late dll code-tracking thermal-noise jitter, and the coherent early-minus-late multipath error envelope with narrow-correlator suppression, and a ranging-code design-trade (m-sequence/gold autocorrelation sidelobe and the three-valued gold cross-correlation bound, plus the code-length-to-unambiguous-range inversion). this is signal-performance analysis, not rf-payload or antenna hardware design. bpsk self-ssc = 2/(3r_c) · sub-metre c/a dll jitter at 45 db-hz · gps c/a gold cross-corr -23.9 db  binary phase-shift keying delay-locked loop global positioning system binary offset carrier modulation root mean square radio frequency"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Modulation, anti-jam &amp; code tracking</b><span class="cx-d">Nav-signal performance</span><span class=cx-p>BPSK self-SSC = 2/(3R_c) · sub-metre C/A DLL jitter at 45 dB-Hz · GPS C/A Gold cross-corr -23.9 dB</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>The signal-performance layer between the link budget and the measurement domain: unit-area power spectral densities for BPSK-R(n) and sine-BOC(m,n), the spectral-separation coefficient that derives the anti-jam Q the jamming model consumes directly from the actual signal and jammer spectra, the RMS (Gabor) bandwidth that sets the ranging-information content (BOC &gt; BPSK), the coherent early–late DLL code-tracking thermal-noise jitter, and the coherent early-minus-late multipath error envelope with narrow-correlator suppression, and a ranging-code design-trade (m-sequence/Gold autocorrelation sidelobe and the three-valued Gold cross-correlation bound, plus the code-length-to-unambiguous-range inversion). This is signal-performance analysis, not RF-payload or antenna hardware design.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>BPSK</b> binary phase-shift keying · <b>DLL</b> delay-locked loop · <b>GPS</b> Global Positioning System · <b>BOC</b> binary offset carrier modulation · <b>RMS</b> root mean square · <b>RF</b> radio frequency</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Resilience" data-q="passive rf geolocation (tdoa/fdoa) passive rf geolocation resilience locate a jammer or spoofer (or an opportunistic source for reverse-pnt) from time-difference-of-arrival hyperboloids across a receiver network, solved by gauss–newton least squares; adding frequency-difference-of-arrival with moving receivers jointly recovers the emitter&#x27;s position and velocity, with the cramér–rao bound on the position covariance from the network geometry. a point-source line-of-sight model — no multipath / nlos, receiver-clock-bias, or refraction terms. noiseless tdoa forward→inverse to 1e-6 m · j·crlb = i · gdop monotone in receivers · estimator attains its crlb over 4000 monte-carlo trials  radio frequency time difference of arrival frequency difference of arrival cramér–rao lower bound geometric dilution of precision positioning, navigation and timing non-line-of-sight reception"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Passive RF geolocation (TDOA/FDOA)</b><span class="cx-d">Passive RF geolocation</span><span class=cx-p>noiseless TDOA forward→inverse to 1e-6 m · J·CRLB = I · GDOP monotone in receivers · estimator attains its CRLB over 4000 Monte-Carlo trials</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Locate a jammer or spoofer (or an opportunistic source for reverse-PNT) from time-difference-of-arrival hyperboloids across a receiver network, solved by Gauss–Newton least squares; adding frequency-difference-of-arrival with moving receivers jointly recovers the emitter&#x27;s position and velocity, with the Cramér–Rao bound on the position covariance from the network geometry. A point-source line-of-sight model — no multipath / NLOS, receiver-clock-bias, or refraction terms.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>RF</b> radio frequency · <b>TDOA</b> time difference of arrival · <b>FDOA</b> frequency difference of arrival · <b>CRLB</b> Cramér–Rao lower bound · <b>GDOP</b> geometric dilution of precision · <b>PNT</b> positioning, navigation and timing · <b>NLOS</b> non-line-of-sight reception</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Interop" data-q="rinex, sp3 &amp; ccsds formats interoperability interop multi-gnss rinex-3 broadcast-ephemeris ingestion (gps, galileo, qzss, beidou, glonass), a rinex-3 observation parser (the rinex 4.00 observation layout is expected to parse but no 4.00 file has been read through it; rinex 4 navigation files are refused by name), an sp3-c/d precise-ephemeris reader/writer with lagrange interpolation, and ccsds oem 2.0 + omm (mean-elements) export for flight-dynamics tools (gmat, orekit, stk), and ccsds-tdm (503) tracking-data-message parse + emit for deep-space radiometric tracking. broadcast-eph + sp3 interp vs rtklib; oem vs an independent ccsds parser oem-interop receiver independent exchange format standard product 3, a precise-orbit format consultative committee for space data systems an open-source gnss positioning library orbit ephemeris message global navigation satellite system global positioning system quasi-zenith satellite system orbit mean-elements message tracking data message"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">RINEX, SP3 &amp; CCSDS formats</b><span class="cx-d">Interoperability</span><span class=cx-p>broadcast-eph + SP3 interp vs RTKLIB; OEM vs an independent CCSDS parser</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Multi-GNSS RINEX-3 broadcast-ephemeris ingestion (GPS, Galileo, QZSS, BeiDou, GLONASS), a RINEX-3 observation parser (the RINEX 4.00 observation layout is expected to parse but no 4.00 file has been read through it; RINEX 4 navigation files are refused by name), an SP3-c/d precise-ephemeris reader/writer with Lagrange interpolation, and CCSDS OEM 2.0 + OMM (mean-elements) export for flight-dynamics tools (GMAT, Orekit, STK), and CCSDS-TDM (503) tracking-data-message parse + emit for deep-space radiometric tracking.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>RINEX</b> Receiver Independent Exchange Format · <b>SP3</b> Standard Product 3, a precise-orbit format · <b>CCSDS</b> Consultative Committee for Space Data Systems · <b>RTKLIB</b> an open-source GNSS positioning library · <b>OEM</b> orbit ephemeris message · <b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System · <b>GPS</b> Global Positioning System · <b>QZSS</b> Quasi-Zenith Satellite System · <b>OMM</b> orbit mean-elements message · <b>TDM</b> tracking data message</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=oem-interop&amp;tab=overview">Run oem-interop in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Interop" data-q="machine-checked verification matrix verification &amp; assurance interop a requirement-to-module-to-test-to-oracle-to-status cross-reference rendered from the engine itself (markdown/csv), with unit-tested honesty invariants: a row may be labelled validated only if it carries an independent external oracle (nist / aiaa / sofa / igs / a published value), so an internal self-consistency check cannot masquerade as a validation — and the hardware/pa capabilities kshana does not provide are recorded as partner-owned gaps. the executable backbone for an ecss-e-st-10-02 verification cross-reference. validated rows require an external oracle (invariant enforced in ci)  continuous integration comma-separated values national institute of standards and technology american institute of aeronautics and astronautics standards of fundamental astronomy international gnss service"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Machine-checked verification matrix</b><span class="cx-d">Verification &amp; assurance</span><span class=cx-p>validated rows require an external oracle (invariant enforced in CI)</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A requirement-to-module-to-test-to-oracle-to-status cross-reference rendered from the engine itself (Markdown/CSV), with unit-tested honesty invariants: a row may be labelled validated only if it carries an independent external oracle (NIST / AIAA / SOFA / IGS / a published value), so an internal self-consistency check cannot masquerade as a validation — and the hardware/PA capabilities Kshana does not provide are recorded as partner-owned gaps. The executable backbone for an ECSS-E-ST-10-02 verification cross-reference.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>CI</b> continuous integration · <b>CSV</b> comma-separated values · <b>NIST</b> National Institute of Standards and Technology · <b>AIAA</b> American Institute of Aeronautics and Astronautics · <b>SOFA</b> Standards of Fundamental Astronomy · <b>IGS</b> International GNSS Service</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Interop" data-q="unit and provenance on every reported quantity verification &amp; assurance interop every numeric field a scenario emits carries a unit and a provenance class drawn from a closed vocabulary, with a definition where one exists, published as a machine-readable schema beside the engine. a ratcheted gate runs every built-in kind and fails on a field that carries none; the kinds not yet complete are named one by one with the reason each is still open, and the list can only shrink. reports that state their own constants — the one-way link budget, the optical and rf budget — let a reader recompute the headline from the report alone. 93 of 93 kinds, 3334 of 3334 numeric fields  radio frequency"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Unit and provenance on every reported quantity</b><span class="cx-d">Verification &amp; assurance</span><span class=cx-p>93 of 93 kinds, 3334 of 3334 numeric fields</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Every numeric field a scenario emits carries a unit and a provenance class drawn from a closed vocabulary, with a definition where one exists, published as a machine-readable schema beside the engine. A ratcheted gate runs every built-in kind and fails on a field that carries none; the kinds not yet complete are named one by one with the reason each is still open, and the list can only shrink. Reports that state their own constants — the one-way link budget, the optical and RF budget — let a reader recompute the headline from the report alone.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>RF</b> radio frequency</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Orbits" data-q="launch, re-entry, coverage &amp; pointing budgets mission analysis orbits first-order mission-design budgets, each a runnable scenario: two-body launch &amp; ascent geometry (launch azimuth, minimum inclination, earth-rotation bonus, dogleg plane-change δv, daily opportunities), an allen–eggers ballistic re-entry corridor (peak deceleration, peak-g and peak-heating velocities), earth-observation coverage geometry (swath / nadir gsd / off-nadir access / revisit via the smad space triangle), a 3-dof attitude &amp; pointing error budget (gravity-gradient torque + rss pointing budget), ground-station pass prediction (aos/tca/los, max elevation, access), and a one-way link budget over the ccsds 401 / dsn 810-005 link equation (fspl, c/n₀, eb/n₀, margin, closure). the pre-hardware analytic layer below stk/gmat/basilisk — modelled first-order budgets, not a 6-dof or radiometric replacement. checked vs closed-form (launch azimuth · allen-eggers · smad)  space mission analysis and design (the reference textbook) ground sample distance degrees of freedom root sum square acquisition of signal time of closest approach loss of signal consultative committee for space data systems deep space network free-space path loss"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Launch, re-entry, coverage &amp; pointing budgets</b><span class="cx-d">Mission analysis</span><span class=cx-p>checked vs closed-form (launch azimuth · Allen-Eggers · SMAD)</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>First-order mission-design budgets, each a runnable scenario: two-body launch &amp; ascent geometry (launch azimuth, minimum inclination, Earth-rotation bonus, dogleg plane-change Δv, daily opportunities), an Allen–Eggers ballistic re-entry corridor (peak deceleration, peak-g and peak-heating velocities), Earth-observation coverage geometry (swath / nadir GSD / off-nadir access / revisit via the SMAD space triangle), a 3-DOF attitude &amp; pointing error budget (gravity-gradient torque + RSS pointing budget), ground-station pass prediction (AOS/TCA/LOS, max elevation, access), and a one-way link budget over the CCSDS 401 / DSN 810-005 link equation (FSPL, C/N₀, Eb/N₀, margin, closure). The pre-hardware analytic layer below STK/GMAT/Basilisk — modelled first-order budgets, not a 6-DoF or radiometric replacement.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>SMAD</b> Space Mission Analysis and Design (the reference textbook) · <b>GSD</b> ground sample distance · <b>DOF</b> degrees of freedom · <b>RSS</b> root sum square · <b>AOS</b> acquisition of signal · <b>TCA</b> time of closest approach · <b>LOS</b> loss of signal · <b>CCSDS</b> Consultative Committee for Space Data Systems · <b>DSN</b> Deep Space Network · <b>FSPL</b> free-space path loss</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Orbits" data-q="navigation-versus-communications aperture sharing mission analysis orbits a contact plan and an aperture count in; navigation duty, communications duty, per-session outage and contention out. the arbitration policy is named in the report rather than implied, and a session that cannot be fully served reports the fraction of it that was lost instead of disappearing into an average. per-session outage and contention, not one duty figure aperture-duty-cycle"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Navigation-versus-communications aperture sharing</b><span class="cx-d">Mission analysis</span><span class=cx-p>Per-session outage and contention, not one duty figure</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A contact plan and an aperture count in; navigation duty, communications duty, per-session outage and contention out. The arbitration policy is named in the report rather than implied, and a session that cannot be fully served reports the fraction of it that was lost instead of disappearing into an average.</p><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=aperture-duty-cycle&amp;tab=overview">Run aperture-duty-cycle in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Orbits" data-q="space-weather &amp; thermospheric density space environment orbits a space-weather environment model: solar (f10.7 / centred-81-day f10.7a) and geomagnetic (kp, with the definitional kp↔ap table) activity indices, the jacchia-1971 exospheric temperature they drive, and the activity-corrected versus static thermospheric neutral density at altitude — the solar-cycle density dependence a static atmosphere omits. a calibrated first-order scale-height coupling, not a data-validated nrlmsise atmosphere. jacchia-71 validated vs published solar min / mean / max space-weather"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Space-weather &amp; thermospheric density</b><span class="cx-d">Space environment</span><span class=cx-p>Jacchia-71 validated vs published solar min / mean / max</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A space-weather environment model: solar (F10.7 / centred-81-day F10.7a) and geomagnetic (Kp, with the definitional Kp↔ap table) activity indices, the Jacchia-1971 exospheric temperature they drive, and the activity-corrected versus static thermospheric neutral density at altitude — the solar-cycle density dependence a static atmosphere omits. A calibrated first-order scale-height coupling, not a data-validated NRLMSISE atmosphere.</p><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=space-weather&amp;tab=overview">Run space-weather in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Machine learning" data-q="rf-impairment detection eval &amp; pnt trade machine-learning evaluation &amp; trade machine learning an rf-impairment detection evaluation testbed: a labelled, parameter-grounded synthetic corpus (nominal / jamming / spoof-time / spoof-position / multipath), a detector-agnostic roc/auc harness scoring any detector with per-class pd at a target pfa, and the in- versus out-of-distribution optimism gap. a deeper optimism-gap study extends this to a 13-detector panel (energy / agc / sqm / parity plus seeded logistic-regression and one-hidden-layer-mlp detectors), in- versus out-of-distribution scaling laws with a permutation null, and a leave-one-out predictor of out-of-distribution degradation; a software-defined-receiver front end (raw iq/if to correlator early/prompt/late taps to sqm) and real-data ingest adapters (rinex, u-blox ubx, gnsslogger, jammertest, yunnan, satgrid) let the same detectors run over recordings supplied locally. plus a quantum-vs-classical pnt trade that quantifies a candidate clock&#x27;s timing and inertial holdover benefit from a measured-adev curve against a classical baseline, with a gnss-denied resilience-vs-time envelope. modelled operating characteristics — never field/iq data, no good/bad verdict. auc harness verified vs binormal closed form φ(d′/√2) (gneiting &amp; vogel 2018); eval metrics validated bit-for-bit vs scikit-learn 1.9.0 — synthetic corpus, not raw-iq/texbat impairment-eval radio frequency positioning, navigation and timing area under the curve in-phase and quadrature samples receiver operating characteristic automatic gain control signal-quality monitoring multilayer perceptron receiver independent exchange format the u-blox binary receiver protocol allan deviation global navigation satellite system"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">RF-impairment detection eval &amp; PNT trade</b><span class="cx-d">Machine-learning evaluation &amp; trade</span><span class=cx-p>AUC harness verified vs binormal closed form Φ(d′/√2) (Gneiting &amp; Vogel 2018); eval metrics validated bit-for-bit vs scikit-learn 1.9.0 — synthetic corpus, not raw-IQ/TEXBAT</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>An RF-impairment detection evaluation testbed: a labelled, parameter-grounded synthetic corpus (nominal / jamming / spoof-time / spoof-position / multipath), a detector-agnostic ROC/AUC harness scoring any detector with per-class Pd at a target Pfa, and the in- versus out-of-distribution optimism gap. A deeper optimism-gap study extends this to a 13-detector panel (energy / AGC / SQM / parity plus seeded logistic-regression and one-hidden-layer-MLP detectors), in- versus out-of-distribution scaling laws with a permutation null, and a leave-one-out predictor of out-of-distribution degradation; a software-defined-receiver front end (raw IQ/IF to correlator early/prompt/late taps to SQM) and real-data ingest adapters (RINEX, u-blox UBX, GnssLogger, JammerTest, Yunnan, SatGrid) let the same detectors run over recordings supplied locally. Plus a quantum-vs-classical PNT trade that quantifies a candidate clock&#x27;s timing and inertial holdover benefit from a measured-ADEV curve against a classical baseline, with a GNSS-denied resilience-vs-time envelope. Modelled operating characteristics — never field/IQ data, no good/bad verdict.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>RF</b> radio frequency · <b>PNT</b> positioning, navigation and timing · <b>AUC</b> area under the curve · <b>IQ</b> in-phase and quadrature samples · <b>ROC</b> receiver operating characteristic · <b>AGC</b> automatic gain control · <b>SQM</b> signal-quality monitoring · <b>MLP</b> multilayer perceptron · <b>RINEX</b> Receiver Independent Exchange Format · <b>UBX</b> the u-blox binary receiver protocol · <b>ADEV</b> Allan deviation · <b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=impairment-eval&amp;tab=overview">Run impairment-eval in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Machine learning" data-q="quantum-vs-classical resilience crossover map machine-learning evaluation &amp; trade machine learning a reproducible map of where a quantum sensor overtakes its classical counterpart under parameter uncertainty: cold-atom versus navigation-grade dead-reckoning and optical versus chip-scale clock holdover, over outage duration and platform vibration, each curve carrying a technology-readiness label. reproducible via cargo run --bin crossover_study. modelled performance budgets — the inputs are published or bracketed coefficients, not flight data. regenerates the inertial and clock crossover studies behind the results figures; trade kernels validated vs scipy 1.17.1 "><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Quantum-vs-classical resilience crossover map</b><span class="cx-d">Machine-learning evaluation &amp; trade</span><span class=cx-p>regenerates the inertial and clock crossover studies behind the Results figures; trade kernels validated vs scipy 1.17.1</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A reproducible map of where a quantum sensor overtakes its classical counterpart under parameter uncertainty: cold-atom versus navigation-grade dead-reckoning and optical versus chip-scale clock holdover, over outage duration and platform vibration, each curve carrying a technology-readiness label. Reproducible via cargo run --bin crossover_study. Modelled performance budgets — the inputs are published or bracketed coefficients, not flight data.</p></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Timing" data-q="conditional timing protection level timing integrity timing a holdover-limited bound on the undetected time error under gnss spoofing: given an independent cross-check, the served-time error is bounded by a k-sigma monitor floor, the van-loan coast variance over the detection latency, and a cusum time-to-alarm. calibrated on a real recorded spoof (jammertest 2024) and reproducible via cargo run --example tpl_jammertest. modelled composition, conditional on detection — there is no finite unconditional bound, since a slow-enough ramp evades a single clock-aided monitor. composition cross-checked vs the multi-step clock_state covariance recursion; calibrated on jammertest 2024 (zenodo 15911589)  global navigation satellite system cumulative sum"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Conditional Timing Protection Level</b><span class="cx-d">Timing integrity</span><span class=cx-p>composition cross-checked vs the multi-step clock_state covariance recursion; calibrated on JammerTest 2024 (Zenodo 15911589)</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A holdover-limited bound on the undetected time error under GNSS spoofing: given an independent cross-check, the served-time error is bounded by a k-sigma monitor floor, the van-Loan coast variance over the detection latency, and a CUSUM time-to-alarm. Calibrated on a real recorded spoof (JammerTest 2024) and reproducible via cargo run --example tpl_jammertest. Modelled composition, conditional on detection — there is no finite unconditional bound, since a slow-enough ramp evades a single clock-aided monitor.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System · <b>CUSUM</b> cumulative sum</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Resilience" data-q="pnt-resilience scoring &amp; decision-instability resilience assessment resilience a framework-aligned scoring engine (dhs rpcf categories) that maps an architecture&#x27;s simulated behaviour to per-dimension sub-scores, then asks whether any single composite score or maturity level is a stable basis for a decision — via a dirichlet weighting simplex, kendall-tau rank instability, a top-1 winner flip rate, and common-mode diversity collapse (hill-n2) — with an integrity-hashed assurance report. reproducible via cargo run --example resilience_report. modelled synthetic architectures aligned to rpcf v2.0; a self-assessment, not a certification. 35 hand-derived oracle tests; byte-deterministic study artifact (fixed seed); aligned to dhs rpcf v2.0 conflict-resilience positioning, navigation and timing united states department of homeland security resilient pnt conformance framework"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">PNT-resilience scoring &amp; decision-instability</b><span class="cx-d">Resilience assessment</span><span class=cx-p>35 hand-derived oracle tests; byte-deterministic study artifact (fixed seed); aligned to DHS RPCF v2.0</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A framework-aligned scoring engine (DHS RPCF categories) that maps an architecture&#x27;s simulated behaviour to per-dimension sub-scores, then asks whether any single composite score or maturity Level is a stable basis for a decision — via a Dirichlet weighting simplex, Kendall-tau rank instability, a top-1 winner flip rate, and common-mode diversity collapse (Hill-N2) — with an integrity-hashed assurance report. Reproducible via cargo run --example resilience_report. Modelled synthetic architectures aligned to RPCF v2.0; a self-assessment, not a certification.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>PNT</b> positioning, navigation and timing · <b>DHS</b> United States Department of Homeland Security · <b>RPCF</b> Resilient PNT Conformance Framework</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=conflict-resilience&amp;tab=overview">Run conflict-resilience in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="GNSS" data-q="detection-miss → integrity regions integrity impact gnss a detection-miss-to-integrity bridge: an undetected bias left by a missed spoof / jam / raim detection inflates the effective position error, which — held against context-specific alert limits — is classified into the stanford-esa integrity regions (available / unavailable / misleading / hazardous). composes the externally-anchored raim stanford classification; adds no new integrity mathematics. stanford-esa integrity regions (tossaint et al., navigation 2007)  european space agency receiver autonomous integrity monitoring"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Detection-miss → integrity regions</b><span class="cx-d">Integrity impact</span><span class=cx-p>Stanford-ESA integrity regions (Tossaint et al., NAVIGATION 2007)</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A detection-miss-to-integrity bridge: an undetected bias left by a missed spoof / jam / RAIM detection inflates the effective position error, which — held against context-specific alert limits — is classified into the Stanford-ESA integrity regions (available / unavailable / misleading / hazardous). Composes the externally-anchored RAIM Stanford classification; adds no new integrity mathematics.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>ESA</b> European Space Agency · <b>RAIM</b> receiver autonomous integrity monitoring</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Machine learning" data-q="cost-per-coverage roi decision support machine learning a cost-per-coverage roi lens — cost per unit of delivered coverage for a constellation / architecture trade. the coverage component composes the validated coverage geometry and the arithmetic is unit-tested, but the cost is a user input — so the ratio is a planning aid, not an externally-validated quantity (hence, honestly, no proof badge).   return on investment"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Cost-per-coverage ROI</b><span class="cx-d">Decision support</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A cost-per-coverage ROI lens — cost per unit of delivered coverage for a constellation / architecture trade. The coverage component composes the validated coverage geometry and the arithmetic is unit-tested, but the cost is a user input — so the ratio is a planning aid, not an externally-validated quantity (hence, honestly, no proof badge).</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>ROI</b> return on investment</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Interop" data-q="kshana interchange format (kif) artifact interchange interop a versioned, self-describing envelope that wraps a scenario result with its kind, schema version, and modelled/validated labels, so a stored artifact stays self-documenting and older envelopes remain forward-compatibly readable. versioned envelope · round-trip + forward-compatible read verified lunar-interop-export kshana interchange format"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Kshana Interchange Format (KIF)</b><span class="cx-d">Artifact interchange</span><span class=cx-p>versioned envelope · round-trip + forward-compatible read verified</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>A versioned, self-describing envelope that wraps a scenario result with its kind, schema version, and modelled/validated labels, so a stored artifact stays self-documenting and older envelopes remain forward-compatibly readable.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>KIF</b> Kshana Interchange Format</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=lunar-interop-export&amp;tab=overview">Run lunar-interop-export in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Maritime" data-q="receiver-trust score for a vessel maritime trust maritime scores whether a vessel&#x27;s navigation fix can be trusted from the nmea 0183 its receiver and instruments already emit: a 0-100 score, three bands and the reasons behind them, per epoch, in batch, live or as a gate. monitors compare the fix with the vessel&#x27;s own motion, heading sensor, speed log and satellite signal levels. advisory software, not type-approved navigation equipment: the operator stays responsible. the monitors&#x27; arithmetic is checked against independent libraries; the score&#x27;s rule and thresholds are the project&#x27;s own and stay modelled. monitor arithmetic checked against pynmea2 and geographiclib "><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Receiver-trust score for a vessel</b><span class="cx-d">Maritime trust</span><span class=cx-p>monitor arithmetic checked against pynmea2 and GeographicLib</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Scores whether a vessel&#x27;s navigation fix can be trusted from the NMEA 0183 its receiver and instruments already emit: a 0-100 score, three bands and the reasons behind them, per epoch, in batch, live or as a gate. Monitors compare the fix with the vessel&#x27;s own motion, heading sensor, speed log and satellite signal levels. Advisory software, not type-approved navigation equipment: the operator stays responsible. The monitors&#x27; arithmetic is checked against independent libraries; the score&#x27;s rule and thresholds are the project&#x27;s own and stay modelled.</p></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Maritime" data-q="crew-training nmea streams maritime trust maritime writes synthetic bridge nmea 0183 for a scripted voyage with instructor-scripted jamming, position drag-off, time-spoof and replay events, with an instructor log, so a crew can see what a degraded fix looks like on their own instruments. text only, never for a vessel&#x27;s live navigation systems; nothing is transmitted and no radio signal is made. the sentences and their decoded values are checked; the receiver, sky and event models are modelled. 225,227 sentences parsed and decoded by pynmea2 "><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Crew-training NMEA streams</b><span class="cx-d">Maritime trust</span><span class=cx-p>225,227 sentences parsed and decoded by pynmea2</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Writes synthetic bridge NMEA 0183 for a scripted voyage with instructor-scripted jamming, position drag-off, time-spoof and replay events, with an instructor log, so a crew can see what a degraded fix looks like on their own instruments. Text only, never for a vessel&#x27;s live navigation systems; nothing is transmitted and no radio signal is made. The sentences and their decoded values are checked; the receiver, sky and event models are modelled.</p></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Maritime" data-q="interference map and route exposure maritime trust maritime builds a per-day geojson map of where aircraft (ads-b) and ships (ais) reported degraded navigation data, from openly licensed inputs, and the share of a route that passes through degraded cells. a degraded cell is a statement about reported accuracy fields or implausible positions, not a finding of interference, and a cell nobody observed is not evidence of a clear route. past reports, not a forecast. code meanings and geometry checked against pymodes, pyais, shapely and geographiclib "><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Interference map and route exposure</b><span class="cx-d">Maritime trust</span><span class=cx-p>code meanings and geometry checked against pyModeS, pyais, shapely and GeographicLib</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Builds a per-day GeoJSON map of where aircraft (ADS-B) and ships (AIS) reported degraded navigation data, from openly licensed inputs, and the share of a route that passes through degraded cells. A degraded cell is a statement about reported accuracy fields or implausible positions, not a finding of interference, and a cell nobody observed is not evidence of a clear route. Past reports, not a forecast.</p></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Maritime" data-q="trust telemetry for security operations maritime trust maritime exports the trust stream as prometheus metrics, opentelemetry (otlp/http) and cef or leef syslog events over stdout, http, udp or tcp, so a security operations centre sees a navigation fault next to its other alerts. a dead collector neither stalls the assessment nor goes unnoticed. prometheus, otlp and cef are read back by independent parsers; leef is checked against its own specification only. prometheus, otlp and cef read back by independent parsers "><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Trust telemetry for security operations</b><span class="cx-d">Maritime trust</span><span class=cx-p>Prometheus, OTLP and CEF read back by independent parsers</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Exports the trust stream as Prometheus metrics, OpenTelemetry (OTLP/HTTP) and CEF or LEEF syslog events over stdout, HTTP, UDP or TCP, so a security operations centre sees a navigation fault next to its other alerts. A dead collector neither stalls the assessment nor goes unnoticed. Prometheus, OTLP and CEF are read back by independent parsers; LEEF is checked against its own specification only.</p></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Maritime" data-q="signed evidence packs maritime trust maritime bundles the window around a trust event into a hash-chained, ed25519-signed pack that anyone can verify offline: a changed, removed, swapped or added file, a re-signed pack or a wrong pinned key is named. an optional rfc 3161 timestamp token is bound to it. a technical record, not a legal opinion, finding of fact or attestation. rfc 8032 vectors byte for byte; a clean-room verifier agrees on 71 packs "><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Signed evidence packs</b><span class="cx-d">Maritime trust</span><span class=cx-p>RFC 8032 vectors byte for byte; a clean-room verifier agrees on 71 packs</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Bundles the window around a trust event into a hash-chained, Ed25519-signed pack that anyone can verify offline: a changed, removed, swapped or added file, a re-signed pack or a wrong pinned key is named. An optional RFC 3161 timestamp token is bound to it. A technical record, not a legal opinion, finding of fact or attestation.</p></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="Interop" data-q="test-bench export interoperability interop writes a scenario&#x27;s vehicle motion and events as a csv trajectory and nmea 0183 for a laboratory gnss simulator: earth-fixed, geodetic and local-velocity columns, attitude and utc. motion and events only: no signal is written and no simulator or receiver performance is claimed. conversions and encodings checked against proj, geographiclib, pynmea2 and scipy  comma-separated values global navigation satellite system coordinated universal time"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Test-bench export</b><span class="cx-d">Interoperability</span><span class=cx-p>conversions and encodings checked against PROJ, GeographicLib, pynmea2 and SciPy</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Writes a scenario&#x27;s vehicle motion and events as a CSV trajectory and NMEA 0183 for a laboratory GNSS simulator: Earth-fixed, geodetic and local-velocity columns, attitude and UTC. Motion and events only: no signal is written and no simulator or receiver performance is claimed.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>CSV</b> comma-separated values · <b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System · <b>UTC</b> Coordinated Universal Time</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Maritime" data-q="compliance mapping to public frameworks maritime trust maritime maps kshana outputs to the clauses of public frameworks (imo guidance for ships, eu nis2 article 21, en 16803, the us dhs resilient-pnt framework and easa aviation guidance) by clause number only, filled from the runs you give it, and says which rows a set of runs supports evidence for, with each row&#x27;s gap. it is not a finding that any framework is met and not a rating or approval by anyone.   united states department of homeland security positioning, navigation and timing"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Compliance mapping to public frameworks</b><span class="cx-d">Maritime trust</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Maps Kshana outputs to the clauses of public frameworks (IMO guidance for ships, EU NIS2 Article 21, EN 16803, the US DHS resilient-PNT framework and EASA aviation guidance) by clause number only, filled from the runs you give it, and says which rows a set of runs supports evidence for, with each row&#x27;s gap. It is not a finding that any framework is met and not a rating or approval by anyone.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>DHS</b> United States Department of Homeland Security · <b>PNT</b> positioning, navigation and timing</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="GNSS" data-q="galileo osnma verification gnss authentication gnss an open, pure-rust receiver-side verifier for galileo open service navigation message authentication: it checks which satellites&#x27; navigation data a receiver can authenticate from i/nav pages or a u-blox ubx stream, through the public-key, key-chain and tag checks of the signal-in-space specification, and reports authentication per satellite and per time. it authenticates data; it does not claim any anti-spoofing performance. all 18 official test-vector sets: 186,382 tags authenticated, 0 failed  global navigation satellite system the u-blox binary receiver protocol"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Galileo OSNMA verification</b><span class="cx-d">GNSS authentication</span><span class=cx-p>all 18 official test-vector sets: 186,382 tags authenticated, 0 failed</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>An open, pure-Rust receiver-side verifier for Galileo Open Service Navigation Message Authentication: it checks which satellites&#x27; navigation data a receiver can authenticate from I/NAV pages or a u-blox UBX stream, through the public-key, key-chain and tag checks of the signal-in-space specification, and reports authentication per satellite and per time. It authenticates data; it does not claim any anti-spoofing performance.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System · <b>UBX</b> the u-blox binary receiver protocol</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="GNSS" data-q="skyline-masked visibility, dop and error ellipse sky geometry gnss which satellites a site can see when buildings, quays, cranes or terrain block part of the sky, and what that does to the dilution of precision (dop) and the horizontal error ellipse. an azimuth-dependent skyline replaces the single elevation cut-off; the visible set, its dop and the ellipse come out per epoch and per constellation. a skyline removes line-of-sight satellites and nothing more: it models no reflections, no diffraction and no signal strength. 1173 look angles and 142 visible sets match orekit 12.2 and gnss_lib_py 1.0.4 to 1e-12; the ellipse matches numpy on 270 cases  dilution of precision"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><b class="cx-n">Skyline-masked visibility, DOP and error ellipse</b><span class="cx-d">Sky geometry</span><span class=cx-p>1173 look angles and 142 visible sets match Orekit 12.2 and gnss_lib_py 1.0.4 to 1e-12; the ellipse matches numpy on 270 cases</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Which satellites a site can see when buildings, quays, cranes or terrain block part of the sky, and what that does to the dilution of precision (DOP) and the horizontal error ellipse. An azimuth-dependent skyline replaces the single elevation cut-off; the visible set, its DOP and the ellipse come out per epoch and per constellation. A skyline removes line-of-sight satellites and nothing more: it models no reflections, no diffraction and no signal strength.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>DOP</b> dilution of precision</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="GNSS" data-q="skyline presets and the sky-geometry functions sky geometry gnss the street-canyon, sector and terrain reductions to a skyline table, four representative presets (city street, harbour approach, fjord, container terminal) and the functions that compose them into a skyplot and a monte carlo cloud, from the cli, mcp, python and the browser. the presets are representative geometries with chosen, stated dimensions, not surveys, and the reductions are checked only against hand-computed values. hand-computed values and the geometry the cloud samples; no external oracle "><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Skyline presets and the sky-geometry functions</b><span class="cx-d">Sky geometry</span><span class=cx-p>hand-computed values and the geometry the cloud samples; no external oracle</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>The street-canyon, sector and terrain reductions to a skyline table, four representative presets (city street, harbour approach, fjord, container terminal) and the functions that compose them into a skyplot and a Monte Carlo cloud, from the CLI, MCP, Python and the browser. The presets are representative geometries with chosen, stated dimensions, not surveys, and the reductions are checked only against hand-computed values.</p></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Maritime" data-q="geometry-aware trust classification maritime trust maritime puts the satellite geometry beside the receiver-trust score and names a class on every deduction: signal loss, poor geometry or an inconsistency between the receiver&#x27;s stated dop and the one derived from the satellites in view. geometry is judged only when it is known, and the label is the headline: only a poor geometry costs points. advisory software, not type-approved navigation equipment. the classification rule and thresholds are the project&#x27;s own, checked against themselves  dilution of precision"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Geometry-aware trust classification</b><span class="cx-d">Maritime trust</span><span class=cx-p>the classification rule and thresholds are the project&#x27;s own, checked against themselves</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Puts the satellite geometry beside the receiver-trust score and names a class on every deduction: signal loss, poor geometry or an inconsistency between the receiver&#x27;s stated DOP and the one derived from the satellites in view. Geometry is judged only when it is known, and the label is the headline: only a poor geometry costs points. Advisory software, not type-approved navigation equipment.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>DOP</b> dilution of precision</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Lunar" data-q="lunar reference-station study lunar lunar one reproducible study of what a lunar south-pole reference station could do under public assumptions: a coverage and accuracy map over the polar region (percentile corrected and uncorrected position error, availability with a reason for every unavailable epoch, protection levels and the correction-link budget), a time laboratory that holds a lunar time reference on an atomic clock, and the geodesy of an added laser retroreflector and vlbi beacon against the five-array datum. every input is labelled with its public source or as illustrative. it is not a statement about the performance of any real system, and kshana is not affiliated with any agency. composes tested lunar differential, clock and frame functions; checked against themselves and a numpy reference, no external flight data  very long baseline interferometry"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Lunar reference-station study</b><span class="cx-d">Lunar</span><span class=cx-p>composes tested lunar differential, clock and frame functions; checked against themselves and a numpy reference, no external flight data</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>One reproducible study of what a lunar south-pole reference station could do under public assumptions: a coverage and accuracy map over the polar region (percentile corrected and uncorrected position error, availability with a reason for every unavailable epoch, protection levels and the correction-link budget), a time laboratory that holds a lunar time reference on an atomic clock, and the geodesy of an added laser retroreflector and VLBI beacon against the five-array datum. Every input is labelled with its public source or as illustrative. It is not a statement about the performance of any real system, and Kshana is not affiliated with any agency.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>VLBI</b> very long baseline interferometry</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Lunar" data-q="optical timing links through clouds and turbulence optical / rf pnt lunar how often an optical time-transfer link is up when the atmosphere is in the path. for each ground site the engine gives the clear-air loss, the hufnagel-valley turbulence state with gamma-gamma scintillation fades and aperture averaging, the availability against the link margin from a stated cloud climate, the ceiling no margin can lift, the outage-duration distribution and the timing error an outage costs a holdover clock. it then shows what extra sites, a larger aperture, another wavelength or adaptive optics buy. the bundled cloud climates are illustrative, not measured; the output is advisory. 7 of 7 pre-registered numerical bars met against an independent mpmath program; no measured availability record has been compared  radio frequency positioning, navigation and timing"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Optical timing links through clouds and turbulence</b><span class="cx-d">Optical / RF PNT</span><span class=cx-p>7 of 7 pre-registered numerical bars met against an independent mpmath program; no measured availability record has been compared</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>How often an optical time-transfer link is up when the atmosphere is in the path. For each ground site the engine gives the clear-air loss, the Hufnagel-Valley turbulence state with gamma-gamma scintillation fades and aperture averaging, the availability against the link margin from a stated cloud climate, the ceiling no margin can lift, the outage-duration distribution and the timing error an outage costs a holdover clock. It then shows what extra sites, a larger aperture, another wavelength or adaptive optics buy. The bundled cloud climates are illustrative, not measured; the output is advisory.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>RF</b> radio frequency · <b>PNT</b> positioning, navigation and timing</small></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="GNSS" data-q="gnss beyond the constellation: the space service volume and past it nav-signal performance gnss for a spacecraft on a sphere (the global shell of the icg space service volume booklet) or on a keplerian trajectory from leo to lunar transfer, counts per constellation (gps, glonass, galileo, bds, qzss, navic) and for all together the signals above a c/n0 threshold: availability of one and of four signals, longest outage, received c/n0, dilution of precision, and main-lobe against sidelobe links. transmit patterns are the booklet&#x27;s, a parametric stand-in or one you supply; a limb-ray ionosphere, plasmasphere and atmosphere model and a weak-signal receiver design are optional; an onboard gnss filter for high and lunar-transfer orbits and formation relative navigation are included. modelled: compared with the booklet&#x27;s tables under a pre-registration committed before the run, the minimum-radiated-power constants and the lunar bar agree, the other bars are not met under the pre-registered reading (almost all through the booklet&#x27;s inconsistent glonass beam angle), and the parametric sidelobe stand-in does not reproduce the measured gps panel patterns. icg ssv booklet tables, pre-registered; most bars not met (xval/gnss-ssv/results.md) gnss-ssv-shell global navigation satellite system low earth orbit global positioning system quasi-zenith satellite system"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">GNSS beyond the constellation: the Space Service Volume and past it</b><span class="cx-d">Nav-signal performance</span><span class=cx-p>ICG SSV booklet tables, pre-registered; most bars NOT MET (xval/gnss-ssv/RESULTS.md)</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>For a spacecraft on a sphere (the global shell of the ICG Space Service Volume booklet) or on a Keplerian trajectory from LEO to lunar transfer, counts per constellation (GPS, GLONASS, Galileo, BDS, QZSS, NavIC) and for all together the signals above a C/N0 threshold: availability of one and of four signals, longest outage, received C/N0, dilution of precision, and main-lobe against sidelobe links. Transmit patterns are the booklet&#x27;s, a parametric stand-in or one you supply; a limb-ray ionosphere, plasmasphere and atmosphere model and a weak-signal receiver design are optional; an onboard GNSS filter for high and lunar-transfer orbits and formation relative navigation are included. MODELLED: compared with the booklet&#x27;s tables under a pre-registration committed before the run, the minimum-radiated-power constants and the lunar bar agree, the other bars are NOT MET under the pre-registered reading (almost all through the booklet&#x27;s inconsistent GLONASS beam angle), and the parametric sidelobe stand-in does not reproduce the measured GPS panel patterns.</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System · <b>LEO</b> low Earth orbit · <b>GPS</b> Global Positioning System · <b>QZSS</b> Quasi-Zenith Satellite System</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=gnss-ssv-shell&amp;tab=overview">Run gnss-ssv-shell in Kshana Studio</a></div></details><details class="cx-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="Lunar" data-q="lunar orbit and surface as navigation and geodetic infrastructure lunar &amp; cislunar lunar five studies in a restricted three-body model: an onboard earth-gnss orbit determination in a near-rectilinear halo orbit against an independent ground range and range-rate determination; joint surface-station and orbiter carrier-phase integer ambiguity fixing (lambda); the fisher information of a seven-parameter lunar datum with and without orbiter-to-surface ranges; gnss, vlbi and laser-ranging offsets against the local tie; and sensitivity to the lunar tidal and libration parameters. modelled with illustrative inputs: the numerics are checked against independent numpy and scipy programs, not the physics. under the pre-registered bars, three are not met: an injected force error is not detected at the stated rate (c3), the bootstrapped success rate differs from the numpy oracle by 1.7e-6 against a bar of 1e-9 (q3), and adding a ranging family does not always lower the pseudo-inverse trace (g3, trace half). numpy/scipy oracles of the numerics; three pre-registered bars not met (xval/lunar-infra/results.md) lunar-infrastructure-od-consistency global navigation satellite system very long baseline interferometry"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><b class="cx-n">Lunar orbit and surface as navigation and geodetic infrastructure</b><span class="cx-d">Lunar &amp; cislunar</span><span class=cx-p>numpy/scipy oracles of the numerics; three pre-registered bars NOT MET (xval/lunar-infra/RESULTS.md)</span></summary><div class="cx-body"><p>Five studies in a restricted three-body model: an onboard Earth-GNSS orbit determination in a near-rectilinear halo orbit against an independent ground range and range-rate determination; joint surface-station and orbiter carrier-phase integer ambiguity fixing (LAMBDA); the Fisher information of a seven-parameter lunar datum with and without orbiter-to-surface ranges; GNSS, VLBI and laser-ranging offsets against the local tie; and sensitivity to the lunar tidal and libration parameters. MODELLED with illustrative inputs: the numerics are checked against independent numpy and scipy programs, not the physics. Under the pre-registered bars, three are NOT MET: an injected force error is not detected at the stated rate (C3), the bootstrapped success rate differs from the numpy oracle by 1.7e-6 against a bar of 1e-9 (Q3), and adding a ranging family does not always lower the pseudo-inverse trace (G3, trace half).</p><small class="abbr-key"><b>GNSS</b> Global Navigation Satellite System · <b>VLBI</b> very long baseline interferometry</small><a class="tbtn" href="/studio/?scenario=lunar-infrastructure-od-consistency&amp;tab=overview">Run lunar-infrastructure-od-consistency in Kshana Studio</a></div></details></div><p class="fl-empty" data-fl-empty hidden>Nothing matches that filter.</p><button class="fl-more" type="button" data-fl-more>Show all 61 capabilities</button></div>
