lled: ocean loading, satellite PCV, integer ambiguity resolution</td><td><code>ppp_real (run_ppp, parse_antex_receiver, solid_tide_ecef, phase_windup)</code></td></tr><tr><td>RINEX 4 navigation file reading</td><td>RINEX 4.00-4.02 navigation files: <code>&gt; EPH</code> records of GPS LNAV, Galileo I/NAV and F/NAV, BeiDou D1/D2 and QZSS LNAV decoded into the broadcast-ephemeris type; CNAV, CNV1-3, FDMA, SBAS, STO, EOP and ION records skipped to the next record header; a record whose header names another satellite, or a truncated record, refused</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>The same day's RINEX 3 merged broadcast file of the IGS (published data, written by a different file generator from the same broadcast messages); bars pre-registered in tests/fixtures/brdc_rinex4_2026200/PREREGISTRATION.md (7aa264b) before the first run</td><td><code>rinex (parse_nav, parse_nav_with)</code></td></tr><tr><td>RINEX 4 observation file reading</td><td>RINEX 4.0x observation headers (DOI, LICENSE OF USE, STATION INFORMATION, MERGED FILE; labels matched in columns 61-80 for version 4 and later) and epoch records</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Format conformance on a real published RINEX 4.00 file (values read back equal the file's printed literals) and a specification-derived header; no independent reader was run, so MODELLED</td><td><code>rinex_obs (parse_obs)</code></td></tr><tr><td>BeiDou GEO broadcast satellite position</td><td>BDS-SIS-ICD-B1I v3.0 §5.2.4.12 geostationary-satellite position from a broadcast ephemeris (inertial-like frame, then Rz(Ω̇e·tk)·Rx(−5°)), opt-in through the include_beidou_geo field of NavParseOptions, so the default reader still skips GEOs</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Pre-registered (7aa264b, 110fef9) against an IGS MGEX precise orbit with the GEOs; no such product was reachable from the session that built it, so the comparison is pending and the row is MODELLED (checked against the published nominal GEO slots only)</td><td><code>rinex (RinexEphemeris::sv_position_ecef_bds_geo, NavParseOptions)</code></td></tr><tr><td>SP3 precise-clock interpolation</td><td>Satellite clock offset between the tabulated SP3 clock nodes by linear interpolation over the two bracketing valid nodes (the RTKLIB pephclk scheme; bad-clock sentinel skipped), and the same nodes fed to the precise-product chain as RINEX-clock-equivalent records</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>IGS final 30 s satellite clock combination (IGS0OPSFIN 30S CLK, 2025-02-24): published clock estimates at the query instants, not interpolated from the SP3; bars pre-registered in tests/fixtures/sp3_clock_igs_oracle/PREREGISTRATION.md before the first run (RMS 0.15 ns, p95 0.30 ns, node median 1e-12 s, discrimination 2x)</td><td><code>sp3 (Sp3File::clock_s, clock_nodes), precise_products (ClockRinex::from_sp3)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Carrier-phase tightly coupled GNSS/INS with float ambiguities</td><td>Error-state EKF over 15 INS states, a two-state receiver clock and one float carrier ambiguity per tracked satellite; pseudorange, carrier phase and optional range rate from an SGP4 Walker constellation (light-time and Sagnac), Klobuchar and Saastamoinen/Niell corrections applied in the loop at the filter's own position; time-differenced-carrier slip detection with a per-satellite threshold widened by the velocity uncertainty; ambiguity re-initialisation on slips and re-acquisition</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Internal consistency: the measurements are synthesised from the models the filter uses (χ² NEES/NIS over seeds, a noise-free convergence, slip injection). MODELLED; no real receiver data or third-party filter is compared</td><td><code>fusion::carrier_tc (run_carrier_tc, CarrierTcCfg), fusion::trajectory (truth_epochs)</code></td></tr><tr><td>GNSS/INS maritime, rail and aviation packs with a tight-coupling option</td><td>Three gnss-ins scenarios on the trajectory library (a vessel weaving at 8 m/s with a five-minute outage; a train through a two-satellite cutting and a tunnel; an aircraft whose outage covers a 25-degree banked turn) and the pack's optional [tight] table: pseudoranges from an SGP4 Walker constellation fused by the closed-loop navigator, every satellite above the mask when nominal and the highest few when degraded</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Internal: each pack's figures of merit against its own truth and a loose/tight comparison on identical inputs. MODELLED; the audit's NaveGo comparison has not been run</td><td><code>fusion::pack (TightCfg, [tight]), scenarios gnss-ins-maritime / gnss-ins-rail / gnss-ins-aviation</code></td></tr><tr><td>Coupled pseudorange PNT filter with bias state and 3-D extension in the fusion pack</td><td>One stacked Kalman filter over position, velocity, clock phase and frequency fed pseudoranges that couple position and clock (Joseph-form updates carrying the cross-covariance); a 1-DOF variant augmented with a constant accelerometer-bias state that the fusion pack runs under [coupled]; and the 3-D 8-state version with line-of-sight rows</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Internal consistency: truth simulated from the filters' own models (χ² NEES over Monte Carlo seeds, the decoupled pack on identical sensors). MODELLED</td><td><code>fusion::coupled (CoupledPntFilter, CoupledPntBiasFilter, CoupledPnt3d), fusion (run_coupled_suite), hybrid (CoupledCfg)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Loosely coupled GNSS/INS EKF with lever arm on the KF-GINS dataset</td><td>The closed-loop 15-state error-state EKF driven by IMU increments with GNSS antenna positions fused through the lever-arm model H = [I 0 −[(C_b^n l_b)×] 0 0], configured from a KF-GINS YAML file (initial state and sigmas, ARW/VRW, Gauss-Markov biases)</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>The dataset's published reference trajectory and the KF-GINS C++ program (GPL-3.0, commit 8291a93e, run as a separate tool); bars pre-registered in xval/kfgins/PREREGISTRATION.md, and KF-GINS was not run on this data before that registration (checked in its Results). All six bars held on the first run. DATA-GATED: the KF-GINS demo dataset (real Leador-A15 IMU and RTK GNSS with a reference trajectory) carries no licence, so it is not vendored; xval/kfgins/fetch.sh fetches it at test time with every file's SHA-256 pinned, and the test fails on a mismatch; the reproduce path, which needs network access, is in xval/kfgins/PREREGISTRATION.md. Promoted to VALIDATED by the owner's decision on data-gated evidence (2026-10-13)</td><td><code>fusion::kfgins (run, loaders, score), fusion::closed_loop (propagate_increments, fuse_position_lever_arm), fusion::gnss_ins_ekf (update_position_lever_arm)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Test-bench export read by a simulator's motion-file readers (gps-sdr-sim)</td><td>The bench-export motion files given to an open-source GNSS simulator's own readers come back as the exported trajectory: the NMEA GGA file (-g), the time/latitude/longitude/height cut of the motion CSV (-x) and its time/x/y/z cut (-u), on 6 gnss-ins scenarios (including the maritime, rail and aviation packs, read to the simulator's 3000-sample cap) and 2 stationary ones. Motion only; no signal is written or generated.</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>WHAT IS EXTERNALLY CHECKED, stated narrowly: gps-sdr-sim (MIT, commit 28ca29a6) readNmeaGGA, readUserMotion and readUserMotionLLH with its own WGS 84 llh2xyz, called through xval/testbench-gpssdrsim/driver.c (no signal function called), against the motion CSV's Earth-fixed columns row by row, with bars pre-registered in xval/testbench-gpssdrsim/PREREGISTRATION.md before any run: every sample read on all 8 scenarios; NMEA worst 1.21 mm (bar 1 cm); LLH cut worst 0.145 mm (bar 1 mm); four-column Earth-fixed cut exact (bar 1 um, amendment 1); gnss-ins rows on the simulator's 10 Hz grid to 2.3e-13 s. FINDING (run 1, pinned): the header-removed full-width CSV is not readable by -u, because the reader's 100-character line buffer splits each 161-character row into two samples; docs/TEST-BENCH.md gives the four-column cut. WHAT IS NOT: the physics of the scenario's truth trajectory; gps-sdr-sim's signal output; any other simulator's reader; any receiver.</td><td><code>interop::testbench (write_motion_csv, write_nmea, export)</code></td></tr><tr><td>GNSS/INS trajectory library and CSV truth import</td><td>Selectable motion profiles for the gnss-ins pack (straight, figure-8, urban stop-and-go, aircraft climb and coordinated turn, survey drone), defined by Euler-rate and along-track-acceleration commands closed on the truth state with the kinematic IMU (centripetal, gravity and Coriolis terms), and imported CSV truth turned into the IMU that reproduces it; the historical drive unchanged when no profile is given</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Internal: rigid-body kinematics identities, the CSV round trip and the filter's own covariance. MODELLED (no real drive log is compared)</td><td><code>fusion::trajectory (TrajectoryCfg, Trajectory, kinematic_imu, parse_truth_csv), fusion::pack ([trajectory])</code></td></tr><tr><td>Strapdown INS mechanization</td><td>Quaternion-attitude, WGS-84 NED strapdown inertial mechanization (coning/sculling-compensated) propagating a navigation state from (Δθ, Δv) increments</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>NaveGo v1.4 (R. Gonzalez et al., LGPL-3) run under Octave — an independent published INS toolbox driven by the identical (Δθ,Δv) increment stream. Attitude matches bit-for-bit (same NED / scalar-first-quaternion / Earth-rate / transport-rate conventions); velocity/position agree to two documented differences — the deflection-of-vertical north-gravity term NaveGo includes and kshana omits by design (plumb-bob gravity; matched to the Groves closed form to every digit, with a sanity-floor assert) and O(dt²) integrator differences — not mechanization errors</td><td><code>inertial::mechanization, inertial::attitude, inertial::imu_errors</code></td></tr><tr><td>Gravity-field functional synthesis (gravity-aided / GNSS-free nav map)</td><td>Spherical-harmonic gravity magnitude + disturbance (mGal) from any ICGEM .gfc model; GRS80 normal gravity</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>GRS80 (Moritz 1980, IAG) Somigliana normal gravity + published γ_e/γ_p; real ICGEM EGM2008 field</td><td><code>gravity_sh</code></td></tr><tr><td>Earth-Moon CR3BP 9:2 NRHO against the orbit CAPSTONE flew</td><td>The circular-restricted 9:2 L2 southern near-rectilinear halo orbit that cr3bp::differential_correct_halo corrects (the Gateway and CAPSTONE orbit family), compared for its mean geometry (period, perilune radius, apolune radius) with the orbit CAPSTONE actually flew. A measurement of how well the CR3BP member describes the real orbit; a CR3BP orbit is not an ephemeris-model orbit and is not compared epoch by epoch</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Measured against the JPL Horizons reconstructed CAPSTONE ephemeris (NAIF -1176, CAPSTONE_merged, Moon-centred ICRF, 2023-03-01..31 at 10 min), committed byte for byte (tests/fixtures/capstone_horizons/, NOTICE.md), pre-registered 4e868170 before it was fetched, as a measurement with bars on the mean geometry. Not a validation: the CR3BP omits the Sun, the Moon's eccentricity and the station keeping CAPSTONE performed, so the bars describe how representative the model orbit is, not an accuracy. Results: docs/design/evidence/cap29d-capstone-nrho/RESULTS.md</td><td><code>cr3bp (differential_correct_halo, PeriodicOrbit)</code></td></tr><tr><td>CR3BP rotating states mapped to Moon-centred ICRF on the DE440 Earth-Moon line</td><td>cr3bp::to_moon_centred_icrf maps a nondimensional Earth-Moon CR3BP rotating state to a Moon-centred ICRF position and velocity on the instantaneous Earth-Moon line (length unit the Earth-Moon distance, time unit the inverse orbital rate, the frame's full angular velocity including its out-of-plane rate from the Moon's acceleration), with the inverse from_moon_centred_icrf, and cr3bp::to_moon_centred_icrf_de440 reads the geocentric Moon state from a DE440 SPK. It maps states; it does not make a CR3BP orbit an ephemeris-model orbit</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>NAIF SPICE Toolkit (CSPICE N0067 through spiceypy 8.3.0) sxform of a two-vector dynamic frame (primary +X along the Moon's position wrt the Earth, secondary +Y along its velocity, de440s), applied to the dimensional rotating state built from SPICE's own Moon state, by a separate program that calls no Kshana code (tests/fixtures/cr3bp_to_mci_spice/, NOTICE.md). Pre-registered 2313eb1b, amended before any run in d5ef79b3 to include the frame's out-of-plane angular velocity (without it the velocity would be up to 8e-2 m/s off). A Library check of the transform only. Results: docs/design/evidence/rm34-cr3bp-to-mci/RESULTS.md</td><td><code>cr3bp (to_moon_centred_icrf, from_moon_centred_icrf, to_moon_centred_icrf_de440)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Solar-system kind on a JPL kernel (planetary_kernel_path)</td><td>The <code>solar-system</code> kind with planetary_kernel_path: each reported body whose own NAIF code the SPK holds takes its heliocentric J2000 position and velocity from the kernel through the engine's own reader (label KERNEL), a link whose two ends are both in the kernel is computed on it, barycentres are never substituted for a planet, and every other body stays analytic with its own label; the report gains a kernel block (path, SHA-256, the bodies and links taken from it) only when the option is set. With de440s.bsp that is Mercury, Venus, the Earth and the Moon</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>NAIF SPICE Toolkit (CSPICE N0067 through spiceypy 8.3.0) spkezr heliocentric states on the full de440s.bsp, a separate program that calls no Kshana code (tests/fixtures/solar_system_kernel/, NOTICE.md); the engine reads bit-for-bit de440s cuts. Pre-registered f8e946ea. A Library check of the wiring: DE440 itself and the analytic bodies are not validated here. Results: docs/design/evidence/cap31-solar-system-kernel/RESULTS.md</td><td><code>solar_system (SolarSystemScenario::planetary_kernel_path, KernelOut); ephem_provider (KernelEphemeris)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Porkchop on the DE440 ephemeris</td><td>maneuver::porkchop_kernel: the launch-date x arrival-date sweep of departure C3 (km²/s²) and arrival V-infinity (km/s) with the single-revolution prograde Lambert solver and the 0.5 deg collinearity guard, on the heliocentric J2000 states of any two NAIF bodies read from a JPL DE440 SPK by the crate's own reader. One revolution, prograde, no launch-vehicle or arrival constraints: a sweep, not a mission design</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>NAIF SPICE Toolkit (CSPICE N0067 through spiceypy 8.3.0) heliocentric states on de440s.bsp and pykep 3.0.1 lambert_problem (ESA, the C++ kep3 core, MPL-2.0), an independent Lambert solver, run as a separate program that calls no Kshana code (tests/fixtures/porkchop_de440/, NOTICE.md); the engine reads a bit-for-bit de440s cut. Pre-registered 2d857fb1. A Library check of the sweep. Results: docs/design/evidence/cap20b-porkchop-de440/RESULTS.md</td><td><code>maneuver (porkchop_kernel, PorkchopGrid); ephem_provider (KernelEphemeris)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Impulsive transfer optimiser on the DE440 ephemeris</td><td>maneuver::optimise_transfer_kernel: the departure and arrival dates of a single-revolution ballistic two-impulse transfer that minimise departure C3 or arrival V-infinity within Type I or Type II, as a continuous optimum (a 2-day grid over both windows, then 2-D Nelder-Mead), on heliocentric states from the DE440 SPK. A date optimiser for one leg, not a mission design: no launch-vehicle, parking-orbit, deep-space-manoeuvre or multi-revolution options</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>An independent optimiser: NAIF SPICE Toolkit (CSPICE N0067 through spiceypy 8.3.0) heliocentric states on the same de440s cut, pykep 3.0.1 lambert_problem (ESA, MPL-2.0) and SciPy 1.18.1 Nelder-Mead, calling no Kshana code. The NASA Earth-to-Mars handbook (MIDAS, ephemeris unstated, 2-day date grid) is a secondary published reference and does not set the status</td><td><code>maneuver (optimise_transfer_kernel, TransferObjective, TransferType, TransferOptimum); ephem_provider (KernelEphemeris)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Multi-revolution Lambert transfer solver</td><td>Every solution of Lambert's problem with up to three complete revolutions (maneuver::lambert_multirev: the single-revolution solution, then the left and right branches of each revolution count, Izzo 2015), with the boundary velocities v1 and v2, for prograde and retrograde transfers, in canonical and heliocentric SI units, including transfers within 1 % of a revolution count's minimum time of flight and near-180 deg geometries. A two-body solver only (no perturbations)</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>pykep 3.0.1 lambert_problem (ESA, the C++ kep3 core, MPL-2.0), an independent library, run as a separate program by scripts/gen_lambert_multirev_ref.py; only its numerical output is committed. Pre-registered a75de851 (case set, bars, outcome rule) before any comparison; the reference was regenerated independently and is byte-identical. Results: docs/design/evidence/lambert-multirev/RESULTS.md</td><td><code>maneuver (lambert_multirev, LambertSolution, LambertBranch)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Lambert two-body transfer solver</td><td>Izzo-2015 single-revolution Lambert solver (r1, r2, time-of-flight → boundary velocities) across LEO→GEO→heliocentric transfers, prograde and retrograde</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>lamberthub 1.0.0 izzo2015 (J. Martínez Garrido, MIT) — an independent third-party Lambert solver. The single-revolution (M=0) Lambert problem has a unique solution, so library-vs-library agreement is a genuine external check; matched to &lt;1e-4 m/s (observed ~1e-11), the same kind of validation DOP gets vs gnss_lib_py</td><td><code>maneuver (lambert)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Reference frames &amp; timescales</td><td>IAU 2006/2000A precession-nutation, CIO GCRS↔ITRS, leap-second timescales</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>IAU SOFA / ERFA reference vectors; Vallado AIAA 2006-6753 (0.1–4 mm)</td><td><code>frames, precession, nutation, cio, timescales</code></td></tr><tr><td>Two-part Julian dates and leap seconds on the Earth-orbit time path</td><td>A UTC calendar date and time to a two-part UTC Julian date with the SOFA dtf2d convention for days that end in a leap second (86 401 s long, 23:59:60.x valid), UTC to TAI and back, and UT1 from UTC through TAI, on two-part dates from 1972-01-01: the time path SGP4 (Sgp4::propagate_at, measured from a two-part epoch) and the IAU 2006/2000A Earth-fixed rotation (sgp4::teme_to_itrs_matrix_eop with the two-part Earth rotation angle) take</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>pyerfa 2.0.1.5 (liberfa 2.0.1, BSD-3-Clause; the SOFA algorithms as released by the ERFA project), an independent library: erfa.dtf2d(&quot;UTC&quot;), erfa.utctai and erfa.taiutc on the committed instants, the gap formed part by part on the two-part dates, bar 1e-6 s fixed before the run (pre-registered e947e69d). Worst gap 9.6e-12 s (dtf2d) and 1.9e-11 s (utctai, taiutc). Every UTC day taken as 86 400 s, or the leap-day fraction left unscaled, turns the test red. UT1 through TAI (iauUtcut1) is checked by the Earth-orbit path row's ERFA leg. Scope: UTC from 1972-01-01; the earlier drifting-rate UTC is not modelled</td><td><code>jd2 (Jd2::from_utc_calendar, utc_to_tai, tai_to_utc, utc_to_ut1, tai_to_tt, earth_rotation_angle); sgp4 (Sgp4::new_at, Sgp4::propagate_at)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Two-part Julian dates through Earth rotation on the orbit path</td><td>Earth rotation from a two-part UT1 Julian date on the orbit path, so the Earth-fixed position keeps the microsecond a single-f64 date loses (about 3e-9 rad near 2020): jd2::gmst82 (IAU 1982 GMST, the TEME convention, on the two parts), cio::gcrs_to_itrs_matrix_jd2 (the CIO GCRS to ITRS matrix with the Earth rotation angle from the two parts), and, built on them and checked internally, frames::teme_to_itrf_jd2 / itrf_to_teme_jd2, orbit::Propagator::position_in_frame_jd2 and own_jd2_utc, the ephemeris scenario's opt-in two_part_jd (the ECEF position, ground track and station view; default off, unchanged), and lossless From conversions between jd2::Jd2 and timescales::TwoPartJd. Precession-nutation and the Cowell force-model epoch stay single-f64, where a 40 us rounding is below 1e-15 rad or far below the analytic Sun/Moon ephemeris error</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>pyerfa 2.0.1.5 (liberfa 2.0.1, BSD-3-Clause; the SOFA algorithms as released by the ERFA project), an independent library: gmst82 and c2t06a on the committed two-part dates, polar motion 0.1 and 0.3 arcsec. Pre-registered b10475ee (dates, bars) before the functions were written and before the oracle was run. The internal tests (the scenario option, the TEME/ITRF round trip, the type conversions) are supporting self-consistency</td><td><code>jd2 (gmst82, From&lt;TwoPartJd&gt;, From&lt;Jd2&gt;); cio (gcrs_to_itrs_matrix_jd2); frames (teme_to_itrf_jd2, itrf_to_teme_jd2); orbit (Propagator::position_in_frame_jd2, own_jd2_utc); ephemeris (EphemerisScenario::two_part_jd)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Frame matrices against ERFA vectors: IAU 2006 bias-precession, TEME to GCRS, and the CIO chain with celestial-pole offsets</td><td>Three frame matrices compared element by element or by rotation angle with ERFA at arbitrary epochs: precession::precession_matrix (IAU 2006 bias-precession by Fukushima-Williams angles), nutation::teme_to_gcrs_matrix (the TEME to GCRS rotation P^T N^T Rz(-EE) with IAU 2006 precession, IAU 2000B nutation and the equation of the equinoxes with its two leading complementary terms), and cio::gcrs_to_cirs_matrix_with_offsets (the GCRS to CIRS rotation with the IERS celestial-pole offsets dX, dY added to the model CIP X, Y before the rotation, the SOFA cookbook convention; new). The model choices themselves (2000B nutation on the TEME path, the two-term complementary series) are not validated, only their implementation</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>pyerfa 2.0.1.5 (liberfa 2.0.1, BSD-3-Clause; the SOFA algorithms as released by the ERFA project), an independent library: pmat06; numat(obl06, nut00b) with the equation of the equinoxes from eect00 (the full complementary series), composed as P^T N^T Rz(-EE); xys06a then c2ixys(X + dX, Y + dY, s). Pre-registered 979c1e39 (epochs, offsets and bars) before the oracle was run. The 1.1e-10 rad TEME residual is the predicted effect of keeping two complementary terms (about 3 mm at GNSS orbit)</td><td><code>precession (precession_matrix); nutation (teme_to_gcrs_matrix); cio (gcrs_to_cirs_matrix_with_offsets)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Pre-1972 UTC (drifting-rate era) on the two-part Julian-date time path</td><td>UTC from 1960-01-01 to 1971-12-31, when UTC ran at an offset rate from TAI and stepped by fractions of a second, on the two-part Julian-date path: TAI - UTC from the drift table of each period (timescales::tai_minus_utc_on_date), the UTC calendar date to a two-part UTC Julian date with the day length 86 400 s + dleap (Jd2::from_utc_calendar), UTC to TAI with the day fraction scaled from UTC to SI seconds (jd2::utc_to_tai) and TAI back to UTC (jd2::tai_to_utc), following SOFA dtf2d, utctai and taiutc. Before 1960 TAI - UTC is taken as 0 (SOFA's value; UTC undefined) and is outside the claim. The single-f64 timescales::tai_minus_utc still clamps to 10 s before 1972 and is not covered</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>pyerfa 2.0.1.5 (liberfa 2.0.1, BSD-3-Clause; the SOFA algorithms as released by the ERFA project), an independent library: erfa.dtf2d(&quot;UTC&quot;), erfa.utctai and erfa.taiutc on the committed instants, the gap formed part by part. Pre-registered 23c40661 (instants, tolerance and the 1972+ checksum) before the engine change and before the oracle was run. The drift table is transcribed from the same SOFA iauDat table the oracle uses, so the comparison covers the conversion arithmetic (day length, UTC-to-SI scaling, iteration), not the historical table itself</td><td><code>timescales (tai_minus_utc_on_date); jd2 (Jd2::from_utc_calendar, utc_to_tai, tai_to_utc)</code></td></tr><tr><td>GLONASS broadcast satellite clock and broadcast-orbit propagation</td><td>The GLONASS broadcast satellite clock bias -tau_n + gamma_n (t - t_b) at a time read on the satellite clock (glonass::GlonassEphemeris::sv_clock_bias_s, the offset corrected for the clock bias by two fixed-point iterations), and the PZ-90 Earth-fixed position of the broadcast state vector propagated by RK4 through the GLONASS ICD equations of motion within +/-15 min of the reference epoch (GlonassEphemeris::position_ecef), from real RINEX 3 GLONASS navigation records. No PZ-90.11 to ITRF transformation is applied (they agree at the centimetre level since 2014; RTKLIB applies none either). Not wired into a scenario output: library function</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>RTKLIB v2.4.2-p13 (T. Takasu, BSD-2-Clause, commit 71db0ffa) geph2clk and geph2pos, compiled from C source in a separate harness (xval/rtklib-glonass) that reads the same RINEX file with RTKLIB's own reader, so the parse, the sign of -tau_n, the units and the iteration are compared as well as the clock and the orbit. Pre-registered e099c42a before the function existed and before the harness was built. Both sides evaluate the same clock closed form, so the clock comparison covers the implementation, not the formula. The orbit integrators differ (RTKLIB steps 60 s then the remainder, Kshana equal steps of at most 60 s) and so do the constants (RTKLIB mu 3.9860044e14, Kshana the PZ-90.11 3.986004418e14); the 1.1 mm worst case is that difference, as predicted before the run. Scope: the broadcast ephemeris within its validity interval, not the true orbit or clock (no comparison with precise products)</td><td><code>glonass (GlonassEphemeris::sv_clock_bias_s, position_ecef, parse_glonass_nav)</code></td></tr><tr><td>One Earth-orbit path: SGP4 element sets to the GCRS and ITRS on the IAU 2006/2000A chain with Earth orientation parameters</td><td>The propagation and frame path every routed Earth-orbit kind shares: an SGP4 mean element set with a two-part UTC epoch (sgp4::MeanElementSet, read from constellation-design elements by MeanElementSet::from_earth_fixed) propagated by SGP4/SDP4 to TEME (sgp4::SgpOrbit), and the TEME -&gt; ITRS and GCRS -&gt; ITRS rotations of the IAU 2006/2000A chain with UT1 - UTC and polar motion (sgp4::teme_to_itrs_matrix_eop, gcrs_to_itrs_matrix_eop, the zero-parameter forms teme_to_itrs_matrix and gcrs_to_itrs_matrix). TEME is the true equator of date with the mean equinox, reached from the GCRS through the IAU 2006 bias-precession, the IAU 2000B nutation and the equation of the equinoxes</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Leg 1: python-sgp4 2.24 (MIT licence; D. Vallado's reference C++ SGP4, the code that produced the AIAA 2006-6753 verification vectors), WGS-72, improved mode. Leg 2: pyerfa 2.0.1.5 (ERFA, BSD-3-Clause, the SOFA routines): c2t06a with TT from utctai/taitt and UT1 from utcut1, and for TEME c2t06a . pnm06a^T . rz(-ee06a). Bars fixed before the run (pre-registered 72ea9eb0). Disclosed: the first run failed leg 2 at 2016-12-31T23:59:59.5 by one second of rotation (UT1 taken from the UTC quasi Julian date on a leap-second day); the engine was corrected (jd2::utc_to_ut1) and the second run agrees at the unchanged bars. Polar motion dropped (46 failures) or WGS-84 constants in place of WGS-72 (4432 failures) turn the test red. This three-leg design replaced an end-to-end Orekit comparison that failed on Orekit's SDP4 at e = 0 and on its IERS 1996 TEME (the finding stays pinned in tests/leo_polar_coverage_full_claim_orekit_oracle.rs)</td><td><code>sgp4 (MeanElementSet, SgpOrbit, Eop, teme_to_itrs_matrix_eop, gcrs_to_itrs_matrix_eop); jd2 (utc_to_ut1, earth_rotation_angle); nutation (teme_to_gcrs_matrix); cio (gcrs_to_cirs_matrix); frames (polar_motion_matrix)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Ranging-code design trade</td><td>m-sequence/Gold sidelobe &amp; cross-correlation bounds, length↔ambiguity</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Published GPS C/A Gold cross-correlation (−23.9 dB); Gold 1967 bound</td><td><code>navsignal (CodeFamily)</code></td></tr><tr><td>GNSS geometry / dilution of precision (DOP)</td><td>GDOP/PDOP/HDOP/VDOP/TDOP from line-of-sight geometry via Q=(HᵀH)⁻¹ with a local ENU split</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>gnss_lib_py 1.0.4 (Stanford NAV Lab) DOP — independent library, matched to 1e-6 relative</td><td><code>orbit (dop)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Position error from geometry: RMS error = sigma_UERE x DOP for the engine's least-squares fix</td><td>dop_error_mc::run_geometry and check: a seeded Monte Carlo (N = 20,000 epochs per geometry, sigma = 1.5 m, ChaCha8 seed 36 + i) of the engine's own iterated least-squares fix (pvt::solve_spp, unit weights, solved from the true position) on the 8 geometries of tests/fixtures/dop/dop_reference.csv, comparing the east, north, up and clock error variances, the H/V/P RMS, the east-north correlation, the mean errors and the fraction inside the 95 % horizontal ellipse with sigma^2 Q, where Q is the covariance factor of the orbit::dop kernel (orbit::dop_cofactor, the same arithmetic) on the same geometry</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Internal consistency against a closed form: under the linear Gaussian model the least-squares covariance is sigma^2 (H^T H)^-1 (Kaplan and Hegarty (eds.), Understanding GPS: Principles and Applications, 2nd ed., Artech House, 2006, ch. 7). Method and tolerances of the 0.36 geometry design note, section 5.2, fixed in a pre-registration commit before the first run: each variance within 5 % (5 standard errors of a sample variance at N = 20,000), each RMS within 2.5 %, the east-north correlation within 0.035 (5/sqrt(N)), each mean within 5 standard errors, the 95 % ellipse fraction within 0.95 +/- 0.0077 (5 binomial standard errors); negative controls (PDOP doubled; errors growing as 1/sin(elevation) against the unweighted Q) must fail. HOW IT COMPOSES: Q is the covariance factor of the orbit::dop kernel, VALIDATED against gnss_lib_py 1.0.4 on these same 8 geometries in tests/dop_reference.rs (row 'GNSS geometry / dilution of precision'); this row adds the step from those factors to metres through the engine's solver and does not borrow that row's status. WHY MODELLED: no external dataset is involved; agreement shows the estimator and noise realise sigma x DOP under the textbook model, not that any receiver's error equals UERE x DOP, and sigma_UERE is an input</td><td><code>dop_error_mc (run_geometry, check, TOLERANCES); dop_error_ci (run, GEOMETRIES); pvt (solve_spp); orbit (dop, dop_cofactor)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Geometry-aware trust classification: satellite geometry beside the receiver-trust score and a class on every deduction</td><td>receiver_trust::geometry: per-epoch satellite geometry (good, degraded with reasons few-satellites/high-pdop/high-hdop/sky-blocked, or unknown when the log gives no GSV, NAV-SAT or GSA) derived from the reported elevations and azimuths through orbit::dop or taken from GSA, with a label always reported and never scored (good, fair, poor, unknown) and the HDOP, PDOP, VDOP, satellite count and signal loss in words; a vessel-score geometry monitor that deducts nothing unless the geometry is poor (ratio max(hdop/4, pdop/6, 5/n_used) above 1), then ramps to a cap of 15 points at 1.5 that geometry alone cannot take below the degraded band (cap, ramp end and floor in [geometry]; no alarm); a class on every deduction (geometry, environment, inconsistency) with loss-of-lock to satellites below 15 deg classed geometry, satellites in view but not tracked classed environment, and a poor-geometry deduction whose stated HDOP is beyond max(1.0, 25 % of the derived HDOP) from the stated sky classed inconsistency</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Internal consistency: the classification is checked against the rules it states and against hand-derived ratios on synthetic edits of the committed demo log, and the no-change pins hold the 0.35.0 outputs: no score changes unless the geometry is poor; the label is