ed and the swapped packet streams that Kshana rejects; the CRC of 8 messages and 57 PEC fields equals CPython binascii.crc_hqx and spacepackets-rs 0.18.0 (check value 0x29B1); 66 CUC and CDS time-code cases equal spacepackets-rs 0.18.0 write and read octet for octet (3 CDS cases also spacepackets-py 0.32.0); 4 spacepackets-py 0.32.0 PusTm packets are byte-identical to Kshana's. Not covered: agency-epoch time codes (7 cases rest on unit tests), TC transfer frames and any link layer below the packet</td><td><code>space_packet (TimeCode, SecondaryHeader, packet error control, segmentation and reassembly, stream decoding)</code></td></tr><tr><td>SPICE SPK export (types 9 and 13)</td><td>Writer and reader of NAIF SPICE SPK kernels of types 9 (Lagrange, unequal time steps) and 13 (Hermite, unequal time steps), with the DAF layout, segment descriptors and epoch directories, and the export of a CCSDS OEM to an SPK kernel (UTC or TDB epochs to ephemeris time)</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>CSPICE N0067 through spiceypy 8.3.0, leapseconds kernel naif0012.tls, pre-registered (5059fd2a) before SPICE read any Kshana kernel: SPICE's inventory of Kshana's four kernels is exact; spkpvn and spkgeo at 5 516 epochs and 690 chain evaluations equal Kshana's own evaluation bit for bit and stay inside the bar at every node (worst 0.022 of it); Kshana reads SPICE-written kernels bit-identically; for the same inputs Kshana's kernels are byte-identical to SPICE's (0 differing bytes in four files); OEM epochs convert to ephemeris time within 6.0e-8 s of str2et (bar 1e-6 s) and SPICE evaluates the OEM export inside the bar (worst 0.060). Synthetic Kepler arcs: nothing about physical accuracy. Other SPK types are not written</td><td><code>spk_writer (SpkSegment, to_bytes, from_bytes, eval, et_from_label, oem_to_spk_segments)</code></td></tr><tr><td>SPICE SPK types 3 and 21 (Chebyshev, extended difference lines)</td><td>Writer and reader of NAIF SPICE SPK segments of type 3 (Chebyshev polynomials for position and velocity over fixed intervals, degree 0 to 27) and type 21 (extended modified difference arrays, the difference lines of JPL integrators, MAXDIM 15 to 25), from given records, in files that also hold type 9 and 13 segments; record selection and evaluation in the order of the SPICE readers. Kshana does not fit Chebyshev coefficients or produce difference lines</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>CSPICE N0067 through spiceypy 8.3.0 (spkw03, spkw13 and, from the same library by its f2c symbol, spkw21; spkpvn, spkgeo), pre-registered (e1cf80d8) before the code existed, the synthetic records drawn from that commit's SHA-1: for three files (type 3 degrees 0, 12 and 27 with 3 to 150 records; type 21 MAXDIM 15, 20 and 25 with 1 to 250 lines, zero to two directory entries and a centre chain; type 3, 21 and 13 in one file with comments) SPICE's inventory of Kshana's kernels is exact, Kshana's kernels are byte-identical to the ones SPICE writes from the same records, and spkpvn and spkgeo equal Kshana's reader bit for bit on Kshana's and on SPICE's files (48 876 components each way, bar 1e-12 relative). Synthetic records: nothing about physical accuracy; real JPL-produced type 21 kernels are not in the case set</td><td><code>spk_writer (SpkType::Chebyshev3, SpkType::DiffLines21, SpkSegment, InterpolatedSpk)</code></td></tr><tr><td>CCSDS Attitude Data Messages (AEM, APM)</td><td>Writer and reader of the CCSDS 504.0-B-2 Attitude Data Messages in KVN and NDM/XML: AEM version 2.0 (and 1.0 in KVN, with its ATTITUDE_DIR, QUATERNION_TYPE and numeric rotation sequences) with quaternion, Euler-angle and spin attitude types and their derivative, angular-velocity and nutation variants, several segments; APM version 2.0 with quaternion, Euler, angular-velocity, spin, inertia and maneuver blocks; the A-to-B quaternion of a record (504.0-B-2 Annex F)</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Orekit 13.1.8 (Apache-2.0) parsers and writers, run as a separate program (xval/adm-orekit), pre-registered (7a6a05fe) before any comparison: Orekit parses every Kshana-written message (14 AEM KVN, 11 AEM XML, 4 APM KVN, 4 APM XML) and every value is inside its bar (quaternions 1e-14, angles 1e-9 deg; worst 0.054 of a bar); Kshana parses the 31 messages Orekit writes from the same inputs inside every bar; the rotation of each of 135 quaternion, Euler and spin records equals AemSegment.getAngularCoordinates within 1e-12 (worst 0.0071 of the bar). All 15 Kshana XML messages validate against the NDM/XML 4.0.0 schema (data-gated, fetched with pinned SHA-256). Finding: Orekit's APM writer refuses a maneuver without MAN_DELTA_MASS (optional in the standard), so APM maneuvers are covered in the Kshana-writes direction only. Outside the case set: AEM 1.0 */RATE types and XML, repeated APM blocks</td><td><code>adm (AemMessage, ApmMessage, quaternion_a_to_b), ndm_xml</code></td></tr><tr><td>CCSDS Orbit Data Messages (OPM, OMM, OCM)</td><td>Writer and reader of the CCSDS 502.0-B-3 Orbit Data Messages in KVN and NDM/XML: OPM 3.0 and 2.0 (state vector, osculating Keplerian elements, spacecraft parameters, covariance, maneuvers, user-defined parameters), OMM 3.0 and 2.0 (mean elements by mean motion or semi-major axis, TLE-related parameters with BSTAR or BTERM and AGOM, covariance), and OCM 3.0 with trajectory (CARTP, CARTPV, CARTPVA; absolute or relative epochs), physical-properties, covariance (CARTP, CARTPV in LTM order), maneuver and user-defined blocks; a full-precision OMM from an SGP4 element set beside the unchanged OmmFile::to_omm_kvn</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Orekit 13.1.8 (Apache-2.0) OPM, OMM and OCM parsers and writers, run as a separate program (xval/odm-orekit), pre-registered (cc6841da) before any comparison: 27 of the 30 Kshana-written messages (6 OPM, 5 OMM and 5 OCM cases in KVN, and XML for version 3.0) parse in Orekit with every value within 1e-14 relative and every epoch within 1e-9 s (904 values, worst 0.021 of a bar), and Kshana reads all 25 messages Orekit writes back out inside the same bars (858 values, worst 0.020); all 14 Kshana XML messages validate against the NDM/XML 4.0.0 schema (data-gated). Findings, pinned, narrowing the claim: Orekit requires ELEMENT_SET_NO (optional in 502.0-B-3), refuses COMMENT in XML user-defined parameters (the schema allows it), will not write BTERM without BSTAR (so BTERM and AGOM are covered in the Kshana-writes direction only), and adds OCM relative epochs as SI seconds, so under TDB they differ from the TDB calendar reading by about 1.9e-10 s/s (UTC, TAI and TT agree). The legacy OmmFile::to_omm_kvn output, which wraps OMM metadata in META_START/META_STOP, is refused by Orekit (recorded). Out of scope: OCM PERT and OD blocks, non-Cartesian element sets, other covariance orderings</td><td><code>odm (OpmMessage, OmmMessage, OcmMessage, Section, OcmBlock), ndm_xml</code></td></tr><tr><td>3-DOF attitude &amp; pointing error budget (AOCS)</td><td>Gravity-gradient worst-case disturbance torque ((3/2)(μ/R³)ΔI) as the peak over attitude (the validated claim), plus an RSS pointing-error budget over named 1σ contributors with the dominant term (a quadrature sum of caller-supplied numbers, outside the validated claim). Runnable from the CLI/bindings as the <code>attitude-budget</code> scenario kind (scenarios/attitude-budget.toml)</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Basilisk 2.9.1 GravityGradientEffector (AVSLab, ISC), an independent C++ implementation of the full-tensor gravity-gradient torque: Kshana's peak (3/2)(μ/R³)ΔI equals Basilisk's torque at the 45 deg attitude within 1e-12 relative (worst 2.1e-15) over 5 altitudes and 8 inertia tensors, and bounds Basilisk's torque over 2000 random attitudes per case; tolerance fixed before the comparison. Validated claim: the gravity-gradient torque peak. The RSS pointing budget is a quadrature sum of caller-supplied numbers with no external truth and stays outside it; the hand-coded Hipparchus full-tensor maximisation remains as supporting self-consistency. No control-loop/6-DoF/flexible-mode simulation</td><td><code>attitude_budget</code></td></tr><tr><td>Ground-station pass prediction (ground segment)</td><td>Time-domain visibility passes (AOS/TCA/LOS, max elevation, duration) of an engine propagator over a station above an elevation mask, with interpolated rise/set crossings and total access: passes::predict_passes, the geometric scheduler (no refraction or light time). The <code>passes</code> scenario kind runs the apparent predictor of the row 'Apparent ground-station pass prediction with refraction and light time'</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Orekit 12.2 (CS GROUP, Apache-2.0) + Hipparchus 3.1 — an independent flight-dynamics library: ElevationDetector (Brent root-finder) + EventsLogger over an ITRF ephemeris, station as a WGS-84 TopocentricFrame. AOS/LOS/max-elevation/pass-count/total-access matched on identical orbit+station+mask+window (committed fixture; driver xval/orekit-passes)</td><td><code>passes</code></td></tr><tr><td>Apparent ground-station pass prediction with refraction and light time</td><td>Visibility passes (AOS, TCA, LOS, maximum apparent elevation, duration) of an SGP4 element set over a WGS-84 station on the validated Earth-orbit path (IAU 2006/2000A, optional Earth orientation parameters), with ITU-R P.834-9 tropospheric refraction (equations 9 to 14, the Recommendation's printed coefficients) and the downlink light time, each switchable; crossings solved by bisection to 1e-7 s and the culmination by golden-section search. Runnable from the CLI/bindings as the <code>passes</code> scenario kind (scenarios/passes.toml), both corrections on by default</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Orekit 13.1.8 (CS GROUP, Apache-2.0) with Hipparchus 4.0.3: TLEPropagator on the same element sets in a TEME Orekit builds to the stated definition (its IERS 2010 TOD rotated by GAST - GMST), ElevationDetector and ElevationExtremumDetector with and without ITURP834AtmosphericRefraction, and AngularAzEl with AngularRadioRefraction