>Surface positioning and timing availability by site and season at the lunar poles (<code>lunar-surface</code>)</td><td>The <code>lunar-surface</code> kind and phase: relay availability and PDOP, Earth and Sun visibility and a disciplined surface clock for each site and season, with the terrain skyline of <code>moonlight-service-volume</code>; a single-site run equals that kind's availability and PDOP to the bit</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Exact equality with the existing kind for one site, and monotonicity checks, in the tests. The relay set is ILLUSTRATIVE and no surface measurement is compared</td><td><code>lunar_surface_map (LunarSurfaceMapScenario); lunar_phase</code></td></tr><tr><td>Powered ascent from a lunar polar site to an insertion orbit (<code>lunar-ascent</code>)</td><td>The <code>lunar-ascent</code> kind and phase: the five-state per-axis inertial error model run over a powered ascent with specific force along the path, aided by AFS fixes and lunar time, reporting the insertion position and velocity covariance carried to orbit and the engine-cutoff dispersion (reported, not carried)</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>The closed-form inertial drift and monotonicity checks in the tests. No flown ascent is compared</td><td><code>lunar_ascent (LunarAscentScenario); lunar_phase</code></td></tr><tr><td>The lunar force model propagation against public reconstructed lunar-orbiter and cislunar trajectories</td><td>The propagator the lunar kinds share (GRAIL GL0660B degree and order 150, Earth and Sun third bodies, relativity, DE440 lunar orientation) compared with the public JPL Horizons trajectories of LRO (24 h, 10 minute samples) and CAPSTONE (one 6.5 day revolution, one hour samples), under bars fixed in a committed file before the run</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>JPL Horizons spacecraft ephemerides for LRO and CAPSTONE, against bars pre-registered in xval/mission-thread-lunar/PREREGISTRATION.md (Amendments 1 to 6). LRO: MET, 33 m largest 3-D difference and 2.4 m RMS radia