the pass gives the tracked-component C/N0 and band-limited code jitter per epoch; a least-squares C/N0 line in sin(elevation), the leo-navmsg message's SISRE (fitted at the pass satellite's orbit, plus a stated orbit-determination term in root-sum-square) and the design's carrier and chip rate go to every LEO system of a leo-pvt joint fix and, optionally, to the LEO cases of leo-ppp; every hand-off is reported with its value and unit</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Self-consistency of the hand-offs: each handed-on value equals the upstream stage's own output, and mutating an upstream input moves every downstream figure that depends on it. The stages keep their own labels; the chain adds no physics and no external oracle</td><td><code>leo_pnt_chain (LeoPntChainScenario); leo_pass (BandCfg::signal, design_tracking); leo_navmsg (LeoNavmsgScenario::broadcast_sisre); leo_fusion::pvt_kind; leo_fusion::ppp</code></td></tr><tr><td>Spoofing detection by LEO Doppler and pass-geometry consistency</td><td>The <code>leo-pass</code> kind's <code>[spoofer]</code> section: a spoofer counterfeits, self-consistently in range and range rate, the signals seen at a claimed position that jumps and/or is pushed from the true one after an onset (per LEO band and for the MEO GNSS signal); the receiver predicts every range rate from the broadcast orbit and an independent prior position and velocity, and tests the measured range rates with a generalised least-squares chi-square statistic over a window of epochs (clock drift a nuisance or with a prior), on the GNSS channels, the LEO channels and all channels, with frequency-lock-loop thermal noise from C/N0 (Kaplan &amp; Hegarty 2006, section 5.6.2); detection probability from the non-central chi-square law at a stated false-alarm probability, detection declared at a stated missed-detection probability</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Closed forms and identities, not an external measurement: the range-rate gradient against a central difference, the information-form statistic against the dense covariance form, a pure clock drift giving zero, a prior-explained push giving at most |dx|^2/sigma_p^2, the statistic quadratic in a jump. No detection figure is compared with a measured attack; the spoofer is idealised (no power, angle-of-arrival or correlation-peak signature)</td><td><code>leo_link::spoof (range_rate_position_gradient, line_of_sight, fll_frequency_jitter_hz, doppler_consistency_window, doppler_consistency); leo_pass (SpooferCfg, spoof_section)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Spoofing detection by cross-band consistency of a multi-band LEO signal</td><td>For every LEO satellite and pair of ranging bands the <code>[spoofer]</code> section forms the geometry-free pseudorange combination, removes the receiver's ionospheric model and tests its epoch-to-epoch step against the two bands' code noise and a stated tolerated unmodelled ionospheric rate (two-sided Gaussian, exact detection probability); a spoofer that counterfeits some bands and not others, or every band without the ionosphere, is seen at its onset; one that counterfeits every band and simulates the ionosphere is not, which the result states</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Identities: the onset step of an ionosphere-free spoofer equals the pair's modelled ionospheric delay difference, the threshold is the two-sided normal quantile times the step's standard deviation plus the rate bound. The receiver's ionospheric model is the engine's, so the authentic residual is zero in the mean by construction; no external oracle</td><td><code>leo_link::spoof (cross_band_step); leo_pass (spoof_section: pairs, cross_band)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Ionosphere sounding: slant TEC from the dual-band delay of a LEO pass</td><td>For every band pair of a <code>leo-pass</code> satellite the report gives the slant total electron content the geometry-free code combination recovers at the pass peak, (P2 - P1) f1^2 f2^2 / (40.3 (f1^2 - f2^2)), and its 1-sigma from the two bands' thermal code noise, so any multi-band LEO-PNT design can be traded as an ionosphere sounder</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>The first-order dispersion identity, exact by construction against the engine's own first-order delay (whose 1/f^2 scaling is VALIDATED against IS-GPS-200 in its own row); the precision is thermal code noise only, without multipath or inter-frequency biases. 0.30 round 2, measured (pre-registered 5e795f93), a finding (stays MODELLED): the CODE global ionosphere map CODG1330.18I against ABMF C1C/C2W code with the CODE biases gives 33 of 36 arc means within 3 TECU (worst +6.84 TECU on an evening arc; per-epoch RMS 3.95 TECU), against a bar of every arc; not validated</td><td><code>leo_pass (IonoFreePair::geometry_free_stec_tecu, geometry_free_stec_sigma_tecu); leo_link::iono::geometry_free_stec_tecu</code></td></tr><tr><td>Lunar frame datum covariance with the stations estimated, against 50-digit extended precision</td><td>The seven-parameter Helmert datum covariance of the lunar-frame-campaign scenario with the Earth stations estimated (station 1 anchored), as lunar_frame_campaign computes it in binary64: the joint station-and-beacon Fisher information accumulated from the delay Jacobian, the station block marginalised by a Schur complement through a Jacobi spectral inverse, H = AᵀSA formed with the Helmert design and inverted spectrally. The problem is ill-conditioned by construction (Helmert condition number 2.1e8 to 2.3e8, joint information condition number 1.6e12 to 3.0e13): a 1e-9 relative change in the Schur correction moves the datum sigmas by up to 28 %. Reported: the seven datum sigmas, the Helmert condition number and the weakest direction. CLAIM, NARROWED: the engine's datum sigmas and condition number lie within the a-priori worst-case error bound of a backward-stable double-precision pipeline on this problem; this does not claim they are as accurate as double precision allows (a factorisation route is about a thousand times more accurate on the same inputs, and the opt-in square-root solver about ten million times), and the weakest direction is validated only where its bar is below a right angle (the stations-fixed control)</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>mpmath 1.3.0 (BSD-3-Clause) at 50 significant digits, self-checked at 80 (agreement 1.1e-38 or better), on the engine's committed Jacobian, weights and Helmert design: mpmath's own LU solve for the Schur complement, its inverse for H and its Jacobi eigen-decomposition (P2, an independent numerical library). Pre-registered 804662d5 before the fixture existed, with bars from a first-order backward-error formula: constant (3n+1)n + m times the unit roundoff (Higham, Accuracy and Stability of Numerical Algorithms, Theorem 10.4, the Tier B form), times each quantity's exact first-order sensitivity to the joint information, the Schur complement and H. Measured: datum sigmas within 4.8e-6 on 2026-03-18 (bars 9.6e-2 to 2.2e-1), 9.7e-6 on 2024-01-01 (bars 2.8e-2 to 6.9e-2) and 2.0e-14 with the stations fixed (bars 1.0e-11 to 2.8e-7); condition number within 4.6e-6, 1.3e-5 and 4.1e-14. On the stations-estimated scenarios the bars are worst-case and four orders of magnitude above the measured error, and the weakest-direction bar there exceeds a right angle, so that one check is not counted; on the stations-fixed control the weakest direction agrees to 6.6e-16 rad against a 9.4e-11 bar, measured by the chord between unit vectors (an earlier acos comparator could not resolve angles below 1.5e-8 rad; the bar is unchanged). Diagnostic: NumPy 2.3.5 Cholesky and QR routes reach 9e-9 and 5e-9 on the 2024-01-01 inputs, its inverse and eigen routes 2.9e-6 and 6.2e-6: the engine is within its bound but about a thousand times less accurate than a factorisation route. Validates the linear algebra on the committed inputs, not the Jacobian (the SPICE leg of the campaign row does that) and not the physical accuracy of the illustrative campaign</td><td><code>lunar_frame_campaign, fim</code></td></tr><tr><td>Square-root information datum solver against 50-digit extended precision</td><td>An opt-in square-root information solver (lunar-frame-campaign solver = &quot;srif&quot;; default unchanged) that never forms or inverts an information matrix for the datum: Householder QR (orthogonal-triangular decomposition) of the whitened delay Jacobian as a square-root information filter measurement update, the Earth stations marginalised through the trailing triangular block (whose Gram product is the Schur complement), a second QR of R_bb A for the seven-parameter Helmert datum, sigmas as the row norms of the triangular inverse, and the spectrum (condition number, weakest direction) from a one-sided Jacobi singular value decomposition. Inner products are compensated with a Dekker-split error-free product, so every operation is a correctly rounded IEEE-754 basic operation or square root; identical results on every platform are argued from that, not tested across platforms</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>mpmath 1.3.0 (BSD-3-Clause) at 50 significant digits, self-checked at 80 (agreement 1.4e-43 or better), on the engine's committed Jacobian, weights and Helmert design (P2, an independent numerical library: mpmath's own LU solve, inverse, Cholesky and Jacobi eigen-decomposition). Pre-registered b41908c9 before the solver was written, with bars from a first-order backward-error bound in square-root form: Householder QR constant mn plus (3n+1)n times the unit roundoff (Higham, Accuracy and Stability of Numerical Algorithms, Theorem 19.4 and Chapter 8) times exact sensitivities. Measured: datum sigmas within 3.0e-13 on 2026-06-09 (bars 4.1e-7 to 5.3e-7), 1.9e-15 with the stations fixed (bars down to 4.8e-12), 3.8e-13 on 2026-03-18 and 2.3e-13 on 2024-01-01, where the default spectral solver is off by 4.8e-6 and 9.7e-6; condition number within 4.9e-13; weakest direction within 1.4e-13 rad (control 1.7e-16 against 5.1e-10). LIMIT OF THE CHECK: the bars certify only datum errors below about 2e-7 relative; the measured 1e-13 figures are observations, not what the bars certify. The check rejects the default spectral solver (sigmas 9.7e-6 against 2.9e-7 on 2024-01-01; on the binding date 1.3e-6 against 4.9e-7, a margin of 1.3 to 2.6) and a mis-taken triangular inverse (column norms: red), but a flipped Householder sign, an uncompensated dot product, and sigmas from a spectral inverse of the H formed from the square-root factor (3.7e-9) all pass, so it does not show that each square-root step is needed. Validates the opt-in solver's linear algebra on committed inputs, not the Jacobian and not the default solver; bit-reproducibility across platforms is argued from the operations used and tested only as rerun identity on one platform</td><td><code>linalg_sr, lunar_frame_campaign</code></td></tr><tr><td>Maritime trust monitors: the arithmetic they rest on (NMEA decoding, geodesic offsets, the kinematic and sensor-residual statistics)</td><td>For a vessel, <code>receiver_trust</code> decodes the NMEA 0183 sentences a receiver and its instruments emit (GGA, RMC, VTG, HDT, VHW, VBW, ZDA, GSV), forms the east-north offset between fixes (<code>maritime::en_offset_m</code>), and from these the statistics its monitors decide on: the implied speed, dead-reckoning residual, implied acceleration and implied turn rate over 30 s windows, the heading-versus-course, speed-log-versus-ground-speed and antenna-altitude residuals (10 s medians), and the spread and rise of the satellite signal-to-noise.</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>WHAT IS EXTERNALLY CHECKED, stated narrowly: (1) the decoding of every field the monitors read, against pynmea2 1.19.0 (MIT) on 8 synthetic logs (1,086 epochs), to 1e-9 in the field's unit (latitude and longitude, altitude, geoid separation, HDOP, speeds, course, heading, signal-to-noise; integers and dates exactly; time of day to 1e-6 s), largest differences at the level of the last digit of a double; (2) the east-north offset between two fixes, as distance and bearing, against the WGS84 geodesic of geographiclib 2.1 (MIT) on 223 pairs of fixes with baselines up to 2 km, from -45 to 70 degrees latitude and across the antimeridian, to 0.01 m and 0.001 degrees (the bearing against the geodesic's mean azimuth, the direction of the chord); (3) the monitors' input statistics at 341 scored epochs of a 400 s synthetic voyage with a turn and a position step, against Python recomputations that use geographiclib for distance and azimuth, pynmea2 for decoding and numpy for medians and standard deviations: implied speed to 0.005 m/s, dead-reckoning residual to 0.02 m, acceleration to 5e-4 m/s^2, turn rate to 1e-3 deg/s, the residual medians and the signal-to-noise spread and rise to 1e-6. WHAT IS NOT: the window lengths, allowances and thresholds of the monitors are this project's own rules; the Python reproduces them from the same documentation, so agreement corroborates the implementation of the arithmetic, not the rules. The THS sentence has no pynmea2 class and the ROT sentence is not read; neither is covered. The table from an NMEA talker and satellite number to a RINEX-style satellite id is this project's own (only the number, the talker and the value are compared). The time-step, host-clock and authentication-status inputs have no oracle. All data are synthetic text from receiver_trust::synth; nothing here is a measurement and no statement is made about how any monitor performs on real interference. Bearing: compared with the geodesic mean azimuth. The pre-registered comparison against the forward azimuth at fix 1 failed at 0.001 degrees (77 of 223 pairs, worst 8.3e-3 degrees) because of a definitional mismatch, not arithmetic; the amendment was made after the first run and both results are recorded (the forward-azimuth comparison stays in the test as a descriptive, non-pre-registered bound of 0.01 degrees). Disclosed: the first runs found inputs that exercised too little (acceleration and turn rate were clamped to zero at every epoch, and the antimeridian track never crossed it), a bug in the synthetic generator (no longitude wrap) and, for the bearing, that the registered oracle quantity (the geodesic's forward azimuth at the first fix) differs from the chord direction by about half the meridian convergence (77 of 223 pairs outside 0.001 degrees, worst 8.3e-3); the inputs were corrected and the bearing compared against the mean azimuth at the same tolerance (amendments 1 to 3 in the pre-registration, made after seeing the results and so not blind); the course and heading comparisons were made circular (360 and 0 degrees are one course, amendment 5). Mutation: a 1e-4 scaling of the east offset fails the geodesy comparison.</td><td><code>receiver_trust (ingest, maritime)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Maritime trust score: the aggregation of the monitors' evidence into a 0-100 score and its three bands</td><td>Each monitor reduces its evidence to a ratio of statistic to threshold; the score is 100 minus the sum over monitors of weight times a clamped linear ramp of the ratio (0 up to 0.5, 1 at 1.5), clamped to 0 to 100 and rounded to 0.1, with bands at 90 and 55; each epoch lists the monitors that deducted and their points.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Implementation against specification, NOT an external oracle: a clean-room Python re-implementation (scripts/maritime_score_cleanroom.py), written by a reader given only docs/RECEIVER-TRUST.md and docs/MARITIME-TRUST.md and not the Rust source, agrees with score_from_ratios on 600 ratio vectors (score, band, points and the order of deductions, difference 0), after the documentation was completed where the first reader found it silent or inconsistent (tie order, rounding, the ratio of a pass-or-fail monitor, two examples whose numbers did not match the formula). That shows the documentation is sufficient and the code does what it says. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED, and why the row stays MODELLED: the rule itself (the weights, the ramp, the band edges, the evidence hold) is this project's own, pre-registered but with no external source, and nothing here shows that a score of 80 or 40 means what a navigator would want it to mean on real interference; no figure of that kind is given. The monitors' thresholds are likewise this project's own (Row A checks only their arithmetic).</td><td><code>receiver_trust (score, monitors, live)</code></td></tr><tr><td>NMEA 0183 training-stream conformance and decoded values</td><td>Synthetic bridge NMEA 0183 streams for crew training (kshana nmea-scenario): GGA, RMC, VTG, ZDA, GNS, GSA, GSV, HDT, VBW and the PKSHT marker, generated from a truth track with modelled receiver, sky, jamming and spoofing effects. Text output only; nothing is transmitted.</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>WHAT IS EXTERNALLY CHECKED, stated narrowly: all 225,227 sentences of eight synthetic training streams (four library scenarios, seeds 1 and 7; GGA, RMC, VTG, ZDA, GNS, GSA, GSV, HDT, VBW and the PKSHT marker) parse in pynmea2 1.19.0, an independent NMEA parser, with a valid checksum and the expected talker and type. The position, speed over ground, course over ground, heading, UTC time and date, fix status and GGA satellite count it decodes equal the generator's truth track within tolerances fixed before the first comparison (1.5e-6 deg, 0.06 kn, 0.07 km/h, 0.11 deg course, 0.5 deg heading, 0.02 s), run by scripts/gen_nmea_training_ref.py with the fixture in tests/fixtures/nmea_training_ref/. The GGA satellite-count expectation was corrected after the first run to the NMEA convention (00 without a fix); numeric tolerances unchanged from the pre-registration commit. WHAT IS NOT: that any receiver, chart plotter or bridge system accepts the streams; the realism of the receiver, sky, jamming or spoofing models (nominal sky, modelled noise, drag and replay profiles, all MODELLED); HDOP, altitude, and the DOP and GSA/GSV content beyond syntax and counts; the instructor log. Nothing is transmitted, and no anti-jam or anti-spoof performance is claimed.</td><td><code>nmea_synth (track, sentences, scenarios); cli nmea-scenario</code></td></tr><tr><td>Interference map: accuracy-code meanings, AIS not-available values, grid cell assignment, route length per cell state, and inland masking</td><td>The reading of the ADS-B navigation accuracy and integrity codes the map uses (the EPU bound a NACp code stands for, the containment-radius bound a NIC code stands for, and the low and good classes the method derives from them); the AIS position and speed not-available values the AIS reader discards; the fixed latitude/longitude grid cell a position falls in; the route length, and its split by cell state (degraded, not degraded, unassessed, not observed), that route exposure reports; and whether a position lies inland of a land polygon by more than a buffer. A cell flagged degraded or anomalous is a statement about reported accuracy fields or implausible positions, not a finding of interference, and nothing here checks that.</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>WHAT IS EXTERNALLY CHECKED, stated narrowly: five independent implementations read the same synthetic inputs. (1) pyModeS 2.21 decodes synthetic DF17 operational-status and airborne-position frames; Kshana's NACp EPU bounds agree to 0.5 m (largest difference 0.4 m), its NIC containment bounds for codes 5 to 11 agree to 1 m, and its low and good classes put all 12 NACp codes and every NIC code the oracle produces (0 to 11) on the same side of the thresholds the oracle's bounds imply (0 disagreements). (2) pyais 3.3.0 decodes AIVDM type 1 sentences whose payload bits the script packs from the raw ITU-R M.1371 field values; Kshana refuses exactly the decoded not-available positions (latitude 91, longitude 181) and speed (102.3 kn), and keeps 0 to 102.2 kn as decoded (0 disagreements over 94 frames). (3) shapely 2.2.0 covers() on cell polygons agrees with grid::Grid::cell_of for 1770 points over cell sizes 0.5, 0.25, 1 and 0.07 degrees, including near-edge points, both poles and the antimeridian (0 disagreements). (4) shapely 2.2.0 route/cell intersection with GeographicLib 2.1 WGS84 geodesic lengths agrees with route exposure on 8 synthetic routes, including two that cross the antimeridian: total length to 0.6% (largest difference 0.49%, the sphere against the ellipsoid) and each cell state's length to 0.6% plus 125 m per piece (largest 21% of that tolerance). (5) shapely 2.2.0 contains() with pyproj azimuthal-equidistant distance to the coastline agrees with the 2000 m inland mask on 992 lattice-centre points around four synthetic islands, one with a lake (0 disagreements; 4 points within 20 m of the buffer were excluded and counted). The tolerances were fixed in tests/fixtures/interference_map_ref/PREREGISTRATION.md before any comparison, and not changed after; two defects in the synthetic inputs found by the first run are recorded there. WHAT IS NOT: Kshana does not decode raw Mode S frames or AIVDM sentences, so this validates its reading of already-decoded values, not a decoder. The oracle's NIC containment radii for codes 1, 2 and 4 differ from DO-260B (20, 8 and 2 NM), so only codes 5 to 11 are compared numerically. The (0, 0) position rule, the altitude floor, the source-type filter, the identifier hashing, the thresholds and statistics of the method, and the cell size choice are Kshana's own and are not checked against anything. The route comparison uses the same straight-in-latitude-and-longitude path definition on both sides, so it checks lengths and cell assignment, not that this path is the right one for a vessel or aircraft. Nothing here shows that a flagged cell is interference. Inputs are synthetic; the oracle fixtures are written by scripts/gen_interference_map_ref.py (pyModeS is GPL-3.0 and is used in that offline script only; CI needs no Python).</td><td><code>interference_map (adsb::nacp_epu_bound_m, adsb::nic_rc_bound_range_m, adsb::AdsbParams::classify, ais::ais_position_is_valid, ais::ais_speed_kn, grid::Grid::cell_of, route::exposure, land::LandMask::is_inland)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Interference source region: a feasible emitter region from aircraft and ship position reports</td><td>A feasible region for where a source of degraded navigation reports can be, from ADS-B (NIC/NACp) or AIS position reports: radio line of sight on a 4/3-earth radius from the report height and an emitter antenna height range, a limited effective range for the unknown power, affected units pulling the posterior toward their reach and unaffected units with line of sight pushing against it, grouped into sessions in time and space. Output is highest-density regions at stated confidence levels, a posterior centroid with a moment ellipse, session start, end, duration, affected area and distinct-aircraft count above the publication minimum, a time-of-day profile and hours per day. Terrain-free; a single source per session; the credibility is conditional on the model. Optional fusion with a TDOA fix from geolocation.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>None external yet. Synthetic forward-inverse round trips only, from a generator that shares the estimator's model, so they check the code and the calibration of the stated levels under that model and nothing about real emitters. An external check against the published Jammertest 2025 transmitter sites was pre-registered (docs/emitter-region/PREREGISTRATION.md, signed off by the owner before any scored run) and run once (docs/emitter-region/RESULTS.md): ADS-B NOT ASSESSABLE (2 scheduled sessions, fewer than the registered minimum of 4) and AIS NOT ASSESSABLE (no published session), so no bar was tested and the row stays MODELLED</td><td><code>interference_map::emitter (estimate, sessions, build_units, profile, GaussianFix::from_tdoa), interference_map::emitter_out (locate), kshana interference-map locate</code></td></tr><tr><td>Trust telemetry wire formats: Prometheus text exposition and OpenTelemetry OTLP/HTTP JSON</td><td>GNSS trust (score, band, reasons, gate and, on request, position) as Prometheus text exposition served on /metrics and as OTLP/HTTP JSON metrics, with a cap on reason label cardinality.</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>WHAT IS EXTERNALLY CHECKED, stated narrowly: our Prometheus text exposition for an 84-line synthetic stream (label values with quotes, backslashes, line feeds and non-ASCII text, 64 reason series plus an 'other' fold, gate and position gauges) is accepted by prometheus_client 0.26.0's text parser, and the metric families, types, help text, label sets and values it reads equal the registry's state exactly (values by f64 equality). Our OTLP/HTTP JSON for the same registry parses with protobuf 7.36.2 json_format.Parse(ignore_unknown_fields=False) into opentelemetry-proto 1.45.1's ExportMetricsServiceRequest, and every metric name, description, unit, kind, monotonicity, aggregation temporality, attribute, value, start time and time equals the registry's state exactly. Both parsers were shown to reject an unknown field, a mistyped value and a malformed line (scripts/gen_telemetry_formats_ref.py). Schema 1.3 (0.36), pre-registered (fb226057) before any tool read it: for a 64-line synthetic schema-1.3 stream (geometry statuses, DOPs, labels and signal loss present, null, absent and unknown; deduction classes; unparsable lines) the same two parsers read the geometry series (Prometheus kshana_trust_geometry*, OTLP kshana.geometry.*) and the class label on kshana_trust_reason_active exactly as the registry implies (24 families and 54 samples; 12 metrics and 31 points), promtool 3.5.0 check metrics exits 0 with no lint problem, and the OpenTelemetry Collector contrib 0.135.0 accepts the OTLP/HTTP JSON (status 200, nothing rejected) and its file exporter records the request unchanged (xval/telemetry-1.3, data-gated: the release tarballs are fetched with pinned SHA-256 and their outputs committed). WHAT IS NOT: the HTTP endpoint, delivery, timeouts and reconnection are not checked by an oracle, nor the Collector's prometheus receiver; the registry is read through accessors, so a fault in the registry state itself would not be caught; the OTLP 'committed output is current' check runs only with the otlp feature; the metric names and their usefulness to an operations centre are not validated; CEF and LEEF are not covered (see the next row).</td><td><code>telemetry (prometheus: Registry, render; otlp: build_payload, Exporter (feature otlp); sample)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Trust telemetry SIEM events: Common Event Format (CEF) over syslog</td><td>Band-change (or per-epoch) events as Common Event Format payloads in an RFC 5424 envelope over UDP or TCP, with a documented field mapping and CEF escaping.</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>WHAT IS EXTERNALLY CHECKED, stated narrowly: against two independent open-source parsers (oracles: pycef 1.11 (PyPI, MIT); the Logstash CEF codec (logstash-codec-cef 6.2.7 in Logstash 8.15.3)), on a corpus of CEF lines this crate wrote (157 before amendment 4 below, 170 after it, 176 after amendment 5): every band change and every epoch of a committed 84-line synthetic trust stream, plus adversarial text in hosts, reasons, time labels and gate names containing equals signs, backslashes, backslash-equals, pipes, line feeds, carriage returns, tabs, runs of spaces, multi-byte and astral characters and key lookalikes, extreme scores and offsets, every band with and without a previous band. pycef parses the lines whose header needs no escaping (153 before amendment 4, 166 after it, 172 after amendment 5) and recovers, exactly, the header fields, the severity, the exact extension key set and every value (numbers by f64 equality), after the CEF specification's unescape rules, which pycef does not apply and which scripts/gen_cef_ref.py applies. The Logstash codec, which unescapes by itself, parses every line (157, then 170, then 176), meets the same bar, and also recovers four header fields that need escaping (a pipe, a backslash and an equals sign in the version string), which pycef's header pattern cannot read, so header escaping rests on the Logstash codec alone. The comparison bar was registered in commits 5172e579 (pycef) and 0a7598aa (Logstash) before either parser was run, and both met it at the first comparison. Five amendments are disclosed. Amendments (1) to (3) touched no comparison rule and no expected value: (1) a corpus-size assertion was corrected from 5 to 4 band changes, a miscount, since the stream has 4; (2) the Logstash runner's pipeline delimiter wiring was fixed after a first attempt that produced no events; (3) the trust score and epoch offset were moved from the CEF integer fields cn1 and cn2 to the floating-point fields cfp1 and cfp2, a wire-format change made in this release, after which the corpus was regenerated and both oracles were re-run with the bar unchanged and met it again. Amendment (4), 0.36, extended the corpus and the expected key set, registered in d6331429 before either parser ran on the new lines: schema 1.3 inputs add the satellite geometry in the custom fields cs6 (label geometry: status, then ';' and the deduction classes), cfp3 (geometryHdop) and cfp4 (geometryPdop); 13 geometry cases (the corpus to 170 lines) and the three new keys; both met the bar, otherwise unchanged, at the first comparison (pycef 166 of 170 lines parsed, the 4 tier 2 header cases as before; Logstash 170 of 170). Amendment (5), 0.36, appended 6 geometry-label cases (the corpus to 176 lines), registered in 9a8e4fa9 before either parser ran on them: from inputs that carry the geometry label, cs6 gains a third field (';' and the label) and cn1 (geometryUsedSatellites) the satellites used; the Logstash codec's mapping table was read first (no flexString fields, hence cs6 rather than flexString1); both met the bar at the first comparison (pycef 172 of 176, Logstash 176 of 176), and every earlier reference entry was reproduced unchanged. WHAT IS NOT: neither parser type-checks the cfp values (both keep them as text), so the use of the floating-point fields rests on the CEF dictionary rather than on an oracle; no vendor SIEM was run; the extension key names and their mapping are this crate's choice, and the parsers show only that they are read back as written; the RFC 5424 envelope, delivery, timeouts and reconnection are not covered; pycef is a small parser last released in 2020, and the Logstash codec is the more substantial oracle.</td><td><code>telemetry (syslog: cef, rfc5424, SyslogSink; time)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Trust telemetry SIEM events: Log Event Extended Format (LEEF) over syslog</td><td>Band-change (or per-epoch) events as Log Event Extended Format 2.0 payloads in an RFC 5424 envelope over UDP or TCP, with a documented field mapping.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>WHAT IS CHECKED: this crate's own exact-string tests of the documented LEEF field mapping, and the RFC 5424 envelope fields; no maintained open-source LEEF parser exists (a search of the Python package index found none), so the format is checked against its own specification read by the same author, not against an external oracle. WHAT IS NOT: whether the LEEF payloads are accepted by any vendor's parser or SIEM; the escaping rules beyond the documented ones; behaviour of a real syslog collector.</td><td><code>telemetry (syslog: leef, rfc5424, SyslogSink; time)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Evidence pack integrity: signature, hash chain and verification procedure</td><td>A signed, hash-chained evidence pack of a GNSS trust event: SHA-256 over every file and a chain over the manifest, an Ed25519 signature over the exact manifest bytes, and a verifier that names every failure (a changed, removed, renamed, swapped or added file, a reordered or edited manifest, a manifest re-hashed without the key, a re-signed pack, a wrong pinned key, and a timestamp token that is stripped, replaced, junk or over another document).</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>WHAT IS EXTERNALLY CHECKED, stated narrowly: (1) the Ed25519 functions the pack uses to sign and to verify strictly (evidence::bundle::sign_detached, evidence::verify::verify_detached) reproduce the public keys and signatures of RFC 8032 section 7.1 test vectors 1, 2, 3 and 1024 byte for byte, accept them, and reject every flipped bit of vector 1 and every seventh bit of the others and a non-canonical S+L signature; the vectors are parsed from the RFC text and reproduced by two independent implementations (cryptography/OpenSSL and PyNaCl/libsodium) in scripts/gen_ed25519_rfc8032_ref.py. (2) evidence::bundle::sha256_hex, the hash behind every pack hash and the chain, reproduces the FIPS 180-4 SHA-256 examples (abc; the 448-bit message; one million a), the empty message and hashlib digests of a 0 to 130 byte ladder across the padding boundaries, exactly. (3) A verifier written in Python from docs/EVIDENCE-PACKS.md alone (scripts/evidence_verify_cleanroom.py: hashlib and cryptography; its author read no Rust code) agrees with the Rust verifier on 71 synthetic intact and tampered packs, exactly, on the verdict, the set of failures with file names, the signature verdict, and the chain head recomputed from the files and the one the manifest records. First run 64 of 71 agreed; the disagreements were one specification ambiguity (checking a pinned key against an unreadable manifest), one oracle interface deviation and one incompletely built case; the specification sentence was clarified, the oracle re-implemented from the text and the case completed, with no tolerance or expectation changed (recorded in commit d54e14c6). WHAT IS NOT: the clean-room verifier is an independent implementation by a different author, not a third-party authority, and it shares the specification's choices, so it corroborates this implementation of the rules, not the rules; the vectors validate the primitives, not the pack format's design. In this row the RFC 3161 token is read for its message imprint only and the test tokens are synthetic and unsigned; the authority's signature and chain are verified, opt-in (<code>kshana evidence verify --tsa-ca</code>), by the separate row 'RFC 3161 timestamp-token signature and chain verification'. The tamper cases are synthetic. Nothing here is a legal opinion on, or an attestation of, any pack.</td><td><code>evidence (bundle: create_bundle, sign_detached, chain_link, sha256_hex; verify: verify_bundle, verify_detached; tsr; assemble; cli)</code></td></tr><tr><td>RFC 3161 timestamp-token signature and chain verification</td><td>evidence::tsa::verify_token, and opt-in <code>kshana evidence verify --tsa-ca &lt;anchors.pem&gt;</code> for a pack's timestamp.tsr: an RFC 3161 TimeStampResp or bare TimeStampToken (CMS SignedData over a TSTInfo) checked offline against trusted anchors the way <code>openssl ts -verify</code> checks it: the signed attributes' content type and message digest over the TSTInfo; the signature over the signed attributes (RSA PKCS#1 v1.5, RSASSA-PSS, ECDSA P-256 and P-384; RSA public operations on crypto-bigint); the ESS signing-certificate v1 (SHA-1) or v2 hash naming the signer certificate, found among the token's and the caller's untrusted certificates; the signer's critical, sole timeStamping extended key usage; the chain to a trusted self-issued anchor with each certificate's signature, CA status and key usage, and every certificate valid at the token's genTime; and the message imprint against the data. Not checked: revocation (CRL, OCSP), name and policy constraints, the nonce</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>OpenSSL 3.0.13 (<code>openssl ts -verify -attime genTime</code>, and <code>openssl cms -verify -cades -purpose timestampsign</code> plus the TSTInfo imprint for RSA-PSS tokens), run by tests/fixtures/evidence_tsa/run_openssl.sh on the 27 cases of cases.tsv: RSA PKCS#1 v1.5 with SHA-256 and SHA-512 and ESS v1 and v2, an ECDSA P-384 root over a P-256 intermediate and P-256 and P-384 TSAs, RSA-PSS, a token without the signer certificate with and without it supplied, CAdES-signed tokens, a flipped signature byte (three algorithms), a changed TSTInfo (two), a flipped intermediate signature, an ESS twin certificate, wrong anchors (four), an expired TSA certificate, a non-critical and a wrong extended key usage, and wrong data (three). Pre-registered in tests/fixtures/evidence_tsa/PREREGISTRATION.md (committed alone, 30ad70b2, before the verifier existed; the OpenSSL verdicts re-run afterwards, byte-identical). First run: all 27 verdicts agree (B1) and every genTime agrees to the second (B2). Scope, stated narrowly: self-generated test certificates and tokens, made by the same OpenSSL that judges them; OpenSSL is an independent implementation, not a timestamp authority; no public authority's token is vendored (freetsa.org states no licence). Nothing here attests any real timestamp</td><td><code>evidence::tsa (verify_token, pem_certificates); evidence::verify (VerifyOptions::tsa_anchors, Failure::TimestampAuthorityInvalid); evidence::cli (--tsa-ca)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Receiver-trust interference thresholds calibrated on the JammerTest 2024 dataset</td><td>receiver_trust::calibration::run_dataset: the pre-registered calibration of the C/N0-drop, AGC and jamming-indicator monitors on the public JammerTest 2024 dataset (Zenodo 15911589, GPL-3.0-or-later, fetched at run time, never vendored): per folder a 1 s timeline from mon_rf.csv and rinex.csv, the monitors run once, each post-calibration epoch labelled clean or jamming from the folder's attack_log (shifted from CEST to UTC, 60 s guard), every threshold swept over a fixed grid, the smallest threshold with pooled clean false-alarm probability at most 0.01 selected, and leave-one-folder-out held-out counts; a UBX reader cross-check. The selection ships only as the opt-in profile [monitors] profile = &quot;jammertest-2024-calibrated&quot; (cn0_drop_db 1.5, agc_k_sigma 3.5, jam_ind_threshold 29); the default rule and every output without it are unchanged</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>The dataset's published event logs (attack_log windows), as event truth, under the pre-registration xval/jammertest-trust/PREREGISTRATION.md (committed alone, 0fa012b, before the harness ran). First and only run (xval/jammertest-trust/RESULTS.md): 14 of 24 folders evaluable (7 with jamming), 3,502 clean and 9,560 jamming epochs. Held-out clean false-alarm probability: cn0_drop_db 0.0231 (81/3502) against a 0.02 bar, FAIL; agc_k_sigma 0.0003, pass; jam_ind_threshold 0.0094, pass; union of the three: false alarms 0.0328, detection 0.807 against 0.05 and 0.50, pass. By the registered rule the row is MODELLED (one bar failed). Narrow: one receiver type (u-blox ZED-F9P), one event, scheduled one-minute slots as truth (detection a lower bound; clean means outside the logged windows); calibrated on the CSV logs, and the UBX reader gives more false alarms on two folders. Maritime trust-score bands are not calibrated</td><td><code>receiver_trust::calibration (run_dataset); receiver_trust::monitors (MonitorConfig::profile, JAMMERTEST_2024_CALIBRATED); realdata::jammertest; examples/jammertest_trust_calibration.rs</code></td></tr><tr><td>Jammertest scoring: a receiver log scored per scheduled test (detection latency, missed alarms, false alarms, time to recover) against the published test plan</td><td>jammertest::{plan, score, dataset} (kshana jammertest import|score): the NPRA test catalogue and transmission plan (MIT, fetched with a pinned SHA-256, never vendored) read as scheduled truth with the catalogue ids preserved; a receiver-trust run scored per test (first alarm minus planned start, detected/late/missed against a tolerance, time to the first alarm-free stretch, alarms outside every slot and its guard), with a signed evidence pack that carries the scorecard. The schedule is planned slots, not recorded on-air times</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>The plan's slot counts per test area (76, 77 and 45 slots, of which 61, 63 and 32 transmit) were counted independently with a script over the same JSON; the 2024 dataset's attack_log windows as event truth, under xval/jammertest-score/PREREGISTRATION.md (committed alone, c2e6d3b, before the scorer ran). First and only run (xval/jammertest-score/RESULTS.md): the registered minimum-data rule FAILED (4 scorable jamming windows from 4 of 12 UBX folders against a minimum of 10 windows from 2 folders: 5 folders dropped by the baseline rule, 3 UBX logs carry only GPS time of week), so bars B1 to B4 are not assessable; nothing was loosened or re-run. v2 (xval/jammertest-score/PREREGISTRATION-v2.md, committed alone, data handling only: GPS-time-only logs placed from the date, the 12 CSV-only folders, a moving-platform session for moving rovers; same bars and minimum-data rule): 14 of 24 folders kept but 7 scorable jamming windows, minimum-data rule failed again, no bar assessable (RESULTS-v2.md). Scheduled truth: a planned slot is not an on-air record, a miss may be a test that never ran, a latency is from the planned start, and clean means outside the planned slots. Detection by measurement-domain monitors says nothing about a low-power spoofer. One receiver type (u-blox ZED-F9P). The 2025 SimulaMet NMEA dataset is not used (no stated licence, subscription-gated)</td><td><code>jammertest::plan (parse_catalog, parse_plan, import, import_attack_log, events_toml); jammertest::score (score_run); jammertest::dataset (run_dataset); jammertest (score_log, score_pack); examples/jammertest_score_2024.rs</code></td></tr><tr><td>Test-bench export (kshana bench-export): Earth-fixed, geodetic and local-velocity columns, attitude columns and NMEA 0183 output</td><td>The motion CSV's Earth-fixed position, geodetic position, Earth-fixed velocity, heading/pitch/roll and UTC columns, and the NMEA GGA/RMC sentences, written from synthetic scenarios (gnss-ins including automotive-urban-canyon, jamming, gnss-sim). Motion and events only; no signal is written and no simulator or receiver performance is claimed.</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>WHAT IS EXTERNALLY CHECKED, stated narrowly: against pyproj 3.8.0 (PROJ 9.8.1), geographiclib 2.1, pynmea2 1.19.0 and scipy 1.18.1, run by scripts/gen_testbench_ref.py on 4 synthetic scenarios, with tolerances fixed before the first comparison (each the writer's rounding worst case): the Earth-fixed columns equal the WGS 84 conversion of the geodetic columns (worst 1.3e-4 m, bar 3e-4) and the reverse (1.3e-9 deg, bar 2e-9; height 1.3e-4 m, bar 3e-4) on every row; the Earth-fixed velocity rotated to north-east-down by PROJ's topocentric operation equals the engine's true NED velocity (7.5e-5 m/s, bar 2e-4); heading, pitch and roll equal scipy's yaw-pitch-roll of the engine's true quaternion (5.0e-7 deg, bar 1e-6); every NMEA sentence parses in pynmea2 with a valid checksum and its decoded position (1.2e-3 m, bar 5e-3), height, speed, course (5.0e-4 deg, bar 1e-3) and time equal the motion values; the UTC column equals independent date-time arithmetic. The SHA-256 of the exact CSV and NMEA bytes checked is pinned. WHAT IS NOT: the scenario's truth trajectory itself (the driving profile and its integration); the conventions (heading is the body yaw, not the course, and differs from it by up to about 77 degrees on automotive-urban-canyon; GGA quality, satellite count and HDOP are placeholders); whether any laboratory simulator reads the files as intended; any simulator or receiver performance. The oracles check conversions and encodings, not the physics of the scenario. Local-frame handling is checked through the velocity rotation and the NMEA speed and course only (the CSV has no local position column).</td><td><code>interop::testbench (write_motion_csv, write_nmea, trajectory_of, export); fusion::pack::truth_trajectory</code></td></tr><tr><td>Compliance mapping to public frameworks</td><td>compliance (kshana compliance-report): a mapping from Kshana outputs to clauses of public frameworks by number only, filled from the runs given; a result counts for a capability only when it carries the evidence the mapping names, an empty set evidences nothing, and the wording is &quot;supports evidence for&quot;; the report is not a finding that any framework is met and not a rating or approval by anyone.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>References checked against a script-extracted heading index of the cited public documents read 2026-10-10 (tests/compliance_references.rs). WHAT IS CHECKED: 49 cited clause, section, paragraph or heading references exist in the cited document, and 7 cited URLs resolved on the recorded date (2 via a second route); negative controls fail. WHAT IS NOT: that any paraphrase, mapping or gap is correct; EN 16803 clauses (paid, catalogue text only); later revisions; the index is not an independent implementation.</td><td><code>compliance</code></td></tr><tr><td>Marine integrations: Signal K plugin, OpenCPN gate stream and score panel, reference build</td><td>The trust score, band, reasons, gate state and receiver-reported position published under navigation.gnss.kshana in a Signal K server with a warn, alarm and normal notification (integrations/signalk); the gated NMEA stream, with the fix marked invalid while trust is collapsed and a PKSHT sentence per cycle, served to OpenCPN over TCP by the gate itself or by a small relay, and a native OpenCPN score panel that reads the PKSHT sentence (integrations/opencpn); and systemd units and a container that run the gate (deploy/reference-build). Where these show output they carry the advisory statement: Advisory software, not type-approved navigation equipment (IEC 61108, IEC 61162): the operator remains responsible for the navigation of the vessel.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>WHAT IS CHECKED, stated narrowly: that these integrations work against the real software they are written for. EVIDENCE is committed at integrations/signalk/evidence/README.md (with its JSON files) and integrations/opencpn/evidence/README.md (with its screenshots and logs), each with the script that reproduces it. (1) Signal K: the plugin loaded into the pinned signalk-server 2.33.0 npm package, in a private loopback-only network namespace, fed synthetic data. The server's own REST and WebSocket interfaces showed the published paths, the score metadata, and a notification that went absent, warn, alarm, normal (after the hold) and warn again when the data stopped. With the real kshana run by the plugin on the NMEA the server itself received, the notification went warn then alarm and the reported position was published; the server's own navigation.position was written by its NMEA provider, never by the plugin. Re-run against live JSON schema 1.2. (2) OpenCPN: the plugin built against the OpenCPN plugin API header and wxWidgets 3.2.4, loaded into the Ubuntu 24.04 package of OpenCPN 5.8.4 under a virtual display; OpenCPN loaded it, ran its initialisation and handed it the PKSHT sentences of the gated stream, and the panel turned red on the untrusted band. In one clean run, OpenCPN's own ship position stopped updating, and its fix indicator dropped, when the gate marked the fix invalid. A second run fed directly by the gate's TCP listener showed delivery to the plugin but not a clean before-and-after. (3) The relay tests replay a recorded excerpt of the gated stream through a real TCP socket and check what a consumer receives (the bytes unchanged, valid checksums on every sentence including the rewritten ones, GGA and RMC agreeing, one PKSHT per cycle with the gate field where the fix is invalid), and the end-to-end script checks that a consumer of the gate's listener receives exactly the bytes the same gate writes to standard output. (4) The PKSHT parsers (JavaScript and C++) are run over sentences recorded from kshana output and reject a wrong checksum, version or a score outside 0 to 100. (5) The systemd units are checked with systemd-analyze verify (systemd 255, binaries stubbed) and with guards for a shell in ExecStart, the restart policy and the sandbox keys; the container entrypoint's argument handling is checked with a stub binary. WHAT IS NOT: there is no external oracle for any value these integrations show. The trust values themselves are the rows above, and an integration run shows only that the plumbing carries them; that is why this row is MODELLED and not VALIDATED (docs/VALIDATION.md). No real vessel, receiver, long run, other version of either host, other operating system or desktop was used; all data are synthetic text and nothing here is a measurement. The notification's sound and visual handling by a real Signal K client, OpenCPN's toolbar button, preferences and stale state, and packaging for the OpenCPN plugin manager were not exercised. The systemd sandbox keys were checked for syntax only, not run under a live systemd, and the container image build and its health check have no committed evidence here. No detection or false-alarm figure is claimed. Advisory software, not type-approved navigation equipment (IEC 61108, IEC 61162): the operator remains responsible for the navigation of the vessel.</td><td><code>receiver_trust (live: the gate, its TCP listener, PKSHT); outside the crate: integrations/signalk (JavaScript), integrations/opencpn (JavaScript relay, C++ native plugin), deploy/reference-build (systemd units, container)</code></td></tr><tr><td>BOC(1,1) tracking: DLL scaling and bump-jump ambiguity resolution (opt-in)</td><td>The boc field of iq::track::LoopConfig (opt-in, None by default): the three DLL discriminators rescaled to read the code error in chips with unit slope on the sine BOC(1,1) main peak, and a bump-jump monitor on very-early and very-late correlators at one subcarrier half-period (0.5 chip) that jumps the code NCO towards the main peak when an up/down counter reaches N (8 by default). Without the option every output is unchanged.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Internal consistency against injected truth and closed forms, under bars pre-registered at 6b68f6cc (docs/design/evidence/boc-bump-jump/). A BeiDou B1C data signal (PRN 19, 5 MS/s) generated by iq::scene: with the option off a side-peak start stays false-locked at the predicted ±(0.5 + d/4) chip (measured ±0.549); with it on, one jump towards the main peak at 0.08 s and a late-window RMS of 0.0008 chip at 45 dB-Hz and 0.0048 chip at 35 dB-Hz; no jump from the main peak at 35, 40 and 45 dB-Hz over 4 s; the scaled EMLP slope 0.94 to 0.99 on the sampled signal (unscaled 3.6 to 3.8). The bump-jump detector alone, on correlator triples with the exact BOC(1,1) noise covariance, made no false jump in 100 000 updates at 25 to 45 dB-Hz and detected 1000 of 1000 side-peak starts at 35 dB-Hz. NOT MET (finding F1): the pre-registered unit-test grid for the scaling includes offsets outside the discriminators' linear range, where the scaled readings miss the offset by up to 48 %. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: field IQ, band-limited front ends, composite MBOC/QMBOC replicas, and any external receiver.</td><td><code>iq::track::boc (BocKind, BocTracking, BumpJump, boc11_dll_scale); iq::track::channel (the bump-jump taps and Channel::bump_jumps)</code></td></tr><tr><td>IQ ionospheric scintillation channel (Cornell model) against measured 50 Hz GPS L1 scintillation</td><td>iq::channel::scint, the Cornell scintillation model (Rice intensity, second-order Butterworth spectrum, phase arg z) driven at a measured window's S4 and intensity decorrelation time, compared on what it generates beyond those two inputs; realdata::scint50 reads the measured log, detrends it and runs the comparison.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Measured data under bars pre-registered at b6ccb499 (xval/scint-malindi/): Zenodo record 16941224 (INGV, Malindi, 18 March 2023, GPS PRN 26, 50 Hz intensity and carrier phase, CC BY 4.0, SHA-256 checked). 22 qualifying 60 s windows (N1 met). MET: the intensity distribution (Kolmogorov-Smirnov D 0.0415, bar 0.05) and the phase scintillation tied to S4 (median measured/model sigma-phi 0.779, bar 2/3 to 3/2). NOT MET (finding F1): the model's deep-fade fraction is 2.38 times the measured (bar 0.5 to 2) and its mean fade 3.51 times as long (bar 2/3 to 3/2). Finding F2: the registered processing reads the model's wrapped phase, which a self-check exposed; B3 repeated post hoc with the phase unwrapped gives 0.804. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: other nights, stations, latitudes or signals; receiver noise in the model. The row stays MODELLED.</td><td><code>iq::channel::scint (Scintillation, ScintParams); realdata::scint50 (parse_log, segments, measured_windows, model_windows, compare)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Cell-averaging CFAR option in IQ acquisition</td><td>iq::acq::ca_cfar and ca_cfar_cells (opt-in): a cell of an acquisition grid is compared with the mean of reference cells of its Doppler row (guard and stride lags, wrapping round the period), at a factor alpha set from the closed-form CA-CFAR false-alarm probability for M non-coherent blocks (ca_cfar_pfa, ca_cfar_alpha). The sample-power detector stays the default and every existing output is unchanged.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Closed form, under bars pre-registered at 3724aefa (docs/design/evidence/acq-cfar/). The CA-CFAR false-alarm probability for Gamma(M) cells, against Monte Carlo of the exact model: 18 of 18 cases within 3 binomial sigma. On every cell of iq::acq searches (GPS L1 C/A, 2.5 MS/s, 210 000 cells each) at a design 1 %: 0.977 % and 0.969 % on Gaussian noise and 1.006 % and 0.999 % on 2-bit quantised noise (M = 1 and 4; bar plus or minus 20 %); decisions unchanged when the input is scaled. NOT MET (finding F1): on a noise-free 2-bit signal the detection and its cell are right, but 1.28 % of the off-peak cells of the peak's row (bar 1 %) exceed the threshold, at the code's deterministic correlation sidelobes. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: other CFAR variants, two-dimensional windows, band-limited front ends, field data.</td><td><code>iq::acq (CfarConfig, ca_cfar, ca_cfar_cells, ca_cfar_pfa, ca_cfar_alpha)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Spectrum model: GLONASS, BeiDou and Galileo E6 bands (opt-in)</td><td>spectrum::default_band resolves, by name only (MORE_BAND_NAMES), GLONASS L1OF and L2OF on every FDMA channel K = -7 to 6 (BPSK, 0.511 Mchip/s), BeiDou B1I (BPSK(2)), B1C (MBOC(6,1,1/11)), B2a and B3I (BPSK(10)) and Galileo E6 (BPSK(5)), with the carriers and minimum received powers of their interface control documents; the default band set and every existing output are unchanged.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Published interface control documents and closed forms, under bars pre-registered at 5806f28b (docs/design/evidence/spectrum-bands/): GLONASS ICD Edition 5.1, BDS-SIS-ICD B1I 3.0, B1C 1.0, B2a 1.0 and B3I 1.0, Galileo OS SIS ICD Issue 2.1. MET: every carrier, chip rate and received power as transcribed (28 GLONASS channels), BPSK first nulls at the chip rate, the B1C composite equal to MBOC(6,1,1/11) as its ICD's power table gives, the C/N0 path identity, and byte-identical spectrum scenarios. NOT MET: the unit-area bar, which was unattainable as written (finding F1; the areas equal the closed-form in-band fraction), and the per-bin agreement of the IQ layer's B1I and B1C waveforms with the model envelope (1.26 and 1.56 dB against 0.5 dB, finding F2; GLONASS 0.07 dB), because Gold and Weil codes have uneven line spectra; band-averaged the main lobes agree within 0.25 dB. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: recorded IQ, other signals, intra-system interference.</td><td><code>spectrum (default_band, MORE_BAND_NAMES, GLONASS_CHANNELS)</code></td></tr><tr><td>BeiDou B1I primary ranging codes against an independent generator</td><td>iq::signals::beidou::b1i_bits and b1i: the BDS-SIS-ICD-B1I-3.0 Gold codes truncated to 2046 chips, PRN 1 to 63, as the IQ layer generates, acquires and tracks them.</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>GNSS-SDR v0.0.19 (tag v0.0.19, commit 1ddd4562), an independent open-source implementation of the same ICD: its beidou_b1i_code_gen_int, compiled standalone, generated all 63 codes. Only its program output is committed: tests/fixtures/iq_signals/beidou_b1i_gnss_sdr.txt, a 64-chip prefix and the SHA-256 of each 2046-chip code. That output is the BeiDou B1I PRN codes the public BDS-SIS-ICD-B1I-3.0 defines; no GNSS-SDR source or binary is in the repository. Bar pre-registered at 911106d1 (xval/beidou-b1i-gnss-sdr/): all 2046 chips of all 63 PRNs identical, no inversion, shift or relabelling. Result: 63 of 63 identical; the fixture was regenerated from a fresh v0.0.19 checkout (source file SHA-256 as recorded) and is byte-identical. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: the NH20 secondary code, the GEO PRN list, the carrier and chip rate.</td><td><code>iq::signals::beidou (b1i_bits, b1i)</code></td></tr><tr><td>GPS L1C ranging and overlay codes against the IS-GPS-800J printed check columns</td><td>iq::signals::gps::l1c_p_bits, l1c_d_bits and l1c_o_bits: the L1CP and L1CD Weil ranging codes (computed Legendre sequence, Weil index, expansion sequence inserted before chip p) and the L1CO S1 overlay codes, PRN 1 to 63.</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>IS-GPS-800J (01-AUG-2022, distribution statement A), the check columns it prints beside the generator parameters: Table 3.2-2 initial and final 24 chips of every L1CP and L1CD code, Table 3.2-3 initial and final 11 bits of every L1CO code, Table 6.2-1 the length-10223 Legendre sequence; transcribed by scripts/extract_is_gps_800_tables.py from the PDF (SHA-256 c4aad710...). Bars pre-registered at fe372e26 (docs/design/evidence/l1c-codes/): 252 of 252 columns, 63 of 63 overlays and 10 223 of 10 223 Legendre bits identical; the extraction --check passes; a negative control fails relabelled, inverted and shifted codes. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: the generator parameters themselves (they come from the same tables), the chips between the printed columns, the TMBOC subcarrier pattern and the L1CO S2 codes.</td><td><code>iq::signals::gps (l1c_weil, l1c_ranging_bits, l1c_p_bits, l1c_d_bits, l1co_s1_run, l1c_o_bits); iq::signals::tables</code></td></tr><tr><td>LunaNet AFS-I and AFS-Q primary codes against the LSIS-AFS V1.0 printed check columns</td><td>iq::signals::lunanet::afs_i_bits and afs_q_primary_bits (signal names lunanet-afs-i and lunanet-afs-q in kshana iq): the AFS-I Gold code (2047 short-cycled to 2046, G2 delayed per PRN) and the AFS-Q Weil primary code (computed Legendre sequence, Weil index, expansion sequence inserted before chip p), PRN 1 to 210.</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Against LSIS-AFS V1.0 printed tables (marked TBC in the spec): LunaNet Interoperability Specification V5, Applicable Document 1 Volume A: LunaNet Signal-In-Space Recommended Standard, Augmented Forward Signal (LSIS-AFS) V1.0, 29 January 2025 (NASA; PDF SHA-256 986e0795...), the check columns it prints beside the generator parameters: Tables C-1 to C-5 and D-2 to D-6 first and last 24 chips of every AFS-I and AFS-Q code, Table D-1 the length-10223 Legendre sequence; extracted by scripts/extract_lunanet_afs_tables.py (xval/lunanet-afs/oracle/). Bars pre-registered at b3ba5d5 (xval/lunanet-afs/PREREGISTRATION.md) before the generator existed: B1 420 of 420, B2 identical, B3 420 of 420. The document marks its code tables {LSIS-TBC} and says they are for test and integration only, so this validates the construction against that edition's tables, not a later one's. A pre-registered claim about the printed G2 initial state (B1b) was stated with the wrong direction and is NOT MET (0 of 210), pinned; the corrected relation (2047 minus the delay) holds 210 of 210 and is recorded as post hoc. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: the generator parameters themselves (same tables), the chips between the printed columns, the tertiary code, the message layer and the LDPC coding.</td><td><code>iq::signals::lunanet (afs_i_bits, afs_q_primary_bits, legendre_bits); iq::signals::lunanet_tables; iq::lunanet_afs::check_spec</code></td></tr><tr><td>LunaNet AFS tertiary codes and message layer (SB1 BCH, CRC-24Q, interleaver) against LSIS-AFS V1.0</td><td>iq::signals::lunanet::afs_q_tertiary_bits, afs_q_tiered; iq::lunanet_afs: synchronisation pattern, SB1 BCH(51,8) encoder and 256-hypothesis decoder, CRC-24Q, the 60 x 98 block interleaver, a symbol-stream checker (kshana lunanet-afs-check) and a test-stream generator with placeholder subframes. The rate-1/2 LDPC coding of subframes 2 to 4 is not implemented (its matrices are in a separate annex).</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>LSIS-AFS V1.0 (as above): the length-1499 Legendre sequence (Table E-1, identical) and the tertiary first and last 24 chips (Tables E-2 to E-6: 419 of 420 columns identical; the document prints the same last 24 chips, A14850, for PRN 147 and PRN 151 and the generator reproduces PRN 151's, so the PRN 147 entry is most likely a misprint; the bar of 420 of 420 is NOT MET and the miss is pinned); the Figure 8 worked example of the SB1 code (one example: 0x045 encodes to 0x229F61DBB84A0); CRC-24Q against the catalogue check value 0xCDE703 for ASCII 123456789; the document's own frame arithmetic. Bars pre-registered at b3ba5d5. One miss on record and one worked example for the message layer, so the row stays MODELLED. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: LDPC and the CRC words inside subframes 2 to 4, message content, and any recorded stream</td><td><code>iq::lunanet_afs; iq::signals::lunanet (afs_q_tertiary_bits, afs_q_overlay_bits, afs_q_tiered); surface (lunanet_afs_check_spec, lunanet_afs_check_symbols, lunanet_afs_generate)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Standards packs: scenarios scored against bars stated before the run</td><td>standards_pack (kshana standards-pack): the DHS Resilient PNT framework test profiles for levels 1 to 4, IMO MSC.1/Circ.1575 accuracy levels and integrity evaluation, and ICAO Doc 9849 Appendix F monitor, assess and mitigate packs: each test runs a bundled scenario kind and compares result fields with bars stated in the pack, and tests that need a receiver log are listed as NEEDS INPUT and not scored.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>None external for the scenario results: every pack test runs a MODELLED scenario kind. The bars are the pack author's stated profile (the DHS framework states no values; the IMO accuracy limits are Table C-1 of MSC.1/Circ.1575, read in full; the ICAO pack follows ICAO's own RFI Mitigation Plan presentation because Doc 9849 is a paid publication and was not read), committed before any scored run in xval-style PREREGISTRATION.md files under standards/. A MET bar says a modelled scenario met a stated bar, nothing about a product</td><td><code>standards_pack; compliance (standards-pack facts in compliance-report)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory round-trip evidence pack (scenario, test conditions, receiver log, score)</td><td>evidence::roundtrip (kshana evidence round-trip) and the kshana.test-conditions/1 receiver-log export of kshana bench-export: a signed pack that records the scenario hash, the stated conditions' hash, the log hash and the score against the stated events, and refuses conditions that disagree with the scoring events or a log whose hash is not the stated one. No signal is written, replayed or scheduled.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>None external: the pack links hashes and a MODELLED receiver-log score, checked on synthetic data (the exported NMEA of a bundled scenario treated as the receiver's log). No laboratory run is claimed</td><td><code>evidence::roundtrip, evidence::assemble, interop::testbench (write_conditions_json), iq::campaign::conditions</code></td></tr><tr><td>IQ tracking engine against GNSS-SDR 0.0.19 on independent recordings</td><td>The full IQ-layer tracking engine (iq::track through kshana iq track: acquisition hand-off, correlators, NCOs, second-order 15 Hz Costas PLL, first-order 2 Hz carrier-aided EML-envelope DLL, lock indicator and C/N0 estimators) on GPS L1 C/A at 30 to 45 dB-Hz.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>GNSS-SDR 0.0.19 (GPL-3.0-or-later, the Ubuntu 24.04 package, run as a separate program; only derived numbers committed) tracking the same numpy recordings (independent of the engine), both measured against the analytic truth. Bars pre-registered at 48edd4a5, amended before any test recording at 1e4288c4 (a 10 Hz FLL during pull-in only in both receivers, after the registered calibration failed on a wrong GNSS-SDR hand-off, finding F3). MET at 30, 35, 40 and 45 dB-Hz: carrier jitter within 4.3 %, code jitter within 4.6 % and Doppler mean and spread of GNSS-SDR's. NOT MET: the engine's NWPR C/N0 reads 2.3 to 7.1 dB below GNSS-SDR's SNV (finding F4, the known NWPR limit; its M2M4 is within 0.06 dB of truth); the phase lock indicators differ by definition (F5); at 30 dB-Hz GNSS-SDR slipped twice and the engine not (F6). GNSS-SDR's own hand-off locked to a wrong frequency in 3 of 30 calibration runs. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: other signals, data-bit wipe-off, re-acquisition, field recordings. The row stays MODELLED.</td><td><code>iq::track (Channel, LoopCore, design); iq::cli::track</code></td></tr><tr><td>CRPA antenna gains in the jamming kind against an independent numpy recomputation</td><td>The jamming kind's optional [crpa] section: a per-channel beam toward each satellite (null-steering with the jammer as the null, or MVDR on sigma^2 I + P_j a_j a_j^H), scaling each link's noise by ||w||^2 and the jammer by |w^H a_j|^2 before the existing anti-jam C/N0 equation.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>An independent numpy recomputation (xval/crpa-jamming/oracle.py, no shared code: numpy steering vectors, numpy.linalg.solve and inv) of every link of the jamming-demo geometry with a 4-element square array, under both weightings. Bars pre-registered at 08d83b4d. MET: A1 (all 150 scenario results byte-identical without the section, after a fix: the first build added the crpa units to every jamming run's units table), A2 (one element equals no array to 1e-12 dB), A3 on the array noise gain and effective C/N0 (912 link-weightings within 1e-9 dB, worst 2.1e-14 dB) and on the null-steering jammer null (below -200 dB on both sides), A4 (MVDR within 0.1 dB of null-steering at a JNR of 80 dB). NOT MET: A3 on the MVDR jammer gain, 194 of 456 links over 1e-9 dB on 0.36 portable maths (202 on host maths; docs/revisions/STUDIO15-batch4-rebaseline.md) (worst 1.7e-6 dB) on residuals of -100 to -188 dB, where float64 cancellation limits both implementations (numpy's inv and solve disagree with each other on 169 links, worst 7.9e-7 dB); the effective C/N0 these feed is unaffected. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: mutual coupling, element mismatch, wideband processing, multiple jammers, any antenna hardware. A recomputation of the same equations, not an external oracle; the row stays MODELLED.</td><td><code>jamming (CrpaCfg, crpa_weights, run_jamming); crpa (null_steering_weights, mvdr_weights, covariance)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Terrain shadowing of the jammer in the jamming kind against an independent numpy recomputation on a real SRTM tile</td><td>The jamming kind's optional [terrain] section: the great-circle terrain profile between jammer and receiver on an SRTM .hgt tile with a k-factor Earth bulge, the dominant knife edge (largest Fresnel-Kirchhoff nu) and the single knife-edge loss of ITU-R P.526-15 section 4.1, subtracted from the jammer's received power.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>An independent numpy recomputation (xval/jamming-terrain/oracle.py, no shared code: its own .hgt read, bilinear interpolation, WGS 84 round trip, profile, bulge, nu and J(nu)) of ten jammer placements around a receiver at Badwater Basin on the committed real SRTM tile tests/fixtures/terrain/N36W117_sub6.hgt. Bars and placements pre-registered at 92cd2bfd. All MET: additivity (all 150 scenario results byte-identical without the section); every placement's profile points and obstacle equal and nu and loss within 1.6e-10 (bar 1e-9); the loss never rises as the jammer rises from 2 to 4902 m; the loss lowers every J/S by exactly itself and nothing else; J(nu) within 1e-12 dB of the oracle's. Findings: placements across the Black and Funeral Mountains lose 44 to 48 dB; two valley-floor placements lose 8 and 15 dB from the Earth bulge alone at 2 m antenna heights, the single knife-edge model's grazing case. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: any measured propagation, multiple-edge diffraction, ground reflection, clutter, the DEM's own error. A recomputation of the same model, not an external oracle; the row stays MODELLED.</td><td><code>jamming_terrain (TerrainCfg, jammer_terrain, knife_edge_loss_db); jamming (run_jamming_sky)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Acquisition hysteresis and the front-end quantiser in the jamming kind against independent recomputations</td><td>The jamming kind's optional [acquisition] section (a satellite is acquired at or above an acquisition threshold after a re-acquisition delay and held while at or above the tracking threshold) and [front_end] section (the AGC reading 10 log10(1 + J/N) and the ADC's Gaussian-input quantiser loss taken off every link, flagged as not valid for non-Gaussian jammers).</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>An independent Python recomputation of every acquisition flag (xval/jamming-acq-agc/hysteresis.py) from the engine's per-epoch effective C/N0, an independent numpy Monte Carlo of the quantiser loss (xval/jamming-acq-agc/quantiser_mc.py, its own optimum-step search, 2^22 samples), and the IQ layer's AGC loop. Bars pre-registered at 70c72fcc, with a supplementary run set at 37793290. MET: additivity (all 150 scenario results byte-identical without the sections); every flag equal on the supplementary runs (1824 links, 732 of them showing the hysteresis); monotone in the threshold; the quantiser loss within 0.0007 dB of the Monte Carlo at 1 to 4 bits (bar 0.02 dB); the AGC reading within 0.012 dB of the AGC loop (bar 0.05 dB). NOT MET: the registered hysteresis runs (jamming-demo at 10 to 30 dBW) were uninformative, with every link lost, and their H2 and H4 are met only trivially. Finding: with a CW jammer a 1-bit ADC loses about 5 dB more than the Gaussian figure the engine applies, and flags as not valid. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: the acquisition search itself, AGC dynamics in time, any receiver hardware. Recomputations of the same model, not an external oracle; the row stays MODELLED.</td><td><code>jamming_rx (AcquisitionCfg, AcqState, FrontEndCfg, front_end); jamming (run_jamming_sky); iq::frontend::quant (optimum_step_sigma)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Capped FLL pull-in below the default design's FLL-to-PLL hand-over floor (opt-in)</td><td>iq::track's opt-in carrier.fll_pull_in_max_s (the fll_pull_in_max_s field of iq::track::LoopConfig): under fll_assist = pull-in, the FLL hands over to the PLL after a fixed pull-in time whatever the PLI, with a matching return dwell until the PLI first reaches fll_off_pli.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Internal consistency against injected truth: a synthetic data-free GPS L1 C/A source (PRN 13, 1500 Hz, 4.1 MHz, 8 s, ten noise seeds) whose carrier phase is known, under bars pre-registered at 58789af0 (docs/design/evidence/fll-handover-floor/). MET: the control (the uncapped default never hands over in 9 of 10 seeds at 33 dB-Hz); additivity (default hash and the existing tracking tests unchanged); pull-in of a 100 Hz-off hand-off at 45 dB-Hz. NOT MET: with a 1.0 s cap, hand-over and hold in every run (16 of 20 at 33 dB-Hz, 8 of 20 at 31 dB-Hz; the rest cycle between forced hand-overs and the FLL's return) and phase jitter within 25 % of the PLL alone (10.8 against 6.1 degrees at 33 dB-Hz). WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: data bits, other signals and sample rates, field recordings. The default is unchanged; the row stays MODELLED.</td><td><code>iq::track (LoopConfig, FllGate, design); iq::track::channel (the FLL gate)</code></td></tr><tr><td>RTKLIB .pos solution reader for receiver-trust</td><td>Reads an RTKLIB solution file (calendar or GPS week/TOW time, latitude/longitude or ECEF position, satellites used) into the receiver-trust timeline (receiver_trust::machines::rtklib).</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>None external: no RTKLIB-produced file was read, and the column layout is from the RTKLIB manual. Checked on self-written files only; the ECEF conversion reuses frames::ecef_to_geodetic, which has its own rows</td><td><code>receiver_trust::machines::rtklib (read_rtklib_pos); receiver_trust::LogFormat::RtklibPos</code></td></tr><tr><td>NovAtel OEM7 ASCII and binary logs (BESTPOS, RANGE, RXSTATUS) for receiver-trust</td><td>Reads BESTPOS (position), RANGE (C/No per signal) and RXSTATUS (the receiver's jammer flag, as the epoch's self-report) from ASCII and binary OEM7 logs, CRC-checked, read-only (receiver_trust::machines::novatel).</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>None external as a pass bar. The field layouts and CRC follow the public OEM7 documentation (docs.novatel.com); the CRC routine was checked once against the CRC the documentation states for its own BESTPOSA example (cited, not quoted). Fixtures are self-written; no log from a real receiver was read</td><td><code>receiver_trust::machines::novatel (read_novatel, novatel_crc32); receiver_trust::LogFormat::Novatel</code></td></tr><tr><td>RTCM 3 MSM4 and MSM7 carrier-to-noise reader for receiver-trust</td><td>Reads the C/N0 of every signal cell of RTCM 3 MSM4 and MSM7 messages (GPS, GLONASS, Galileo, SBAS, QZSS, BeiDou, NavIC) from a frame stream, CRC-24Q checked (receiver_trust::machines::rtcm3). Layouts from the BSD-licensed pyrtcm tables, not from the RTCM standard text.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>ReferenceImpl in a one-off check, not a test in the suite: pyrtcm 1.2.0's decoder reads the same self-written frames to the same C/N0 and epoch time. It shares its tables' source with this reader, so it checks the bit handling, not the layout. No capture from a real receiver or base station was read</td><td><code>receiver_trust::machines::rtcm3 (read_rtcm3, crc24q); receiver_trust::LogFormat::Rtcm3</code></td></tr><tr><td>GNSS-SDR monitor adapter for receiver-trust</td><td>Reads GNSS-SDR Monitor (Gnss_Synchro) and PVT-monitor messages, relayed to JSON lines with the proto field names, into per-channel C/N0, fix, satellites used, HDOP and sky (receiver_trust::machines::gnss_sdr; integrations/gnss-sdr/relay.py).</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>None external: field names come from the GNSS-SDR documentation; neither the reader nor relay.py was run against a live GNSS-SDR or a real capture</td><td><code>receiver_trust::machines::gnss_sdr (read_gnss_sdr_monitor); receiver_trust::LogFormat::GnssSdr</code></td></tr><tr><td>IMU log ingest, gyro heading input and Allan fit of hybrid sensor parameters</td><td>Reads an IMU CSV (documented columns, or the EuRoC layout); integrates the yaw rate into the heading the heading-course monitor reads ([log.imu]); and fits the accelerometer and gyro noise parameters of the hybrid and fusion scenarios from a stationary log by overlapping Allan deviation (imu-fit).</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>None external: the fit is checked on synthetic white-noise logs with known coefficients, the heading on constant yaw-rate logs. No real IMU log was fitted, and q_aa is not fitted</td><td><code>receiver_trust::machines::imu, imu_fit; surface::imu_fit; scenario [log.imu]</code></td></tr><tr><td>Home Assistant MQTT discovery and state exporter for trust telemetry</td><td>A JSON state document and Home Assistant MQTT discovery messages for the trust stream, printed as text on every surface and published over MQTT 3.1.1 QoS 0 by the CLI (telemetry::mqtt; trust-telemetry --mqtt-tcp, --print-mqtt, format mqtt).</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>None external: the packets were read by a loopback listener written for the test, not by a real broker, and Home Assistant was not run</td><td><code>telemetry::mqtt (discovery, state_json, connect_packet, publish_packet, MqttSink); surface::TelemetryFormat::Mqtt</code></td></tr><tr><td>ROS 2 receiver-trust package and gnss_lib_py bridge</td><td>A ROS-free bridge that writes fix and satellite messages as NMEA and scores them through the Python binding (integrations/ros2/kshana_trust), and a gnss_lib_py NavData to receiver-trust bridge through the rtklib-pos format (integrations/gnss_lib_py).</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>None external: the ROS node was not run on a live ROS 2 graph, and the ublox_ubx_msgs field names were not checked against a running driver. gnss_lib_py (MIT) was run for the bridge test</td><td><code>integrations/ros2/kshana_trust (bridge.py, trust_node.py); integrations/gnss_lib_py/kshana_gnss_lib_py.py</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
+<div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Capability</th><th>Status</th><th>Oracle</th><th>Module</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Power-law noise identification by lag-1 autocorrelation</td><td>allan::lag1_noise_id: the Riley and Greenhall (2004) lag-1 autocorrelation noise identifier (decimated phase with its quadratic removed, or group-averaged frequency with its line removed; repeated first differences until delta = r1/(1+r1) &lt; 0.25 or d = dmax), returning the integer and unrounded power-law exponent alpha (+2 white PM to -2 random-walk FM) at any averaging factor; it sets the noise-type degrees of freedom of the measured clock library's device cards</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>allantools 2024.6 autocorr_noise_id (A. Wallin and contributors, LGPL-3.0-or-later, run as a separate program by the fixture generator): an independent implementation of the published Riley-Greenhall algorithm, a uniquely defined quantity under the stated conventions. Validates the identifier, not the noise content of any real clock</td><td><code>allan (lag1_noise_id, lag1_acf)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Galileo OSNMA receiver-side navigation-data authentication</td><td>osnma + <code>kshana osnma verify</code>: an open, pure-Rust receiver-side verifier for Galileo OSNMA (SIS ICD Issue 1.1, Receiver Guidelines Issue 1.3) from I/NAV pages or a u-blox UBX-RXM-SFRBX stream: Merkle-tree public keys (DSM-PKR), the ECDSA P-256/P-521 signature of the DSM-KROOT, TESLA key-chain verification (SHA-256 or SHA3-256), MACSEQ and tag verification (HMAC-SHA-256 or CMAC-AES) for ADKD 0, 4 and 12 with cross-authentication, chain end/revocation/renewal, public-key renewal and revocation, and the alert message; per-satellite status Authenticated/Failed/Unavailable with a reason for every failure or delay. Advisory: not type-approved navigation equipment and not a navigation integrity service; no anti-spoofing performance is claimed</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>The official Galileo OSNMA test vectors published by the European GNSS Service Centre as an annex to the Receiver Guidelines (Test_vectors.zip, sha256 ef9b9afc6ef9e1c57393415cf2a1dc80c4035e7c06123907ea0bb97dd3ccf370): 18 of 18 sets, 186,382 tags authenticated, 0 failed, run offline on 2026-10-10 (evidence record in kshana-pro docs/evidence). Scope: the vectors validate the cryptographic verification (Merkle, KROOT/ECDSA, TESLA chain, MACK tags, ADKD 0/4/12) and its outcomes on the published scenarios; they use only HMAC-SHA-256, a SHA-256 chain, 128-bit keys, 40-bit tags and look-up entries 33 and 34, so CMAC-AES, SHA3-256 chains and other parameters are covered by published primitive known-answer tests and synthetic tests only. The trusted-time, data-binding, expiry and header-confirmation hardening is tested by the synthetic attack tests, not by an external oracle. Not checked: certificate revocation lists, live signals or a live receiver (the UBX word layout is checked on synthetic frames only), SHA3-256 Merkle trees, freshness without an independent time. Not an EU service</td><td><code>osnma (page, subframe, dsm, merkle, signature, tesla, mac, maclt, navdata, verifier, ubx, cli)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Frequency stability characterisation</td><td>Allan/modified/Hadamard deviation + power-law noise ID with χ² CIs</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>NIST SP 1065 (Riley) / Stable32 reference deviations on NBS14</td><td><code>allan</code></td></tr><tr><td>Frequency stability on a real measured clock</td><td>Overlapping Allan + overlapping Hadamard deviation on a real caesium standard</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Stable32 overlapping ADEV/HDEV on the measured 5071A caesium phase series (allantools)</td><td><code>allan</code></td></tr><tr><td>Allan estimator parity on the canonical Stable32 reference series</td><td>Overlapping Allan + modified Allan + time deviation across the full AF ladder</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Stable32 reference deviations for PHASE.DAT (Riley; the standard regression series)</td><td><code>allan</code></td></tr><tr><td>Extended-range frequency stability (Theo1 / TOTDEV)</td><td>Theo1 and total deviation (TOTVAR) — extended-range long-tau stability estimators reaching ~75% of the record where the Allan deviation gives out near ~50%; the bias-removed ThêoH hybrid (allan::theoh_curve) built on them stays MODELLED</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>allantools 2024.06 — an independent third-party frequency-stability library — theo1 (NIST SP 1065 eq 30) and totdev (NIST SP 1065 eq 25) on the hermetic NIST SP 1065 §12.4 LCG data set; regenerable offline via tests/fixtures/theo1_totvar/generate_theo1_totvar_reference.py</td><td><code>allan</code></td></tr><tr><td>Maximum Time Interval Error (MTIE) — telecom wander metric</td><td>MTIE(τ): the maximum peak-to-peak time-error swing over any sliding window of τ = m·tau0, the ITU-T G.810/G.823/G.8261 wander statistic synchronisation-network limits (MTIE masks) are written against — an extremal (max/min) figure distinct from the RMS Allan family</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>allantools 2024.06 — an independent third-party frequency-stability library — mtie() on the hermetic NIST SP 1065 §12.4 LCG phase series; MTIE is a pure max/min statistic, so the estimator output is bit-exact against allantools on the identically-built phase array (the committed 15-significant-figure reference constants sit within 1 ULP). Regenerable offline via tests/fixtures/mtie/generate_mtie_reference.py</td><td><code>allan (mtie, mtie_curve)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Modified Allan / Time deviation (MDEV / TDEV)</td><td>MDEV(τ): the overlapping modified Allan deviation (second-difference sliding-window estimator that separates white- from flicker-phase noise), and TDEV(τ) = τ/√3·MDEV(τ), the ITU-T G.811/G.812/G.823 time-domain wander statistic the sync masks are written against</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>allantools 2024.06 — an independent third-party frequency-stability library — mdev() and tdev() on the hermetic NIST SP 1065 §12.4 LCG phase series (same series as the Theo1/TOTDEV/MTIE rows), computing the same uniquely-defined estimators; matched to &lt;1e-9 relative. Regenerable offline via tests/fixtures/mdev_tdev/generate_mdev_tdev_reference.py. (The data-gated phasedat_reference.rs also checks these against Stable32 to 1e-3; this is the tight, always-on hermetic cross-check)</td><td><code>allan (modified_adev, time_deviation)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Optical-clock frequency stability on a real measured curve</td><td>Power-law (white-FM + red-noise-floor) NNLS recovery from a published measured ⁸⁸Sr optical-clock-transition Allan deviation — reproducing σ_y(τ) and the headline 4.7e-16/√τ short-τ scaling with a genuine measured long-τ floor, rather than the synthesised optical-class floor holdover.rs otherwise assumes</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Norcia, Young, Eckner, Oelker, Ye, Kaufman, Science 366:93 (2019), Fig. 4 measured ADEV; curve vendored verbatim from Zenodo 10.5281/zenodo.3382347 (CC-BY-4.0). Scoped to reproducing the published measured stability curve — the clock-class holdover-to-threshold device figures stay MODELLED</td><td><code>quantum_trade (qparams_from_adev_curve), powerlaw</code></td></tr><tr><td>Integrity (RAIM/ARAIM/SBAS)</td><td>ARAIM multiple-hypothesis solution separation per the ARAIM Airborne Design Document (ADD) v4.2 (araim_reference::add_v42_protection_levels and add_v42_protection_levels_ecef: subset determination by the P_THRES and FC_THRES rules, VPL, HPL, the effective monitor threshold EMT and sigma_acc); chi-squared snapshot RAIM fault detection with single-satellite exclusion (raim::snapshot_raim, raim::snapshot_raim_fde) and slope-based HPL/VPL on the geodetic local level; and the SBAS DO-229E protection-level combination (sbas::sbas_protection_level) on the L1 service. NOT covered, stated plainly: the uniform-sigma convenience functions raim::araim_raim and raim::araim_dual_raim are NOT ADD-conformant (on matched inputs they miss Stanford MAAST by 0.75 m VPL / 4.04 m HPL and 0.39 m VPL / 1.93 m HPL, a finding); the dual-frequency multi-constellation (DFMC) L5 SBAS comparison is a finding (308 user-epoch pairs with no GEO in view are protected by Kshana and not by MAAST, whose GEO-reception gate Kshana does not model); raim::solution_separation_raim has no external comparison</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Three independent tools, pre-registered (ba7a01a7) before any fixture or oracle output existed, tolerances fixed then. (1) ARAIM: Stanford MAAST for ARAIM 2 (commit ab70e2a3, BSD-3) mhss_raim_baseline_v5.m, run as a separate program under GNU Octave 8.4 with a driver-side unique() compatibility shim (disclosed, no algorithm change), at the ADD TOL_PL of 0.05 m: the Rust reproduces the ADD reference implementation on 270 real cases. Disclosed: the Kshana ADD path was written after the pre-registration, following the ADD as MAAST implements it; an independent hold-out by the reviewer (270 new user geometries and a second integrity support message, MAAST re-run against the frozen code) passed at the same bar. (2) Snapshot RAIM: RTKLIB v2.4.2-p13 (BSD-2) estpos/valsol/raim_fde in a separate C harness for detection and exclusion; the slope PL from RTKLIB matinv/xyz2enu plus SciPy 1.18.1 chi2/ncx2, where both sides evaluate the same closed form (Brown; Parkinson and Axelrad), so the PL check covers the implementation, not the formula. (3) SBAS: Stanford MAAST (commit 7d32b049) usr_vhpl, unmodified, on MAAST own recorded WAAS broadcasts: L1 levels within 1e-4 m after the K rescaling and an equal protected set. The same comparisons produced the findings stated in the capability: the uniform-sigma ARAIM functions are not ADD-conformant, and the L5 set equality fails on 308 pairs with no GEO in view (the levels on the 3267 pairs both tools protect agree to 1e-13 m). The DO-229E/DO-316 K-factors are transcribed constants and the real geometry is an input; neither is counted as an oracle. 0.30 revision: snapshot_raim protection levels moved from the radial to the geodetic local level (up to 0.31 m HPL and 0.14 m VPL at 16 deg N)</td><td><code>araim_reference (add_v42_protection_levels, add_v42_protection_levels_ecef); raim (snapshot_raim, snapshot_raim_fde, araim_raim, araim_dual_raim, solution_separation_raim); sbas (sbas_protection_level); lunar</code></td></tr><tr><td>Orbit propagation &amp; determination</td><td>SGP4/SDP4, Cowell 6-DOF + perturbations, batch/sequential OD</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>AIAA 2006-6753 SGP4 verification vectors</td><td><code>sgp4, propagator, orbit_determination, precise_od</code></td></tr><tr><td>Numerical Cowell propagator &amp; force model</td><td>Cowell numerical propagator with a hierarchical force model (two-body, J2–J6 zonal, Sun/Moon third-body, cannonball SRP, exponential drag); RK4 step-doubling / DP5(4) / DOP853</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Orekit 12.2 (CS GROUP, Apache-2.0) NumericalPropagator/DormandPrince853 — an independent library, a different integrator and spherical-harmonic recursion. The conservative tiers (two-body → J2–J6 zonal → Sun/Moon third-body → cannonball SRP) agree to sub-metre over a 24 h arc, validating the integrator + force algebra; the drag tier and the absolute Sun/Moon-ephemeris / density input fidelity stay MODELLED (characterisation only)</td><td><code>propagator, forces</code></td></tr><tr><td>Oblate-Earth SRP shadow (opt-in)</td><td>SRP lit fraction with the WGS-84 ellipsoid as the occulting body (apparent Earth radius from the ellipsoid limb towards the Sun, dual-disk lens formula; srp_shadow::oblate_conical_shadow), opt-in on the Cowell force model through propagator::ForceModel::srp_shadow (default: the spherical conical shadow, unchanged)</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Orekit 12.2 (Apache-2.0) SolarRadiationPressure.getLightingRatio with a WGS-84 and a spherical OneAxisEllipsoid occulting body, run as a separate program with the Sun fixed at the same inertial position. Bars pre-registered in xval/srp-oblate-shadow-orekit/PREREGISTRATION.md (c6b751e9): 1e-6 on both, and a &gt; 1e-2 oblate-vs-sphere spread; all met. The ellipsoid axis is the inertial z axis on both sides (no precession-nutation)</td><td><code>srp_shadow, forces (srp_accel_with_lit_fraction), propagator</code></td></tr><tr><td>Box-wing and ECOM2 solar radiation pressure, with partials (opt-in)</td><td>Non-cannonball SRP: a box-wing flat-plate model (Vallado eq. 8-44; single-sided body panels, sun-pointing wings) with the Galileo FOC, IOV and IOV end-of-life tables of the GSC satellite metadata, in nominal GNSS yaw steering, with analytic partials for the radiation factor and every panel's specular and diffuse coefficients; and the ECOM2 empirical model (D2/B1) with its partials. The box-wing is opt-in on the Cowell propagator (propagator::ForceModel::box_wing_srp, setting its srp_boxwing field) and on the precise orbit-determination model (precise_od::PreciseForceModel::box_wing_srp), replacing the cannonball term with cr as the radiation factor; both default to None, leaving every existing path byte-identical. ECOM2 is not wired into a force model</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Orekit 12.2 (Apache-2.0) BoxAndSolarArraySpacecraft with FixedPanel and PointingPanel, SolarRadiationPressure(dRef = 1 au, pRef = 1361/c), GenericGNSS nominal yaw and ECOM2(nD = 2, nB = 1), run as a separate program with the Sun at fixed inertial positions; the radiation-factor derivative from Hipparchus Gradient, per-panel derivatives as central differences of Orekit (Orekit has none). Bars pre-registered in xval/srp-boxwing-orekit/PREREGISTRATION.md (42e864bb), before any Orekit output existed: 1e-12 relative (1e-11 end to end, 1e-9 for the differences); all met (RESULTS.md). VALIDATED covers the model evaluation only: the GSC tables are transcribed inputs, and the adequacy of a box-wing for real Galileo orbits, thermal re-emission, the modified low-beta yaw law and eclipse turns are not covered; the Galileo SRP prediction stays MODELLED</td><td><code>srp_boxwing, propagator, precise_od</code></td></tr><tr><td>Solar limb-darkened SRP shadow (opt-in)</td><td>SRP lit fraction as the un-occulted fraction of the limb-darkened solar flux (Eddington-approximation law I(mu)/I(1) = 2/5 + 3/5 mu, Gauss-Legendre quadrature) over a spherical or WGS-84 Earth (srp_shadow::limb_darkened_shadow, the ShadowModel variant OblateEarthLimbDarkened)</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>None external: Orekit 12.2 has no limb-darkened lighting ratio. Checked against closed forms of the same law only</td><td><code>srp_shadow</code></td></tr><tr><td>NRLMSISE-00 thermospheric density</td><td>A port of the NRLMSISE-00 empirical thermosphere (gtd7 and gtd7d: species densities, total mass density with anomalous oxygen, temperatures; the 24 switches; daily-Ap and 3-hourly ap-array modes) from the public-domain C version of the NRL Fortran</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>pymsis 0.13.0 (MIT) version=0, which runs the NRL Fortran GTD7D in single precision, and the NRLMSISE-00 C port (release 20190709, public domain) compiled as a separate program, plus the C distribution's 17 published test outputs. Bars pre-registered in xval/nrlmsise00-pymsis/PREREGISTRATION.md (a6504ec9) before the port existed; all met (RESULTS.md). Validates the port, not the model's accuracy against measured density</td><td><code>nrlmsise00</code></td></tr><tr><td>NRLMSISE-00 drag-density tier (opt-in)</td><td>forces::DensityModel (Exponential default, Nrlmsise00 { f107a, f107, ap }) and propagator::ForceModel::drag_density: quadratic drag against the co-rotating atmosphere with the NRLMSISE-00 density at the WGS-84 geodetic point (Earth rotation angle with UT1 = TT, local solar time UT + lon/15), constant space weather per model; default off and bit-identical</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Orekit 12.2 (Apache-2.0) NumericalPropagator/DormandPrince853 with DragForce over Orekit's own NRLMSISE00 implementation, run as a separate program with the same Earth-rotation-angle frame, WGS-84 shape, local-solar-time convention (a mean Sun) and TT as UT; the convention setup was checked oracle against oracle (Orekit vs pymsis, 5.2e-5 in density). Bars pre-registered in xval/drag-tier-orekit/PREREGISTRATION.md (f380f42c): 0.5 m + 1e-3 of the drag displacement, and two &gt; 100 m sensitivity checks; all met. Not covered: the model's accuracy against measured density, time-varying space weather, a GMAT leg</td><td><code>forces (density_at, drag_accel_with_density), propagator</code></td></tr><tr><td>Default exponential drag against an independent implementation (equatorial)</td><td>The default drag of the Cowell propagator: quadratic drag against the co-rotating atmosphere with forces::atmospheric_density, the piecewise-exponential table of Vallado Table 8-4 at the spherical altitude (propagator::ForceModel::drag with the default Exponential variant of forces::DensityModel)</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>GMAT R2022a (NASA GSFC, Apache-2.0) GmatConsole with the Exponential atmosphere (the same 28-band table), point-mass Earth and PrinceDormand78, run, never vendored. Bars pre-registered in xval/drag-exponential-gmat/PREREGISTRATION.md (d870eb71) on the drag displacement of each side (drag minus no-drag run), which cancels the two Earth mu values and integrators; all met (RESULTS.md). GMAT takes geodetic height and kshana spherical altitude: they coincide only on the equator, so VALIDATED covers equatorial orbits; off the equator the models differ by design (10.4 % of 24 h displacement at 51.6 deg, 400 km). GMAT's public build has no NRLMSISE-00 (plugin absent), so the NRLMSISE-00 tier has the Orekit oracle only. The table's accuracy against measured density is not covered</td><td><code>forces (atmospheric_density, drag_accel), propagator</code></td></tr><tr><td>DE440 Sun and Moon in the Cowell force model (opt-in)</td><td>Opt-in ephemeris tier for the third-body and SRP terms of the numerical Cowell ForceModel: the geocentric Sun and Moon from any EphemerisProvider (a DE440 SPK kernel through KernelEphemeris) at TDB = TT + (TDB - TT), refusing a provider that cannot serve the epoch and counting any fallback to the analytic series; default off</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Orekit 12.2 (Apache-2.0) NumericalPropagator/DormandPrince853 with ThirdBodyAttraction and SolarRadiationPressure on Orekit's own DE440 reader (orekit-data lnxp1990.440), run as a separate program; kshana reads a bit-for-bit, SPICE-checked cut of NAIF de440s.bsp. Bars pre-registered in xval/propagator-de440-orekit/PREREGISTRATION.md (6386b3a2). The orbit bar (0.5 m) and the sensitivity control are met, but the Sun/Moon position bar (3 m / 0.2 m) missed at 6.39 m / 0.244 m. SPICE reading the same kernel at its exact TDB misses Orekit by the same amount (6.25 m / 0.240 m), so the gap lies between NAIF's de440s path and Orekit's DE440 reader or time scale; cause not isolated. Recorded and pinned, so the row stays MODELLED</td><td><code>propagator (ForceModel::with_ephemeris, ForceEphemeris), ephem_provider</code></td></tr><tr><td>S1 atmospheric tide (opt-in)</td><td>The diurnal S1 air-pressure tide as a geopotential perturbation: Biancale and Bode (2006) model N1, degrees 2 to 6, through the IERS 2010 Eq. 6.21 load formula (tides::atmospheric_tide_s1, s1_air_tide_acceleration), opt-in on the Cowell and precise force models (air_tide_s1, default off)</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>None external for the geopotential effect. The 24 coefficients are transcribed from GFZ STR06/01 Table 1 model N1 and were re-checked row by row against the report (SHA-256 890865ae); degrees 0, 1, 7 and 8 are not used. Ray and Ponte (2003) print no spherical-harmonic table, so no second source was compared</td><td><code>tides, propagator, precise_od</code></td></tr><tr><td>S2 atmospheric tide coefficients in the IERS convention</td><td>Ray (2001) S2 air-pressure harmonics (n &lt;= 4, prograde and retrograde) converted to the fully-normalised, sine-lead form of IERS 2010 Eqs. 6.20-6.21 (amplitude divided by N_nm, epsilon = 90 deg - psi) and applied as the S2 term of tides::atmospheric_tide</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Biancale and Bode, GFZ Scientific Technical Report STR06/01 (2006), Table 5, model A2: Ray (2001) published in fully-normalised harmonics with sine-lead phases, an independent rendering of the same model. Pre-registered in xval/s2-air-tide-fix/PREREGISTRATION.md (d2c3d297) with the fix it validates. Covers the coefficient conversion only; the air tide's geopotential effect has no external oracle</td><td><code>tides (s2_air_waves, atmospheric_tide)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Tesseral geopotential in the Cowell propagator</td><td>Opt-in EGM2008 tesseral field (to degree/order 70) as the central gravity of the numerical Cowell ForceModel, evaluated in an Earth-fixed frame rotated by the IAU 2000 Earth rotation angle (or the CIO GCRS-to-ITRS chain); default off, so no existing output moves</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Orekit 12.2 (CS GROUP, Apache-2.0) NumericalPropagator/DormandPrince853 with HolmesFeatherstoneAttractionModel over the same coefficients (tools/egm2008_to70.gfc) in a body frame rotated by the same Earth-rotation-angle formula, run as a separate program. Bars pre-registered (bdab936, amended before any comparison in 2c91443): |dr| &lt;= 0.5 m and |dv| &lt;= 5e-4 m/s, and both negative controls missing by &gt; 100 m; all met, bars then tightened to 0.1 m and 1e-4 m/s. Not validated: Earth-orientation fidelity (both sides use UT1 = TT without precession-nutation in the Era frame), the Cio frame option (internal check only), degrees above 70</td><td><code>propagator (Geopotential, EarthFixedFrame), gravity_sh</code></td></tr><tr><td>High-degree spherical-harmonic gravity (EGM2008 to 2190)</td><td>Underflow-safe, pole-safe evaluation of a fully-normalized field to degree 2190 (SphericalHarmonicField::acceleration_hf / potential_hf, Holmes-Featherstone 2002 modified forward column with the gradient free of 1/cos phi), a streaming ICGEM .gfc loader (from_gfc_file / from_gfc_reader; the full EGM2008 is fetched at run time, not bundled), and the Holmes-Featherstone evaluator selectable on the opt-in Cowell Geopotential</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Orekit 12.2 (Apache-2.0) HolmesFeatherstoneAttractionModel and pyshtools 4.14.1 (BSD-3-Clause) MakeGravGridPoint, run as separate programs; bars pre-registered in xval/high-degree-gravity/PREREGISTRATION.md (73b2c75d) and xval/cowell-tesseral-200-orekit/PREREGISTRATION.md (876be39d). Orekit agrees everywhere it is defined, but pyshtools misses its pre-registered bar at the two near-pole points (up to 7.3e-10 m/s^2; a 50-digit mpmath check puts the error in pyshtools), there is no oracle value exactly on the polar axis, and the 24 h orbit's pre-registered degree-70 sensitivity control missed (0.726 m against &gt; 1 m). Each miss is recorded, not tuned away, so the row stays MODELLED</td><td><code>gravity_sh, propagator (FieldEvaluator)</code></td></tr><tr><td>DOP853 integrator</td><td>Hairer DOP853 8(5,3) explicit Runge-Kutta with the combined 5th/3rd-order error estimator, the SciPy/Hairer step controller, automatic initial step, a fail-closed step guard and 7th-order dense output; selectable as the propagator Integrator variant Dop853 / propagator::propagate_dop853</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>SciPy 1.18.1 (BSD-3-Clause) solve_ivp(method=DOP853) and its single-step, error-norm, dense-output and initial-step routines, an independent implementation; exact two-body states from mpmath 1.3.0 at 40 digits. Bars pre-registered in xval/dop853-scipy/PREREGISTRATION.md (8b23409) before the first comparison; all met (xval/dop853-scipy/RESULTS.md). SciPy's first step is given to both solvers in the full runs; the initial-step routine is compared separately. Not covered: RKF7(8), stiffness detection, event location</td><td><code>integrator, propagator</code></td></tr><tr><td>Robust Kepler-equation solver</td><td>Elliptic Kepler solver for all 0 &lt;= e &lt; 1 and all mean anomalies: Markley (1995) cubic starter and fifth-order correction, a cancellation-free residual (1-e)E + e(E - sin E) - M, and guarded Newton polishing (kepler::solve_kepler_markley, propagator::solve_kepler_robust)</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>mpmath 1.3.0 (BSD-3-Clause) 50-digit roots from the exact binary inputs. Bars pre-registered in xval/kepler-mpmath/PREREGISTRATION.md (73bab12), stated relative to |E| with the e -&gt; 1, M -&gt; 0 conditioning argument; all met (xval/kepler-mpmath/RESULTS.md). The legacy solvers (solve_kepler_checked, orbit::solve_kepler, ephem::solve_kepler) are numerically unchanged, because committed outputs depend on them, and are not covered by this row</td><td><code>kepler, propagator</code></td></tr><tr><td>Range-rate and az/el batch orbit determination with station masking</td><td>Recover an epoch state from mixed range, range-rate and azimuth/elevation tracking above an elevation mask by Gauss-Newton on H = (dh/dx(t)) Phi(t, t0): analytic measurement partials and a variational state-transition matrix with analytic two-body + J2 partials (RK4-integrated, not a closed-form STM)</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Orekit 12.2 (Apache-2.0) BatchLSEstimator with custom instantaneous geometric RangeRate and AzEl measurements (cross-checked against Orekit's TopocentricFrame), run as a separate program on identical masked, seeded observations. Bars pre-registered in xval/od-measurements-orekit/PREREGISTRATION.md (acfd14c). Every bar met except B4, the noiseless state agreement of 1 cm, missed on 2 of 4 scenarios (1.46, 1.74 cm): Orekit's own noiseless estimate sits that far from its simulated truth (cause on the Orekit side not isolated). Recorded and pinned, so the row stays MODELLED. Not modelled: light time, aberration, refraction, Earth rotation, a sequential filter for these measurement types</td><td><code>orbit_determination (determine_orbit_batch_tracking, propagate_with_stm, is_visible), batch_ls (gauss_newton_analytic)</code></td></tr><tr><td>SP3 accuracy-code observation weighting</td><td>Map SP3 accuracy codes (header ++ exponents in 2^n mm, the %f base, per-record base^n mm standard deviations) to per-observation position sigmas for the precise orbit-determination engine (sp3::parse_sp3_accuracy, Sp3Accuracy::position_sigma_m)</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>None external: the weights are a modelling choice (the SP3 format defines the code only as the producer's accuracy indicator; it is read here as a per-axis one-sigma). The existing recorded agency runs are unchanged</td><td><code>sp3, precise_od</code></td></tr><tr><td>Planetary gravity fields from PDS SHADR tables</td><td>Read PDS SHADR spherical-harmonic tables (from_shadr, on SphericalHarmonicField in src/gravity_pds.rs: unit conversion, C00 = 1 supplied, completeness and normalisation checks) and evaluate the body-fixed gravity of Mars GMM-3 (d/o 120) and the GRAIL lunar field GL0660B (d/o 660)</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>pyshtools 4.14.1 (BSD-3-Clause) reading the same PDS files and evaluating point gravity, run as a separate program, and the published PDS product headers and labels. Bars pre-registered in xval/planetary-gravity-pyshtools/PREREGISTRATION.md (6d2674d); all met (RESULTS.md). Data: NASA PDS Geosciences Node, public domain; degree-20 crops bundled with NOTICE, full files fetched at test time</td><td><code>gravity_pds, gravity_sh</code></td></tr><tr><td>Attach a published gravity field to a body</td><td>with_gravity_field (on Body, in src/gravity_pds.rs): attach any loaded spherical-harmonic field (for example GMM-3 or GL0660B) to a Body so the propagator evaluates it body-fixed; opt-in, default bodies unchanged</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Internal consistency only. Each published field is defined in its producer's body-fixed frame (GMM-3 in the Konopliv et al. 2006 Mars frame, GL0660B in the DE421 lunar principal-axis frame), while the field is evaluated in the IAU frame; the offset is not modelled</td><td><code>gravity_pds, body, propagator, mars_frame</code></td></tr><tr><td>Batch &amp; sequential orbit determination</td><td>Recover an epoch state [r,v] from ground-station ranges via a Gauss–Newton batch differential corrector and a sequential filter, over a two-body+J2 force model</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Orekit 12.2 (CS GROUP, Apache-2.0) BatchLSEstimator (Levenberg–Marquardt) + KalmanEstimator (EKF) — an independent third-party estimation library on a matched force model; recovered epoch state + post-fit residual RMS agree to &lt;1e-3 m noiseless and &lt;3 m at a 5 m noise floor</td><td><code>orbit_determination, precise_od (batch_ls::gauss_newton, fusion::ukf)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Deep-space radiometric light-time solver</td><td>Retarded (down-leg) one-way light-time solution τ = |r_rx − r_target(t−τ)|/c for deep-space radiometric navigation (Earth→Mars/Sun/Moon)</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>ANISE 0.10 (Nyx Space, MPL-2.0) converged-Newtonian aberration light time (Aberration::CN; SPICE spkapo-equivalent) over JPL DE440 — an independent Rust SPICE implementation; kshana's fixed-point retarded solver matched to sub-nanosecond. The broader Doppler / Shapiro / reduced-dynamic OD figures stay MODELLED</td><td><code>radiometric (light_time_solution)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Broadcast-ephemeris satellite position (multi-GNSS RINEX)</td><td>IS-GPS-200 / Galileo-OS / BeiDou-OS broadcast-ephemeris Keplerian → ECEF satellite position from a parsed RINEX-3 navigation record</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>RTKLIB v2.4.2-p13 eph2pos() compiled from C source (T. Takasu, BSD-2-Clause) — an independent IS-GPS-200/SIS-ICD implementation, fed the identical RINEX-3 nav records; satellite ECEF position matched to ~62 nm. (SP3 precise-ephemeris interpolation is validated separately — see 'SP3 precise-ephemeris interpolation')</td><td><code>rinex (parse_nav, RinexEphemeris::sv_position_ecef)</code></td></tr><tr><td>SP3 precise-ephemeris interpolation</td><td>IGS-standard Lagrange interpolation of SP3 precise-ephemeris satellite positions with the per-node Earth-rotation correction (rotate each node by ω⊕·(t_node−t) into the query instant's Earth-fixed frame before the polynomial fit)</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>RTKLIB peph2pos() compiled from C source (preceph.c; T. Takasu, BSD-2-Clause) — the de-facto IGS reference, an independent implementation; kshana's interpolator (now carrying the same Earth-rotation node correction) matched to ~15 nm on a real SP3 product, down from ~5.5 cm before the correction</td><td><code>sp3 (Sp3Interpolator::position_ecef)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Navigation-message encoders beyond GPS LNAV (GPS CNAV, Galileo I/NAV and F/NAV)</td><td>GPS CNAV message types 10/11/30, Galileo I/NAV word types 1-5 in nominal even/odd pages and Galileo F/NAV page types 1-4: ICD field packing and quantisation, CRC-24Q, rate-1/2 K=7 convolutional coding (G2 inverted for Galileo), block interleaving and sync patterns, with matching test decoders</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Internal round trip and ICD known answers; no third-party decoder has been run (the RTKLIB/GNSS-SDR harness reading KSHANA_NAVMSG_ORACLE_JSON is data-gated). MODELLED</td><td><code>gnss_navmsg (gps_cnav, gal_inav, gal_fnav, fec)</code></td></tr><tr><td>IONEX global ionosphere map reader and interpolation on a real CODE GIM</td><td>IONEX 1.0 reader (header, fixed-column I5 values, TEC and RMS maps, absolute epochs, missing values) with bilinear spatial interpolation, the format's nearest / consecutive / rotated-map time interpolation (eqs. 1-3) and thin-shell slant TEC</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>The CODE GIM's printed values (exact at every node; the map is fetched at test time, not vendored, its licence being unconfirmed) and an independent Python stdlib re-implementation of the IONEX 1.0 interpolation formulas committed with the fixture (bars pre-registered 79beaab, 1e-9 TECU). Third-party check (B6, pre-registered, first run): RTKLIB v2.4.2-p13 iontec (BSD-2-Clause, run as a separate tool) at 6885 query points agrees with IonexFile::vtec_at to 3.6e-13 TECU in