al trade as honest TradeEvidence; honest observability note (bias unobservable without a fix, so error grows).</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Reused inertial budgets cross-checked against an independent Octave double-integration of the same dead-reckoning ODE (different runtime, shared model → ReferenceImpl) plus the Freier-2016 published short-term noise as a one-sided anchor; the quantum-vs-classical composite stays MODELLED</td><td><code>quantum_nav_od</code></td></tr><tr><td>Fault/anomaly detection for quantum PNT systems</td><td>Labelled quantum-fault catalog (clock frequency-jump/drift/lock-loss; sensor bias-step/dropout), a detection-statistic ROC AUC with a bootstrap CI, and a minimum-detectable fault at a fixed false-alarm rate; a quantum-clock-aided monitor detects smaller faults than a classical one, emitted as honest TradeEvidence.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Closed-form Gaussian AUC = Phi(mu/(sigma*sqrt2)) cross-checked against the externally-validated eval_stats::bootstrap_auc_ci (vs scikit-learn) + detection analytic thresholds</td><td><code>quantum_faults</code></td></tr><tr><td>Torque-free rigid-body attitude dynamics</td><td>Euler's rotational equations of motion (I ω̇ = τ − ω × Iω, principal-axis and general inertia tensor) coupled to quaternion attitude kinematics (q̇ = ½ q ⊗ ω) and propagated with a fixed-step RK4 integrator that re-normalises the quaternion each step</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Basilisk 2.9.1 spacecraft hub rigid-body propagation (AVSLab, ISC; MRP attitude through its own coupled hub equations, svIntegratorRKF78 at relative tolerance 1e-12), an independent implementation of the torque-free rigid-body equations: Kshana's RK4 quaternion (dt 0.01 s) and body rates agree within 1e-9 over 1e4 s on two general non-diagonal inertias (worst 3.3e-11 and 3.4e-12 rad/s); tolerance fixed before the comparison. Validated claim: torque-free motion; the external-torque term is not compared. The conservation-law and symmetric-top checks remain as supporting self-consistency. No flexible-body / control-loop / external-torque environment</td><td><code>attitude_dynamics</code></td></tr><tr><td>Clohessy–Wiltshire / Hill relative-motion dynamics</td><td>Relative motion of a chaser about a target on a circular reference orbit in the LVLH frame: the linearised Clohessy–Wiltshire equations (ẍ−2nẏ−3n²x=0, ÿ+2nẋ=0, z̈+n²z=0) solved by the closed-form 6×6 state-transition matrix Φ(n,t) (Clohessy–Wiltshire 1960; Vallado Alg. 48) with the bounded relative-orbit condition ẏ₀=−2n·x₀, and a closed-form second-order correction (cw_dynamics::second_order_correction, the CW response to the quadratic terms of the exact circular-chief equations; Karlgaard and Lutze 2003, Newman, Lovell and Pratt 2015) composed as cw_dynamics::propagate_second_order. The validated output is propagate_second_order; the linear Φ alone is characterised against nonlinear truth, not validated</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Orekit 12.2 (Apache-2.0) NumericalPropagator, two-body, LOFType.QSW, run as a separate program (fixture unchanged since round 1). Pre-registered bar 1e-3 m on every sample of every case; the second-order engine fix was re-run against it unchanged (amendment dec81b7c, written before the propagator existed, disclosing that the first-order gaps were known). propagate_second_order agrees within 1.15e-7 m (C1), 9.8e-8, 5.4e-8 and 2.2e-8 m; halving the second-order term fails at 1.85e-3 to 3.14e-3 m. The linear Φ alone departs from nonlinear truth by 1.2 to 6.3 mm at 100 m over a third of an orbit (the omitted second-order term; its strict test is kept ignored), so it is characterised, not validated. Internal checks kept: Φ against an RK4 integration of the Hill equations, Φ(t)Φ(−t)=I, the bounded-orbit and −12π·x₀ drift identities, and the third-order residual scaling of the second-order term. Scope: circular chief, separations near 100 m, up to a third of an orbit; no eccentricity (Tschauner–Hempel), J2 or differential-drag terms; small-separation assumption</td><td><code>cw_dynamics (propagate, second_order_correction, propagate_second_order)</code></td></tr><tr><td>TDOA/FDOA passive emitter geolocation</td><td>Locate an emitter (jammer/spoofer, or an opportunistic source for reverse-PNT) from time-difference-of-arrival across a receiver network — the τᵢ=(Rᵢ−R₀)/c hyperboloid intersection solved by Gauss–Newton least squares — and, adding frequency-difference-of-arrival (range-rate differences) with moving receivers, jointly recover position and velocity; with the Cramér–Rao lower bound on the position covariance from the measurement geometry</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Self-consistency of the estimator and geometry: forward→inverse round trips, the Fisher/CRLB identity J·CRLB=I, GDOP monotonicity, and the estimator attaining its own Cramér–Rao bound under Monte-Carlo noise — internal-consistency checks, NOT an external dataset, so the row stays InternalConsistency. MODELLED passive geolocation — point-source line-of-sight model; no multipath / NLOS, receiver-clock-bias, or atmospheric-refraction terms. 0.30 round 2, against Ho and Chan 1993 and Ho and Xu 2004 (pre-registered 034e1d1b, 1 % of each printed value), a finding (stays MODELLED): the surface-constrained TDOA bound reproduces the 40 N geometric factors (-0.11 %, -0.02 %) and the maximum TDOA; the joint TDOA+FDOA bound reproduces Fig. 6 (+0.06 %, -0.17 %) and, at the source the authors own code names for Fig. 7 (amendment written after that result was seen, disclosed), Fig. 7 (+0.11 %, -0.02 %); it equals the authors published bound code (BSD-style, run under GNU Octave) to 3e-12; the covariance-weighted maximum-likelihood solver attains the printed bound (+0.3 to +0.4 %). The Ottawa factors the paper prints from a Monte Carlo (251, 7.1) differ by +1.3 % and +4.4 %, outside the 1 % bar</td><td><code>geolocation</code></td></tr><tr><td>3-DoF ballistic entry peak deceleration against the published Stardust nominal</td><td>A 3-DoF point-mass entry over a rotating spherical Earth through the US Standard Atmosphere 1976, integrated with RK4 from an inertial entry state, reporting the peak deceleration and its altitude; the planet-relative entry state from the inertial one. Runnable as the <code>entry-heating</code> scenario kind.</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>The Stardust nominal of Desai, Mitcheltree and Cheatwood (1999, NASA NTRS 20050042027): maximum deceleration 33.4 g from 12.6 km/s planet-relative with the 45.8 kg launch mass, a 6-DoF POST trajectory with the full aerodynamic database; entry interface from Desai, Lyons, Tooley and Kangas (NTRS 20090004445): radius 6503.14 km, inertial 12.9 km/s at -8.2 deg. Bars pre-registered in xval/entry-stardust/PREREGISTRATION.md before any run on these inputs; both held on the first run. WHAT IS NOT: one published case and one quantity (peak deceleration, which in the ballistic approximation does not depend on the ballistic coefficient); the entry latitude and heading are assumed; heating, heat load and the landing point are not checked against it; point mass with constant C_D, not 6-DoF.</td><td><code>entry_eo_ascent::entry_heating (simulate_entry, inertial_to_relative, EntryHeatingScenario)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Stagnation-point convective entry heating (Sutton-Graves) on a 3-DoF entry</td><td>Stagnation-point convective heat flux q = k sqrt(rho/R_n) V^3 with the Sutton-Graves constants for Earth air and the Mars atmosphere (NASA TR R-376, 1971), its peak and the integrated heat load along a 3-DoF entry, ballistic or lifting at constant bank, on Earth or Mars.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Internal consistency: the relation and constants as printed in NASA TR R-376, a half-step convergence check, and the expected ordering of heating and deceleration peaks. WHAT IS NOT: no published heating value has been compared (the Stardust papers give a design limit and a convective-plus-radiative estimate of about 1200 W/cm2, which a convective-only relation cannot match); no radiative heating, wall-temperature or catalycity correction, or thermal-protection response.</td><td><code>entry_eo_ascent::entry_heating (stagnation_heat_flux, simulate_entry)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Electro-optical imager radiometry, MTF, relative edge response and GIQE 4 NIIRS</td><td>Nadir imager signal-to-noise budget (Lambertian scene radiance, camera equation, shot, dark, read and quantisation noise), the system MTF at Nyquist as the product of unobscured-aperture diffraction, detector aperture, linear smear and Gaussian jitter, the edge response and relative edge response integrated from that MTF, Q = lambda F / p, and the GIQE version 4 NIIRS with no MTF compensation. Runnable as the <code>eo-radiometry</code> scenario kind.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Closed forms: the diffraction MTF at half cutoff, the one-pixel ramp edge of a pure detector aperture (RER 1), the normal-CDF edge of a Gaussian blur (RER = erf(0.5/(sigma sqrt 2))), shot-noise scaling, and the GIQE 4 coefficients as published (Leachtenauer et al., Applied Optics 36(32), 1997). WHAT IS NOT: no external imager, measured MTF or rated image has been compared; the GIQE is an empirical regression with its own error; no stray light, atmospheric MTF, MTF compensation or aliasing.</td><td><code>entry_eo_ascent::eo_radiometry (Imager, snr_budget, giqe4_niirs, EoRadiometryScenario)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Launch ascent gravity, drag, steering and back-pressure losses</td><td>Planar gravity-turn ascent of a multi-stage vehicle over a non-rotating spherical Earth through the US Standard Atmosphere 1976 (density and pressure), with a pitch kick, thrust reduced by the nozzle back-pressure, stage burnouts at exact step boundaries, and the gravity, drag, steering and back-pressure losses integrated beside the state. Runnable as the <code>ascent-losses</code> scenario kind.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Internal consistency: the exact identity burnout speed = sum Isp g0 ln(m0/mf) minus the four integrated losses (residual below 1e-6 m/s), Tsiolkovsky's equation on a vertical vacuum burn with gravity the only loss, and the US76 sea-level and tropopause pressures. WHAT IS NOT: no published vehicle's losses have been compared; no Earth rotation, coast, throttling or guidance beyond the kick; constant C_D; not a trajectory optimiser.</td><td><code>entry_eo_ascent::ascent (simulate_ascent, AscentScenario); reentry::us76_pressure</code></td></tr><tr><td>TWSTFT two-way equation, uncertainty budget and link stability with flicker and diurnal non-reciprocity</td><td>The TWSTFT two-way equation solved for T_A - T_B with station transmit and receive delays, the Ku-band ionospheric up/down asymmetry, the transponder delay difference and the Sagnac terms, against its exact forward model; a quadrature uncertainty budget with the link TDEV as the type A term; a two-way series with white and flicker phase noise (Kasdin-Walter), a diurnal non-reciprocity and a constant reciprocity residual; and the rate, residual RMS, TDEV and ADEV of supplied Circular T, MJD-CSV or IGS RINEX station-clock series. Runnable as the <code>time-transfer-budget</code> scenario kind.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Internal consistency: the forward model and the solved equation agree to 1e-15 s, the Sagnac term equals the module's independent BIPM 2A omega/c^2 loop form, the ionospheric term is 40.3 TEC (1/f_up^2 - 1/f_down^2)/c, and the noise processes show their expected TDEV shapes. WHAT IS NOT: no real TWSTFT measurement or calibration report has been compared; the stated delays and uncertainties are inputs, not measured; the stabilities of supplied series are reported, not validated.</td><td><code>twstft_budget (counter_readings, solve_offset, combined_uncertainty, flicker_phase, two_way_series, series_stability); time_transfer_budget (TimeTransferBudgetScenario, read_series)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Optical slant-path atmosphere numerics: Rytov variance, scintillation, gamma-gamma fade probability, aperture averaging, cloud and clear-air optical depth</td><td>Hufnagel-Valley Cn2 integrated along a slant path for the plane- and spherical-wave Rytov variance (uplink and downlink), the Andrews-Phillips scintillation index and gamma-gamma parameters, the gamma-gamma fade probability and fade margin, the weak-turbulence aperture-averaging factor, the cloud optical depth of a liquid-water column and the Kim-visibility plus Rayleigh clear-air optical depth, replacing the lumped atmospheric loss of the optical link budget when an atmosphere is supplied. Runnable inside the <code>optical-atmosphere</code> scenario kind.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>The numerics are compared with an independent program (xval/optical-atmosphere/oracle.py, mpmath 1.4.1 at 30 digits, tanh-sinh quadrature on the original height variable and Bessel-K gamma-gamma densities) that shares no code with Kshana, under bars fixed before any implementation existed (xval/optical-atmosphere/PREREGISTRATION.md). WHY MODELLED: that oracle evaluates the same published formulas, so it checks the implementation of the stated formulas and not the atmosphere; no ITU-R validation example and no measured scintillation or slant-path extinction record is used (the ITU values are an open licence decision and are kept out of the repository). The Cn2 profile level, the visibility, the aerosol and molecular scale heights, the aperture-averaging path length and the moment-matching used to apply aperture averaging to the gamma-gamma parameters are stated inputs or conventions. A source line in the module records that one widely copied survey prints the second scintillation exponent as 7/6 where Andrews and Phillips give 5/6; the 5/6 form is implemented</td><td><code>optical_atmosphere (rytov_variance, scintillation, gamma_gamma_cdf, fade_margin_db, aperture_averaging_factor, cloud_optical_depth, clear_air_optical_depth); optical_linkbudget (optical_link_budget_atmosphere)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Weather-driven optical ground-site availability and distance-correlated site diversity</td><td>Availability of an optical link against a stated link margin from a log-normal cloud liquid-water climate (ITU-R P.840 amount, geometric-optics extinction) and the independent gamma-gamma turbulence fade, the link margin needed for target availabilities and the ceiling no margin lifts, log-normal outage durations, enhancement-technique what-ifs, and a one-factor Gaussian-copula network availability whose pair correlation is exp(-distance/decorrelation length). Runnable as the <code>optical-atmosphere</code> scenario kind.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Internal consistency: closed-form limits and monotonicity of the diversity model, the cloud-ceiling closed form, and the log-normal outage identities. NO external oracle is claimed and none has been compared: no measured optical-ground-station availability or cloud-free-line-of-sight record is used. WHY MODELLED: the cloud climate (probability of cloud, median and spread of the liquid-water column, outage durations), the independence of cloud and turbulence, the Gaussian-copula form, the decorrelation length and every technique gain are assumptions; the bundled scenarios state ILLUSTRATIVE climates, not a dataset. Upgrading this row needs a cited measured availability record and a bar registered before the comparison</td><td><code>optical_weather; optical_sitediv; optical_techniques; optical_atmosphere_scenario</code></td></tr><tr><td>Timing and ranging continuity through optical outages</td><td>The outage-duration distribution of an optical site coupled to the closed-form coast variance of the clock holdover model: timing and ranging error at stated outage quantiles, the holdover to a timing threshold, the fraction of outages and of outage time inside it and the timing availability A + U E[min(D, T_h)]/E[D], for the classical and quantum clock classes. Runnable inside the <code>optical-atmosphere</code> scenario kind.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Internal consistency with the holdover module, bit for bit; NO external oracle is claimed. The class PSDs are the synthesised long-tau floors the holdover module already carries, so the figures inherit its stated caveat that holdover to a tight threshold is governed by the assumed red-noise floor and not by the cited Allan deviation. The outage durations are a log-normal model with stated parameters, not a measured cloud-dwell record</td><td><code>optical_outage_holdover (couple, clock_psds); holdover (coast_phase_sigma, holdover_seconds, phase_to_range_m)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Optical link margin against time: one seeded realisation of a site's weather</td><td>An opt-in <code>[timeseries]</code> table (duration, step, seed) makes the <code>optical-atmosphere</code> kind also write, per site, one seeded realisation of the stated site model through time: cloud episodes (clear spells exponential, cloud outages log-normal with the stated mean and spread, the share of time in cloud set to the stated cloud probability), one log-normal liquid-water column per cloud episode, one gamma-gamma scintillation sample per step, and from them the link margin, the cloud and scintillation fades and the link-up flag, with the realised availability, cloud fraction, outage count and longest outage. Without the table the result is the 0.37 document. Draws go through the portable distribution and maths routes, so native and wasm32 give the same bytes.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Internal consistency only; NO external oracle is claimed and none exists for a weather realisation. The checks compare one draw with the statistical output of the same model, so a pass says the generator samples the stated distributions (the draw's availability and cloud share sit inside pre-registered tolerances of the stated figures), not that any real site behaves so. The series is one realisation, not the climate: its availability differs from the stated one by the draw's sampling error, and scintillation samples at different steps are independent, a stated simplification of a process that decorrelates in milliseconds. The weather inputs stay the scenario's assumptions.</td><td><code>optical_timeseries (site_series, SiteModel, SeriesSpec, Series); optical_atmosphere_scenario (TimeseriesSpec)</code></td></tr><tr><td>TRK-2-18 Orbit Data File reader and Mars ramped 2-way Doppler model</td><td>A reader for DSN Orbit Data Files (TRK-2-18): the 36-byte record groups (file label, identifier, orbit data, per-station ramps, clock offsets, summary, end of file), and in each orbit-data record the time tag since 1950, downlink and uplink delays, observable, stations, data type, bands, validity, channel, spacecraft, the 46-bit reference frequency and the compression time; ramp start and end times, rates and start frequencies. On top of it, a ramped 2-way X-band Doppler model (integrated uplink ramps, round-trip light time with Shapiro and troposphere, DSN stations through the IAU 2006/2000A CIO chain and IERS EOP, station time) and a Mars-orbiter batch orbit determination (GMM-3 field, third bodies, SRP, drag).</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>The PDS3 label that the DSN navigation data-conditioning process produced with the file (NASA PDS Geosciences Node, MRO-M-RSS-1-MAGR-V1.0), checked against bars pre-registered in xval/mro-odf/PREREGISTRATION.md before the reader existed. MODELLED because one registered bar (the end-of-file table's rows, 121 in the file against 120 in the label) missed; the label is internally inconsistent there. Stage 2 (xval/mro-od/PREREGISTRATION.md, amendments A1 and A2 before the scored result): the Doppler predicted from the MRO navigation team's reconstruction (mro_psp5, NAIF) matches the observed ramped 2-way Doppler to 12.5 mHz on passes not used in development, bar met; the registered orbit determination failed on the scored arc (normal matrix not positive definite) and produced no orbit, so the orbit bars were not met. Run 2 (xval/mro-od/PREREGISTRATION-RUN2.md, a damped estimator, the same arc and bars