largest step. Every limit is the one the telecom-timing kind transcribes; the two times are the operator's statement and are not verified</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Hand computation on records built from formulas whose slope, step, extremum and recovery time are known before the run (the tests state them), and the masks, envelope and budgets of the telecom-timing rows above. No laboratory measurement of a real holdover was scored and no external oracle was compared, so the kind makes no claim about any clock or system; a timing-lab partner is needed before any per-clock-class figure could be promoted</td><td><code>holdover_test (HoldoverTestScenario, recovery, linear_fit); telecom_timing (check_mask, check_envelope, budget_results)</code></td></tr><tr><td>GNSS signal availability in the Space Service Volume and beyond, compared with the ICG SSV booklet</td><td>The <code>gnss-ssv</code> kind's availability sweeps: per constellation (GPS, GLONASS, Galileo, BDS, QZSS, NavIC) and combined, the share of (user, epoch) pairs with at least one and at least four signals and the longest outage, for a user on a sphere (the booklet's 2,562-point global shell, 14 days at 1 minute) or on a trajectory (GEO, HEO, lunar transfer), from the Keplerian orbits of the booklet's annex C, a constant minimum radiated power inside the reference off-boresight angle, Earth blockage by the sphere plus 50 km and a C/N0 threshold; user antennas from the booklet's table B10</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>The ICG Space Service Volume booklet (UN OOSA ST/SPACE/75, 2018), itself a simulation, compared under a pre-registration committed before the run (xval/gnss-ssv/PREREGISTRATION.md; results in xval/gnss-ssv/RESULTS.md, pinned by tests/ssv_booklet_results.rs, misses included). Run 1 on the pre-registered reading: the minimum-radiated-power constants and the lunar bar agree (S0, S6 met); the upper and lower SSV, RF-threshold, GEO and HEO bars are NOT MET, almost all through GLONASS, for which the booklet gives 20 and 28 degrees in tables B2 and B6 and 26 and 34 degrees in table 4.2. A diagnostic variant with table 4.2's angles agrees in every cell of the upper SSV, lower SSV and the six GEO missions (it does not replace run 1; RF, HEO and lunar cells still miss). The measured GPS L1 panel patterns are not reproduced by the parametric stand-in (RMS 6 to 9 dB against a 3 dB bar). Agreement shows the booklet's stated method is reproduced, not that GNSS signals behave so</td><td><code>ssv (constellations, geom, sweep, pattern, check)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Limb-ray ionosphere, plasmasphere and atmosphere effects on grazing GNSS signals, and the weak-signal receiver design, in the <code>gnss-ssv</code> kind</td><td>A Chapman ionosphere, a Gallagher-type plasmasphere and a neutral atmosphere (refractivity bending, oxygen absorption, defocusing) integrated along the straight ray between transmitter and user: slant total electron content, its plasmaspheric part, excess delay, tangent altitude and the loss on grazing links; and a weak-signal receiver design (coherent and non-coherent integration, data-bit limit, aiding, Doppler search) that sets the C/N0 threshold from the acquisition and tracking-loop models</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Closed forms and published magnitudes in the tests (a Chapman layer's peak and the bending of the surface limb, a few TECU of plasmasphere on a GPS-to-GEO ray); the receiver design reuses the acquisition and tracking-loop models and their tests. No measured limb-sounding or weak-signal reception data is compared with it</td><td><code>ssv (limb, weak)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Onboard GNSS navigation filter for high-orbit and lunar-transfer spacecraft</td><td>The <code>gnss-ssv</code> kind's <code>[navigation]</code> mode: a nine-state extended Kalman filter (position, velocity, receiver clock bias and drift, a solar-radiation-pressure scale) on pseudoranges whose availability, noise and C/N0 come from the same link geometry as the sweeps; truth is integrated with J2, Sun and Moon third-body and radiation pressure with shadow, the filter models J2 and the third bodies and estimates the radiation pressure; reports position and velocity errors inside and above the GNSS orbits, the longest measurement gap and the mean NEES</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Simulated truth against the filter's own estimate (a consistent filter's mean normalised estimation error squared is about 3 for the position) and determinism for a fixed seed. No flight or recorded high-orbit GNSS data is compared; the Sun and Moon are low-precision circular stand-ins</td><td><code>ssv::nav (run_nav, Ekf)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Relative navigation of a multi-spacecraft formation in a highly elliptical orbit from GNSS</td><td>The <code>gnss-ssv</code> kind's <code>[formation]</code> mode: a chief and deputies each run the absolute filter; the deputy's position relative to the chief is estimated by subtracting the absolute estimates and by a relative filter on single-differenced pseudoranges of the signals both craft track, with the relative dynamics the difference of two nonlinear trajectories</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>The same simulated truth as the absolute filter: the relative filter's error against the true relative state, and the check that it does at least as well as subtracting two independent absolute fixes. No flight formation data is compared</td><td><code>ssv::formation (run_formation, RelEkf)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Navigation and timing error budget of a chain of mission phases from launch to entry (<code>mission-thread</code>)</td><td>The <code>mission-thread</code> kind: a document of phases, each one of eight registered kinds, run in order with the 6x6 covariance, the clock and the frame handed on under a stated rule (<code>carry</code>; <code>inflate</code>, white acceleration noise over a stated gap on a straight-line model; <code>reacquire</code>, dead reckoning then the source floor, never below it, never a reset); frame conversions G