idor probability, peak deceleration and landing footprint (<code>entry</code>)</td><td>The <code>entry</code> phase: the return coast of <code>tli-and-cruise</code> with no burn, ending at the entry interface; the flight-path angle and its sigma from the full covariance by a finite-difference gradient; the probability that the angle is inside a stated corridor; the peak deceleration at the nominal angle and at both corridor edges from the engine's planar ballistic point-mass entry (skip-out detected) beside the Allen-Eggers closed form; the landing footprint from a stated downrange sensitivity and the interface covariance across the orbit plane. No lift, guidance or heating is modelled</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Hand calculations in the tests (the angle sigma is the perpendicular velocity sigma over the speed; the Gaussian mass in a corridor). The planar entry is the engine's own and the Allen-Eggers form is a closed-form comparison; no flown entry is compared</td><td><code>mission_thread::earth::entry (Entry); reentry</code></td></tr><tr><td>Ground-network coverage and tracking duty for a thread phase (<code>[ground_network]</code> of <code>mission-thread</code>)</td><td>The ground-network overlay: stations with a minimum elevation, the visibility of the vehicle on a stated geometry (the entry state flown two-body with J2, the Moon's position for the cruise, or a stated duty), station handovers that lose a stated time each, and the resulting duty passed to the phase's ground-tracking update rate; the longest interval with no station in view is a budget row</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Zenith and antipode geometry checks and the expected visibility of a distant target in the tests. No real network, antenna or schedule is modelled</td><td><code>mission_thread::earth::ground_network</code></td></tr><tr><td>Relative navigation to docking in a lunar orbit (<code>lunar-orbit-rendezvous</code>)</td><td>The <code>lunar-orbit-rendezvous</code> kind and phase: a joint two-vehicle 