>Surface positioning and timing availability by site and season at the lunar poles (<code>lunar-surface</code>)</td><td>The <code>lunar-surface</code> kind and phase: relay availability and PDOP, Earth and Sun visibility and a disciplined surface clock for each site and season, with the terrain skyline of <code>moonlight-service-volume</code>; a single-site run equals that kind's availability and PDOP to the bit</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Exact equality with the existing kind for one site, and monotonicity checks, in the tests. The relay set is ILLUSTRATIVE and no surface measurement is compared</td><td><code>lunar_surface_map (LunarSurfaceMapScenario); lunar_phase</code></td></tr><tr><td>Powered ascent from a lunar polar site to an insertion orbit (<code>lunar-ascent</code>)</td><td>The <code>lunar-ascent</code> kind and phase: the five-state per-axis inertial error model run over a powered ascent with specific force along the path, aided by AFS fixes and lunar time, reporting the insertion position and velocity covariance carried to orbit and the engine-cutoff dispersion (reported, not carried)</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>The closed-form inertial drift and monotonicity checks in the tests. No flown ascent is compared</td><td><code>lunar_ascent (LunarAscentScenario); lunar_phase</code></td></tr><tr><td>The lunar force model propagation against public reconstructed lunar-orbiter and cislunar trajectories</td><td>The propagator the lunar kinds share (GRAIL GL0660B degree and order 150, Earth and Sun third bodies, relativity, DE440 lunar orientation) compared with the public JPL Horizons trajectories of LRO (24 h, 10 minute samples) and CAPSTONE (one 6.5 day revolution, one hour samples), under bars fixed in a committed file before the run</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>JPL Horizons spacecraft ephemerides for LRO and CAPSTONE, against bars pre-registered in xval/mission-thread-lunar/PREREGISTRATION.md (Amendments 1 to 6). LRO: MET, 33 m largest 3-D difference and 2.4 m RMS radial against 500 m and 60 m. CAPSTONE: NOT MET, a 136.8 km end-position difference against 25 km and a 28.4 km periapsis difference against 10 km, the causes not isolated. The row is Modelled because the CAPSTONE bar is missed and the comparison tests the shared propagator, not any filter, signal or budget</td><td><code>lunar_od (LunarForceModel); precise_od; mission_thread_lunar_oracles (example)</code></td></tr><tr><td>The cislunar mean-state propagation against the public Artemis I trajectory (<code>tli-and-cruise</code>)</td><td>The mean-state propagation of the <code>tli-and-cruise</code> phase (Cowell, Earth point mass and J2, the Sun and Moon third bodies, the Moon from a DE440 cut) compared with the public JPL Horizons trajectory of Artemis I over a 72 h coast arc from 2022-11-16 18:00 TDB, at 12 hour epochs, under bars fixed in a committed file before the run</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>JPL Horizons target -1023 (the NASA navigation as-flown post-TLI trajectory), against bars pre-registered in xval/mission-thread/PREREGISTRATION.md (Amendment 1 changes only the request span). NOT MET and asserted as missed: 45.3 km largest position difference against 5 km, and 0.249 m/s velocity difference at 72 h against 0.05 m/s. A diagnostic written after the verdict places the miss in five impulsive velocity steps of 0.09 to 0.22 m/s in the reference (the pre-registered reading that the window held no burn was wrong); over the other 67 hours the model reproduces the next hourly state to a median of 0.38 m. Tests the mean state of one coast arc only: no covariance, filter, update or other phase</td><td><code>mission_thread::earth::tli_cruise; propagator; mission_thread_oracle (example)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Cold-atom interferometer error terms: Raman laser-phase noise, wavefront-curvature bias and fringe-ambiguity resolution</td><td>inertial::cai_error_terms (library functions, not yet reachable from a scenario kind): the Raman-phase weighting function of a square-pulse three-pulse interferometer (Cheinet et al. 2008, Eq. 8) with the white-phase closed form (pi/2)^2 S_phi0/tau_R and a numeric route for any spectrum; the laser frequency noise the retro-reflection delay does not cancel (Le Gouet et al. 2007, Eqs. 9 to 11); the pure-defocus wavefront bias sigma_v^2/R of a thermal cloud and its reduction by a detection radius; the wrong-fringe-index probability erfc(pi/(sqrt(2) s)) for a classical-accelerometer aid and for two interrogation times, and its inverse</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Each closed form against an independent numerical route in the tests (frequency-domain quadrature of the exact transfer function and delay filter, a Monte Carlo over atoms for the wavefront bias, a Monte Carlo over shots for the fringe index) and its limiting cases. One published worked value: Cheinet et al. 2008 (arXiv:physics/0510197, Sec. IV) state that at tau_R = 20 us a white Raman phase noise below -111 dB rad^2/Hz is needed for 1 mrad per shot; the white term alone gives 0.99 mrad (the paper derives the level with its Eq. 17, flicker plus white). Internal consistency and one worked value, not an external validation; higher-order aberrations and any specific laser's noise are not modelled</td><td><code>inertial::cai_error_terms</code></td></tr><tr><td>Greenhall and Riley exact equivalent degrees of freedom of the Allan, modified Allan and Hadamard variances</td><td>allan::edf_greenhall (with the edf_greenhall_adev, _mdev and _hdev wrappers): the full algorithm of Greenhall and Riley, Proc. 35th PTTI (2003), Section 3 (Eqns 7 to 13 and the large-J coefficients of Tables 1 to 3), for difference orders 1 to 3, noise exponents +2 to -4 wherever alpha + 2d &gt; 1, overlapping and non-overlapping, modified and unmodified estimators; NaN where it does not apply, including the unmodified white-PM case with ceil(M/S) &lt;= d, whose closed form is not implemented. assurance::uncertainty::edf now shares the code and keeps its outputs bit for bit</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>allantools 2024.6 ci.edf_greenhall, a third-party implementation of the published algorithm, run by scripts/gen_greenhall_edf_ref.py over the grid pre-registered in xval/greenhall-edf/PREREGISTRATION.md (committed alone, 696f68e6, before any comparison): at all 4 876 points where it returns a finite edf, kshana agrees within a relative 6.0e-13 (bar 1e-9); at its 46 NotImplementedError points kshana returns NaN; every case label occurs; the wrappers equal the core bit for bit; uncertainty::edf is bit-identical over 274 280 values (pinned as a SHA-256 before the refactor). At 32 points (alpha -4, d 3, unmodified, case 2.2) allantools fails in its Table 2 lookup, so they have no oracle value and are not counted. Stable32 was not run. 0.36 revision (docs/revisions/STUDIO15-greenhall-edf-portable.md): on portable maths the kernel takes its logarithm from a double-double evaluation rounded once (allan::greenhall_ln), and the unchanged Bar 1 holds again at all 4 876 points, worst relative 6.0e-13; the uncertainty::edf guard is re-based to the 0.36 portable dump (3 746 of the 274 280 values moved, at most 1.6e-12 relative, from the host-maths base)</td><td><code>allan (edf_greenhall, greenhall_edf_core); assurance::uncertainty (edf)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Oscillator holdover presets from public datasheets</td><td>Four presets — OCXO (Microchip OX-208), rubidium (Microchip 8040C), caesium (Microchip 5071A high-performance tube) and CSAC (Microchip SA.45s) — each carrying its datasheet's Allan-deviation maxima, aging and temperature bound; a non-negative least-squares white + flicker + random-walk frequency-noise fit scaled to envelope every datasheet point, a flicker floor never below the longest-τ figure, linear aging and a sinusoidal temperature term, synthesised as a seeded holdover after a GNSS loss</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>The four named Microchip datasheets (document numbers and URLs in each preset and in docs/TELECOM-TIMING.md) for the input figures; the c