+<h2 id="linux-pps-time-stamps-pps-assert">Linux PPS time stamps (<code>pps-assert</code>)<a class="anchor" href="#linux-pps-time-stamps-pps-assert" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<p><strong><code>pps-assert</code></strong>: the time stamps the Linux PPS subsystem gives for each pulse, one per line as
+<code>/sys/class/pps/ppsN/assert</code> shows them: <code>&lt;seconds&gt;.&lt;nanoseconds&gt;#&lt;sequence&gt;</code>, the nanoseconds
+always nine digits. The pulse marks a whole second, so the offset is the time stamp&#39;s distance from
+the nearest second, in seconds: a host clock ahead of the pulse has a positive offset. The
+reading&#39;s time is that whole second. A line with the same sequence number as the line before it is
+the same pulse read twice and is not a new reading; other lines are skipped and counted. There is
+one source, so <code>clock</code> is refused. What is measured is the host clock (with the interrupt latency of
+the PPS input) against the pulse; how good the pulse is, is not known from the log.</p>
+<h2 id="live-kshana-clock-live">Live: <code>kshana clock-live</code><a class="anchor" href="#live-kshana-clock-live" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<pre data-lang="shell"><code class="hljs language-bash">journalctl -f -o <span class="hljs-built_in">cat</span> -u ptp4l | kshana clock-live --format ptp4l --from -
+kshana clock-live --format chrony-tracking --from /var/log/chrony/tracking.log --follow
+kshana clock-live --format pps-assert --from /sys/class/pps/pps0/assert --poll-s 0.2
+<span class="hljs-built_in">cat</span> /dev/ttyACM0 | kshana clock-live --format ticc --clock chA --tau0 1 --from -</code></pre>
+<p><code>clock-live</code> reads a <code>ptp4l</code>, <code>phc2sys</code>, <code>ts2phc</code>, <code>chrony-tracking</code>, <code>chrony-statistics</code>,
+<code>pps-assert</code> or <code>ticc</code> log as it is written: from standard input, <code>tcp:host:port</code>, a file (with <code>--follow</code>, it
+waits at the end for more lines), or with <code>--poll-s</code> a file read whole at every poll (the PPS
+<code>assert</code> file). It prints one JSON line per reading:</p>
+<pre data-lang="json"><code class="hljs language-json"><span class="hljs-punctuation">{</span><span class="hljs-attr">&quot;source&quot;</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">:</span><span class="hljs-string">&quot;-&quot;</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">,</span><span class="hljs-attr">&quot;t_s&quot;</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">:</span><span class="hljs-number">1003.0</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">,</span><span class="hljs-attr">&quot;offset_s&quot;</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">:</span><span class="hljs-number">4e-8</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">,</span><span class="hljs-attr">&quot;locked&quot;</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">:</span><span class="hljs-literal"><span class="hljs-keyword">true</span></span><span class="hljs-punctuation">,</span><span class="hljs-attr">&quot;seq&quot;</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">:</span><span class="hljs-literal"><span class="hljs-keyword">null</span></span><span class="hljs-punctuation">,</span><span class="hljs-attr">&quot;kept&quot;</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">:</span><span class="hljs-literal"><span class="hljs-keyword">true</span></span><span class="hljs-punctuation">,</span><span class="hljs-attr">&quot;n_kept&quot;</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">:</span><span class="hljs-number">1</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">,</span>
+ <span class="hljs-attr">&quot;rms_s&quot;</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">:</span><span class="hljs-number">4e-8</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">,</span><span class="hljs-attr">&quot;max_abs_s&quot;</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">:</span><span class="hljs-number">4e-8</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">,</span><span class="hljs-attr">&quot;since_last_s&quot;</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">:</span><span class="hljs-literal"><span class="hljs-keyword">null</span></span><span class="hljs-punctuation">}</span></code></pre>
+<p><code>kept</code> is true exactly for the readings the whole-log reader keeps: the source named by <code>--clock</code>
+(without it, the first source seen; every line of a chrony tracking log), linuxptp readings from the
+servo&#39;s first locked state (<code>s2</code>) on, each PPS pulse once, and the TICC channel named by <code>--clock</code> (time stamps need <code>--tau0</code>, which the
+whole-log reader would find from every stamp; the phase is the same femtosecond arithmetic, and a missing
+tick is a reading the replay fills, not one live invents). So the kept offsets of a live run are
+the phase series a replay of the same log gives (<code>tests/clock_live.rs</code>, on logs read whole and
+followed while they are written). <code>rms_s</code> and <code>max_abs_s</code> are over the last 64 kept offsets. It only
+reads: nothing is sent to the servo, the daemon or the device.</p>
+<h2 id="ntpd-and-white-rabbit-logs">ntpd and White Rabbit logs<a class="anchor" href="#ntpd-and-white-rabbit-logs" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<p><strong><code>ntp-loopstats</code></strong>, <strong><code>ntp-peerstats</code></strong>: ntpd&#39;s <code>loopstats</code> and <code>peerstats</code> statistics files,
+read by column position. Each reading starts with the modified Julian day and the seconds past
+midnight UTC.</p>
+<ul>
+<li>Loopstats: column 3, the local clock&#39;s offset on each update, in seconds; one local clock.</li>
+<li>Peerstats: column 5, a peer&#39;s measured offset, in seconds; one series per peer address
+(column 3), and the log needs <code>clock = &quot;&lt;address&gt;&quot;</code> when it holds more than one.</li>
+<li>Epochs are seconds from 1980-01-06 00:00:00 UTC (MJD 44244), with no leap-second handling.
+The spacing follows the poll interval; <code>tau0</code> is the median spacing, as for chrony.</li>
+<li>A line that does not have the columns or whose fields are not numbers is skipped and counted.</li>
+</ul>
+<p><strong><code>wr-stat</code></strong>: the <code>stat</code> lines of a White Rabbit PTP core: white-space separated <code>key:value</code>
+pairs. The reader needs <code>sec</code> (seconds, TAI) and <code>cko</code> (the clock offset, picoseconds), reads
+<code>nsec</code> when present, and reads <code>lock</code> as the servo state: readings before the first <code>lock:1</code> are
+a pull-in and are left out and counted, and a log that never locks is refused, as for linuxptp.
+Every other key (<code>crtt</code>, <code>setp</code>, <code>mu</code>, <code>temp</code>, ...) is ignored. A line without <code>sec</code> and <code>cko</code>
+is skipped and counted.</p>
+<h2 id="cggtts-time-transfer-files">CGGTTS time-transfer files<a class="anchor" href="#cggtts-time-transfer-files" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<p><strong><code>cggtts</code></strong>: BIPM CGGTTS version 2E track files, the common-view format that time laboratories
+publish. A data line is one satellite track (<code>SAT CL MJD STTIME TRKL ELV AZTH REFSV SRSV REFSYS ... CK</code>).</p>
+<ul>
+<li>A line counts only when its <code>CK</code> checksum holds: the sum, modulo 256, of the byte values of
+every character before <code>CK</code>, including the separating space. Lines that fail are skipped and
+counted, never dropped silently. The rule was checked on every data line of a public BIPM
+file (<code>scripts/fetch_cggtts.sh</code>, <code>tests/cggtts_bipm_reference.rs</code>).</li>
+<li><code>REFSV</code> and <code>REFSYS</code> are in units of 0.1 ns. A field of all 9s is a missing value.</li>
+<li>The time of a track is its start (<code>MJD</code>, <code>STTIME</code>, UTC) plus half of <code>TRKL</code>, as seconds from
+1980-01-06 00:00:00 UTC.</li>
+<li>The phase read is, per track start, the median <code>REFSYS</code> over satellites (the laboratory&#39;s
+reference minus GNSS system time), labelled <code>all in view</code>. With <code>clock = &quot;G04&quot;</code>, one
+satellite&#39;s <code>REFSYS</code> only.</li>
+<li>Tracks follow a 16-minute schedule that moves 4 minutes a day, so the spacing is uneven at a day
+change; <code>tau0</code> is the median spacing (960 s).</li>
+<li>Two laboratories&#39; files give a common-view difference (<code>kshana::cggtts::common_view</code>):
+<code>REFSV(a) − REFSV(b)</code> on the same satellite and track cancels the satellite clock. Its
+comparison with BIPM Circular T is pre-registered in
+<code>docs/design/evidence/r6-1-cggtts/PREREGISTRATION.md</code>.</li>
+</ul>
+<h2 id="trading-clocks-divergence-from-utc-against-rts-25-library">Trading clocks: divergence from UTC against RTS 25 (library)<a class="anchor" href="#trading-clocks-divergence-from-utc-against-rts-25-library" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<p><code>kshana::rts25::assess</code> takes a clock&#39;s measured offset from UTC (any log above, for example <code>ptp4l</code>,
+<code>chrony-tracking</code> or <code>cggtts</code>) and the activity the clock serves. It reports the largest divergence,
+the samples and stretches beyond the limit, and gaps longer than a stated interval. The limits are the
+maximum divergence from UTC in the Annex of Commission Delegated Regulation (EU) 2017/574 (RTS 25,
+MiFID II):</p>
+<div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr>
+<th>Activity</th>
+<th>Maximum divergence</th>
+<th>Granularity</th>
+</tr>
+</thead><tbody><tr>
+<td>Venue, gateway-to-gateway latency above 1 ms</td>
+<td>1 ms</td>
+<td>1 ms or finer</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>Venue, gateway-to-gateway latency 1 ms or less</td>
+<td>100 µs</td>
+<td>1 µs or finer</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>High-frequency algorithmic trading</td>
+<td>100 µs</td>
+<td>1 µs or finer</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>Voice trading; request-for-quote needing a human response; negotiated transactions</td>
+<td>1 s</td>
+<td>1 s or finer</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>Any other trading activity</td>
+<td>1 ms</td>
+<td>1 ms or finer</td>
+</tr>
+</tbody></table></div>
+<ul>
+<li>The outcome is <code>within limit</code>, <code>beyond limit</code> or <code>not assessable</code>. A record with no samples, or a
+gap longer than the stated interval and no excursion, is <code>not assessable</code>, because the divergence
+during the gap is unknown.</li>
+<li>What it does not do: it does not show traceability to UTC (the documented chain from the time source,
+which the Regulation also requires), and it does not measure the granularity of the firm&#39;s own
+timestamps. It describes a measured record against a stated limit and is not a regulatory assessment.</li>
+</ul>
