+<h2 id="imu-live-kshana-sensor-live---kind-imu">IMU live (<code>kshana sensor-live --kind imu</code>)<a class="anchor" href="#imu-live-kshana-sensor-live---kind-imu" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<pre data-lang="shell"><code class="hljs language-bash">my-imu-to-csv /dev/ttyUSB1 | kshana sensor-live --kind imu --format csv --from - --heading0 90
+ros2 topic <span class="hljs-built_in">echo</span> /imu/data sensor_msgs/msg/Imu --csv | kshana sensor-live --kind imu --format ros2-csv --from -
+kshana sensor-live --kind imu --format euroc --from imu0/data.csv --follow</code></pre>
+<p><code>sensor-live</code> reads the same two layouts a line at a time, from standard input, <code>tcp:host:port</code> or a
+file (<code>--follow</code> waits for more lines), and prints one JSON line per sample: <code>t_s</code>, <code>gyro</code>, <code>accel</code> and
+<code>heading_deg</code>, the gyro heading integrated from <code>--heading0</code> (default 0; <code>--z-up</code> for a body z axis
+pointing up). The line reader is the one <code>[log.imu]</code> uses, and the heading is summed in the same order as
+the <code>heading-course</code> monitor sums it, so the live samples and headings equal a replay of the same file, bit
+for bit (<code>tests/clock_live.rs</code>). A serial IMU is read through the tool that turns it into CSV lines; Kshana
+opens no device.</p>
+<h2 id="nmea-2000-from-a-can-bus-kshana-sensor-live---kind-n2k">NMEA 2000 from a CAN bus (<code>kshana sensor-live --kind n2k</code>)<a class="anchor" href="#nmea-2000-from-a-can-bus-kshana-sensor-live---kind-n2k" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<pre data-lang="shell"><code class="hljs language-bash">candump -L can0 | kshana sensor-live --kind n2k --from -
+kshana sensor-live --kind n2k --from recorded-candump.log &gt; vessel.nmea</code></pre>
+<p>Reads SocketCAN <code>candump -l</code>/<code>-L</code> lines (29-bit identifiers; 11-bit frames are not NMEA 2000 and are
+passed over) and prints NMEA 0183 sentences: PGN 129025 (position, rapid update) gives <code>$GPRMC</code> with the
+latest PGN 129026 course and speed; PGN 127250 (vessel heading) gives <code>$HEHDT</code> (true) or <code>$HCHDM</code>
+(magnetic); PGN 126992 (system time) dates the sentences, otherwise the frame&#39;s capture time does. Values
+marked &quot;not available&quot; are left empty. The layouts are the openly documented ones (<code>canboat</code>); the NMEA 2000
+standard is not reproduced. Multi-frame (fast-packet) groups, such as GNSS position data, are not read yet.
+The conversion is the same function over a live stream and over a recorded log (<code>kshana::n2k::convert</code>),
+and the output is read by the NMEA reader unchanged (<code>tests/clock_live.rs</code>). USB-CAN gateways that present a
+SocketCAN interface (<code>slcan</code>, <code>gs_usb</code>) work the same way; nothing is sent on the bus.</p>
+<h2 id="timing-and-health-of-live-inputs-clock-live-sensor-live">Timing and health of live inputs (<code>clock-live</code>, <code>sensor-live</code>)<a class="anchor" href="#timing-and-health-of-live-inputs-clock-live-sensor-live" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<p>Every live reading line carries <code>host_s</code>: the host clock when the line arrived, minus the device&#39;s
+latency (<code>--latency-s</code>, measured once against a common reference such as a PPS edge). The host clock is
+the one chrony, PTP or PPS disciplines, so readings from several devices on one machine share one time
+base whatever their own clocks report.</p>
+<p>Each live command watches its sensor and prints events as JSON lines beside the readings (<code>sensor-live --kind n2k</code> prints them on standard error, since its output is NMEA):</p>
+<div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr>
+<th>Flag</th>
+<th>Event</th>
+<th>When</th>
+</tr>
+</thead><tbody><tr>
+<td><code>--stale-s &lt;s&gt;</code></td>
+<td><code>stale</code>, then <code>stale-cleared</code></td>
+<td>no reading for that long, then one again</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td><code>--frozen-n &lt;n&gt;</code></td>
+<td><code>frozen</code>, then <code>frozen-cleared</code></td>
+<td>the same value <code>n</code> times in a row, then a change</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td><code>--range &lt;lo,hi&gt;</code></td>
+<td><code>out-of-range</code>, then <code>in-range</code></td>
+<td>the value leaves <code>[lo, hi]</code>, then returns</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td><code>--reconnect</code></td>
+<td><code>gap</code> with <code>from_s</code> and <code>host_s</code></td>
+<td>the device or connection ended or failed; it is opened again every second, and the time without input is reported when it is back</td>
+</tr>
+</tbody></table></div>
+<p>The watched value is the kept offset for <code>clock-live</code> and the magnitude of the specific force for an IMU.
+Each condition is reported once when it starts and once when it clears. A gap is marked, never filled: no
+reading is made up for it. <code>--sensor &lt;name&gt;</code> names the sensor in events. <code>tests/clock_live.rs</code> checks a
+TCP source dropped and brought back (the gap reported, the readings on both sides equal to the replay),
+and the frozen and range events. What needs the bench (L8): two sensors on one PPS agreeing within a
+pre-registered skew, and a real unplug and replug.</p>
