     <div class="prose"><h1 id="scenario-kinds">Scenario kinds</h1>
-<p>The 92 built-in scenario kinds that <code>kshana::api::run_toml</code> dispatches over, each with its one-line description and its required / optional TOML (Tom&#39;s Obvious, Minimal Language) fields.</p>
+<p>The 93 built-in scenario kinds that <code>kshana::api::run_toml</code> dispatches over, each with its one-line description and its required / optional TOML (Tom&#39;s Obvious, Minimal Language) fields.</p>
 <p>This file is <strong>generated</strong> from <code>api::list_scenario_kinds()</code> — the single source of truth — by <code>cargo run --bin gen_validation_artifacts</code>; edit the source, not this file. Every binding (the Python package, the MCP (Model Context Protocol) server&#39;s <code>list_scenario_kinds</code> tool, and the WebAssembly (WASM) playground) exposes this same catalogue, so what is listed here is exactly what every surface can run. The descriptions are terse and do not always expand their abbreviations; every one is spelled out in <a href="/docs/glossary">GLOSSARY.md</a>.</p>
 <p>Every scenario except <code>clock</code> must also set <code>kind = &quot;&lt;name&gt;&quot;</code> at the top level: a document without <code>kind</code> runs as <code>clock</code>, which is why <code>kind</code> is not repeated in any kind&#39;s required fields below. A required entry may name alternatives: <code>|</code> separates forms of which at least one must be present and <code>+</code> joins fields that must appear together, so <code>tle|orbit+epoch</code> reads &quot;a <code>tle</code>, or an <code>orbit</code> with an <code>epoch</code>&quot;.</p>
 <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr>
@@ -552,6 +552,11 @@
 <td><a href="#mission-thread"><code>mission-thread</code></a></td>
 <td>MODELLED navigation and timing error budget of a stated chain of mission phases, from launch to entry: launch and ascent, low-Earth-orbit rendezvous and docking (repeatable), trans-lunar injection and cislunar cruise, lunar-orbit rendezvous, descent, surface stay, ascent and the return and entry, each phase one of eight registered kinds with its own sensors and filter. The 6x6 covariance, the clock and the frame are handed from phase to phase under a stated rule (carry, inflate or reacquire); frame and timescale conversions (GCRF, MCI, MCMF, UTC, TT, TDB, LTC) are their own budget rows, with the Moon&#39;s state from an optional DE440 kernel cut and the analytic series as the stated fallback. An optional ground-network overlay sets the tracking duty of each phase. Reports the per-phase budget (entry, peak and exit 1-sigmas, every contribution with its unit), the margin against a stated requirement, the weakest link, the events and a timeline figure. MODELLED: every number is a stated input; the chain follows one stack and no vehicle is modelled. Public sources only; not affiliated with or endorsed by any agency; advisory software, not certified navigation equipment.</td>
 </tr>
+<tr>
+<td align="right">93</td>
+<td><a href="#tip-and-cue"><code>tip-and-cue</code></a></td>
+<td>MODELLED revisit and tip-and-cue latency of a two-layer maritime survey over the Baltic Sea: an RF survey layer and an optical layer, each a Walker star pattern of 550 km sun-synchronous orbits propagated with the secular J2 drift on a spherical Earth, with an elevation mask for the RF layer and an off-nadir field of regard (and optionally daylight) for the optical layer. Reports R, the area-weighted mean gap between RF accesses per point; F, the mean wait until every sea point has been in view; and Q, the mean wait from an RF access to an optical look, each with its median, 95th percentile and largest value. Geometry and timing only: no detection, weather or tasking latency. A cross-check of published simulation figures, never a validation.</td>
+</tr>
 </tbody></table></div>
 <h2 id="clock"><code>clock</code><a class="anchor" href="#clock" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
 <p>Clock holdover vs spec; optional Monte-Carlo ensemble (runs &gt; 1).</p>
@@ -1105,6 +1110,12 @@
 <li><strong>Required fields:</strong> <code>epoch_utc</code>, <code>entry</code>, <code>phase</code></li>
 <li><strong>Optional fields:</strong> <code>kind</code>, <code>title</code>, <code>seed</code>, <code>moon_kernel_path</code>, <code>ground_network</code></li>
 </ul>
+<h2 id="tip-and-cue"><code>tip-and-cue</code><a class="anchor" href="#tip-and-cue" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<p>MODELLED revisit and tip-and-cue latency of a two-layer maritime survey over the Baltic Sea: an RF survey layer and an optical layer, each a Walker star pattern of 550 km sun-synchronous orbits propagated with the secular J2 drift on a spherical Earth, with an elevation mask for the RF layer and an off-nadir field of regard (and optionally daylight) for the optical layer. Reports R, the area-weighted mean gap between RF accesses per point; F, the mean wait until every sea point has been in view; and Q, the mean wait from an RF access to an optical look, each with its median, 95th percentile and largest value. Geometry and timing only: no detection, weather or tasking latency. A cross-check of published simulation figures, never a validation.</p>
+<ul>
+<li><strong>Required fields:</strong> <em>(none)</em></li>
+<li><strong>Optional fields:</strong> <code>kind</code>, <code>title</code>, <code>seed</code>, <code>area</code>, <code>step_s</code>, <code>span_days</code>, <code>m2</code>, <code>cases</code></li>
+</ul>
 </div>
