 <h2 id="syslog-cef-and-leef">Syslog: CEF and LEEF<a class="anchor" href="#syslog-cef-and-leef" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
 <p>Events are sent in an RFC 5424 envelope (facility local0, app <code>kshana</code>, message id
 <code>GNSSTRUST</code>) over UDP (one datagram each; IPv4 or IPv6) or TCP, newline-terminated by
@@ -440,6 +444,71 @@ label, so an input without one renders exactly as before.</p>
 <p>Empty fields are left out (no <code>cs2=</code> without reasons). The host name is cut to 255
 characters. CEF header values escape <code>\</code> and <code>|</code>; extension values escape <code>\</code>, <code>=</code> and line breaks.
 LEEF values have <code>^</code> and line breaks replaced by spaces.</p>
+<h2 id="common-alerting-protocol-cap-12">Common Alerting Protocol (CAP 1.2)<a class="anchor" href="#common-alerting-protocol-cap-12" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<p><code>kshana::telemetry::cap::alerts</code> turns the trust stream into OASIS CAP 1.2 messages, the format that
+public-warning, maritime and aviation alerting systems take in. One message is written for each band
+change after calibration:</p>
+<div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr>
+<th>Change</th>
+<th><code>msgType</code></th>
+<th><code>urgency</code> / <code>severity</code></th>
+</tr>
+</thead><tbody><tr>
+<td>nominal → degraded</td>
+<td><code>Alert</code></td>
+<td><code>Expected</code> / <code>Moderate</code></td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>nominal → untrusted</td>
+<td><code>Alert</code></td>
+<td><code>Immediate</code> / <code>Severe</code></td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>degraded ↔ untrusted</td>
+<td><code>Update</code> (references the previous message)</td>
+<td>as above</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td>back to nominal</td>
+<td><code>Update</code> (references the previous message)</td>
+<td><code>Past</code> / <code>Minor</code>: the all-clear</td>
+</tr>
+</tbody></table></div>
+<ul>
+<li>Every message carries the band, the monitors behind it, the score and the advisory statement. Its
+area is a circle around the receiver&#39;s reported position, or the configured station position.</li>
+<li><code>status</code> (<code>Actual</code>, <code>Exercise</code>, <code>System</code>, <code>Test</code>, <code>Draft</code>) and <code>scope</code> have no default, so an exercise
+cannot go out as <code>Actual</code> by accident.</li>
+<li>The messages are checked against the OASIS CAP 1.2 schema (<code>tests/telemetry_cap.rs</code>; the schema is
+fetched by <code>scripts/fetch_cap_xsd.sh</code>, not vendored).</li>
+</ul>
+<p>From the command line, <code>--cap-config cap.toml</code> turns alerting on, <code>--cap-dir &lt;dir&gt;</code> writes each message
+to <code>&lt;dir&gt;/&lt;identifier&gt;.xml</code>, and <code>--webhook</code> posts it (next section):</p>
+<pre data-lang="toml"><code class="hljs language-ini"><span class="hljs-attr">sender</span> = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;kshana@station.example&quot;</span>
+<span class="hljs-attr">status</span> = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;Exercise&quot;</span>            <span class="hljs-comment"># Actual, Exercise, System, Test or Draft: no default</span>
+<span class="hljs-attr">scope</span> = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;Restricted&quot;</span>           <span class="hljs-comment"># Public, Restricted or Private: no default</span>
+<span class="hljs-attr">area_desc</span> = <span class="hljs-string">&quot;Example port and approaches&quot;</span>
+<span class="hljs-attr">station</span> = [<span class="hljs-number">59.44</span>, <span class="hljs-number">24.75</span>]       <span class="hljs-comment"># used when the stream carries no position</span>
+<span class="hljs-attr">radius_km</span> = <span class="hljs-number">25.0</span></code></pre>
+<pre data-lang="shell"><code class="hljs language-bash">kshana receiver-trust live session.toml --tcp 192.0.2.10:10110 --json - \
+  | kshana trust-telemetry --cap-config cap.toml --cap-dir alerts/</code></pre>
+<p>The date of <code>t_s = 0</code> comes from the first epoch&#39;s UTC time label when it carries one, else from the
+clock of the machine running <code>trust-telemetry</code>.</p>
+<h2 id="signed-webhooks">Signed webhooks<a class="anchor" href="#signed-webhooks" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
+<p><code>kshana::telemetry::webhook::sign</code> adds a signature to any alert body (a CAP message, a trust sample as
+JSON) so the receiving system can check that it came from the holder of a shared secret, was not
+changed, and is fresh:</p>
+<pre data-lang="text"><code class="hljs">Kshana-Signature: t=&lt;UNIX seconds&gt;,v1=&lt;hex HMAC-SHA256 of &quot;&lt;t&gt;.&lt;body&gt;&quot;&gt;</code></pre>
+<p><code>verify</code> recomputes the signature, compares in constant time, and refuses a timestamp further than a
+stated tolerance from the receiver&#39;s clock (the replay window). While a secret is being rotated, two
+<code>v1=</code> values may be sent, and either may match. The HMAC is checked against the RFC 4231 test vectors
+(<code>tests/telemetry_webhook.rs</code>). Keep the secret out of logs and scenario files.</p>
+<p><code>--webhook http://host:port/path --webhook-secret-env &lt;VAR&gt;</code> (with <code>--cap-config</code>) POSTs each CAP message
+as <code>application/cap+xml</code>, signed with the secret read from the environment variable <code>&lt;VAR&gt;</code>, never from
+the command line. The webhook is plain HTTP: for HTTPS, post to a local relay. A delivery that fails, or
+is answered with anything other than 2xx, is counted. The count is printed at the end, and a failed
+delivery never stops the stream. <code>tests/telemetry_alerts.rs</code> runs the command against a local receiver:
+every POST verifies, and the messages equal the library&#39;s for the whole stream.</p>
