istics log (s, by column position, UTC date and time) becomes a uniform phase grid through the same gap policies as the other formats; a source selector (<code>clock</code>) names one of several interfaces, clocks or addresses; the logs feed the telecom-timing series, the clock kind and the holdover-test kind</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Fixtures written for Kshana from the public line layouts of the daemons, with made-up values (NOTICE.md in the fixture folder); no log from a real installation was read and no oracle compares the parse with another reader, so a layout that differs between daemon versions can still be refused or misread. The estimators fed by the phase series are the ones validated against allantools in the rows above</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>clock_log::tests (ptp4l_reads_the_locked_offsets_in_ns; linuxptp_reads_only_the_named_program; ts2phc_gap_is_refused_or_filled; linuxptp_reads_syslog_prefixed_lines_and_picks_an_interface…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Holdover test scored from an operator-supplied time-error record</span><code class="lg-mod">holdover_<wbr>test <wbr>(HoldoverTestScenario,<wbr> <wbr>recovery,<wbr> <wbr>linear_<wbr>fit); <wbr>telecom_<wbr>timing <wbr>(check_<wbr>mask,<wbr> <wbr>check_<wbr>envelope,<wbr> <wbr>budget_<wbr>results)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>holdover-test</code> kind: a time-error record (a linuxptp, chrony, TICC, TimeLab or RINEX clock log, or time_s,time_error_ns pairs) is split at the two times the operator states for GNSS removal and restoration into a baseline, a holdover and a recovery step; the baseline is measured against the ITU-T masks written for a locked clock and the holdover against those written for holdover or an autonomous clock, the ePRTC-A holdover envelope and the time-error budgets; the holdover reports the least-squares frequency offset the system held, and the recovery whether and when |TE| came inside a stated band and stayed for a stated dwell, with the peak and the largest step. Every limit is the one the telecom-timing kind transcribes; the two times are the operator&#x27;s statement and are not verified</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Hand computation on records built from formulas whose slope, step, extremum and recovery time are known before the run (the tests state them), and the masks, envelope and budgets of the telecom-timing rows above. No laboratory measurement of a real holdover was scored and no external oracle was compared, so the kind makes no claim about any clock or system; a timing-lab partner is needed before any per-clock-class figure could be promoted</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>holdover_test::tests (the_record_is_split_at_the_stated_times; holdover_figures_match_a_hand_computation; recovery_is_timed_from_the_restoration; masks_are_measured_on_the_step_their_condition_names…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">GNSS signal availability in the Space Service Volume and beyond, compared with the ICG SSV booklet</span><code class="lg-mod">ssv <wbr>(constellations,<wbr> <wbr>geom,<wbr> <wbr>sweep,<wbr> <wbr>pattern,<wbr> <wbr>check)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>gnss-ssv</code> kind&#x27;s availability sweeps: per constellation (GPS, GLONASS, Galileo, BDS, QZSS, NavIC) and combined, the share of (user, epoch) pairs with at least one and at least four signals and the longest outage, for a user on a sphere (the booklet&#x27;s 2,562-point global shell, 14 days at 1 minute) or on a trajectory (GEO, HEO, lunar transfer), from the Keplerian orbits of the booklet&#x27;s annex C, a constant minimum radiated power inside the reference off-boresight angle, Earth blockage by the sphere plus 50 km and a C/N0 threshold; user antennas from the booklet&#x27;s table B10</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>The ICG Space Service Volume booklet (UN OOSA ST/SPACE/75, 2018), itself a simulation, compared under a pre-registration committed before the run (xval/gnss-ssv/PREREGISTRATION.md; results in xval/gnss-ssv/RESULTS.md, pinned by tests/ssv_booklet_results.rs, misses included). Run 1 on the pre-registered reading: the minimum-radiated-power constants and the lunar bar agree (S0, S6 met); the upper and lower SSV, RF-threshold, GEO and HEO bars are NOT MET, almost all through GLONASS, for which the booklet gives 20 and 28 degrees in tables B2 and B6 and 26 and 34 degrees in table 4.2. A diagnostic variant with table 4.2&#x27;s angles agrees in every cell of the upper SSV, lower SSV and the six GEO missions (it does not replace run 1; RF, HEO and lunar cells still miss). The measured GPS L1 panel patterns are not reproduced by the parametric stand-in (RMS 6 to 9 dB against a 3 dB bar). Agreement shows the booklet&#x27;s stated method is reproduced, not that GNSS signals behave so</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>ssv::geom::tests (the_grid_has_the_booklets_2562_points_on_the_unit_sphere; the_interval_form_equals_the_general_predicate; a_user_behind_the_earth_is_blocked_and_one_beside_the_limb_is_not); ssv::sweep::tests…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Limb-ray ionosphere, plasmasphere and atmosphere effects on grazing GNSS signals, and the weak-signal receiver design, in the <code>gnss-ssv</code> kind</span><code class="lg-mod">ssv <wbr>(limb,<wbr> <wbr>weak)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>A Chapman ionosphere, a Gallagher-type plasmasphere and a neutral atmosphere (refractivity bending, oxygen absorption, defocusing) integrated along the straight ray between transmitter and user: slant total electron content, its plasmaspheric part, excess delay, tangent altitude and the loss on grazing links; and a weak-signal receiver design (coherent and non-coherent integration, data-bit limit, aiding, Doppler search) that sets the C/N0 threshold from the acquisition and tracking-loop models</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Closed forms and published magnitudes in the tests (a Chapman layer&#x27;s peak and the bending of the surface limb, a few TECU of plasmasphere on a GPS-to-GEO ray); the receiver design reuses the acquisition and tracking-loop models and their tests. No measured limb-sounding or weak-signal reception data is compared with it</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>ssv::limb::tests (the_chapman_layer_peaks_at_its_height_with_its_density; the_surface_limb_bends_about_one_and_a_third_degrees; plasmaspheric_tec_is_a_few_tecu_for_an_equatorial_gps_to_geo_ray); ssv::weak::tests…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Onboard GNSS navigation filter for high-orbit and lunar-transfer spacecraft</span><code class="lg-mod">ssv::<wbr>nav <wbr>(run_<wbr>nav,<wbr> <wbr>Ekf)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>gnss-ssv</code> kind&#x27;s <code>[navigation]</code> mode: a nine-state extended Kalman filter (position, velocity, receiver clock bias and drift, a solar-radiation-pressure scale) on pseudoranges whose availability, noise and C/N0 come from the same link geometry as the sweeps; truth is integrated with J2, Sun and Moon third-body and radiation pressure with shadow, the filter models J2 and the third bodies and estimates the radiation pressure; reports position and velocity errors inside and above the GNSS orbits, the longest measurement gap and the mean NEES</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Simulated truth against the filter&#x27;s own estimate (a consistent filter&#x27;s mean normalised estimation error squared is about 3 for the position) and determinism for a fixed seed. No flight or recorded high-orbit GNSS data is compared; the Sun and Moon are low-precision circular stand-ins</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>ssv::nav::tests (the_filter_converges_where_signals_are_plentiful_and_is_deterministic; the_covariance_is_consistent_with_the_error; without_signals_the_filter_coasts_and_the_gap_is_reported); ssv::scenario::tests…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Relative navigation of a multi-spacecraft formation in a highly elliptical orbit from GNSS</span><code class="lg-mod">ssv::<wbr>formation <wbr>(run_<wbr>formation,<wbr> <wbr>RelEkf)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>gnss-ssv</code> kind&#x27;s <code>[formation]</code> mode: a chief and deputies each run the absolute filter; the deputy&#x27;s position relative to the chief is estimated by subtracting the absolute estimates and by a relative filter on single-differenced pseudoranges of the signals both craft track, with the relative dynamics the difference of two nonlinear trajectories</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>The same simulated truth as the absolute filter: the relative filter&#x27;s error against the true relative state, and the check that it does at least as well as subtracting two independent absolute fixes. No flight formation data is compared</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>ssv::formation::tests (differencing_beats_subtracting_absolute_estimates_and_is_deterministic); ssv::scenario::tests (navigation_and_formation_run_and_report; formation_needs_navigation_and_one_mode_only)</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Navigation and timing error budget of a chain of mission phases from launch to entry (<code>mission-thread</code>)</span><code class="lg-mod">mission_<wbr>thread <wbr>(scenario,<wbr> <wbr>handover,<wbr> <wbr>conversions,<wbr> <wbr>budget,<wbr> <wbr>phase)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>mission-thread</code> kind: a document of phases, each one of eight registered kinds, run in order with the 6x6 covariance, the clock and the frame handed on under a stated rule (<code>carry</code>; <code>inflate</code>, white acceleration noise over a stated gap on a straight-line model; <code>reacquire</code>, dead reckoning then the source floor, never below it, never a reset); frame conversions GCRF, MCI and MCMF and timescale conversions through TT, each its own budget row, with the Moon&#x27;s state from an optional DE440 kernel cut and the analytic series as the stated fallback; the margin against a stated requirement, the weakest link, the events and a timeline figure. Every number is a stated input or a model of one</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Hand calculations in the tests (the straight-line covariance growth, the mean sidereal rotation, the secular lunar clock rate) and, for the Moon&#x27;s state only, the committed DE440 kernel cuts against pinned JPL Horizons values (worst 2.6 m and 7e-6 m/s). No mission data is compared with the chain; its phases are rows of their own. The document is a stated chain with ILLUSTRATIVE inputs, public sources only and no agency endorsement</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>mission_thread::scenario::tests::a_thread_runs_hands_over_converts_and_names_the_weakest_link; mission_thread::scenario::tests::bad_documents_fail_with_the_reason…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Launch and ascent to a parking orbit with GNSS acquisition and fade (<code>launch-ascent</code>)</span><code class="lg-mod">mission_<wbr>thread::<wbr>earth::<wbr>launch <wbr>(LaunchAscent)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>launch-ascent</code> phase: a constant-velocity position and velocity covariance with white acceleration noise (the dead-reckoning grade), a GNSS fix of stated accuracy applied after the acquisition time and outside stated fades, a bias-and-drift clock disciplined by the same fixes, and the parking-orbit insertion state</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>The Riccati steady state and the hand calculation of the prediction in the tests. Nothing here models a launch vehicle or a receiver; every number is a stated input</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>mission_thread::earth::launch::tests::with_gnss_the_sigma_settles_near_the_fix_and_an_outage_lets_it_grow; mission_thread::earth::launch::tests::before_acquisition_the_sigma_only_grows_and_a_longer_acquisition_is_worse…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Relative navigation to the docking envelope in low Earth orbit over sensor legs (<code>leo-rendezvous</code>)</span><code class="lg-mod">mission_<wbr>thread::<wbr>earth::<wbr>rendezvous <wbr>(LeoRendezvous); <wbr>cw_<wbr>dynamics; <wbr>mission_<wbr>thread::<wbr>sensors</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>leo-rendezvous</code> phase: the relative state in the target&#x27;s LVLH axes with the closed-form Clohessy-Wiltshire transition, a 6x6 Kalman covariance with white acceleration noise and position updates whose variance is the sensor&#x27;s at the current range, over legs of a stated sensor (ILLUSTRATIVE optical camera, lidar and radio-ranging forms); the docking window is the time from which the relative sigma stays inside the capture envelope to the end of the phase. The chaser&#x27;s absolute covariance is handed on unchanged and a row says so</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>A one-axis hand calculation of the predict and update in the test; the Clohessy-Wiltshire transition is the one cw_dynamics checks against an independent numerical integration. The sensors are ILLUSTRATIVE and no real rendezvous is compared</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>mission_thread::earth::rendezvous::tests::the_relative_sigma_falls_through_the_legs_and_opens_a_docking_window; mission_thread::earth::rendezvous::tests::without_relative_sensing_the_sigma_only_grows_and_no_window_opens…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Trans-lunar injection and cislunar cruise: propagated mean state and covariance with tracking updates (<code>tli-and-cruise</code>)</span><code class="lg-mod">mission_<wbr>thread::<wbr>earth::<wbr>tli_<wbr>cruise <wbr>(TliCruise); <wbr>propagator; <wbr>ssv::<wbr>sweep</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>tli-and-cruise</code> phase: the mean state by the engine&#x27;s Cowell propagator (Earth point mass and J2, the Sun and Moon as third bodies, the Moon from the thread&#x27;s DE440 kernel where it covers the segment and the Sun from the analytic series); the covariance by a forward-difference state-transition matrix of that propagation (the nominal run and six perturbed runs per segment) with white acceleration noise; the TLI burn&#x27;s size and pointing error; <code>gnss-ssv</code> fixes whose availability and PDOP come from the space-service-volume sweep along an osculating orbit standing in for the arc; range and range-rate tracking along the geocentric direction scaled by the ground-network duty, with the Joseph-form update</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Liouville&#x27;s theorem (the determinant of the state-transition matrix of the conservative part is one) and a finer difference in the tests; the mean state is compared with a public trajectory in the row below, which it misses. The burn error, the updates and the covariance are not compared with anything</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>mission_thread::earth::tli_cruise::tests::the_stm_is_symplectic_for_the_conservative_part_and_matches_a_finer_difference; mission_thread::earth::tli_cruise::tests::with_no_tracking_the_covariance_is_the_stm_propagation_…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Entry interface covariance, corridor probability, peak deceleration and landing footprint (<code>entry</code>)</span><code class="lg-mod">mission_<wbr>thread::<wbr>earth::<wbr>entry <wbr>(Entry); <wbr>reentry</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>entry</code> phase: the return coast of <code>tli-and-cruise</code> with no burn, ending at the entry interface; the flight-path angle and its sigma from the full covariance by a finite-difference gradient; the probability that the angle is inside a stated corridor; the peak deceleration at the nominal angle and at both corridor edges from the engine&#x27;s planar ballistic point-mass entry (skip-out detected) beside the Allen-Eggers closed form; the landing footprint from a stated downrange sensitivity and the interface covariance across the orbit plane. No lift, guidance or heating is modelled</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Hand calculations in the tests (the angle sigma is the perpendicular velocity sigma over the speed; the Gaussian mass in a corridor). The planar entry is the engine&#x27;s own and the Allen-Eggers form is a closed-form comparison; no flown entry is compared</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>mission_thread::earth::entry::tests::the_flight_path_angle_and_its_sigma_follow_the_velocity_error_over_the_speed; mission_thread::earth::entry::tests::the_corridor_probability_is_the_gaussian_mass_inside…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Ground-network coverage and tracking duty for a thread phase (<code>[ground_network]</code> of <code>mission-thread</code>)</span><code class="lg-mod">mission_<wbr>thread::<wbr>earth::<wbr>ground_<wbr>network</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The ground-network overlay: stations with a minimum elevation, the visibility of the vehicle on a stated geometry (the entry state flown two-body with J2, the Moon&#x27;s position for the cruise, or a stated duty), station handovers that lose a stated time each, and the resulting duty passed to the phase&#x27;s ground-tracking update rate; the longest interval with no station in view is a budget row</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Zenith and antipode geometry checks and the expected visibility of a distant target in the tests. No real network, antenna or schedule is modelled</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>mission_thread::earth::ground_network::tests::a_vehicle_over_a_station_is_seen_and_one_on_the_other_side_of_the_earth_is_not; mission_thread::earth::ground_network::tests::the_moon_is_in_view_of_three_well_spread_statio…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Relative navigation to docking in a lunar orbit (<code>lunar-orbit-rendezvous</code>)</span><code class="lg-mod">lunar_<wbr>rendezvous <wbr>(LunarRendezvousScenario); <wbr>lunar_<wbr>phase; <wbr>mission_<wbr>thread::<wbr>lunar</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>lunar-orbit-rendezvous</code> kind and phase: a joint two-vehicle batch Gauss-Newton estimate over the arc (twenty-one parameters: both states and persistent signal-in-space position biases, common and independent), grown over a quarter, a half and the whole arc with step halving, from an absolute fix per vehicle (AFS pilot code-tracking bound, ILLUSTRATIVE relay set) and a crosslink range, under the crate&#x27;s lunar force model; the mean normalised estimation error squared, the Clohessy-Wiltshire check on a circular orbit and the 6x6 covariance handed on</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Self-consistency under a pre-registration (xval/mission-thread-lunar/PREREGISTRATION.md, results and the estimator history in RESULTS.md): the mean NEES of the relative state is 5.85 to 5.93 against the band 5.2 to 6.8 in four configurations, MET on the fourth estimator design after three sequential-filter designs missed; the truth and the estimator share one force model, so this says nothing about model error. The propagator it shares is compared with LRO and CAPSTONE in a row below</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>lunar_rendezvous::tests::b1_mean_nees_is_inside_the_registered_band_in_all_four_configurations; lunar_rendezvous::tests::circular_cw_check_is_within_the_pre_registered_bar…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Landing-dispersion budget at a lunar polar site (<code>lunar-descent</code>)</span><code class="lg-mod">lunar_<wbr>landing <wbr>(LunarLandingScenario); <wbr>lunar_<wbr>phase; <wbr>terrain_<wbr>nav</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>lunar-descent</code> kind and phase: a five-state per-axis inertial error model (position, velocity, accelerometer bias, tilt, gyro bias) over a powered descent, aided by AFS fixes (standalone or differential, with the clock-aided three-relay case), a terrain-relative fix (the engine&#x27;s terrain-nav matcher on synthetic relief, or a Cramer-Rao bound on a LOLA DEM with an aperture-problem rule) and lunar time, with a Monte Carlo touchdown ellipse</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>The closed-form inertial drift, the reused kinds&#x27; own results and the calibration of the 3-sigma ellipse (the fraction inside agrees with its expectation) in the tests. No flown or tested landing is compared</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>lunar_landing::tests::b4_with_no_aid_the_landing_error_is_the_closed_form_inertial_drift; lunar_landing::tests::b4_each_aid_reports_exactly_what_the_reused_kind_gives; lunar_landing::tests::b3_ellipse_calibration…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Surface positioning and timing availability by site and season at the lunar poles (<code>lunar-surface</code>)</span><code class="lg-mod">lunar_<wbr>surface_<wbr>map <wbr>(LunarSurfaceMapScenario); <wbr>lunar_<wbr>phase</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>lunar-surface</code> kind and phase: relay availability and PDOP, Earth and Sun visibility and a disciplined surface clock for each site and season, with the terrain skyline of <code>moonlight-service-volume</code>; a single-site run equals that kind&#x27;s availability and PDOP to the bit</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Exact equality with the existing kind for one site, and monotonicity checks, in the tests. The relay set is ILLUSTRATIVE and no surface measurement is compared</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>lunar_surface_map::tests::b5_equals_the_service_volume_for_a_single_site; lunar_surface_map::tests::b5_equals_the_service_volume_with_the_real_terrain; lunar_surface_map::tests::terrain_never_adds_availability…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Powered ascent from a lunar polar site to an insertion orbit (<code>lunar-ascent</code>)</span><code class="lg-mod">lunar_<wbr>ascent <wbr>(LunarAscentScenario); <wbr>lunar_<wbr>phase</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>lunar-ascent</code> kind and phase: the five-state per-axis inertial error model run over a powered ascent with specific force along the path, aided by AFS fixes and lunar time, reporting the insertion position and velocity covariance carried to orbit and the engine-cutoff dispersion (reported, not carried)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>The closed-form inertial drift and monotonicity checks in the tests. No flown ascent is compared</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>lunar_ascent::tests::with_no_aid_the_insertion_error_is_the_closed_form_inertial_drift; lunar_ascent::tests::the_aid_never_hurts_and_the_velocity_is_carried_with_its_correlation…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">The lunar force model propagation against public reconstructed lunar-orbiter and cislunar trajectories</span><code class="lg-mod">lunar_<wbr>od <wbr>(LunarForceModel); <wbr>precise_<wbr>od; <wbr>mission_<wbr>thread_<wbr>lunar_<wbr>oracles <wbr>(example)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The propagator the lunar kinds share (GRAIL GL0660B degree and order 150, Earth and Sun third bodies, relativity, DE440 lunar orientation) compared with the public JPL Horizons trajectories of LRO (24 h, 10 minute samples) and CAPSTONE (one 6.5 day revolution, one hour samples), under bars fixed in a committed file before the run</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>JPL Horizons spacecraft ephemerides for LRO and CAPSTONE, against bars pre-registered in xval/mission-thread-lunar/PREREGISTRATION.md (Amendments 1 to 6). LRO: MET, 33 m largest 3-D difference and 2.4 m RMS radial against 500 m and 60 m. CAPSTONE: NOT MET, a 136.8 km end-position difference against 25 km and a 28.4 km periapsis difference against 10 km, the causes not isolated. The row is Modelled because the CAPSTONE bar is missed and the comparison tests the shared propagator, not any filter, signal or budget</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/mission_thread_lunar_oracles.rs::the_stored_numbers_meet_or_miss_the_registered_bars_as_recorded; tests/mission_thread_lunar_oracles.rs::the_fixtures_match_their_recorded_hashes</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">The cislunar mean-state propagation against the public Artemis I trajectory (<code>tli-and-cruise</code>)</span><code class="lg-mod">mission_<wbr>thread::<wbr>earth::<wbr>tli_<wbr>cruise; <wbr>propagator; <wbr>mission_<wbr>thread_<wbr>oracle <wbr>(example)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The mean-state propagation of the <code>tli-and-cruise</code> phase (Cowell, Earth point mass and J2, the Sun and Moon third bodies, the Moon from a DE440 cut) compared with the public JPL Horizons trajectory of Artemis I over a 72 h coast arc from 2022-11-16 18:00 TDB, at 12 hour epochs, under bars fixed in a committed file before the run</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>JPL Horizons target -1023 (the NASA navigation as-flown post-TLI trajectory), against bars pre-registered in xval/mission-thread/PREREGISTRATION.md (Amendment 1 changes only the request span). NOT MET and asserted as missed: 45.3 km largest position difference against 5 km, and 0.249 m/s velocity difference at 72 h against 0.05 m/s. A diagnostic written after the verdict places the miss in five impulsive velocity steps of 0.09 to 0.22 m/s in the reference (the pre-registered reading that the window held no burn was wrong); over the other 67 hours the model reproduces the next hourly state to a median of 0.38 m. Tests the mean state of one coast arc only: no covariance, filter, update or other phase</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/mission_thread_oracle.rs::the_stored_numbers_miss_the_registered_bars_as_recorded; tests/mission_thread_oracle.rs::the_diagnostic_places_the_miss_in_five_impulsive_events_not_in_the_force_model…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="quantum"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Cold-atom interferometer error terms: Raman laser-phase noise, wavefront-curvature bias and fringe-ambiguity resolution</span><code class="lg-mod">inertial::<wbr>cai_<wbr>error_<wbr>terms</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>inertial::cai_error_terms (library functions, not yet reachable from a scenario kind): the Raman-phase weighting function of a square-pulse three-pulse interferometer (Cheinet et al. 2008, Eq. 8) with the white-phase closed form (pi/2)^2 S_phi0/tau_R and a numeric route for any spectrum; the laser frequency noise the retro-reflection delay does not cancel (Le Gouet et al. 2007, Eqs. 9 to 11); the pure-defocus wavefront bias sigma_v^2/R of a thermal cloud and its reduction by a detection radius; the wrong-fringe-index probability erfc(pi/(sqrt(2) s)) for a classical-accelerometer aid and for two interrogation times, and its inverse</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Each closed form against an independent numerical route in the tests (frequency-domain quadrature of the exact transfer function and delay filter, a Monte Carlo over atoms for the wavefront bias, a Monte Carlo over shots for the fringe index) and its limiting cases. One published worked value: Cheinet et al. 2008 (arXiv:physics/0510197, Sec. IV) state that at tau_R = 20 us a white Raman phase noise below -111 dB rad^2/Hz is needed for 1 mrad per shot; the white term alone gives 0.99 mrad (the paper derives the level with its Eq. 17, flicker plus white). Internal consistency and one worked value, not an external validation; higher-order aberrations and any specific laser&#x27;s noise are not modelled</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>inertial::cai_error_terms::tests (raman_transfer_short_pulse_limit_matches_the_acceleration_transfer_function; white_raman_phase_closed_form_matches_quadrature; short_pulse_band_limited_closed_form_matches_quadrature…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Greenhall and Riley exact equivalent degrees of freedom of the Allan, modified Allan and Hadamard variances</span><code class="lg-mod">allan <wbr>(edf_<wbr>greenhall,<wbr> <wbr>greenhall_<wbr>edf_<wbr>core); <wbr>assurance::<wbr>uncertainty <wbr>(edf)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>allan::edf_greenhall (with the edf_greenhall_adev, _mdev and _hdev wrappers): the full algorithm of Greenhall and Riley, Proc. 35th PTTI (2003), Section 3 (Eqns 7 to 13 and the large-J coefficients of Tables 1 to 3), for difference orders 1 to 3, noise exponents +2 to -4 wherever alpha + 2d &gt; 1, overlapping and non-overlapping, modified and unmodified estimators; NaN where it does not apply, including the unmodified white-PM case with ceil(M/S) &lt;= d, whose closed form is not implemented. assurance::uncertainty::edf now shares the code and keeps its outputs bit for bit</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>allantools 2024.6 ci.edf_greenhall, a third-party implementation of the published algorithm, run by scripts/gen_greenhall_edf_ref.py over the grid pre-registered in xval/greenhall-edf/PREREGISTRATION.md (committed alone, 0db0bb7a, before any comparison): at all 4 876 points where it returns a finite edf, kshana agrees within a relative 6.0e-13 (bar 1e-9); at its 46 NotImplementedError points kshana returns NaN; every case label occurs; the wrappers equal the core bit for bit; uncertainty::edf is bit-identical over 274 280 values (pinned as a SHA-256 before the refactor). At 32 points (alpha -4, d 3, unmodified, case 2.2) allantools fails in its Table 2 lookup, so they have no oracle value and are not counted. Stable32 was not run. 0.36 revision (docs/revisions/STUDIO15-greenhall-edf-portable.md): on portable maths the kernel takes its logarithm from a double-double evaluation rounded once (allan::greenhall_ln), and the unchanged Bar 1 holds again at all 4 876 points, worst relative 6.0e-13; the uncertainty::edf guard is re-based to the 0.36 portable dump (3 746 of the 274 280 values moved, at most 1.6e-12 relative, from the host-maths base)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/greenhall_edf_allantools.rs (bars_1_to_3_against_allantools; bar_4_wrappers_equal_the_core); tests/greenhall_edf_no_moved_output.rs (uncertainty_edf_is_bit_identical_to_the_0_36_portable_baseline)…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Oscillator holdover presets from public datasheets</span><code class="lg-mod">telecom_<wbr>timing <wbr>(PRESETS,<wbr> <wbr>fit_<wbr>noise,<wbr> <wbr>with_<wbr>flicker_<wbr>floor,<wbr> <wbr>synthesize_<wbr>holdover); <wbr>models <wbr>(ClockModel)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Four presets — OCXO (Microchip OX-208), rubidium (Microchip 8040C), caesium (Microchip 5071A high-performance tube) and CSAC (Microchip SA.45s) — each carrying its datasheet&#x27;s Allan-deviation maxima, aging and temperature bound; a non-negative least-squares white + flicker + random-walk frequency-noise fit scaled to envelope every datasheet point, a flicker floor never below the longest-τ figure, linear aging and a sinusoidal temperature term, synthesised as a seeded holdover after a GNSS loss</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>The four named Microchip datasheets (document numbers and URLs in each preset and in docs/TELECOM-TIMING.md) for the input figures; the closed-form aging ramp and the fit&#x27;s envelope property as internal checks. How the figures become a noise model — the fit, the floor rule, the 30-day month, the linear temperature reading — is a modelling choice; no preset is a measurement of a unit. 0.30 round 2, one-sided bars against measured units (pre-registered a01c491c), a finding (stays MODELLED): the IGS station THU2 Symmetricom 8040C in ESA MGEX final clocks has an Allan deviation 0.35 to 0.87 of the rubidium preset but a holdover error 1.07 to 1.60 of it; the 5071A record sits outside the caesium preset to 1000 s (measurement white phase noise, 30x at 1 s) and in holdover (1.15 to 8.74); the worst 2011 production SA.45s is 1.26x the CSAC preset at 1 s; the OX-208 has no measured record (blocked)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>telecom_timing::tests (every_preset_carries_a_named_datasheet_and_figures; the_noise_fit_envelopes_every_datasheet_point; pure_aging_matches_its_closed_form — the discrete aging ramp within 0.1 % of (1/2)·D·t² over a…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Holdover prediction from a measured clock record, checked on held-out data</span><code class="lg-mod">slot_<wbr>timing <wbr>(ClockNoise::<wbr>from_<wbr>phase_<wbr>record,<wbr> <wbr>fit_<wbr>weighted,<wbr> <wbr>slot_<wbr>budget,<wbr> <wbr>breach_<wbr>after_<wbr>sync_<wbr>s)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>slot-timing</code> kind&#x27;s measured-record path: the overlapping Allan deviation of a phase record fitted by edf-weighted non-negative least squares in the white-phase and IEEE Std 1139 frequency-modulation basis (slot_timing::fit_weighted), then inverted for the coast time at which the time error reaches a threshold (slot_timing::slot_budget). The red-noise floor is measured from the record instead of assumed</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>A real free-running atomic clock: 556 990 one-second phase samples of a 5071A caesium standard measured against a hydrogen maser (A. Wallin, distributed with allantools; pinned commit and SHA-256 in scripts/fetch_cs5071a.sh), the same record tests/cs5071a_reference.rs uses for the estimators. The held-out two thirds are an external measurement the prediction never saw. Data-gated: the realdata-clock workflow fetches the record and runs the test with KSHANA_REQUIRE_REALDATA=1. Validates the fit-then-invert path on a white-FM-dominated atomic standard over coasts up to about 50 000 s; not the datasheet or class sources, not the deterministic terms. NOT a crystal oscillator: on a measured OCXO record (tests/slot_timing_ocxo_holdout.rs, allantools, 5.5 h against a hydrogen maser) the held-out prediction is conservative, at 0.59 to 0.70 of the measured breach, and mostly outside the bar, because that oscillator&#x27;s noise floor halved during the record; in sample it is within 0.98 to 1.17. The crystal case stays MODELLED. NOT atomic clocks in orbit either: on 10 GPS Block IIF satellite clocks (tests/slot_timing_igs_holdout.rs, IGS final 30 s clocks, 14 days, protocol written before download) 8 land within the bar and 2 (G25, G30) are optimistic by up to about 2x, so under the protocol the orbital case stays MODELLED</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/slot_timing_cs5071a_holdout.rs (holdover_inversion_predicts_the_held_out_caesium_record: fit on the first third of the record, predict the one-sigma breach at 0.5, 0.75, 1, 1.5, 2 and 2.5 ns, measure it on the…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Seconds until a free-running clock leaves a time-indexed slot&#x27;s guard, and the fix cadence that keeps it inside</span><code class="lg-mod">slot_<wbr>timing <wbr>(predicted_<wbr>error_<wbr>s,<wbr> <wbr>breach_<wbr>after_<wbr>sync_<wbr>s,<wbr> <wbr>terms_<wbr>at,<wbr> <wbr>slot_<wbr>budget,<wbr> <wbr>ClockNoise::<wbr>from_<wbr>datasheet,<wbr> <wbr>SlotTimingScenario); <wbr>clock_<wbr>state <wbr>(ClockClass::<wbr>Tcxo,<wbr> <wbr>Ocxo,<wbr> <wbr>Rafs,<wbr> <wbr>source)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>slot-timing</code> kind: the predicted error E(t) = k·√(σ₀² + σ_x²(t)) + (|y₀| + |c_T·ΔT|)·t + ½|D|t² inverted for the breach, the time left, the resynchronisation interval net of the fix latency and fixes per day, each term itemised with the dominant one named; clocks from the six ClockClass defaults (TCXO, OCXO and RAFS added, each citing one datasheet), the telecom-timing presets, an inline datasheet or a measured record; a breach beyond the source&#x27;s longest averaging time flagged as extrapolated</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Closed forms for each term alone and the independent holdover::holdover_seconds root-find; the datasheet figures are the named documents (docs/SLOT-TIMING.md). A class default rests on an assumed red-noise floor and a datasheet source is an envelope of maxima, so neither is a measurement of any unit</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/slot_timing_igs_holdout.rs (10 GPS Block IIF satellite clocks, 14 days of IGS final clocks: 8 within the 1.5 bar, G25 and G30 optimistic by up to about 2x, pinned); tests/slot_timing_ocxo_holdout.rs (a measured…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Timing protection level for a receiver in orbit under GNSS spoofing</span><code class="lg-mod">orbital_<wbr>timing <wbr>(orbital_<wbr>timing,<wbr> <wbr>visibility_<wbr>half_<wbr>angle_<wbr>rad,<wbr> <wbr>max_<wbr>pass_<wbr>s,<wbr> <wbr>crosslink_<wbr>neighbour_<wbr>is_<wbr>outside_<wbr>footprint)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>orbital_timing::orbital_timing, reachable as the <code>slot-timing</code> kind&#x27;s spoofing section: circular-orbit speed and period, the longest time a ground spoofer can reach the satellite per pass, the clock-aided monitor floor and CUSUM detection latency, the conditional protection level (floor plus coast over the latency), the pull a spoofer at a stated maximum ramp rate accumulates before the satellite leaves its footprint or an independent check runs, and whether each ground-contact or crosslink check is independent of one ground spoofer</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Reduction to the existing timing protection level and the circular-orbit and spherical-Earth visibility closed forms. Conditional on detection, one terrestrial spoofer at a stated ramp rate, an overhead pass on a non-rotating Earth; not validated on a spoofed receiver in orbit</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>orbital_timing::tests (reduces_to_the_terrestrial_tpl — exactly tpl::timing_protection_level_ns when the clock has no flicker, white-phase or random-run term; leo_geometry_matches_its_closed_forms…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="signal"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Closed-form L-band signal power spectral densities and spectral separation coefficients</span><code class="lg-mod">navsignal <wbr>(Modulation::<wbr>psd,<wbr> <wbr>spectral_<wbr>separation_<wbr>coeff_<wbr>offset,<wbr> <wbr>q_<wbr>from_<wbr>ssc); <wbr>spectrum <wbr>(psd_<wbr>nulls_<wbr>hz,<wbr> <wbr>psd_<wbr>peak_<wbr>hz,<wbr> <wbr>main_<wbr>lobe_<wbr>null_<wbr>to_<wbr>null_<wbr>hz,<wbr> <wbr>Jammer::<wbr>ssc)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Unit-area power spectral densities of GPS L1 C/A and L2C (BPSK(1)), GPS L5 and Galileo E5a (BPSK(10)), sine-BOC(1,1) and Galileo E1 MBOC(6,1,1/11) (navsignal::Modulation::psd, with an MBOC variant added), their numerically located nulls and maxima (spectrum::psd_nulls_hz, psd_peak_hz, main_lobe_null_to_null_hz), and the spectral separation coefficient of a signal against any spectrum at any offset (navsignal::spectral_separation_coeff_offset) or against a tone, flat noise, a chirp or matched noise (spectrum::Jammer::ssc), with the anti-jam coefficient Q = 1/(R_c kappa)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Published textbook values: the BPSK(n) main lobe of 2n x 1.023 MHz null to null and the anti-jam coefficients Q = 1 for a narrowband (CW) jammer and Q = 1.5 for a spread-spectrum jammer matched to C/A (Kaplan &amp; Hegarty, Understanding GPS/GNSS, 3rd ed., section 9.4); the BOC(m,n) main lobes centred at plus or minus m x 1.023 MHz (Betz, Binary Offset Carrier Modulations for Radionavigation, NAVIGATION 48(4), 2001); the spectral separation coefficients -61.8, -64.8 and -67.8 dB/Hz for C/A with C/A, BOC(1,1) with BOC(1,1) and C/A with BOC(1,1) (Betz 2001; Hein et al., MBOC: The New Optimized Spreading Modulation Recommended for Galileo L1 OS and GPS L1C, Inside GNSS, May/June 2006), reproduced here from their Parseval autocorrelation closed forms. The BOC(1,1) maximum is not at 1.023 MHz: the lobe spans the carrier null to 2.046 MHz and peaks at 0.759 MHz, and the test pins both. The MBOC mix is the ICD definition, checked for unit area and linearity only</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>spectrum::tests (bpsk_main_lobe_null_to_null_is_two_n_times_1_023_mhz — BPSK(1) 2.046 MHz and BPSK(10) 20.46 MHz located numerically on the closed form; boc11_lobes_are_centred_at_plus_minus_1_023_mhz — carrier null…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="nav"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">L-band spectrum waterfall with per-band J/S and effective C/N0 under a scripted jammer timeline</span><code class="lg-mod">spectrum <wbr>(SpectrumScenario,<wbr> <wbr>SpectrumModel::<wbr>bin_<wbr>psd_<wbr>w_<wbr>per_<wbr>hz,<wbr> <wbr>SpectrumModel::<wbr>band_<wbr>state,<wbr> <wbr>effective_<wbr>cn0_<wbr>multi_<wbr>dbhz,<wbr> <wbr>system_<wbr>temp_<wbr>k,<wbr> <wbr>Jammer::<wbr>power_<wbr>fraction_<wbr>in,<wbr> <wbr>Jammer::<wbr>duty)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>spectrum</code> kind: a frequency-by-time grid of the L-band power spectral density (thermal floor k T_sys with T_sys = T_ant + 290 K (F - 1), the signals at their interface-specification minimum received powers, and continuous-wave, narrowband, chirp and matched-noise jammers with on/off times), each cell averaged over its bin and row (chirps exactly over whole and partial sweeps, jammers by duty), per-band effective C/N0 = [1/(C/N0) + sum (J/S) kappa]^-1 per row, J/S per band, in-band J/S, and an SVG waterfall with C/N0 bars. The report carries a cross-check against the <code>jamming</code> kind&#x27;s chain on the same link inputs</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Reduction to the existing <code>jamming</code> kind&#x27;s anti-jam equation and link budget (the same code, called on the same inputs), and the k T0 F noise-floor closed form. The signal spectra underneath are the validated row above; the jammer powers, timeline and front-end bandwidths are scenario inputs, the spectra are continuous (no spreading-code lines), and no automatic gain control, blanking or antenna pattern acts on the jammer. No measured jammed spectrum is in the repository to check the composite against. The <code>jamming</code> kind&#x27;s Q table (CW and narrowband 1, broadband and swept 2 since 0.36: the textbook values, pinned to the navsignal closed forms) agrees with this model&#x27;s CW value and differs from what it derives for noise matched to C/A (1.5) and for flat null-to-null noise without in-band normalisation (2.2); the report prints both</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>spectrum::tests (agrees_with_the_jamming_kind_chain — J/S equal to jamming::j_over_s_db and effective C/N0 equal to jamming::effective_cn0_dbhz with Q = 1/(R_c kappa) to 1e-9 dB, and the 32.105 dB anchor of the jamming…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="signal"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">SigMF recording input and output, and Welch spectral estimates of complex IQ</span><code class="lg-mod">sigmf <wbr>(read,<wbr> <wbr>write,<wbr> <wbr>encode,<wbr> <wbr>decode,<wbr> <wbr>parse_<wbr>meta,<wbr> <wbr>meta_<wbr>to_<wbr>json); <wbr>spectrum <wbr>(welch_<wbr>psd,<wbr> <wbr>fft_<wbr>in_<wbr>place,<wbr> <wbr>synthesise_<wbr>iq)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>sigmf: read and write Signal Metadata Format recordings (JSON .sigmf-meta with the core global, captures and annotations fields; raw .sigmf-data as cf32_le, ci16_le or ci8, the integer decoders shared with realdata::iqif::load_iq), all on strings and byte buffers. spectrum::welch_psd: Hann-windowed, overlapped, averaged periodograms, density-scaled, on an in-crate radix-2 transform (spectrum::fft_in_place). Those two are the validated claim. spectrum::synthesise_iq draws the model as IQ, and the <code>spectrum</code> kind&#x27;s [iq] section runs model to IQ to SigMF to Welch and compares with the model; its [recording] section estimates a real recording (native builds); the synthesis and those model comparisons are outside the validated claim</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>scipy 1.18.1 scipy.signal.welch (BSD-3-Clause) with the same periodic Hann window, overlap, density scaling, no detrend and two-sided mean average: every bin within 1e-12 relative (observed 6.0e-14) over 27 cases, with segment counts taken from scipy&#x27;s own spectrogram. sigmf-python 1.13.0 (LGPL-3.0, run as a tool) writes five third-party recordings (cf32_le, ci16_le, ci8, two captures with annotations, a non-zero first sample_start) that the crate decodes bit-identically with identical core fields, and reads the crate&#x27;s recordings back bit-identically; the SigMF v1.2.6 metadata schema reports zero errors on the crate&#x27;s metadata. Tolerances fixed before the first comparison. Outside the claim: spectrum::synthesise_iq and the [iq] and [recording] comparisons with the model (a synthesis has no single right answer; a periodic chirp shows lines, Fresnel ripple and edge tails the smooth model omits, total power within 2 %), the integer-to-float scaling convention (the crate divides by 32767 and 127, sigmf-python&#x27;s autoscale by 2^15 and 2^7, which the specification leaves open; the comparison uses raw codes), multi-channel recordings and the data types the reader refuses</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>sigmf::tests (cf32_round_trip_is_exact_to_single_precision; ci16_round_trip_is_within_half_a_code; ci16_is_little_endian_i_then_q; integer_encoding_counts_saturation; metadata_uses_the_core_namespace…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Planet positions across the solar system from the JPL Standish Keplerian elements</span><code class="lg-mod">ephem <wbr>(standish_<wbr>state,<wbr> <wbr>standish_<wbr>elements,<wbr> <wbr>standish_<wbr>nominal_<wbr>error,<wbr> <wbr>ecliptic_<wbr>to_<wbr>icrf); <wbr>ephem_<wbr>provider <wbr>(AnalyticSolarSystem); <wbr>solar_<wbr>system <wbr>(SolarSystemScenario)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Heliocentric positions of Mercury, Venus, the Earth-Moon barycentre, the Earth, Mars, Jupiter and Saturn from Standish Table 1 (1800 AD to 2050 AD), and of all eight planets from Tables 2a/2b (3000 BC to 3000 AD, with the b, c, s, f mean-anomaly terms), following the JPL page&#x27;s algorithm step for step and rotated to the ICRF (International Celestial Reference Frame) by the page&#x27;s obliquity; the Earth is split from the barycentre by the Montenbruck &amp; Gill lunar series and the mass ratio. Composed as ephem_provider::AnalyticSolarSystem so any body is available relative to any other, and runnable as the <code>solar-system</code> kind</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>JPL Horizons DE441 geometric heliocentric vectors (ecliptic and ICRF), fetched after the pre-registration commits 80451866, 8d38a28b and 558cc807. Bar: per planet and component, RMS at most 1.0x the approximate errors of the Explanatory Supplement 3rd ed. Table 8.10.1 (the EMB row for the Earth); the Earth-EMB split is checked directly against DE441 399 minus 3, at most 0.3 deg and 8.1 km at every epoch 1950-2050 (the 8.1 km length bar comes from the lunar-series accuracy stated in Kshana own ephemeris documentation, citing Montenbruck and Gill, and was not checked against the book; the measured 6.09 km sits close to it). The RMS statistic was chosen knowing that an earlier 27-epoch comparison reached 1.87x nominal at its maximum; single-epoch errors reach 3.83x (3.87x in the ICRF case). The Supplement does not define its figures as RMS. Case B rotates both sides with the IAU 1976 obliquity typed in the test, so the rotation is not checked against itself. The default table selection of AnalyticSolarSystem is not exercised (the tests force Table 1). Uranus and Neptune from Table 1 are a separate MODELLED row</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/solar_system_standish_preregistered.rs::standish_rms_errors_are_within_explanatory_supplement_table_8_10_1 (barycentres 1800-2050 from Table 1 and 3000 BC-3000 AD from Table 2, 8908 and 21857 epochs, worst RMS…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Sun, Moon, Mercury and Venus positions from the DE440 kernel at UTC epochs (KernelEphemeris)</span><code class="lg-mod">ephem_<wbr>provider <wbr>(KernelEphemeris::<wbr>relative_<wbr>position_<wbr>utc,<wbr> <wbr>utc_<wbr>to_<wbr>tdb_<wbr>jd2); <wbr>naif_<wbr>kernel; <wbr>timescales</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Geometric positions of the Sun, Mercury, Venus, the Earth and the Moon relative to one another (J2000/ICRF axes, metres) from a JPL DE440 kernel through ephem_provider::KernelEphemeris at UTC epochs: body names mapped to NAIF codes, UTC carried through the leap-second table and TT to TDB with the two-term TDB-TT series, the kernel read by the engine&#x27;s own reader. A body the kernel holds only as a system barycentre (Mars to Neptune, Pluto in de440s) is refused, never substituted. The kernel-free Standish row stays the browser and fallback path</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Skyfield 1.54 (MIT), with its own leap-second table, its own TDB-TT and its own reader of the same NAIF de440s.bsp (jplephem), run as a separate program at UTC epochs. Pre-registered (0bfafe6d) before the fixture existed, the grid seeded from that commit&#x27;s hash, with a bar of 5e-5 s times the relative speed (the engine&#x27;s stated two-term TDB-TT accuracy) plus 1e-13 of the barycentric distance plus 1e-5 m, per component. Result: all 600 inside, worst 0.66 of the bar (2.96 m on a fast pair, the TDB-TT series&#x27; own error). Mutation: TT used as TDB fails at 33 times the bar. Both sides read DE440: this validates the provider and its time chain, not DE440. Outside the claim: the planets beyond Venus as bodies (de440s carries their barycentres only), the planetary moons, and precision below the two-term TDB-TT series (a few metres for the fastest pairs)</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/kernel_ephemeris_skyfield_oracle.rs::kernel_ephemeris_matches_skyfield_at_utc_epochs (600 positions at 200 UTC epochs 1973-2026 drawn from the pre-registration hash; worst 0.66 of the bar, 2.96 m)…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="orbit"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Light time between solar-system bodies</span><code class="lg-mod">radiometric <wbr>(light_<wbr>time_<wbr>solution,<wbr> <wbr>two_<wbr>way_<wbr>range,<wbr> <wbr>shapiro_<wbr>delay); <wbr>solar_<wbr>system <wbr>(link,<wbr> <wbr>link_<wbr>on); <wbr>ephem_<wbr>provider <wbr>(AnalyticSolarSystem)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The Newtonian one-way light time from any body to any other, received at an epoch, solved by the radiometric fixed-point light-time solver (radiometric::light_time_solution, transmitter at its retarded position) through solar_system::link_on on any EphemerisProvider; the solar-system kind runs it on the analytic solar system in the heliocentric frame (solar_system::link). The two-way range from radiometric::two_way_range and the Sun Shapiro delay from radiometric::shapiro_delay are reported separately and are not externally compared. Emitted for every body against an observer and for any extra link by the <code>solar-system</code> kind, and for the body-to-Earth downlink by the <code>body-pnt</code> kind</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>JPL Horizons DE441 one-way down-leg Newtonian light time LT (VEC_CORR=LT, observer the Earth centre), pre-registered (480037d3, amendment 54d5c86f changing only the interpolation node spacing after the first fetch failed its 10 m interpolation precondition, before any light time was compared) with a bar of 1e-6 s per light time, the solver fed DE441 barycentric positions so the retarded-transmitter iteration is visible (a first-iterate solver misses by up to 0.1 s). On the analytic Standish positions the light time is only as good as those positions (see the planet-position row); the shipped link treats the Sun as an inertial centre, which stays inside that position error but is not separately validated. two_way_range and shapiro_delay are not externally compared</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/solar_system_light_time_solver_preregistered.rs::light_time_solver_on_de441_positions_matches_horizons_lt_within_1e_6_s (Mercury, Venus, Mars, Jupiter and Saturn barycentres, the Sun and the Moon to the Earth…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="other"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Uranus and Neptune from Standish Table 1, Pluto, and planetary velocities</span><code class="lg-mod">ephem <wbr>(standish_<wbr>state,<wbr> <wbr>StandishElements::<wbr>state_<wbr>at_<wbr>mean_<wbr>anomaly,<wbr> <wbr>Planet::<wbr>Pluto); <wbr>solar_<wbr>system <wbr>(position_<wbr>label)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>Positions of Uranus and Neptune from Standish Table 1 (the default inside 1800 AD to 2050 AD), Pluto from the 1992 Table 1 row the current JPL page no longer lists, and the heliocentric velocity of every planet: the two-body derivative with the mean motion the elements imply plus the turning of the ellipse by the perihelion, inclination and node rates</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Measured against JPL Horizons DE441 (the same committed fixtures), but not validated: Table 1&#x27;s stated figures for Uranus and Neptune are exceeded by up to 2.0 and 5.2 times against DE441 heliocentric positions (Neptune meets them against barycentric positions, which suggests the fit was not heliocentric), the page states no error for Pluto, and no published bound exists for the velocities. Tables 2a/2b meet their own figures for Uranus and Neptune and