by lag-1 autocorrelation</span><code class="lg-mod">allan <wbr>(lag1_<wbr>noise_<wbr>id,<wbr> <wbr>lag1_<wbr>acf)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>allan::lag1_noise_id: the Riley and Greenhall (2004) lag-1 autocorrelation noise identifier (decimated phase with its quadratic removed, or group-averaged frequency with its line removed; repeated first differences until delta = r1/(1+r1) &lt; 0.25 or d = dmax), returning the integer and unrounded power-law exponent alpha (+2 white PM to -2 random-walk FM) at any averaging factor; it sets the noise-type degrees of freedom of the measured clock library&#x27;s device cards</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>allantools 2024.6 autocorr_noise_id (A. Wallin and contributors, LGPL-3.0-or-later, run as a separate program by the fixture generator): an independent implementation of the published Riley-Greenhall algorithm, a uniquely defined quantity under the stated conventions. Validates the identifier, not the noise content of any real clock</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/clock_library_lag1_noise_id_allantools.rs (140 cases: ten Kasdin power-law records, b = 0 to -4, as phase and frequency data, af = 1 to 64; alpha_int and d identical, worst |d alpha| 9.6e-13 and |d rho| 4.8e-13…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="integrity"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Galileo OSNMA receiver-side navigation-data authentication</span><code class="lg-mod">osnma <wbr>(page,<wbr> <wbr>subframe,<wbr> <wbr>dsm,<wbr> <wbr>merkle,<wbr> <wbr>signature,<wbr> <wbr>tesla,<wbr> <wbr>mac,<wbr> <wbr>maclt,<wbr> <wbr>navdata,<wbr> <wbr>verifier,<wbr> <wbr>ubx,<wbr> <wbr>cli)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>osnma + <code>kshana osnma verify</code>: an open, pure-Rust receiver-side verifier for Galileo OSNMA (SIS ICD Issue 1.1, Receiver Guidelines Issue 1.3) from I/NAV pages or a u-blox UBX-RXM-SFRBX stream: Merkle-tree public keys (DSM-PKR), the ECDSA P-256/P-521 signature of the DSM-KROOT, TESLA key-chain verification (SHA-256 or SHA3-256), MACSEQ and tag verification (HMAC-SHA-256 or CMAC-AES) for ADKD 0, 4 and 12 with cross-authentication, chain end/revocation/renewal, public-key renewal and revocation, and the alert message; per-satellite status Authenticated/Failed/Unavailable with a reason for every failure or delay. Advisory: not type-approved navigation equipment and not a navigation integrity service; no anti-spoofing performance is claimed</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>The official Galileo OSNMA test vectors published by the European GNSS Service Centre as an annex to the Receiver Guidelines (Test_vectors.zip, sha256 ef9b9afc6ef9e1c57393415cf2a1dc80c4035e7c06123907ea0bb97dd3ccf370): 18 of 18 sets, 186,382 tags authenticated, 0 failed, run offline on 2026-10-10 (evidence record in kshana-pro docs/evidence). Scope: the vectors validate the cryptographic verification (Merkle, KROOT/ECDSA, TESLA chain, MACK tags, ADKD 0/4/12) and its outcomes on the published scenarios; they use only HMAC-SHA-256, a SHA-256 chain, 128-bit keys, 40-bit tags and look-up entries 33 and 34, so CMAC-AES, SHA3-256 chains and other parameters are covered by published primitive known-answer tests and synthetic tests only. The trusted-time, data-binding, expiry and header-confirmation hardening is tested by the synthetic attack tests, not by an external oracle. Not checked: certificate revocation lists, live signals or a live receiver (the UBX word layout is checked on synthetic frames only), SHA3-256 Merkle trees, freshness without an independent time. Not an EU service</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/osnma_official_vectors.rs (opt-in, KSHANA_OSNMA_VECTORS: all 18 vector sets, 186,382 tags authenticated, 0 failed, per-set floors, chains announced, ADKD 0/4/12, alert verified, authentication resuming after…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st 