ock"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Azimuth-dependent skyline mask: visible set, azimuth and elevation, and DOP under the mask</span><code class="lg-mod">skyline <wbr>(Skyline::<wbr>cut_<wbr>deg,<wbr> <wbr>Skyline::<wbr>visible); <wbr>frames <wbr>(look_<wbr>angles,<wbr> <wbr>is_<wbr>visible_<wbr>skyline); <wbr>orbit <wbr>(UserPath,<wbr> <wbr>sky_<wbr>view,<wbr> <wbr>cofactor_<wbr>from_<wbr>los); <wbr>geometry <wbr>(sky_<wbr>geometry)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>skyline + frames::look_angles + geometry::sky_geometry: the geodetic (WGS-84) azimuth and elevation of a satellite from a ground site, an azimuth/elevation mask table linearly interpolated in azimuth with wrap at 360 deg, the visible set el &gt;= max(mask_deg, mask(az)), and the common-clock DOP and ENU-and-clock cofactor matrix of that set (the kernel behind orbit::dop). It is how the [skyline] section decides visibility in the gnss-sim, jamming, leo-pvt, leo-pass and nmea-scenario kinds and, with the ground-site user_site, in orbit and integrity</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Orekit 12.2 (Apache-2.0): a TopocentricFrame on a WGS-84 OneAxisEllipsoid for the look angles and an ElevationMask per table, run by xval/orekit-skyline/OrekitSkyline.java on committed Earth-fixed states (the propagation is not compared), with gnss_lib_py 1.0.4 (wheel metadata MIT) get_dop for the DOP and the ENU-and-clock DOP matrix of the sets Orekit finds visible. Pre-registered (xval/orekit-skyline/PREREGISTRATION.md, commit d94130d4) before either oracle was run on the comparison inputs; Orekit&#x27;s linear-with-wrap interpolation was confirmed beforehand on a synthetic probe. Scope: table masks, geodetic look angles, the masked visible set and its common-clock DOP and cofactor. NOT checked here: the canyon, sector and terrain reductions and the presets (their 360-point tables entered as inputs; rows &#x27;Street-canyon, sector and terrain reductions to a table&#x27; and