</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>geometry::sky_geometry: the ENU-and-clock cofactor (H^T H)^-1 with one receiver clock in common or one per system (the orbit::cofactor_from_los kernel), and the horizontal error ellipse from its east-north block (semi-axes k sigma sqrt(lambda), k = sqrt(-2 ln(1 - p)), the major axis&#x27;s azimuth from north)</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>numpy 2.5.4 (BSD-3-Clause), xval/numpy-ellipse/ellipse.py: numpy.linalg.inv of G^T G and numpy.linalg.eigh of its horizontal block on the committed inputs, an independent numerical library performing the inverse and the eigen-decomposition by its own algorithms (standard P2). Pre-registered in xval/numpy-ellipse/PREREGISTRATION.md (commit 2dfc9c16) before the run; the driver&#x27;s output formatting was corrected once after the pre-registration (a numpy scalar written as np.float64(...)), with no change to the protocol or the tolerances. The validated claim is the linear algebra on those inputs; the Gaussian error model and the UERE are stated inputs, not a measured accuracy or an integrity bound</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/ellipse_numpy_oracle.rs (ellipse_and_cofactor_agree_with_numpy_as_pre_registered: 270 cases, the 142 committed visible sets of the skyline comparison under one common clock and under one clock per system; worst…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Sky geometry functions: visibility, DOP, covariance, error ellipse, Monte Carlo cloud</span><code class="lg-mod">geometry; <wbr>orbit <wbr>(cofactor_<wbr>from_<wbr>los,<wbr> <wbr>dop_<wbr>cov,<wbr> <wbr>dop); <wbr>surface <wbr>(sky_<wbr>geometry,<wbr> <wbr>sky_<wbr>monte_<wbr>carlo,<wbr> <wbr>skyline_<wbr>presets,<wbr> <wbr>skyline_<wbr>profile,<wbr> <wbr>skyplot_<wbr>svg)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>geometry (sky_geometry, sky_monte_carlo, skyline_presets, skyline_profile, skyplot_svg; CLI <code>kshana sky</code> and <code>kshana skyline</code>, Python and WASM functions of the same names, MCP <code>sky_geometry</code>): from a site, a time and constellation presets (or satellites as azimuth/elevation or ECEF), the satellites visible and masked under a skyline, GDOP/PDOP/HDOP/VDOP/TDOP, the unit-variance cofactor (H^T H)^-1 and the covariance in east-north-up with one receiver clock per system or one in common, the horizontal error ellipse (semi-axes k sigma sqrt(lambda), k = sqrt(-2 ln(1 - p)), orientation 0.5 atan2(2 Q_EN, Q_NN - Q_EE) from north), and n seeded least-squares errors under independent Gaussian range errors with their empirical covariance, RMS and 95 % containment. The cofactor is orbit::cofactor_from_los, the one kernel behind orbit::dop and orbit::dop_cov (dop is dop_cov&#x27;s reduction): no second DOP implementation</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>The single-clock DOP factors rest on the kernel validated against gnss_lib_py 1.0.4 (row &#x27;GNSS geometry / dilution of precision (DOP)&#x27;); this row&#x27;s own checks are internal: sky_geometry reproduces orbit::dop and dop_cov on the same geometry, the ellipse reproduces an analytic 2x2 eigen-decomposition, ECEF and azimuth/elevation inputs agree, and the seeded cloud&#x27;s RMS and 95 % containment agree with the geometry. The ENU covariance with one clock per system and the ellipse are VALIDATED in row &#x27;Horizontal error ellipse and per-system cofactor from the geometry covariance&#x27; (numpy, P2); what stays MODELLED here is the composition: the preset propagation to a representative sky, the Monte Carlo cloud (checked against the geometry it samples, a self-consistency check) and the surfaces. The constellation presets give a representative sky, not a dated one; the Gaussian error model and the UERE are stated inputs, not a measured accuracy or an integrity bound</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>geometry::tests (ellipse_of_an_analytic_two_by_two_case; sky_g