40 · 10^0.4 = 100.48 ns against the 100 ns plateau of G.812 Table 4). A standards transcription with internal consistency checks, not a conformance test and not an external validation. G.812 (06/2004) is listed as superseded by a pre-published 08/2026 edition and G.811 has an Amd. 1 (04/2016); neither could be read, so neither is checked. The free-running OCXO scenario overstates the oscillator&#x27;s contribution at observation intervals long against a loop&#x27;s time constant; it says nothing about any equipment clock</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>telecom_timing::tests (g811_prc_masks_follow_clause_6_1; g812_masks_follow_tables_3_4_6_and_7; g8262_masks_follow_tables_1_3_4_and_5; g8262_1_eeec_masks_follow_tables_1_and_3; a_linear_piece_is_evaluated_without_powf…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Clock-log formats (RINEX clock, TimeLab ASCII, TAPR TICC) read to a normalised phase series</span><code class="lg-mod">clock_<wbr>log <wbr>(read_<wbr>input,<wbr> <wbr>MeasuredPhaseReport,<wbr> <wbr>MEASURED_<wbr>UNITS,<wbr> <wbr>TELECOM_<wbr>LOG_<wbr>UNITS); <wbr>telecom_<wbr>timing <wbr>(SeriesInput::<wbr>clock_<wbr>log); <wbr>api <wbr>(clock <wbr>kind <wbr>[clock_<wbr>log])</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>clock_log::read_input: one parser layer that turns a RINEX clock file (AS/AR records of one named clock, versions 2 and 3.00 to 3.04), a TimeLab-style ASCII phase or frequency log, or a TAPR TICC timestamp or interval log into a uniform phase grid, with tau0 stated, from a header, or the median spacing, every gap, off-grid reading and duplicate counted, and a refuse, longest-run or linear gap policy; wired opt-in into the telecom-timing series (<code>[series.clock_log]</code>, adding a <code>clock_log</code> block) and the clock kind (<code>[clock_log]</code>, adding a <code>measured</code> block with ADEV, TDEV and MTIE, the table&#x27;s SHA-256 and the phase SHA-256; runs = 1). Without the table both kinds are byte-identical</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Pre-registered in tests/fixtures/clock_log/PREREGISTRATION.md (committed alone, 05bb1d17, before the fixture existed); recorded in RESULTS.md. Three seeded synthetic logs, one per format (a TICC gap of three epochs, a RINEX second clock to ignore), read back by an independent reader written from docs/CLOCK-LOG-FORMATS.md by the same author: grid, gap and fill counts equal and the largest phase difference 0 s (bar 1e-13 s). allantools 2024.6 oadev, tdev and mtie on that phase agree within a relative 2.5e-14 (bar 1e-10) at every averaging factor it evaluates; it does not evaluate MTIE over the whole record, which the engine reports, so that one point per log has no oracle value and the bar as worded is not met there. MODELLED: synthetic logs, and the reader oracle shares the author; real TimeLab, TICC and RINEX clock files from instruments were not compared</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>clock_log::tests (hand-written logs per format: headers, separators, units, frequency integration, TICC channels and modes, gaps and policies, refusals); tests/clock_log_allantools.rs (the pre-registered comparison…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Clock-log formats linuxptp (ptp4l, phc2sys, ts2phc) and chrony (tracking, statistics) read to a normalised phase series</span><code class="lg-mod">clock_<wbr>log <wbr>(parse_<wbr>linuxptp,<wbr> <wbr>parse_<wbr>chrony,<wbr> <wbr>grid_<wbr>timed_<wbr>offsets)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>clock_log::read_input with the formats ptp4l, phc2sys, ts2phc, chrony-tracking and chrony-statistics: the logged offset of a linuxptp servo line (ns, the line&#x27;s own bracketed time stamp, readings before the servo first reports s2 left out and counted) or of a chrony tracking or statistics log (s, by column position, UTC date and time) becomes a uniform phase grid through the same gap policies as the other formats; a source selector (<code>clock</code>) names one of several interfaces, clocks or addresses; the logs feed the telecom-timing series, the clock kind and the holdover-test kind</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Fixtures written for Kshana from the public line layouts of the daemons, with made-up values (NOTICE.md in the fixture folder); no log from a real installation was read and no oracle compares the parse with another reader, so a layout that differs between daemon versions can still be refused or misread. The estimators fed by the phase series are the ones validated against allantools in the rows above</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>clock_log::tests (ptp4l_reads_the_locked_offsets_in_ns; linuxptp_reads_only_the_named_program; ts2phc_gap_is_refused_or_filled; linuxptp_reads_syslog_prefixed_lines_and_picks_an_interface…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Holdover test scored from an operator-supplied time-error record</span><code class="lg-mod">holdover_<wbr>test <wbr>(HoldoverTestScenario,<wbr> <wbr>recovery,<wbr> <wbr>linear_<wbr>fit); <wbr>telecom_<wbr>timing <wbr>(check_<wbr>mask,<wbr> <wbr>check_<wbr>envelope,<wbr> <wbr>budget_<wbr>results)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>holdover-test</code> kind: a time-error record (a linuxptp, chrony, TICC, TimeLab or RINEX clock log, or time_s,time_error_ns pairs) is split at the two times the operator states for GNSS removal and restoration into a baseline, a holdover and a recovery step; the baseline is measured against the ITU-T masks written for a locked clock and the holdover against those written for holdover or an autonomous clock, the ePRTC-A holdover envelope and the time-error budgets; the holdover reports the least-squares frequency offset the system held, and the recovery whether and when |TE| came inside a stated band and stayed for a stated dwell, with the peak and the largest step. Every limit is the one the telecom-timing kind transcribes; the two times are the operator&#x27;s statement and are not verified</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Hand computation on records built from formulas whose slope, step, extremum and recovery time are known before the run (the tests state them), and the masks, envelope and budgets of the telecom-timing rows above. No laboratory measurement of a real holdover was scored and no external oracle was compared, so the kind makes no claim about any clock or system; a timing-lab partner is needed before any per-clock-class figure could be promoted</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>holdover_test::tests (the_record_is_split_at_the_stated_times; holdover_figures_match_a_hand_computation; recovery_is_timed_from_the_restoration; masks_are_measured_on_the_step_their_condition_names…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="lunar"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">GNSS signal availability in the Space Service Volume and beyond, compared with the ICG SSV booklet</span><code class="lg-mod">ssv <wbr>(constellations,<wbr> <wbr>geom,<wbr> <wbr>sweep,<wbr> <wbr>pattern,<wbr> <wbr>check)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>The <code>gnss-ssv</code> kind&#x27;s availability sweeps: per constellation (GPS, GLONASS, Galileo, BDS, QZSS, NavIC) and combined, the share of (user, epoch) pairs with at least one and at least four signals and the longest outage, for a user on a sphere (the booklet&#x27;s 2,562-point global shell, 14 days at 1 minute) or on a trajectory (GEO, HEO, lunar transfer), from the Keplerian orbits of the booklet&#x27;s annex C, a constant minimum radiated power inside the reference off-boresight angle, Earth blockage by the sphere plus 50 km and a C/N0 threshold; user antennas from the