 Lunar orbit determination: Earth-GNSS against ground radiometric | Orbit determination in a near-rectilinear halo orbit (or an elliptical frozen orbit) from two independent data types: Earth-GNSS pseudoranges received on board (the link rule of the Earth-GNSS-at-lunar-distance row, plus Moon occultation) and ground two-way range and range-rate from three Deep Space Network stations. Each is a one-step batch least squares about the reference orbit with its formal covariance; a seeded Monte Carlo of the difference between the two estimates tests whether the two covariances explain it (q = d' (P_G + P_R)^-1 d, chi-square with six degrees of freedom when the models match) and what an unmodelled acceleration does to the statistic. | MODELLED | An independent scipy/numpy program integrates the same variational equations (bar O1). Nothing is compared with a measurement of a lunar orbit. The oracle checks the numerics of stated formulas, not the physics, so the row stays MODELLED. Restricted three-body model: no Sun, no lunar harmonics, the Earth rotation axis along the orbit normal, ILLUSTRATIVE receiver and station inputs. | lunar_infra::{orbit, gnss, od} |
 
