+## Linux PPS time stamps (pps-assert)#
+
+pps-assert: the time stamps the Linux PPS subsystem gives for each pulse, one per line as /sys/class/pps/ppsN/assert shows them: <seconds>.<nanoseconds>#<sequence>, the nanoseconds always nine digits. The pulse marks a whole second, so the offset is the time stamp's distance from the nearest second, in seconds: a host clock ahead of the pulse has a positive offset. The reading's time is that whole second. A line with the same sequence number as the line before it is the same pulse read twice and is not a new reading; other lines are skipped and counted. There is one source, so clock is refused. What is measured is the host clock (with the interrupt latency of the PPS input) against the pulse; how good the pulse is, is not known from the log.
+
+## Live: kshana clock-live#
+
+```
+journalctl -f -o cat -u ptp4l | kshana clock-live --format ptp4l --from -
+kshana clock-live --format chrony-tracking --from /var/log/chrony/tracking.log --follow
+kshana clock-live --format pps-assert --from /sys/class/pps/pps0/assert --poll-s 0.2
+cat /dev/ttyACM0 | kshana clock-live --format ticc --clock chA --tau0 1 --from -
+```
+
+clock-live reads a ptp4l, phc2sys, ts2phc, chrony-tracking, chrony-statistics, pps-assert or ticc log as it is written: from standard input, tcp:host:port, a file (with --follow, it waits at the end for more lines), or with --poll-s a file read whole at every poll (the PPS assert file). It prints one JSON line per reading:
+
+```
+{"source":"-","t_s":1003.0,"offset_s":4e-8,"locked":true,"seq":null,"kept":true,"n_kept":1,
+"rms_s":4e-8,"max_abs_s":4e-8,"since_last_s":null}
+```
+
+kept is true exactly for the readings the whole-log reader keeps: the source named by --clock (without it, the first source seen; every line of a chrony tracking log), linuxptp readings from the servo's first locked state (s2) on, each PPS pulse once, and the TICC channel named by --clock (time stamps need --tau0, which the whole-log reader would find from every stamp; the phase is the same femtosecond arithmetic, and a missing tick is a reading the replay fills, not one live invents). So the kept offsets of a live run are the phase series a replay of the same log gives (tests/clock_live.rs, on logs read whole and followed while they are written). rms_s and max_abs_s are over the last 64 kept offsets. It only reads: nothing is sent to the servo, the daemon or the device.
+
+## ntpd and White Rabbit logs#
+
+ntp-loopstats, ntp-peerstats: ntpd's loopstats and peerstats statistics files, read by column position. Each reading starts with the modified Julian day and the seconds past midnight UTC.
+
+- Loopstats: column 3, the local clock's offset on each update, in seconds; one local clock.
+
+- Peerstats: column 5, a peer's measured offset, in seconds; one series per peer address (column 3), and the log needs clock = "<address>" when it holds more than one.
+
+- Epochs are seconds from 1980-01-06 00:00:00 UTC (MJD 44244), with no leap-second handling. The spacing follows the poll interval; tau0 is the median spacing, as for chrony.
+
+- A line that does not have the columns or whose fields are not numbers is skipped and counted.
+
+wr-stat: the stat lines of a White Rabbit PTP core: white-space separated key:value pairs. The reader needs sec (seconds, TAI) and cko (the clock offset, picoseconds), reads nsec when present, and reads lock as the servo state: readings before the first lock:1 are a pull-in and are left out and counted, and a log that never locks is refused, as for linuxptp. Every other key (crtt, setp, mu, temp, ...) is ignored. A line without sec and cko is skipped and counted.
+
+## CGGTTS time-transfer files#
+
+cggtts: BIPM CGGTTS version 2E track files, the common-view format that time laboratories publish. A data line is one satellite track (SAT CL MJD STTIME TRKL ELV AZTH REFSV SRSV REFSYS ... CK).
+
+- A line counts only when its CK checksum holds: the sum, modulo 256, of the byte values of every character before CK, including the separating space. Lines that fail are skipped and counted, never dropped silently. The rule was checked on every data line of a public BIPM file (scripts/fetch_cggtts.sh, tests/cggtts_bipm_reference.rs).
+
+- REFSV and REFSYS are in units of 0.1 ns. A field of all 9s is a missing value.
+
+- The time of a track is its start (MJD, STTIME, UTC) plus half of TRKL, as seconds from 1980-01-06 00:00:00 UTC.
+
+- The phase read is, per track start, the median REFSYS over satellites (the laboratory's reference minus GNSS system time), labelled all in view. With clock = "G04", one satellite's REFSYS only.
+
+- Tracks follow a 16-minute schedule that moves 4 minutes a day, so the spacing is uneven at a day change; tau0 is the median spacing (960 s).
+
+- Two laboratories' files give a common-view difference (kshana::cggtts::common_view): REFSV(a) − REFSV(b) on the same satellite and track cancels the satellite clock. Its comparison with BIPM Circular T is pre-registered in docs/design/evidence/r6-1-cggtts/PREREGISTRATION.md.
+
+## Trading clocks: divergence from UTC against RTS 25 (library)#
+
+kshana::rts25::assess takes a clock's measured offset from UTC (any log above, for example ptp4l, chrony-tracking or cggtts) and the activity the clock serves. It reports the largest divergence, the samples and stretches beyond the limit, and gaps longer than a stated interval. The limits are the maximum divergence from UTC in the Annex of Commission Delegated Regulation (EU) 2017/574 (RTS 25, MiFID II):
+
+Activity |  Maximum divergence |  Granularity |
+
+Venue, gateway-to-gateway latency above 1 ms |  1 ms |  1 ms or finer |
+
+Venue, gateway-to-gateway latency 1 ms or less |  100 µs |  1 µs or finer |
+
+High-frequency algorithmic trading |  100 µs |  1 µs or finer |
+
+Voice trading; request-for-quote needing a human response; negotiated transactions |  1 s |  1 s or finer |
+
+Any other trading activity |  1 ms |  1 ms or finer |
+
+- The outcome is within limit, beyond limit or not assessable. A record with no samples, or a gap longer than the stated interval and no excursion, is not assessable, because the divergence during the gap is unknown.
+
+- What it does not do: it does not show traceability to UTC (the documented chain from the time source, which the Regulation also requires), and it does not measure the granularity of the firm's own timestamps. It describes a measured record against a stated limit and is not a regulatory assessment.
