+## Common Alerting Protocol (CAP 1.2)#
+
+kshana::telemetry::cap::alerts turns the trust stream into OASIS CAP 1.2 messages, the format that public-warning, maritime and aviation alerting systems take in. One message is written for each band change after calibration:
+
+Change |  msgType |  urgency / severity |
+
+nominal → degraded |  Alert |  Expected / Moderate |
+
+nominal → untrusted |  Alert |  Immediate / Severe |
+
+degraded ↔ untrusted |  Update (references the previous message) |  as above |
+
+back to nominal |  Update (references the previous message) |  Past / Minor: the all-clear |
+
+- Every message carries the band, the monitors behind it, the score and the advisory statement. Its area is a circle around the receiver's reported position, or the configured station position.
+
+- status (Actual, Exercise, System, Test, Draft) and scope have no default, so an exercise cannot go out as Actual by accident.
+
+- The messages are checked against the OASIS CAP 1.2 schema (tests/telemetry_cap.rs; the schema is fetched by scripts/fetch_cap_xsd.sh, not vendored).
+
+From the command line, --cap-config cap.toml turns alerting on, --cap-dir <dir> writes each message to <dir>/<identifier>.xml, and --webhook posts it (next section):
+
+```
+sender = "kshana@station.example"
+status = "Exercise"            # Actual, Exercise, System, Test or Draft: no default
+scope = "Restricted"           # Public, Restricted or Private: no default
+area_desc = "Example port and approaches"
+station = [59.44, 24.75]       # used when the stream carries no position
+radius_km = 25.0
+```
+
+```
+kshana receiver-trust live session.toml --tcp 192.0.2.10:10110 --json - \
+| kshana trust-telemetry --cap-config cap.toml --cap-dir alerts/
+```
+
+The date of t_s = 0 comes from the first epoch's UTC time label when it carries one, else from the clock of the machine running trust-telemetry.
+
+## Signed webhooks#
+
+kshana::telemetry::webhook::sign adds a signature to any alert body (a CAP message, a trust sample as JSON) so the receiving system can check that it came from the holder of a shared secret, was not changed, and is fresh:
+
+```
+Kshana-Signature: t=<UNIX seconds>,v1=<hex HMAC-SHA256 of "<t>.<body>">
+```
+
+verify recomputes the signature, compares in constant time, and refuses a timestamp further than a stated tolerance from the receiver's clock (the replay window). While a secret is being rotated, two v1= values may be sent, and either may match. The HMAC is checked against the RFC 4231 test vectors (tests/telemetry_webhook.rs). Keep the secret out of logs and scenario files.
+
+--webhook http://host:port/path --webhook-secret-env <VAR> (with --cap-config) POSTs each CAP message as application/cap+xml, signed with the secret read from the environment variable <VAR>, never from the command line. The webhook is plain HTTP: for HTTPS, post to a local relay. A delivery that fails, or is answered with anything other than 2xx, is counted. The count is printed at the end, and a failed delivery never stops the stream. tests/telemetry_alerts.rs runs the command against a local receiver: every POST verifies, and the messages equal the library's for the whole stream.
