+## Timing and health of live inputs (clock-live, sensor-live)#
+
+Every live reading line carries host_s: the host clock when the line arrived, minus the device's latency (--latency-s, measured once against a common reference such as a PPS edge). The host clock is the one chrony, PTP or PPS disciplines, so readings from several devices on one machine share one time base whatever their own clocks report.
+
+Each live command watches its sensor and prints events as JSON lines beside the readings (sensor-live --kind n2k prints them on standard error, since its output is NMEA):
+
+Flag |  Event |  When |
+
+--stale-s <s> |  stale, then stale-cleared |  no reading for that long, then one again |
+
+--frozen-n <n> |  frozen, then frozen-cleared |  the same value n times in a row, then a change |
+
+--range <lo,hi> |  out-of-range, then in-range |  the value leaves [lo, hi], then returns |
+
+--reconnect |  gap with from_s and host_s |  the device or connection ended or failed; it is opened again every second, and the time without input is reported when it is back |
+
+The watched value is the kept offset for clock-live and the magnitude of the specific force for an IMU. Each condition is reported once when it starts and once when it clears. A gap is marked, never filled: no reading is made up for it. --sensor <name> names the sensor in events. tests/clock_live.rs checks a TCP source dropped and brought back (the gap reported, the readings on both sides equal to the replay), and the frozen and range events. What needs the bench (L8): two sensors on one PPS agreeing within a pre-registered skew, and a real unplug and replug.
 
