+{"engine_version":"0.40.0","file":"celeste-iod-classical-pilot-signals.toml","toml":"# Celeste IOD signal designs: the frequency bands and the \"Classical Pilot\" signal\n# configuration #1 of ESA's Celeste In-Orbit Demonstration (IOD) low Earth orbit (LEO)\n# positioning, navigation and timing (PNT) mission, as parameterised signal designs.\n#\n# THIS FILE IS THE ONLY PLACE IN THE REPOSITORY THAT HOLDS WORKSHOP-DERIVED NUMBERS.\n# Every carrier, bandwidth, allocation, component structure and shape target below\n# marked WORKSHOP was presented at the ESA NAVISP LEO-PNT workshop, 2026 (the\n# Navigation Innovation and Support Programme), and cleared for use; no Celeste signal\n# specification is public. Nothing else in the engine depends on this file, and nothing\n# compiles it in (it is a repository-only scenario, not bundled into the binary):\n# deleting it removes Celeste from a release, and every other scenario, test and oracle\n# still runs. See docs/LEO-SIGNAL.md.\n#\n# What the IOD is for, in ESA's own public words: an in-orbit demonstration of 11\n# satellites plus a spare that tests radionavigation signals in several bands\n# (https://www.esa.int/Applications/Satellite_navigation/Celeste/Celeste_IOD_-_Facts_and_figures).\n# The IOD signals are a vehicle for experiments; later phases (Celeste In-Orbit\n# Pathfinder, a European Union LEO-PNT layer) will use their own signal designs, which\n# is why these are presets of a general signal model, not \"the Celeste signal\".\n#\n# Bands presented (WORKSHOP), with the ITU service allocation:\n#   E5    1191.795 MHz  RNSS      51.15 MHz  (the Galileo E5 centre; 5 x 10.23 MHz wide)\n#   SR    2492.028 MHz  RDSS      15 MHz\n#   SM    2170.00 MHz   MSS       0.2 MHz and 5 MHz (for 5G non-terrestrial-network terminals)\n#   UHF   465.465 MHz   EESS      10 MHz\n#   C     5019.861 MHz  RNSS      19.7 MHz\n#   Cext  5071.011 MHz (B1) and 5122.161 MHz (B2), non-RNSS, 120 MHz and 240 MHz\n# (RNSS Radio Navigation Satellite Service, RDSS Radio Determination Satellite Service,\n# MSS Mobile Satellite Service, EESS Earth Exploration-Satellite Service.)\n#\n# Configuration #1, \"Classical Pilot\" (WORKSHOP), BPSK(n) = binary phase-shift keying at\n# n x 1.023 Mchip/s, FDMA = frequency-division multiple access:\n#   E5#1   pilot BPSK(10); data PfA BPSK(1) or PfB BPSK(10); no acquisition component\n#   S#1    pilot BPSK(5);  data BPSK(1) FDMA; acquisition BPSK(1/3) FDMA\n#   UHF#1  as S#1\n#   CR#1   pilot BPSK(10); data BPSK(10); acquisition BPSK(1/3)\n#\n# ASSUMPTIONS (not presented, each labelled on its component): the power splits (pilot\n# 50 %, the rest shared equally); the code lengths (one 1 ms period; BPSK(1/3) over 1 ms\n# is 341 chips); the FDMA sub-carrier offsets (S#1 at -6.0 and -3.3 MHz, where the\n# measured IOD S-band spectrum shown at the workshop has narrow components, which\n# component sits on which being an assumption; UHF#1 at -4.0 and -2.2 MHz, scaled to the\n# 10 MHz band); and, for the SM and Cext bands, whose modulation was not presented, a\n# REPRESENTATIVE single BPSK component sized to the band. The orbit altitude, 510 km,\n# is public (GMV, https://www.ion.org/gnss/abstracts.cfm?paperID=16907; Thales Alenia\n# Space press release).\n#\n# The [[shape_checks]] entry holds the E5#1 PfA design's band-limited spectrum against\n# the measured IOD E5 spectrum shown at the workshop: a narrow central BPSK(1)-like peak\n# on a BPSK(10)-like main lobe with nulls near +/-10 MHz, and sidelobes near +/-15 MHz\n# some 22 to 25 dB below the peak (IOD-1 peaked near -133 dBW/Hz, sidelobes about -155 to\n# -158 dBW/Hz). MODELLED consistency only: the measurement has no published calibration,\n# so shapes are compared, not levels.\n#\n# The C/N0 sweep reaches 57.5 dB-Hz, the peak of the IOD pass shown at the workshop\n# (a bell-shaped pass from about 41 to 57.5 dB-Hz over some 200 s, 7 to 10 dB above the\n# Galileo satellites tracked beside it).\nkind = \"leo-signal\"\n\n[receiver]\nnoise_figure_db = 2.0\nantenna_temp_k = 290.0\ncn0_dbhz = [40.0, 45.0, 50.0, 55.0, 57.5]\nreference_cn0_dbhz = 50.0\nloop_bandwidth_hz = 1.0\nintegration_time_s = 0.02\ncorrelator_spacings_chips = [1.0, 0.5, 0.2, 0.1]\nreference_spacing_chips = 0.5\ntracking_threshold_dbhz = 25.0\n\n[acquisition]\naltitude_km = 510.0\noscillator_ppm = 0.5\ncoherent_ms = 1.0\nnoncoherent = 1\npfa_cell = 1e-4\n\n[trade]\nreference_signal = \"celeste-iod-e5-1-pfa\"\nslant_tec_tecu = 50.0\nslant_range_km = 1000.0\n\n[[signals]]\nname = \"celeste-iod-e5-1-pfa\"\nsystem = \"Celeste IOD\"\nsource = \"WORKSHOP\"\nsource_ref = \"presented at the ESA NAVISP LEO-PNT workshop, 2026\"\ncentre_mhz = 1191.795\ntx_bandwidth_mhz = 51.15\nallocation = \"rnss\"\norbit_altitude_km = 510.0\nnotes = \"E5#1 Classical Pilot, data option PfA.\"\n\n[[signals.components]]\nrole = \"pilot\"\nmodulation = \"BPSK(10)\"\npower_fraction = 0.5\ncode_length_chips = 10230\nnote = \"power split and code length assumed\"\n\n[[signals.components]]\nrole = \"data\"\nmodulation = \"BPSK(1)\"\npower_fraction = 0.5\ncode_length_chips = 1023\nnote = \"PfA; power split and code length assumed\"\n\n[[signals]]\nname = \"celeste-iod-e5-1-pfb\"\nsystem = \"Celeste IOD\"\nsource = \"WORKSHOP\"\nsource_ref = \"presented at the ESA NAVISP LEO-PNT workshop, 2026\"\ncentre_mhz = 1191.795\ntx_bandwidth_mhz = 51.15\nallocation = \"rnss\"\norbit_altitude_km = 510.0\nnotes = \"E5#1 Classical Pilot, data option PfB.\"\n\n[[signals.components]]\nrole = \"pilot\"\nmodulation = \"BPSK(10)\"\npower_fraction = 0.5\ncode_length_chips = 10230\nnote = \"power split and code length assumed\"\n\n[[signals.components]]\nrole = \"data\"\nmodulation = \"BPSK(10)\"\npower_fraction = 0.5\ncode_length_chips = 10230\nnote = \"PfB; power split and code length assumed\"\n\n[[signals]]\nname = \"celeste-iod-s-1\"\nsystem = \"Celeste IOD\"\nsource = \"WORKSHOP\"\nsource_ref = \"presented at the ESA NAVISP LEO-PNT workshop, 2026\"\ncentre_mhz = 2492.028\n